window.CISI_DATA.regulation = {"key":"regulation","title":"UK Financial Regulation","subtitle":"CISI Level 3","examFormat":"75 questions - 1h30 - CBT (multiple choice)","chapters":[{"id":"reg-ch1","number":1,"title":"The Regulatory Environment","examWeight":"12 of 75 questions (~16%)","summaryHtml":"<p><strong>Chapter overview:</strong> This chapter covers the architecture of UK financial regulation following the split of the FSA into the FCA and PRA (2013), the rules firms and individuals must follow, and the gateway into (and out of) regulated status.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FSMA 2000</strong> is the primary legislation. The <strong>general prohibition (s19)</strong> makes it a criminal offence (max 2 years' imprisonment + unlimited fine) to carry on a regulated activity in the UK without authorisation or exemption.</li>\n<li><strong>Twin peaks regulation:</strong> the <strong>PRA</strong> (part of the Bank of England since the Financial Services Act 2012) prudentially regulates banks, building societies, credit unions, insurers and major investment firms with a single objective (safety and soundness), plus an insurance objective. The <strong>FCA</strong> regulates conduct for all firms and is the sole prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms. FCA objectives: 1 strategic (markets function well) + 3 operational (consumer protection, integrity, competition). Since <strong>FSMA 2023</strong>, both regulators share a secondary objective: international competitiveness and growth.</li>\n<li><strong>12 Principles for Businesses</strong> are the FCA's high-level rulebook; breach = disciplinary sanction. Principle 12 (<strong>Consumer Duty</strong>, live since 2023, extended to closed products <strong>31 July 2024</strong>) disapplies Principles 6 and 7 for in-scope retail business. Firms out of scope must still meet Principle 6 and the <strong>FTOC</strong> six outcomes. The PRA has 8 <strong>Fundamental Rules</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SM&amp;CR</strong> (banking 2016, all firms 2016 onward) has 3 pillars: the <strong>Senior Managers Regime</strong> (SMFs, regulator-approved, Form A, 90-day statutory clock, Statement of Responsibilities, Management Responsibilities Map, Duty of Responsibility), the <strong>Certification Regime</strong> (firm self-certifies \"significant harm\" functions annually — PRA scope = Material Risk Takers only), and the <strong>Conduct Rules</strong> (5 individual rules, 4 additional Senior Manager (SC) rules; Rule 6 replaces Rule 4 where Consumer Duty applies). Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators are excluded from SM&amp;CR. The <strong>Fit and Proper Test (FIT)</strong> assesses honesty/integrity/reputation, competence/capability and financial soundness.</li>\n<li><strong>Prudential standards:</strong> GENPRU/BIPRU (CRD firms), MIFIDPRU (MiFID investment firms, effective 1 Jan 2022) and IPRU-INV (other firms) set capital rules. The CRD/CRR frameworks use 3 Pillars (minimum capital; supervisory add-on; disclosure) and 3 capital tiers (Core Tier 1, Tier 2, Tier 3).</li>\n<li><strong>Regulatory infrastructure:</strong> CMA (competition), HMRC (info-sharing), FPC (systemic risk, meets ≥4x/year, publishes the Financial Stability Report), BoE (monetary stability via the MPC, meeting 8x/year, and financial stability, incl. lender of last resort), HMT (overall responsibility; appoints FCA CEO/chair), TPR (auto-enrolment under Pensions Act 2008, ages 22 to State Pension age). Internationally: FATF (40 Recommendations on AML/CTF), BIS (est. 1930, central bank of central banks) and IOSCO set standards that shape UK rules without being directly binding.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulated activities</strong> require both a specified investment and a specified activity under the RAO 2001. Key exemptions: Appointed Representatives (s39), RIEs/RCHs (s285), Lloyd's members (s316), the FSMA Exemption Order 2001 (central banks, supranationals), and Part 20 professional firms (DPBs). Key exclusions: absence of holding out, group/joint enterprise, newspaper advice, trustees/nominees, employee share schemes, overseas persons.</li>\n<li><strong>Authorisation</strong> requires satisfying the <strong>Threshold Conditions</strong> (legal status, location of offices, prudent conduct, effective supervision, appropriate resources, suitability, business model, claims representatives). Statutory determination: 6 months (complete application) / 12 months (incomplete).</li>\n<li><strong>Training and Competence:</strong> firms must assess and supervise competence (T&amp;C Sourcebook); retail investment advisers need a <strong>Statement of Professional Standing (SPS)</strong>, valid 12 months, requiring QCF Level 4 and <strong>35 hours' CPD</strong> per year (min 21 hours structured).</li>\n</ul>","sections":[{"heading":"1. The Regulatory Environment (FCA, PRA, objectives, supervision)","html":"\n<h4>1.1 FSMA 2000 and the Financial Services Act 2012</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Financial Services and Markets Act (FSMA)</strong> came into effect in 2001 and is the primary UK legislation regulating financial services, replacing the largely self-regulated pre-existing framework. Following the <strong>Financial Services Act 2012</strong> (effective April 2013), the single regulator (the FSA) was split into the <strong>Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)</strong>, the <strong>Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA)</strong> and the <strong>Financial Policy Committee (FPC)</strong> (part of the Bank of England).</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>PRA</th><th>FCA</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Regulates</td><td>Prudential regulation of banks, building societies, credit unions, insurers and major investment firms</td><td>Authorisation &amp; supervision of all other financial institutions (conduct); also prudential regulator of solo-regulated firms (eg, asset managers, stockbrokers)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accountable to</td><td>Not directly accountable to any government body; answerable to Parliament (House of Commons Treasury Committee) via the Bank of England</td><td>UK Government via HM Treasury</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Part of</td><td>Bank of England (Financial Services Act 2016 established the Prudential Regulation Committee, PRC)</td><td>Independent private company (special privilege: not required to use \"limited\" in its name)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Funding</td><td colspan=\"2\">Both funded entirely from fees paid by regulated firms</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The FCA has powers to investigate and prosecute <strong>insider dealing</strong> and <strong>market abuse</strong>, is the UK <strong>Listing Authority</strong>, oversees the <strong>Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS)</strong>, and (jointly with the PRA) the <strong>Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS)</strong>.</p>\n<p>This framework is the <strong>\"twin peaks\" approach</strong>: two supervisors oversee deposit-takers, insurers and investment banks — PRA (prudential) and FCA (conduct/market).</p>\n<p>FSMA applies to all \"persons\" (natural and legal persons, incl. firms, partnerships, trusts). It is a <strong>criminal offence</strong> to conduct regulated activity by way of business unless authorised or exempt — the <strong>general prohibition</strong> (<strong>Section 19 FSMA</strong>).</p>\n<h4>1.2 Statutory Objectives</h4>\n<p><strong>FCA</strong> — 1 strategic objective + 3 operational objectives (Financial Services Act 2012):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Strategic objective</strong> — ensuring relevant markets function well.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer protection objective</strong> — securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers (not preventing all risk of loss); FCA has regard to differing risk levels, consumer experience/expertise, need for advice/information, and the principle that consumers take some responsibility for their decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Integrity objective</strong> — protecting/enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system, which includes: soundness, stability and resilience; not being used for financial crime; not being affected by market abuse; orderly operation of markets; reliability/transparency of price formation; suitability of the listing rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Competition objective</strong> — promoting effective competition in consumers' interests. Factors: needs of different consumer types (incl. need for informed-choice information); ease of switching provider; ease of new entrants; extent competition encourages innovation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>PRA</strong> — single general objective: <em>\"promoting the safety and soundness of PRA-authorised persons\"</em>, advanced by (a) ensuring PRA firms' business avoids adverse effects on UK financial stability, and (b) minimising the adverse effect of a PRA firm's failure on stability. PRA also has an <strong>insurance objective</strong> — appropriate protection for policyholders.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary objective (both regulators, Financial Services and Markets Act 2023):</strong> facilitate the international competitiveness of the UK economy and its medium/long-term growth (subject to international standards) — only applies when advancing existing operational objectives.</p>\n<p><strong>Accountability mechanisms:</strong> Annual report to Treasury (scrutinised by Parliamentary committees); rules must relate to statutory objectives; judicial review if objectives misapplied; regulatory failure accountability for serious supervisory failures.</p>\n<h4>1.2.1 Supervisory approach</h4>\n<p>FCA supervision focuses on senior management competence and delivers <strong>credible deterrence</strong> via a consistent, transparent penalty framework. PRA supervision follows 3 principles: <strong>judgement-based, forward-looking, and focused on key risks</strong> (assessed against the \"Threshold Conditions\"). PRA uses a <strong>risk element framework</strong>: gross risk (potential impact on UK financial stability + external context/business model risk) is offset by mitigating factors (actions the firm takes) to reach a <strong>net risk</strong> assessment.</p>\n<p>Post-crisis, the FCA moved from a \"light touch\" (reactive) model to a more <strong>intrusive, proactive</strong> model with two features: (1) enhanced analysis and risk identification (business model analysis, mystery shopping, on-site visits); (2) greater focus on <strong>outcome testing</strong> rather than just systems and controls.</p>\n<h4>1.2.2 Risk-based approach</h4>\n<p>Following the 2007–08 global financial crisis, the FCA increased focus on firm culture and conduct. In 2019 it published <em>\"FCA Mission: Approach to Supervision\"</em>, outlining 8 supervisory principles. The FCA's supervisory model has <strong>3 types of work</strong>: <strong>Proactive</strong> (pre-emptive harm identification via business model analysis/culture review), <strong>Reactive</strong> (dealing with emerging issues), <strong>Thematic</strong> (wider diagnostic/remedy work across multiple firms).</p>\n<h4>1.2.3 Conduct Risk</h4>\n<p>The FCA does not give a precise definition of <strong>conduct risk</strong> — broadly, the risk to customers and market integrity from how firms and staff conduct themselves. <strong>Treating Customers Fairly (TCF)</strong>, with its 6 principles, was introduced in 2006 and has since been renamed <strong>Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC)</strong>.</p>\n<h4>1.3 Rule-Making Powers</h4>\n<p><strong>Part 9A FSMA</strong> empowers the FCA to make legally binding rules on authorised firms (regulated and unregulated activity) to advance its operational objectives. The PRA has similar powers limited to PRA-authorised persons. FSMA also empowers both regulators to: grant/vary/cancel authorisation (<strong>Part 4A permission</strong>); supervise firms on an ongoing basis; impose disciplinary measures/sanctions; and enforce the regulatory framework using credible deterrence.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2. Principles for Businesses (incl. Consumer Duty and FTOC)","html":"\n<h4>2.1 The FCA's 12 Principles for Businesses</h4>\n<p>Breach of a Principle is a disciplinary offence (Principles are legally binding on all authorised firms):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Integrity</strong> — conduct business with integrity.</li>\n<li><strong>Skill, care and diligence</strong> — conduct business with due skill, care and diligence.</li>\n<li><strong>Management and control</strong> — organise/control affairs responsibly and effectively with adequate risk management systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Financial prudence</strong> — maintain adequate financial resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Market conduct</strong> — observe proper standards of market conduct.</li>\n<li><strong>Customers' interests</strong> — due regard to customers' interests, treat them fairly.</li>\n<li><strong>Communications with clients</strong> — clear, fair, not misleading communications.</li>\n<li><strong>Conflicts of interest</strong> — manage conflicts fairly.</li>\n<li><strong>Customers: relationships of trust</strong> — ensure suitability of advice/discretionary decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Clients' assets</strong> — arrange adequate protection for client assets.</li>\n<li><strong>Relations with regulators</strong> — open/cooperative dealings; disclose appropriately anything the PRA/FCA would reasonably expect notice of.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer Duty</strong> — act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>2.2 Consumer Duty</h4>\n<p><strong>Implementation:</strong> rules effective 2023 for new/existing products; <strong>31 July 2024</strong> for closed products (products only available to existing customers, no new investment, being wound down).</p>\n<p><strong>Scope</strong> (retail customers): consumer credit (CMCOB/FPCOB); deposit-taking (BCOBS) — covers consumers, micro-enterprises, charities with turnover &lt; <strong>£1 million</strong>, and trustees acting outside trade/business/profession; insurance (ICOBS, excludes reinsurance); investments (COBS, business with non-professional clients — Duty applies to the process used to determine client status even if the client later elects professional status); mortgages (MCOB, regulated mortgages only — not unregulated buy-to-let/commercial lending). Applies to prospective as well as existing customers. Firms are only responsible for their own activities unless a regulatory/legal obligation exists (not for overseeing the whole distribution chain).</p>\n<p><strong>Excluded wholesale activities</strong> (non-exhaustive): manufacture for wholesale-only purposes (retail market business definition); non-retail financial instruments with minimum denomination/investment of <strong>£50,000</strong>; certain market activities; insurance for large commercial risks or risk located outside the UK; group insurance policy distribution; benchmark administration activity. Duty applies on a proportionate basis to wholesale firms that can materially influence retail outcomes (eg, product design/price, distribution, communications, customer support).</p>\n<p><strong>Consumer Principle:</strong> Principle 12 applies to in-scope firms; where it applies, <strong>Principles 6 and 7 no longer apply</strong> to that retail business. Firms out of scope continue to comply with Principles 6 and 7 (not 12).</p>\n<p><strong>Cross-cutting rules</strong> — firms must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Act in good faith</strong> towards retail customers (honest, fair, open dealing; consistent with reasonable customer expectations; considered at product/service design stage).</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid foreseeable harm</strong> to retail customers (customers must understand/accept inherent risk; firms not responsible for protecting against ALL foreseeable harm, but must be clear/transparent).</li>\n<li><strong>Enable and support</strong> retail customers to pursue their financial objectives (firms not responsible for customers' own decisions, but must be clear/transparent).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4 \"Good Outcomes\":</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Products and Services</strong> — designed to meet the target market's needs (interacts with PROD sourcebook); manufacturers ensure fit for target market and review performance; distributors ensure marketing/distribution reflects target market and take reasonable steps to prevent sales outside it.</li>\n<li><strong>Price and Value</strong> — FCA does not regulate price itself, but expects fair value (relationship between price and benefit received); fair value ≠ identical outcomes for all; new/existing customer pricing can differ but must be clearly communicated.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer Understanding</strong> — clear, timely communication enabling informed decisions about features, costs/risks/benefits, and suitability for needs; tailored to the target market's financial understanding.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer Support</strong> — appropriate service throughout the relationship; fair, clear, not misleading; matched to target market's financial awareness.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.2.2 Accountability</h4>\n<p>New Conduct Rule under SM&amp;CR for in-scope firms: <strong>Conduct Rule 6</strong> — \"You must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers.\" (Rule 6 does not apply to firms out of scope of the Duty.) Senior managers are held accountable via SM&amp;CR for embedding and ongoing compliance. The requirement for a Consumer Duty \"champion\" was <strong>removed by the FCA in February 2025</strong>.</p>\n<h4>2.3 Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC)</h4>\n<p>Applies to firms <strong>out of scope</strong> of the Consumer Duty (no direct retail relationship / material influence, or product/service falls under a Duty exemption). Expressed via Principle 6 and the <strong>\"client's best interests rule\"</strong>: <em>\"A firm must act honestly, fairly and professionally in accordance with the best interests of its client.\"</em></p>\n<p><strong>6 FTOC outcomes:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fair treatment is central to firms' corporate culture.</li>\n<li>Products/services are designed to meet identified consumer group needs and targeted accordingly.</li>\n<li>Consumers get clear information, kept appropriately informed before/during/after sale.</li>\n<li>Advice given is suitable and takes account of circumstances.</li>\n<li>Products perform as led to expect; service is of an acceptable, expected standard.</li>\n<li>No unreasonable post-sale barriers to change product, switch provider, claim or complain.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>2.4 PRA Fundamental Rules</h4>\n<p>Apply proportionately to all PRA firms. Firms must:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Conduct business with integrity.</li>\n<li>Conduct business with due skill, care and diligence.</li>\n<li>Act in a prudent manner.</li>\n<li>Maintain adequate financial resources at all times.</li>\n<li>Have effective risk strategies and risk management systems.</li>\n<li>Organise and control affairs responsibly and effectively.</li>\n<li>Deal with regulators openly/cooperatively and disclose appropriately.</li>\n<li>Prepare for resolution, so the firm can be resolved in an orderly manner with minimum disruption to critical services.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"heading":"3. Fitness and Propriety (SM&CR, Certification Regime, Conduct Rules, FIT)","html":"\n<h4>3.1 Background</h4>\n<p>Following the 2007–08 crisis and conduct failings (eg, LIBOR manipulation), the <strong>Parliamentary Commission for Banking Standards (PCBS)</strong> recommended a new accountability framework focused on senior management. This led to the <strong>Senior Managers &amp; Certification Regime (SM&amp;CR)</strong>, introduced to banking in <strong>2016</strong> and later extended to <strong>all FSMA-authorised firms</strong>, except <strong>Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators</strong>.</p>\n<p>SM&amp;CR comprises: <strong>Senior Managers Regime (SMR)</strong>, <strong>Certification Regime (CR)</strong>, and <strong>Conduct Rules</strong>.</p>\n<p>For solo-regulated firms, 3 proportionate tiers apply: <strong>Core scope</strong> (baseline requirements, majority of firms), <strong>Limited scope</strong> (fewer requirements), <strong>Enhanced scope</strong> (significantly greater requirements).</p>\n<p>A senior manager is only guilty of misconduct if the FCA <strong>proves</strong> they failed to take reasonable steps to prevent a regulatory breach (burden of proof on the FCA), though senior managers should be able to evidence reasonable steps taken.</p>\n<h4>3.2 Senior Managers Regime (SMR)</h4>\n<p>Captures individuals performing <strong>Senior Management Functions (SMFs)</strong> — eg, CEO, CFO, CRO, and others such as head of compliance, MLRO, head of internal audit. SMF holders require <strong>regulatory approval</strong> and are subject to <strong>annual fitness and propriety assessments</strong>. Persons holding SMFs include the Chair and NEDs, who can be held accountable for boardroom decisions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Form A</strong> — application submitted by the firm for regulatory approval of an SMF candidate; an abbreviated form may apply for existing approved persons moving to a similar function.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulatory references</strong> — firms must take all reasonable steps to obtain references from the candidate's current and all previous employers over the past <strong>6 years</strong>, before submitting Form A.</li>\n<li><strong>Statement of Responsibilities</strong> — sets out the aspects of the firm's affairs the SMF holder will manage; each responsibility is normally allocated to a single senior manager (if shared, division must be clearly articulated).</li>\n<li><strong>90-day statutory clock</strong> — FSMA requires the FCA/PRA to determine fitness and propriety within <strong>90 days</strong> of receiving Form A; the clock stops if the regulator requests further information, resuming once received.</li>\n<li><strong>Prescribed Responsibilities</strong> — allocated to the most senior SMF holders; recorded in the Statement of Responsibilities.</li>\n<li><strong>Management Responsibilities Map</strong> — documents a firm's management/governance arrangements, incl. allocation of prescribed responsibilities, for transparency of accountability.</li>\n<li><strong>Duty of Responsibility</strong> — requires SMF holders (esp. those with prescribed responsibilities) to take reasonable steps to fulfil their responsibilities and to prevent, stop or remedy breaches.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2.1 Application to Banks and Deposit Takers (selected SMFs)</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>SMF</th><th>Function</th><th>Regulator</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>Chief Executive</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Chief Finance</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>Executive Director</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Chief Risk</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>Head of Internal Audit</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>Head of Key Business Area</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>Group Entity Senior Manager</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8</td><td>Credit Union SMF (small credit unions only)</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>9</td><td>Chairman</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10</td><td>Chair of Risk Committee</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>11</td><td>Chair of Audit Committee</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12</td><td>Chair of Remuneration Committee</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>13</td><td>Chair of Nominations Committee</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>14</td><td>Senior Independent Director</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>15</td><td>(Insurance companies only)</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>16</td><td>Compliance Oversight</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>17</td><td>Money Laundering Reporting Officer (MLRO)</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>Other Overall Responsibility</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>SMFs 9–14 should be held by <strong>approved Non-Executive Directors (NEDs)</strong>, not executives.</p>\n<h4>3.2.2 Application to FCA Solo-Regulated Firms</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Firm type</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Limited Scope</td><td>Fewer requirements — eg, limited permission consumer credit firms, sole traders, non-mainstream authorised professional firms, oil/energy market participants, service companies, local authority/RSL subsidiaries, certain insurance intermediaries, internally managed AIFs.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Core</td><td>\"Baseline\" SM&amp;CR requirements.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enhanced</td><td>Small proportion of solo-regulated firms; extra rules apply.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Core/Enhanced firms typically require: SMF1 (CEO), SMF3 (Executive Director), SMF9 (Chair), SMF16 (Compliance Oversight), SMF17 (MLRO), SMF18 (Other Overall Responsibility — Enhanced only), SMF27 (Partner). Sole traders with no employees need only SMF16. Limited Permission Consumer Credit firms use SMF29 (Limited Scope Function, equivalent to the old APER CF8).</p>\n<h4>3.3 The Certification Regime (CR)</h4>\n<p>Certified individuals are <strong>not approved by the regulator</strong> — the firm itself assesses and certifies (annually) that they are \"fit and proper,\" issuing an annual <strong>certificate</strong>. Firms must assess at recruitment and annually thereafter.</p>\n<p><strong>PRA scope:</strong> limited to <strong>Material Risk Takers (MRTs)</strong> / \"Code staff\" as defined in the Capital Requirements Regulation (CRR) — subject to the Remuneration Code.</p>\n<p><strong>FCA scope</strong> includes \"significant harm functions\": CASS oversight, benchmark submission/administration, proprietary trading, significant management, qualification-required functions, managers of certified staff, and MRTs — as well as legacy SIF roles now outside the SMR.</p>\n<p>Where a certificate is refused (individual not fit and proper), the firm must give written notice of proposed steps and notify the FCA. Multiple certification functions performed by one individual require fitness/propriety assessment of each, but can be covered by a single certificate.</p>\n<p><strong>Overseas staff:</strong> MRTs/Code staff are always in scope, wherever located. Individuals dealing with UK clients from overseas are in scope. Overseas individuals not dealing with UK clients and not MRTs are not certified persons.</p>\n<h4>3.4 The Conduct Rules</h4>\n<p>Apply to all relevant employees except ancillary staff (eg, cleaners, catering). Breaches and formal disciplinary action must be notified to the regulator.</p>\n<p><strong>Individual Conduct Rules (all staff):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><td>Rule 1</td><td>Act with integrity.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 2</td><td>Act with due skill, care and diligence.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 3</td><td>Be open and cooperative with the FCA, PRA and other regulators.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 4</td><td>Pay due regard to customers' interests and treat them fairly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 5</td><td>Observe proper standards of market conduct.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 6</td><td>Act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers (Consumer Duty firms only).</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rules 4 and 6 are mutually exclusive</strong>: Rule 6 applies (replacing Rule 4) only for individuals at firms in scope of the Consumer Duty; Rule 4 continues to apply at firms out of scope. So only 5 individual rules apply to any one individual at any one time.</p>\n<p><strong>Senior Manager Conduct Rules (SC1–SC4)</strong> — additional to the 5 individual rules:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td>SC1</td><td>Take reasonable steps to ensure the business for which you are responsible is controlled effectively.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SC2</td><td>Take reasonable steps to ensure the business complies with relevant regulatory requirements/standards.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SC3</td><td>Take reasonable steps to ensure delegation is to an appropriate person, with effective oversight.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SC4</td><td>Disclose appropriately any information the FCA/PRA would reasonably expect notice of.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Detail is found in the FCA's <strong>SYSC</strong> sourcebook: SYSC 23 (introduction/classification), SYSC 24 (allocation of prescribed responsibilities), SYSC 25 (management responsibilities maps/handover), SYSC 26 (overall/local responsibility), SYSC 27 (certification regime).</p>\n<h4>3.5 The Fit and Proper Test (FIT)</h4>\n<p>Individuals performing SMFs must be approved as fit and proper by the regulator before taking up the role; the firm bears responsibility for satisfying the regulator. Three key criteria:</p>\n<h5>3.5.1 Honesty, Integrity and Reputation</h5>\n<p>Regard to whether the person has: been convicted of a criminal offence (esp. fraud/dishonesty/financial crime); been subject to an adverse civil finding/settlement; been subject to regulatory investigation/disciplinary proceedings; been the subject of a justified complaint; been refused/had revoked a trading licence; been involved in an insolvent business; or been disqualified as a director/dismissed from a position of trust. Each case assessed on its merits, considering seriousness and time elapsed.</p>\n<h5>3.5.2 Competence and Capability</h5>\n<p>Regard to whether the person satisfies the Training and Competence Sourcebook requirements, has demonstrated relevant experience/training, and has adequate time to perform the function.</p>\n<h5>3.5.3 Financial Soundness</h5>\n<p>Regard to whether the person is subject to an outstanding judgment/award, or has filed for bankruptcy/been adjudged bankrupt/had assets sequestrated/made arrangements with creditors. The FCA will not normally require a statement of assets/liabilities; limited financial means alone does not affect suitability.</p>\n"},{"heading":"4. Prudential Standards (Capital Adequacy)","html":"\n<h4>4.1 Capital Adequacy Requirements</h4>\n<p><strong>Principle 4</strong> (Financial prudence) requires firms to \"maintain adequate financial resources\" — capital and liquidity resources sufficient to meet liabilities as they fall due.</p>\n<p>Three FCA Handbook modules set the standards:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>GENPRU</strong> (General Prudential Sourcebook) — high-level standards/principles/capital requirements for firms subject to the UK Capital Requirements Directive (UK CRD).</li>\n<li><strong>MIFIDPRU</strong> (Prudential Sourcebook for UK MiFID Investment Firms) — effective <strong>1 January 2022</strong>; applies to UK MiFID firms that are not banks/credit institutions.</li>\n<li><strong>IPRU-INV</strong> (Interim Prudential Sourcebook for Investment Businesses) — capital rules for firms not subject to CRD/CRR or MIFIDPRU (eg, authorised professional firms, Lloyd's underwriting agents/members' advisers, fund managers incl. SIPP operators, exempt CAD/local firms, service companies, financial advisory firms under a MiFID exemption, credit unions offering junior ISAs).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>CRD/CRR</strong> apply to credit institutions (banks/deposit takers), are managed by the PRA, and use a <strong>3-Pillar</strong> framework:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pillar 1</strong> — minimum capital requirements for credit, market and operational risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Pillar 2</strong> — firm/supervisor judgement on whether additional capital is needed beyond Pillar 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Pillar 3</strong> — public disclosure of risk, capital and risk management information to improve market discipline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A firm must notify its regulator <strong>immediately</strong> if it knows its capital resources have fallen below the prescribed minimum. CRD firms must also consider the loss-absorbency (quality) of capital, split into 3 <strong>tiers</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Core Tier One</strong> — eg, permanent share capital, reserves, externally verified interim profits.</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Two</strong> — eg, long-term subordinated debt, revaluation reserves.</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Three</strong> — eg, short-term subordinated debt, interim trading book profit and loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under IPRU-INV, all firms must comply with two overarching requirements: (1) hold the amount/type of financial resources required by the rules at all times; (2) notify the regulator immediately if in breach, or expecting to be in breach, of the financial resources rule.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Investment Firms Prudential Regime (IFPR)</strong> simplifies requirements for FCA solo-regulated MiFID investment firms, focusing on the potential harm a firm poses to consumers/markets and ensuring sufficient capital/liquid assets for an orderly wind-down. It follows the EU's Investment Firms Directive/Regulation (effective 2021 in the EU), with the UK regime effective <strong>2022</strong>, contained in <strong>MIFIDPRU</strong>. FCA-stated outcomes: business-model-aligned prudential regime; meaningful/consistent requirements; less time on complex capital calculations; understandable, accessible rules consolidated into a single sourcebook.</p>\n"},{"heading":"5. The UK Regulatory Infrastructure","html":"\n<h4>5.1 Competition and Markets Authority (CMA)</h4>\n<p>Not a financial services body, but works closely with the FCA. Promotes competition for consumers by: investigating mergers that could restrict competition; conducting market studies/investigations; investigating breaches of anti-competitive agreement/dominant position prohibitions; bringing criminal cartel-offence proceedings; enforcing consumer protection legislation; cooperating with sector regulators; advising on rights/obligations; and monitoring government subsidies.</p>\n<h4>5.2 HM Revenue &amp; Customs (HMRC)</h4>\n<p>The FCA, PRA and HMRC have agreed to share information and expertise on banks' behavioural responses to regulatory/tax changes, to identify and address issues more quickly and in a coordinated way.</p>\n<h4>5.3 Financial Policy Committee (FPC)</h4>\n<p>Since 2009 the Bank of England has had a statutory objective to \"contribute to protecting and enhancing the stability of the financial systems of the UK.\" From April 2013, the independent <strong>FPC</strong> was created at the BoE to identify, monitor and act on systemic risks. The FPC meets <strong>at least 4 times a year</strong> and publishes records of formal meetings, plus the BoE's <strong>biannual Financial Stability Report (FSR)</strong>.</p>\n<h4>5.4 The Bank of England (BoE)</h4>\n<p>Central bank functions split into two areas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Monetary stability</strong> — stable prices/currency confidence; the <strong>Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)</strong> sets the base interest rate, meeting <strong>8 times a year</strong> (roughly every 6 weeks), to meet the Government's inflation target.</li>\n<li><strong>Financial stability</strong> — surveillance/market intelligence to detect threats; BoE may act as <strong>lender of last resort</strong> in exceptional circumstances. Works with HM Treasury and other central banks/international organisations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.5 HM Treasury (HMT)</h4>\n<p>Government department responsible for public finance and economic policy; has overall responsibility for the UK financial services sector. The <strong>FCA reports directly to HMT</strong>. The <strong>Chancellor of the Exchequer appoints the FCA's CEO and chair</strong>.</p>\n<h4>5.6 Occupational Pension Schemes</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Pensions Regulator (TPR)</strong> protects workplace pensions and safeguards member rights by regulating employers, trustees, pension specialists and advisers. Schemes may be <strong>contributory</strong> (employee + employer contributions) or <strong>non-contributory</strong> (employer only); and <strong>defined contribution (DC)</strong> (no guaranteed final pot value) or <strong>defined benefit (DB)</strong> (guaranteed % of final salary, eg, final salary schemes).</p>\n<p>The <strong>Pensions Act 2008</strong> introduced <strong>automatic enrolment</strong>: employers must auto-enrol eligible employees (those <strong>aged 22 to State Pension age</strong>, earning above a set threshold) into a workplace pension and contribute. Any employer with at least one employee has this legal duty. TPR enforcement includes compliance notices, penalty notices and fines; late compliance requires backdating contributions to the date the employee first met the age/earnings criteria; unpaid debts can be recovered through the courts.</p>\n<h4>5.7 Impact of Overseas Legislation on UK Financial Services</h4>\n<p>The <strong>European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018</strong> converted direct EU legislation into UK law and preserved UK laws implementing EU obligations (\"assimilated law\"), so the same rules applied post-Brexit as before, as far as possible. EU Guidelines/Guidance from ESMA and the EBA continue to apply until replaced by the FCA/PRA.</p>\n<p>UK firms lost automatic <strong>passporting</strong> rights into the EU (and vice versa); local authorisation (or local exemptions) is now needed.</p>\n<p><strong>UK MiFIR</strong> (onshored version of Regulation (EU) No 600/2014) has applied to all UK regulated firms since <strong>1 January 2021</strong>, alongside <strong>UK MiFID</strong> (implementing MiFID 2014/65/EU via FCA rules).</p>\n<p><strong>HMT's Wholesale Markets Review (2021)</strong> assessed how much retained EU regulation needed adapting for UK markets, guided by: upholding high regulatory standards; promoting openness/competitiveness; delivering fair/proportionate regulation; supporting economic growth.</p>\n<p><strong>Financial Services and Markets Act 2023</strong> — received Royal Assent <strong>June 2023</strong>; some provisions effective <strong>August 2023</strong>, remainder <strong>1 January 2024</strong>. Gives HMT, PRA and FCA powers to revoke retained EU law and replace it with UK-specific rules, and introduces the new secondary objective on international competitiveness/growth for both regulators.</p>\n<h5>5.7.1 Why Overseas Legislation Matters</h5>\n<p>Global firms face overlapping extraterritorial regimes. Example: <strong>Dodd-Frank</strong> and <strong>EMIR</strong> both have extraterritorial reach based on client identity/domicile — eg, a UK bank branch has a UK EMIR reporting obligation regardless of where a derivative trade is executed or the counterparty's domicile. Listing/disclosure obligations also depend on where an issuer lists.</p>\n<h5>5.7.2 International Intergovernmental Bodies</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FATF</strong> (Financial Action Task Force) — global AML/counter-terrorist-financing standard-setter; has developed <strong>40 Recommendations</strong>; over <strong>200 countries</strong> have committed to implementing them; monitors compliance and holds non-compliant countries to account.</li>\n<li><strong>BIS</strong> (Bank for International Settlements) — established <strong>1930</strong>; \"bank for central banks\"; owned/governed by central banks; Board of Directors has <strong>18 members</strong>, including <strong>6 ex-officio Directors</strong> (Central Bank Governors of Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, the UK and the USA); <strong>63 central banks/monetary authorities</strong> are members; the <strong>Governor of the BoE</strong> represents the UK.</li>\n<li><strong>IOSCO</strong> (International Organisation of Securities Commissions) — global securities regulation standard-setter; forum for regulators from over <strong>130 jurisdictions</strong>; Board has <strong>34</strong> securities regulators; the <strong>CEO of the FCA</strong> represents the UK on the IOSCO Board.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.8 Oversight and Other Influencing Bodies</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Internal Audit</strong> — independent, informed view of firm risks based on full access to records/data; reports directly to the audit committee/board, helping oversee senior management.</li>\n<li><strong>External Audit</strong> — must have relevant industry knowledge/competence, be objective/independent, report directly to the supervisor (or indirectly via the firm if direct reporting isn't permitted), exercise professional scepticism, and identify/respond to risks of material misstatement.</li>\n<li><strong>Trustees</strong> — hold/manage assets on behalf of a beneficiary/organisation; can delegate investment management but remain ultimately responsible; may use an investment sub-committee to advise the trustee board.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance</strong> — starts at the top; independent, sufficiently resourced, and subject to periodic review by internal audit. The <strong>BIS</strong> issued 10 principles for banks (applicable, proportionately, to all firms). ESMA guidelines on MiFID II compliance functions continue to apply post-Brexit for Level 3 measures existing pre-Brexit, but subsequent ESMA changes do not automatically apply in the UK.</li>\n<li><strong>Industry Bodies</strong> — generalist (not product-specific), champion the sector's success (eg, <strong>TheCityUK</strong>, promoting the UK as a global financial centre). Members subject to a code of conduct (eg, the <strong>FX Global Code</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Professional Bodies</strong> — voluntary membership (except accountancy/actuarial sectors, which have statutory powers); members bound by codes of conduct that take precedence over an employer's internal code (potential ethical dilemma). Responsible for professional exams (eg, <strong>CISI</strong>, Chartered Banker Institute). CISI's objective: set standards of professional excellence/integrity for securities, investment, wealth and financial planning professionals.</li>\n<li><strong>Trade Associations</strong> — represent members in a specific sector; advocate for proportionate rules with legislators/regulators. <strong>Product-specific</strong>: ISLA (securities lending), ICMA (bonds/repos), ISDA (OTC derivatives), FIA (exchange-traded derivatives). <strong>Generalist</strong>: AFME, UK Finance, ABI, Association of Foreign Banks.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"heading":"6. Regulated and Prohibited Activities","html":"\n<h4>6.1 The General Prohibition</h4>\n<p>FSMA Part II sets out the <strong>general prohibition</strong>: no person may carry on (or purport to carry on) a regulated activity in the UK unless authorised or exempt. Contravention is a <strong>criminal offence</strong> — max <strong>2 years' imprisonment and an unlimited fine</strong>. Agreements made in contravention are <strong>unenforceable</strong> by the offender; the other party can recover money/property transferred and claim compensation. <strong>Defence</strong>: the person took all reasonable precautions and exercised all due diligence to avoid committing the offence.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> X buys shares via UNA Ltd, an unauthorised broker. X can either keep the shares and do nothing, or sue UNA to recover money/damages (returning the shares) for breach of the general prohibition. UNA's staff also face criminal liability (up to 2 years + unlimited fine).</p>\n<p><strong>Key exemption provisions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Section 39 FSMA + FSMA (Appointed Representatives) Regulations 2001 — appointed representatives (ARs).</li>\n<li>Section 285 FSMA — recognised investment exchanges/clearing houses.</li>\n<li>Section 312A FSMA — MTF operators exercising certain rights.</li>\n<li>Section 316 FSMA — members of the Society of Lloyd's.</li>\n<li>FSMA Exemption Order 2001 — central banks, supranational bodies, local authorities, charities, industrial/provident societies, certain government-overseen bodies.</li>\n<li>Part 20 FSMA — special regime for professional firms.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2 Prohibition Orders</h4>\n<p><strong>Section 56 FSMA</strong> allows the FCA/PRA to make a <strong>prohibition order</strong> against an individual not considered fit and proper, barring them from a specified function (or functions generally). Breach = criminal offence, liable to a fine (defence: reasonable care/due diligence). An authorised firm must take reasonable care that individuals it is responsible for do not breach prohibition orders; orders can be varied/revoked on application. Prohibition orders can also apply to exempt persons/professional firms under the Part 20 regime.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 59 FSMA</strong> — a firm must take reasonable care that no one performs a role requiring regulatory approval (SMF/controlled function) without confirmed approval.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 71 FSMA</strong> — a private person can sue a firm for damages for loss suffered from breach of statutory duty where an individual acted in breach of a s56 prohibition order or performed a controlled function without approval (s59); non-private persons (businesses, trusts) can also sue in prescribed circumstances.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 138D, Financial Services Act 2012</strong> — a person suffering loss from an authorised person's breach of an FCA rule (unless stated otherwise) has a right to claim damages; claimant need only show the rule breach and resulting loss.</p>\n<h4>6.3 Regulated Activities (RAO)</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (Regulated Activities) Order 2001 (\"RAO\")</strong> defines regulated activities by reference to (a) specified investments and (b) specified activities carried on in relation to them. An activity is only \"regulated\" if BOTH a specified activity AND a specified investment are involved.</p>\n<h5>6.3.1 Specified Investments (selected, with key thresholds)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deposits</strong> — repayable money (excludes e-money, advance payment for goods/services, security deposits).</li>\n<li><strong>Electronic money</strong> — stored monetary value, electronic substitute for cash.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under contracts of insurance</strong> — long-term and general insurance.</li>\n<li><strong>Shares</strong> — excludes OEIC shares (captured as CIS units) and some building society shares (captured as deposits).</li>\n<li><strong>Debentures</strong> — incl. debenture stock, loan stock; excludes trade bills/cheques and insurance/government securities.</li>\n<li><strong>Government and public securities</strong> — eg, gilts, US treasuries; excludes NS&amp;I products.</li>\n<li><strong>Warrants</strong> — entitlement to subscribe for shares/debentures/gilts.</li>\n<li><strong>Certificates representing securities</strong> — eg, ADRs, GDRs.</li>\n<li><strong>Units in a collective investment scheme (CIS)</strong> — authorised or unregulated; incl. AUT units, OEIC/ICVC shares.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a stakeholder pension scheme</strong> (Welfare Reform and Pensions Act 1999).</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a personal pension scheme</strong> — incl. SIPPs.</li>\n<li><strong>Options</strong> — on securities/contractually based investments, currencies, certain precious metals (gold/silver), or futures/CFDs.</li>\n<li><strong>Futures</strong> — excludes commercial (non-investment) purpose contracts.</li>\n<li><strong>Contracts for differences (CFDs)</strong> — eg, spread bets, interest rate swaps.</li>\n<li><strong>Lloyd's syndicate capacity and membership.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a funeral plan contract.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a regulated mortgage contract*</strong> — secured by first legal charge, at least <strong>40%</strong> used as the borrower's (or related party's) dwelling; incl. lifetime mortgages.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a home reversion plan*</strong> — sell part/all of home for lump sum/payments, retain right to occupy.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a home purchase plan*</strong> — Sharia-compliant mortgage alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a regulated sale and rent-back agreement*</strong> — seller remains in occupation of at least <strong>40%</strong> of the property.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit agreements</strong> (4 sub-types), consumer hire agreements, greenhouse gas emission allowances, alternative finance investment bonds (sukuk), rights/interests in other specified investments, emission allowances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>* Investments marked with an asterisk are collectively \"home finance\" transactions.</p>\n<h5>6.3.2 Specified Activities (selected)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Accepting deposits; issuing e-money; effecting/carrying out contracts of insurance as principal; insurance risk transformation.</li>\n<li><strong>Dealing in investments as principal or agent</strong> — as principal: securities and contractually-based investments; as agent: securities, contractually-based investments, plus pure protection/general insurance rights.</li>\n<li>Arranging home finance transactions; operating an MTF; operating an OTF (MiFID II — equity-like instruments not permitted on OTFs); bidding in emission auctions; credit broking; operating electronic lending systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Managing investments</strong> — discretionary management of another's securities/contractually-based investments (non-discretionary management is instead \"dealing\"/\"advising\").</li>\n<li>Assisting in administration/performance of an insurance contract; debt adjusting, counselling, collecting, administration.</li>\n<li><strong>Safeguarding and administering investments</strong> — must do BOTH to be caught (eg, a custodian bank); sub-divided into with/without arranging.</li>\n<li><strong>Advising on investments</strong> — covers securities/relevant investments; excludes advice on deposits and generic advice/information (not tailored recommendations); sub-divided into (a) advising on investments (except pension transfers/opt-outs) and (b) advising on pension transfers/opt-outs.</li>\n<li>Sending/allowing dematerialised instructions (eg, CREST); managing/acting as trustee or depositary of a UCITS or AIF; establishing/operating/winding up a CIS or a pension scheme; basic advice on stakeholder products; Lloyd's market activities; entering a funeral plan contract as provider; advising on home finance transactions; agreeing to carry on specified activity; dormant account fund operation; specified benchmarks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Advising, dealing, arranging and administration activity connected with insurance contracts is collectively \"insurance mediation activity.\"</p>\n<h4>6.4 Exemptions</h4>\n<h5>6.4.1 Appointed Representatives (ARs)</h5>\n<p>An AR contracts with an authorised <strong>principal</strong>, who accepts legal responsibility for the AR's regulated activity, making the AR an exempt person. FCA/PRA rules do not apply directly to the AR (the principal must hold the relevant permissions). Governors and customer-function individuals within the AR must be approved. Permitted AR activities include: dealing in investments as agent (pure protection/general insurance only); arranging deals/home finance transactions; assisting with insurance contract admin; arranging safeguarding/administration of assets; advising on investments/home finance/stakeholder products; agreeing to carry on a regulated activity; operating electronic lending platforms; certain consumer credit activities. <strong>ARs cannot deal as principal or manage investments.</strong></p>\n<h5>6.4.2 Recognised Investment Exchanges (RIEs) and Recognised Clearing Houses (RCHs)</h5>\n<p>FSMA gives the FCA responsibility for recognising, regulating and supervising exchanges (eg, the <strong>London Stock Exchange</strong>) and clearing houses. Once recognised, they are exempt persons — not requiring separate authorisation for exchange/clearing business.</p>\n<h5>6.4.3 FSMA Exemption Order 2001</h5>\n<p>Exempts the <strong>BoE, ECB and EEA central banks</strong> from authorisation for any regulated activity <strong>except effecting/carrying out contracts of insurance</strong>. Also exempts supranational bodies (eg, EIB, IMF). Municipal banks, local authorities and charities are exempt <strong>only for the regulated activity of accepting deposits</strong>.</p>\n<h5>6.4.4 Members of Lloyd's</h5>\n<p>The general prohibition is disapplied for Lloyd's members in relation to contracts of insurance written at Lloyd's (extended to former members who ceased underwriting after 1996), because Lloyd's activity is supervised by the Society of Lloyd's.</p>\n<h5>6.4.5 Members of the Professions</h5>\n<p><strong>Part 20 FSMA</strong> covers <strong>5 professions</strong>: accountants, solicitors, actuaries, chartered surveyors, licensed conveyancers — regulated via <strong>Designated Professional Bodies (DPBs)</strong> (eg, ICAEW, the Law Society, Institute of Actuaries, RICS) rather than direct FCA/PRA authorisation. Regulated activity must be incidental to the professional service, and firms must return to the client any commissions received (only pecuniary reward is from the client). Excluded activities include: accepting deposits; dealing as principal (market making); lending/administering home finance transactions (unless trustee/personal representative); establishing/operating/winding up a CIS or pension scheme; managing Lloyd's underwriting capacity/advising on syndicate participation; effecting/carrying out insurance contracts; providing funeral plan contracts.</p>\n<h4>6.5 Exclusions from Authorisation</h4>\n<h5>6.5.1 Exclusions from Dealing as Principal</h5>\n<p><strong>Absence of holding out</strong> — dealing as principal is only \"regulated\" for those holding themselves out as market makers, regularly soliciting the public. Other exclusions: bank lending/taking a debt instrument; a company issuing/buying back its own shares (Companies Act 1985 treasury shares); using options/futures/CFDs purely for risk management (mainly unregulated business); acting as bare trustee/nominee; sale of goods/services; group/joint enterprise; sale of a body corporate; employee share scheme; overseas person; incoming electronic commerce provider.</p>\n<h5>6.5.2 Group and Joint Enterprise Exclusions</h5>\n<p>Covers: dealing as principal; dealing as agent; arranging deals; managing investments; safeguarding/administering investments; sending dematerialised instructions; advising on investments.</p>\n<h5>6.5.3 Advice in Newspapers</h5>\n<p>No authorisation needed for advice in a newspaper/journal/periodical or regularly updated news service, provided the publication's principal purpose is news, not investment advice (subscription \"tipping\" periodicals DO require authorisation).</p>\n<h5>6.5.4 Trustees, Nominees and Personal Representatives</h5>\n<p>Excluded if the person is acting as a representative, not generally holding out as carrying on regulated activities, and not receiving additional remuneration. Covers dealing as principal, arranging, managing/safeguarding investments, sending dematerialised instructions, advising, and home finance activities. <strong>Not available</strong> for activity connected with a contract of insurance.</p>\n<h5>6.5.5 Employee Share Schemes</h5>\n<p>Excludes 4 activities: dealing as principal; dealing as agent; arranging deals/making arrangements; safeguarding and administering investments.</p>\n<h5>6.5.6 Overseas Persons</h5>\n<p>Excluded where there is no permanent UK place of business and business is done via a UK authorised/exempt person, or follows an unsolicited approach from a UK client. Covers dealing (principal/agent), arranging, advising, agreeing to carry on certain activities, operating MTF/OTF, and home finance activity/administration.</p>\n<h4>6.6 Authorisation</h4>\n<p>Persons carrying on regulated activity \"by way of business\" (broadly, commercially) must be authorised or exempt. Factors relevant to \"by way of business\": degree of continuity, commercial element, scale, and proportion relative to other (unregulated) activities.</p>\n<h5>6.6.1 The Permissions Regime</h5>\n<p><strong>Part 4A permission</strong> specifies precisely which regulated activities a firm may carry out, the relevant investments, and any special conditions (eg, a restriction preventing a firm from holding client money).</p>\n<h5>6.6.2 The Authorisation Process</h5>\n<p>Dual-regulated firms apply to the <strong>PRA as lead regulator</strong>, but the PRA can only authorise once the <strong>FCA has also consented</strong>. Application pack covers: core details (legal form, ownership, systems); controllers; business supplement/regulatory business plan (incl. capital adequacy, PI insurance, compliance procedures, governance); disclosure of significant events; approved persons forms (SMF/controlled function); checklist and declaration, incl. the application fee (FCA uses <strong>10 pricing categories</strong> reflecting complexity — <strong>non-refundable</strong> under any circumstances). <strong>Statutory deadlines: 6 months for complete applications; up to 12 months for incomplete applications.</strong> If a previously unincorporated firm incorporates (with no material change to activities), a simplified \"change of legal status\" pack with reduced fee may be used.</p>\n<h5>6.6.3 The Threshold Conditions (TCs)</h5>\n<p>Minimum standards for becoming/remaining authorised. The <strong>FSMA (Threshold Conditions) Order 2013</strong> redistributed TC responsibility between FCA and PRA; the latest TCs were issued in <strong>August 2023</strong>. For dual-regulated firms, the FCA assesses conduct issues and the PRA assesses prudential issues; for solo-regulated firms, the FCA assesses both.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Legal status</strong> — restrictions apply, eg, individuals/partnerships cannot hold banking deposits or conduct insurance business.</li>\n<li><strong>Location of offices</strong> — UK-incorporated companies need head/registered office in the UK; non-body-corporate firms with UK head office must carry on business in the UK.</li>\n<li><strong>Business to be conducted in a prudent manner</strong> — appropriate (not just \"adequate\") financial and non-financial resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Effective supervision</strong> — incl. the former \"close links\" condition (eg, group structure impact); no impediment from activity complexity, products offered, or business organisation.</li>\n<li><strong>Appropriate resources</strong> — considers quality as well as quantity; nature/scale of business; continuity risk; non-financial resources (systems, controls, plans, policies, information, human resources).</li>\n<li><strong>Suitability</strong> — fit and proper in light of proposed activities; conducted soundly/prudently; minimising financial crime risk; cooperative with the regulator; management acting with probity and adequate skills/experience.</li>\n<li><strong>Business model</strong> — strategy suitable for the regulated activities, sound/prudent, in the interests of consumers and market integrity.</li>\n<li><strong>Appointment of claims representatives</strong> — motor insurers only.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Condition</th><th>FCA-only firm</th><th>Dual-regulated: FCA</th><th>Dual-regulated: PRA</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Legal status</td><td>N/A</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Location of offices</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Prudent conduct</td><td>N/A</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effective supervision</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Appropriate resources</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Suitability</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Business model</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n</table>\n"},{"heading":"7. Training and Competence","html":"\n<h4>7.1 Overall Requirements</h4>\n<p><strong>Principle 3</strong> (management and control) requires firms to take reasonable care to organise/control affairs responsibly and effectively — including ensuring employees achieve and maintain competence for regulated activity. The FCA Handbook/PRA Rule Book <strong>SYSC</strong> chapters contain a high-level competence requirement applying to ALL UK-authorised firms (incl. wholesale). This is supplemented, for firms dealing with retail customers (and in limited cases other customers), by the <strong>Training and Competence (T&amp;C) Sourcebook</strong>, which focuses on outcomes.</p>\n<h4>7.2 Requirements for Retail Firms: Assessing and Maintaining Competence</h4>\n<p><strong>Assessment</strong> — firms must not deem an employee competent for specified activities until they have demonstrated competence and (where required) passed each module of an appropriate exam; the assessment need <strong>not</strong> occur before the employee starts the activity.</p>\n<p><strong>Supervision</strong> — employees must be appropriately supervised at all times; supervision should be significantly more intensive <strong>before</strong> an employee is assessed as competent than after. Firms must have clear criteria/procedures for when reduced supervision becomes appropriate, considering relevant experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Supervisors</strong> — must have the necessary coaching/assessment skills and technical knowledge; firms should consider requiring supervisors to pass an appropriate exam if their supervisees have not yet been assessed as competent. <strong>Where the employee advises on packaged products to retail customers, the supervisor MUST have passed an appropriate examination.</strong></p>\n<h4>7.3 Qualification Requirements Before Starting Activities</h4>\n<p>Firms must not allow employees to undertake (other than an overseeing activity) a specified activity requiring a qualification without first passing the relevant regulatory module. Activities requiring a qualification before starting: certain advising/dealing activities; acting as a broker fund adviser; advising on Lloyd's syndicate participation; acting as a pension transfer specialist.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemption conditions</strong> (for advising on packaged products, broker fund advising, Lloyd's syndicate advice, pension transfer specialism) — the employee must have: at least <strong>3 years'</strong> up-to-date relevant experience gained outside the UK; not previously been required to fully comply with the exam requirements; and passed the relevant exam module. (Last two conditions do not apply where the <strong>30-day rule</strong> exemption is used, unless the person is advising retail clients on packaged products/broker funds.)</p>\n<p><strong>30-day rule</strong> — the \"customer function\" does not extend to an individual based overseas who, in a 12-month period, spends no more than <strong>30 days</strong> in the UK, provided they are appropriately supervised by an approved person.</p>\n<p>Firms select an appropriate qualification from the FCA's list (<strong>TC App 4</strong> — \"appropriate qualification tables\"). Firms must assess training needs at the outset and at regular intervals (incl. on role change), and review training quality/effectiveness.</p>\n<h4>7.3.1 Maintaining Competence</h4>\n<p>Firms must regularly review competence, considering: technical knowledge and its application; skills/expertise; and changes in the market, products, legislation and regulation. <strong>Continuing Professional Development (CPD)</strong> — conferences, webinars, seminars, reading regulator/industry publications — helps maintain competence.</p>\n<p><strong>Activities within T&amp;C scope</strong>: designated investment business for retail clients (advising, dealing, managing, day-to-day overseeing); regulated mortgage/reversion activity (advising, designing scripted questions for non-advised sales, overseeing non-advised sales); non-investment insurance business (advising).</p>\n<h4>7.4 Statements of Professional Standing (SPS)</h4>\n<p>Under the Adviser Charging rules, retail investment advisers must hold a valid <strong>SPS</strong> from an FCA-accredited body (eg, the CISI). The SPS confirms the adviser has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Completed a <strong>QCF Level 4</strong> qualification on the FCA's approved list.</li>\n<li>Completed an annual programme of <strong>35 hours of CPD</strong>, with at least <strong>21 hours structured learning</strong>.</li>\n<li>Acted in accordance with APER and/or SM&amp;CR requirements.</li>\n<li>Agreed to adhere to a code of ethics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The SPS is <strong>valid for 12 months</strong> and contains: the adviser's name; the accredited body's name/contact details and a named signatory; the verification end date (max 12 months from verification); confirmation the qualification(s) are verified; confirmation of the adviser's annual declaration (knowledge kept up to date; adherence to ethical standards); the adviser's FCA Register reference number; and a recommendation to check the FCA Register.</p>\n<p>Qualification requirements (per <strong>TC App 4</strong> / Appendix 1.1.1R of the T&amp;C Sourcebook) apply to individuals: advising on (not dealing in) securities; advising on (not dealing in) derivatives; advising on packaged products; advising on friendly society tax-exempt policies; advising on AND dealing in securities; advising on AND dealing in derivatives.</p>\n"}],"mcqs":[{"question":"Under the 'twin peaks' model of UK financial regulation, which statement correctly distinguishes the roles of the FCA and the PRA?","options":["The PRA regulates conduct for all firms, while the FCA regulates prudential standards for banks and insurers","The PRA is responsible for prudential regulation of banks, building societies, credit unions, insurers and major investment firms, while the FCA regulates conduct for all firms and is also the prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms","The FCA and PRA have identical objectives and jointly authorise every regulated firm","The PRA regulates only insurers, while the FCA regulates only banks"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA's remit is prudential regulation of deposit-takers, insurers and major investment firms (part of the Bank of England). The FCA regulates conduct for all authorised firms and is the sole prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms such as asset managers and stockbrokers."},{"question":"To which body is the FCA directly accountable for how it carries out its functions?","options":["The Bank of England","HM Treasury","The Financial Policy Committee","The Pensions Regulator"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA is accountable to the UK Government via HM Treasury. The PRA, by contrast, is not directly accountable to any government body but is answerable to Parliament (Treasury Committee) via the Bank of England."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the FCA's statutory objectives under the Financial Services Act 2012?","options":["The strategic objective of ensuring relevant markets function well","The consumer protection objective","The prudential soundness objective for PRA-authorised persons","The competition objective"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Promoting the safety and soundness of PRA-authorised persons is the PRA's single general objective, not an FCA objective. The FCA has one strategic objective (markets function well) and three operational objectives: consumer protection, integrity and competition."},{"question":"The PRA and FCA secondary objective introduced by the Financial Services and Markets Act 2023 requires the regulators to:","options":["Prioritise consumer protection above all other objectives","Facilitate the international competitiveness of the UK economy and its medium to long-term growth, subject to international standards","Merge into a single regulator by 2025","Adopt identical rulebooks to the EU"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FSMA 2023 introduced a shared secondary objective to facilitate international competitiveness and growth, subject to international standards, which only applies when the regulators are advancing their existing operational objectives."},{"question":"A firm carries on a regulated activity in the UK without authorisation or an applicable exemption. Under Section 19 FSMA (the general prohibition), what is the maximum criminal penalty?","options":["6 months' imprisonment and a fine of £5,000","2 years' imprisonment and an unlimited fine","5 years' imprisonment only, with no fine","There is no criminal penalty, only a civil claim for damages"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Contravention of the general prohibition (s19 FSMA) is a criminal offence carrying a maximum sentence of 2 years' imprisonment and an unlimited fine. It is a defence to show all reasonable precautions and due diligence were taken."},{"question":"When Principle 12 (Consumer Duty) applies to a firm's retail business, what happens to Principles 6 and 7?","options":["They continue to apply in addition to Principle 12","They cease to apply in respect of the products/services covered by the Consumer Duty","They are replaced permanently for all of the firm's business, including wholesale business","They are suspended for 12 months only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where Principle 12 applies, Principles 6 (customers' interests) and 7 (communications with clients) no longer apply to the firm in respect of the retail products/services in scope of the Consumer Duty."},{"question":"From what date did the Consumer Duty rules take effect for closed products and services (products only available to existing customers, being wound down)?","options":["31 July 2023","1 January 2024","31 July 2024","1 January 2025"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Consumer Duty rules applied from 2023 for new and existing (open) products/services, and from 31 July 2024 for closed products and services."},{"question":"Under the deposit-taking (BCOBS) scope of the Consumer Duty, which entities are treated as within scope alongside consumers?","options":["Micro-enterprises and charities with a turnover of less than £1 million, and trustees acting outside their trade/business/profession","All corporate customers regardless of size","Only individuals classified as vulnerable consumers","Professional clients who have opted in writing to retail treatment"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"For deposit-taking activities, the Consumer Duty scope extends to consumers, micro-enterprises, charities with turnover under £1 million, and natural persons acting as trustees if acting for purposes outside their trade, business or profession."},{"question":"Which of the following is one of the Consumer Duty's three cross-cutting rules?","options":["Maximise short-term product sales","Act in good faith towards retail customers","Guarantee a minimum investment return","Refer all complaints to the Financial Ombudsman Service within 24 hours"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The three cross-cutting rules require firms to: act in good faith towards retail customers; avoid foreseeable harm to retail customers; and enable and support retail customers to pursue their financial objectives."},{"question":"The Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC) initiative applies to which firms?","options":["Only firms that are dual-regulated by the FCA and PRA","Firms out of scope of the Consumer Duty, either because they do not materially impact retail customer outcomes or their products/services are exempt","Only wholesale firms dealing exclusively with professional clients","All FCA-regulated firms without exception, regardless of Consumer Duty status"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FTOC applies to firms that are out of scope of the Consumer Duty requirements — because they don't deal directly with (or materially impact) retail customers, or their products/services fall within a Consumer Duty exemption."},{"question":"How many PRA Fundamental Rules are there?","options":["5","6","8","12"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"There are 8 PRA Fundamental Rules, covering integrity, skill/care/diligence, prudent conduct, adequate financial resources, risk management, organisation/control, open dealings with regulators, and resolution planning."},{"question":"Which two categories of firm are specifically excluded from the Senior Managers & Certification Regime (SM&CR)?","options":["Sole traders and partnerships","Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators","Banks and building societies","Investment firms subject to MIFIDPRU"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The SM&CR applies to all FSMA-authorised firms except Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators."},{"question":"Before submitting Form A for a candidate to a Senior Management Function, a firm must take all reasonable steps to obtain a regulatory reference covering the candidate's employment over what period?","options":["The past 2 years","The past 3 years","The past 5 years","The past 6 years"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under SM&CR, firms must take all reasonable steps to obtain regulatory references from the candidate's current employer and any previous employers within the past 6 years, prior to submitting Form A."},{"question":"FSMA imposes a statutory obligation on the FCA and PRA to reach a determination on a person's fitness and propriety within how many days of receiving Form A?","options":["30 days","60 days","90 days","180 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FSMA requires the regulators to determine an SMF application within 90 days of receiving Form A, although the statutory 'clock' stops if the regulator requests further information from the firm."},{"question":"Under the Certification Regime, the PRA limits its scope to which category of individual?","options":["All customer-facing staff regardless of seniority","'Material Risk Takers' (Code staff) as defined in the Capital Requirements Regulation","Only individuals holding a Senior Management Function","Ancillary staff such as cleaners and catering staff"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA's Certification Regime scope is limited to 'material risk takers' (MRTs)/'Code staff' as defined in the CRR, who are subject to the Remuneration Code. The FCA's scope is broader, covering various significant-harm functions."},{"question":"A firm is in scope of the Consumer Duty. Which individual Conduct Rule now applies to its staff in place of Conduct Rule 4?","options":["Conduct Rule 1 (Integrity)","Conduct Rule 3 (Open and cooperative with regulators)","Conduct Rule 6 (Act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers)","SC2 (Comply with regulatory requirements)"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Where the Consumer Duty applies, Conduct Rule 6 ('You must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers') replaces Rule 4 for that firm's staff. Rule 4 continues to apply only at firms out of scope of the Duty — the two rules are mutually exclusive."},{"question":"How many additional Conduct Rules (the 'SC' rules) apply specifically to Senior Managers, on top of the individual Conduct Rules?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"There are 4 Senior Manager Conduct Rules (SC1–SC4), covering effective control of the business, compliance with regulatory requirements, appropriate delegation/oversight, and appropriate disclosure to regulators."},{"question":"When assessing an individual's fitness and propriety under the FIT test, which three factors are the most important criteria considered?","options":["Educational background, nationality and length of service","Honesty/integrity/reputation, competence/capability, and financial soundness","Age, physical health and marital status","Salary level, seniority and number of direct reports"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FIT test considers a person's honesty, integrity and reputation; competence and capability; and financial soundness. Limited financial means alone does not, in itself, affect suitability."},{"question":"The Prudential Sourcebook for UK MiFID Investment Firms (MIFIDPRU) came into effect on which date?","options":["1 January 2021","1 January 2022","31 July 2023","1 January 2024"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MIFIDPRU, containing the UK's Investment Firms Prudential Regime (IFPR), came into effect on 1 January 2022, applying to UK MiFID investment firms that are not banks/credit institutions."},{"question":"How frequently does the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) meet to set the base interest rate?","options":["Once a year","4 times a year","8 times a year (roughly every 6 weeks)","Every month"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The MPC meets 8 times a year, roughly every six weeks, to set the Bank of England's base interest rate in pursuit of the Government's inflation target."},{"question":"Under the Pensions Act 2008, automatic enrolment obligations apply to employees who are:","options":["Aged 18 to 65, regardless of earnings","Aged 22 to State Pension age, earning above a certain threshold","Aged 16 to 21 only","Any age, provided they have opted in writing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Pensions Act 2008 requires employers to auto-enrol eligible employees — those aged 22 to State Pension age, earning above a set threshold — into a workplace pension scheme."},{"question":"How many Recommendations has the Financial Action Task Force (FATF) developed to ensure a coordinated global response to money laundering, terrorist financing and organised crime?","options":["10","25","40","50"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FATF has developed 40 Recommendations, which over 200 countries have committed to implementing, covering AML, counter-terrorist financing and related organised crime/corruption issues."},{"question":"For rights under a regulated mortgage contract to be a 'specified investment' under the RAO, what minimum proportion of the property must be used as the borrower's (or a related party's) dwelling?","options":["25%","40%","50%","75%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A regulated mortgage contract requires that at least 40% of the mortgaged property is used as the borrower's, or a related party's, dwelling. The same 40% occupation threshold applies to regulated sale and rent-back agreements."},{"question":"An Appointed Representative (AR) is exempt from the general prohibition under Section 39 FSMA. Which of the following activities is the AR NOT permitted to carry out on an exempt basis?","options":["Advising on investments","Arranging deals in investments","Dealing in investments as principal","Assisting in the administration and performance of a contract of insurance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Appointed Representatives are specifically prohibited from dealing in investments as principal, or managing investments, under the AR exemption regime — these activities fall outside the scope of permitted AR activity."},{"question":"Under the FSMA Exemption Order 2001, the Bank of England, the ECB and EEA central banks are exempt from authorisation for any regulated activity EXCEPT:","options":["Accepting deposits","Effecting or carrying out contracts of insurance","Dealing in investments as principal","Advising on investments"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FSMA Exemption Order 2001 exempts central banks (BoE, ECB, EEA central banks) from authorisation for any regulated activity, apart from effecting or carrying out contracts of insurance."},{"question":"A firm submits a complete application for authorisation to the FCA. Within what statutory maximum period must the FCA determine the application?","options":["3 months","6 months","9 months","12 months"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The regulators have statutory deadlines to determine authorisation applications: 6 months for complete applications, and up to 12 months for incomplete applications."},{"question":"Under the Threshold Conditions applicability table, which of the following conditions applies to a dual-regulated firm's PRA assessment but NOT its FCA assessment?","options":["Suitability","Effective supervision","Legal status","Appropriate resources"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"For dual-regulated firms, 'Legal status', 'Location of offices' and 'Prudent conduct' are assessed by the PRA, not the FCA. 'Appropriate resources' and 'Business model' are, conversely, FCA (not PRA) conditions for dual-regulated firms. 'Suitability' and 'Effective supervision' apply to both."},{"question":"Under the Training and Competence Sourcebook, when must an employee advising retail clients on packaged products have a supervisor who has passed an appropriate examination?","options":["Only if the supervisor also advises clients directly","The supervisor must always have passed an appropriate examination in this scenario","Only if the employee has fewer than 3 years' experience","Never — supervisors are exempt from examination requirements"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where an employee is advising on packaged products to retail customers, the firm must ensure the supervisor has passed an appropriate examination — this is a mandatory requirement, not merely something to 'consider'."},{"question":"Under the T&C Sourcebook's '30-day rule', an overseas-based individual performing a customer function is excluded from the qualification requirement provided they spend no more than how many days in the UK in a 12-month period (and are appropriately supervised)?","options":["10 days","20 days","30 days","90 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The customer function does not extend to an overseas-based individual who spends no more than 30 days in the UK in a 12-month period, provided they are appropriately supervised by an approved person."},{"question":"A Statement of Professional Standing (SPS) for a retail investment adviser is valid for a maximum of how long, and how many hours of CPD must be completed annually?","options":["6 months validity; 20 hours CPD, 10 structured","12 months validity; 35 hours CPD, at least 21 structured","24 months validity; 50 hours CPD, all structured","12 months validity; 15 hours CPD, no structured minimum"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The SPS is valid for a maximum of 12 months. Advisers must complete a minimum of 35 hours of relevant CPD each year, of which at least 21 hours must be structured learning."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the 'General Prohibition' and where does it come from?","back":"Section 19 of FSMA 2000: it's a criminal offence to carry on a regulated activity in the UK by way of business without authorisation or exemption. Max penalty: 2 years' imprisonment + unlimited fine (Crown Court); 6 months + £5,000 (magistrates)."},{"front":"If a firm breaches the General Prohibition, what happens to contracts made with clients?","back":"They are voidable at the OPTION OF THE CLIENT — not automatically void, and not voidable at the firm's or the FCA's discretion."},{"front":"Name 4 things that are NOT specified investments even though they seem like they should be.","back":"Currencies, gold/silver bullion itself, physical commodities, and National Savings Certificates. (But options on precious metals ARE specified investments.)"},{"front":"What's the difference between 'authorisation' and 'Part 4A permission'?","back":"Authorisation confers regulated status generally. Part 4A permission is the specific grant defining exactly which regulated activities a firm can actually carry out."},{"front":"What are the 8 threshold conditions a firm must meet for Part 4A permission?","back":"Legal status, location of offices, effective supervision (incl. close links), appropriate resources, suitability, business model, prudent conduct, and appointment of a claims representative (insurers)."},{"front":"Who assesses which threshold conditions for a dual-regulated firm?","back":"The PRA leads and makes the final decision, but must get FCA consent. FCA assesses conduct issues; PRA assesses prudential issues (capital, liquidity, prudent conduct)."},{"front":"What is 'twin peaks' regulation?","back":"Since 2013: FCA regulates conduct for ALL firms + is prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms. PRA regulates prudential matters for banks, insurers, building societies, credit unions and major investment firms (dual-regulated firms)."},{"front":"Who is the PRA accountable to?","back":"The Court of the Bank of England (NOT directly to HM Treasury or Parliament like the FCA). Common trap: people assume PRA reports to HMT like the FCA does."},{"front":"Who is the FCA accountable to, and who appoints its CEO?","back":"Accountable to the UK Government via HM Treasury. The Chancellor of the Exchequer appoints the FCA's CEO and chair."},{"front":"What are the FCA's 4 objectives?","back":"1 strategic: markets function well. 3 operational: consumer protection, market integrity, and effective competition."},{"front":"What is the PRA's objective?","back":"A single general objective: safety and soundness of PRA-authorised firms, plus a specific objective for policyholder protection (insurance)."},{"front":"FPC vs MPC — what's the difference?","back":"FPC (Financial Policy Committee): systemic risk across the whole financial system, meets 4x/year, publishes biannual Financial Stability Report. MPC (Monetary Policy Committee): sets interest rates, meets monthly (8x/year)."},{"front":"How often must the FCA report to HM Treasury?","back":"At least annually — covering how it discharged its functions and the extent to which its objectives were met."},{"front":"What are the 3 pillars of SM&CR?","back":"Senior Managers Regime (regulator pre-approved), Certification Regime (firm self-certifies annually), and Conduct Rules (baseline standards for almost everyone)."},{"front":"Name 3 roles that are ALWAYS Senior Manager Functions.","back":"Any director (executive, non-executive, Chairman), MLRO, and Compliance oversight function."},{"front":"Give 2 examples of Certification Functions (not Senior Manager Functions).","back":"Retail customer advisers / financial planners, and material risk takers. (Investment advisers are certification, not senior manager, functions — a classic trap.)"},{"front":"How is a Senior Manager approved vs how is a Certified person approved?","back":"Senior Manager: pre-approved by the FCA/PRA via Form A before starting the role. Certified person: self-certified by the FIRM (not the regulator) as fit and proper, repeated annually."},{"front":"From what point must an individual comply with the Conduct Rules?","back":"Immediately from appointment — for ALL relevant staff, including certification functions (not just senior managers)."},{"front":"What are the 5 individual Conduct Rules that apply to virtually everyone under SM&CR?","back":"1) Act with integrity 2) Act with due skill, care and diligence 3) Be open and cooperative with regulators 4) Pay due regard to customers' interests / treat them fairly (or Rule 6 Consumer Duty where it applies) 5) Observe proper standards of market conduct."},{"front":"What extra Conduct Rules apply ONLY to Senior Managers?","back":"Take reasonable steps to ensure the business area you're responsible for complies with regulatory requirements; is controlled effectively; that delegation is to an appropriate person and overseen; and disclose appropriately anything the regulator would expect notice of."},{"front":"What are the 3 pillars of the Fit and Proper Test (FIT)?","back":"Honesty/integrity/reputation, competence/capability, and financial soundness. No fixed time limit on considering past convictions if relevant."},{"front":"What are the FCA's Principles for Businesses in essence, and how do they differ from detailed Rules?","back":"12 high-level overarching standards that apply at all times — a firm can be disciplined for breaching a Principle even without breaching any specific detailed rule."},{"front":"What replaced Principles 6 and 7 for in-scope retail business, and when?","back":"The Consumer Duty (Principle 12), live since 2023, extended to closed products from 31 July 2024. Out-of-scope firms still follow Principle 6 (customers' interests) and Principle 7 (communications)."},{"front":"What are the 4 outcomes of the Consumer Duty?","back":"Products & services, price & value, consumer understanding, and consumer support."},{"front":"Name the cross-cutting rules of the Consumer Duty.","back":"Act in good faith towards retail customers; avoid causing foreseeable harm; and enable/support customers to pursue their financial objectives. ('Suitability of advice' is NOT one of these — that's a COBS suitability rule, not a cross-cutting Duty rule.)"},{"front":"What is a Recognised Investment Exchange (RIE) and does membership authorise a firm?","back":"An exchange granted recognised status by the FCA, exempt from needing its own Part 4A permission. Membership of an RIE does NOT itself authorise a member firm to do investment business."},{"front":"What is a Multilateral Trading Facility (MTF)?","back":"A system bringing together multiple buyers/sellers of financial instruments under non-discretionary rules; the operator does not take proprietary positions against client orders."},{"front":"What is an Appointed Representative (AR), and who's legally responsible for their conduct?","back":"A firm/person conducting regulated activities under an authorised 'principal' firm's umbrella without its own authorisation. The principal firm takes full legal responsibility. A person/firm cannot be both authorised AND exempt (as an AR) for the same activities at the same time."},{"front":"When are members of a Designated Professional Body (DPB, e.g. solicitors, accountants) exempt from authorisation?","back":"Only when the regulated activity is INCIDENTAL to their main professional business and they are NOT separately remunerated for it."},{"front":"What's excluded from needing Part 4A authorisation regarding intra-group dealing?","back":"Dealing as principal/agent, arranging deals, managing investments, and safeguarding/administering investments — when done WITHIN a company group or joint enterprise."},{"front":"What sourcebook sets out training/competence standards and who is responsible for defining them?","back":"The Training and Competence (T&C) Sourcebook applies further requirements to RETAIL firms. It's ultimately up to the FIRM to define job roles and competence standards, not the FCA directly. SYSC applies broader competence requirements to ALL firms."},{"front":"How long do employees have to obtain relevant exams under T&C rules?","back":"Generally within 30 months of starting the role requiring it. An employee must pass the regulatory module before dealing with RETAIL clients."},{"front":"What is the Competition and Markets Authority's (CMA) role vs the FCA's?","back":"CMA investigates mergers, anti-competitive behaviour and general consumer protection issues across ALL sectors — not a financial regulator. Works closely with the FCA but is a separate government body."},{"front":"What is the role of The Pensions Regulator (TPR)?","back":"Safeguards members of WORK-BASED pension schemes (formed 2005) and enforces automatic enrolment duties. The FCA regulates advisers/providers of PERSONAL pension plans instead."},{"front":"Under the Pensions Act 2008, who has a duty to auto-enrol employees?","back":"ANY employer with at least one employee — no minimum employee-count threshold."},{"front":"What are the FCA's supervision approaches (proactive, reactive, thematic) — quick definitions?","back":"Proactive: preventative, structured conduct assessment of firms. Reactive: remedial work on problems that have already emerged. Thematic: wider diagnostic/remedy work where harm exists across MULTIPLE firms."},{"front":"What's the difference between an FCA Handbook 'Rule' (R) and 'Guidance' (G)?","back":"Rules (R) are BINDING on authorised persons — breach can trigger disciplinary action. Guidance (G) is NOT binding — it just helps interpret the rules and reduces (but doesn't eliminate) breach risk."},{"front":"What powers does s165 FSMA 2000 give the FCA, and how fast must a firm respond?","back":"Powers to require firms to produce documents/information during an investigation. Firms must respond within 'a reasonable time period' — not a fixed number of hours/days."},{"front":"What's the order of an FCA enforcement investigation into a firm?","back":"1) FCA launches investigation → 2) sends preliminary findings letter → 3) Regulatory Decision Committee sends decision notice → 4) disciplinary action."}],"cisiMcqs":[{"question":"Under the individual accountability regime, which of the following is a conduct rule applying to certified persons?","options":["Act with due skill, care and diligence","Combat financial crime","Deliver the best possible result to clients","Communicate information in a way which is clear, fair and not misleading"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is Conduct Rule 2."},{"question":"An authorised firm must ensure that only certificated persons perform certification functions. Which of the following is most likely to be an example of a certification function?","options":["External auditor","Compliance oversight function","Money laundering reporting officer","Retail customer adviser"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Retail customer advisers are certification functions. Compliance oversight and MLRO are senior manager functions."},{"question":"According to the Regulated Activities Order 2001, which of the following are specified investments?","options":["Commercial property, including buy-to-let investments","Gold bullion","Insurance covering vehicle breakdown","Units in a collective investment scheme"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Tangible assets and car breakdown insurance are not specified investments."},{"question":"Which of the following are specified investments, as defined by the Regulated Activities Order?\nIOptions on the FTSE 100 index\nIICurrencies\nIIIOptions on gold\nIVGold","options":["III only","I and III","I, II and III","I, III and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Neither tangible assets (including precious metals such as gold or silver), nor currencies are specified investments within the meaning of the RAO and FSMA 2000. Options are classified as specified investments under FSMA 2000 if the underlying asset of the option is itself a specified investment (such as an option on a future), or is a precious metal (i.e. gold, silver, platinum or palladium)."},{"question":"Under the Individual Accountability Regime a firm must take reasonable care to ensure that individuals undertaking significant harm functions are:","options":["Appointed representatives","Approved persons","Certified persons","Authorised persons"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Individuals undertaking such functions are known as certified persons."},{"question":"On which of the following trading venues is an exchange trade reported automatically and published immediately?","options":["CREST","SEAQ","SETSqx","SETS"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Reporting and publication are automatic and immediate respectively on SETS."},{"question":"When providing services to a professional client, for which of the following investments would a firm not need to gain Part 4A Permission?","options":["Options on a copper future","Gold","Regulated mortgages","Gilts"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"All are specified investments except gold."},{"question":"Which of the following is a regulated activity?","options":["Dealing in currencies","Administering the estate of a deceased person","Establishing a stakeholder pension","Advising on secondary mortgages"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Currencies are not a specified investment, personal representatives and executors of a will are excluded activities, and advising on secondary mortgages are not regulated mortgage products."},{"question":"Which of the following activities would be 'out of scope' under the FCA's Consumer Duty initiative?","options":["Giving advice on listed shares to a retail customer","A fund management firm managing a regulated collective investment scheme for a range of customers","Providing group life assurance products to employees of a company","A retail bank providing deposit-taking activities to its customers"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Providing group life assurance products is specifically excluded under the Consumer Duty, whether to employees of a company or to anybody else. The other three activities are all, either directly or indirectly, provided to retail customers."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a specified investment under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Gilt repo","Funeral plan","Residential property","Spread bet"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Property is not specified under FSMA 2000."},{"question":"Which of the following does the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 make responsible for ensuring a firm complies with the appropriate regulations?","options":["FCA","The firm itself","The Treasury","The RDC"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"It is the responsibility of the firm to ensure it complies with the appropriate regulations."},{"question":"Which of the following may cause an individual not to be 'fit and proper'?","options":["Undischarged bankruptcy","Conviction for tax evasion","Refusal of entry into a trade association associated within financial services","All may cause an individual to fail the test"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All would be considered in assessing if an individual is 'fit and proper'."},{"question":"According to the Capital Requirements Directive, what should a firm do if its financial resources fall below the financial resources requirement set by the PRA?","options":["Borrow money from a bank to increase its financial resources","Change its financial resource requirement","Inform the PRA immediately","Cease trading immediately"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A firm must notify the PRA immediately if it has breached or believes it may shortly breach the financial resources requirement."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT regulated under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Managing a Lloyd's of London syndicate","Producing tip sheets","Share advice in a newspaper column","Managing a unit trust invested in property"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Investment advice in newspapers is not a specified investment according to the Regulated Activities Order."},{"question":"When a firm is found in breach of the regulations, place the following in the order that procedures would occur:\nIRegulatory Decision Committee sends a decision notice\nIIDisciplinary action\nIIIFCA sends a preliminary findings letter\nIVFCA launches an investigation","options":["III, IV, I, and II","II, IV, I, and III","IV, III, I, and II","IV, I, III, and II"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The first stage is to launch an investigation, followed by sending a preliminary findings letter. Having considered representations from the firm, the Regulatory Decision Committee sends a decision notice to the firm."},{"question":"Which of the following is true with regard to the Training and Competence Sourcebook?","options":["Authorised firms are only required to ensure that their certified persons are competent for their role in the firm","​​​​​​FCA firm employees must have their competency reviewed regularly​","Appropriate examinations must be obtained for relevant roles within three years of working in investment banking","All employees must take appropriate examinations"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under FCA Principle for Businesses 3, management and control, all employees must be competent. The approved examinations must generally be passed within 30 months. Appropriate examinations are only a requirement for employees undertaking certain specific functions."},{"question":"Section 168 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 permits the FCA to appoint competent persons to carry out investigations on its behalf if:","options":["An offence has been committed","Too many complaints have been received","The firm is being liquidated","The Ombudsman insists that this is the case"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"S168 permits the FCA to do this if an offence has been committed. This could be for a wide range of offences including, but not exclusively, market abuse."},{"question":"Under which piece of legislation is the requirement for a firm to be authorised set out?","options":["Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Companies Act 2006","Criminal Justice Act 1993"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In order to be authorised, a person applies to the Regulator under Part 4A of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000."},{"question":"Under the SM&CR, a firm must take all reasonable steps to obtain a 'regulatory reference' from a candidate's current employer and anyone who has been a candidate's employer in how many past years?","options":["Three years","Five years","Six years","Seven years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Prior the application being submitted to the regulators a firm must, under the SM&CR, take all reasonable steps to obtain a 'regulatory reference' from a candidate's current employer and anyone who has been a candidate's employer for the past six years."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2012, which of the following is NOT a statutory objective of the Financial Conduct Authority?","options":["Protecting the integrity of the UK financial system","Promoting effective competition in the interests of consumers","Securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers","Reducing financial crime"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The need to reduce financial crime was a statutory objective of the FSA. The FCA is accountable for reducing financial crime - but this is now subsumed into the integrity objective."},{"question":"A bank wants its compliance function to align with recognised good practice. Which arrangement is most consistent?","options":["Locating compliance within front office teams to improve commercial responsiveness","Making compliance independent from business areas and adequately resourcing it","Restricting compliance to specialist staff and excluding wider employee responsibility","Allowing compliance to operate without periodic review by internal audit"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The compliance function should be independent from the business units it oversees, have adequate resources and be subject to periodic and independent review by internal audit. Compliance is not solely the responsibility of specialist staff, so the other options conflict with the text."},{"question":"S71 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 allows private persons to claim damages for losses caused by which of the following?","options":["An authorised firm","Any firm, whether authorised or not","Authorised firms and approved individuals","Approved individuals"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under s71, a private person can sue an authorised firm for loss resulting from the employment of a prohibited individual or allowing an unapproved person to undertake a controlled function."},{"question":"According to the Financial Services Act 2012, which of the following bodies are the primary financial services regulators?\nIFinancial Services Authority\nIIPrudential Regulation Authority\nIIIFinancial Conduct Authority\nIVFinancial Policy Committee","options":["I and II","II and III","III and IV","II and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA and the FCA are the primary regulators for firms in the financial services industry."},{"question":"The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 states that a Recognised Investment Exchange (RIE) is exempt from the need for authorisation in respect of S19 - the General Prohibition. Which of the following bodies recognises these investment exchanges?","options":["The Department of Trade and Industry","His Majesty's Treasury","The Home Office","The Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The status of RIE is granted by the Financial Conduct Authority."},{"question":"S56 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 allows the FCA to prohibit individuals from carrying out specified functions in relation to regulated activities, i.e. a 'prohibition order'. What penalty can the FCA impose on an individual in breach of S56 FSMA 2000?","options":["6 months in jail","2 years in jail","A fine","A jail term the regulator considers appropriate"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"S56 FSMA allows the FCA and/or the PRA to prohibit individuals from carrying out specified functions in relation to regulated activities, i.e. a 'prohibition order'. A prohibition order may be issued to anyone - whether they are approved or not. To issue a prohibition order, the regulator must first issue a warning notice followed by a decision notice. The individual may appeal to the Tax and Chancery Chamber of the Upper Tribunal. A breach of a prohibition order is a criminal matter, but is not an imprisonable offence."},{"question":"Which of the following statements about the Financial Conduct Authority is FALSE?","options":["The Chancellor of the Exchequer appoints the CEO of the FCA","The FCA is a company","The board of the FCA can delegate its statutory objectives to another body","The FCA is the conduct regulator for the financial services industry"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Although answerable to the Treasury, the FCA is a company - it is limited by guarantee, directly by the UK government. Its board cannot delegate its statutory objectives to another body."},{"question":"Which of the following is a statutory objective of the FCA?","options":["To levy specific fines in the case of money laundering activities","To ensure financial promotions issued by authorised firms are not misleading","To ensure that the relevant markets function well","To promote guidelines as to the conduct of authorised firms"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA has four objectives.\nStrategic objective: ensuring that the relevant markets function well\nOperational objectives: securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers, protecting and enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system, promoting effective competition in the interests of consumers."},{"question":"Who is responsible for reducing risks to the financial system as a whole?","options":["Financial Policy Committee","National Crime Agency","Financial Conduct Authority","Her Majesty's Treasury"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FPC has responsibility for reducing risks to the financial system as a whole.\nThe PRA takes instructions from the Financial Policy Committee (FPC) at the Bank of England."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is CORRECT concerning a multi-lateral trading facility?","options":["It is a facility where a firm engages in multiple core investment services","It is a system where a firm provides similar services to those offered by exchanges","It is a system where a firm takes proprietary positions with a client","It is a facility where a firm operates in more than one location"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An MTF is a system which provides an alternative order matching system to an exchange. The client orders match with other clients so that the firm operating the MTF does not take proprietary positions."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be exempt from requiring authorisation?","options":["Recognised investment exchange","Appointed representative","Lloyd's of London","Members of certain professions"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Lloyds of London is not exempt. It is only the members of Lloyds' syndicates that are exempt."},{"question":"Which of the following bodies oversees the macro-economic and financial issues that could cause instability in the UK?","options":["Competition and Markets Authority","Prudential Regulation Authority","Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Policy Committee"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"This is the role of the FPC. Its aim is to gain a bigger picture of the financial services and markets in order to better predict problems that may occur. It exists within the Bank of England."},{"question":"Which of the following is not a PRA Senior Manager Function for a UK bank?","options":["Chief risk function","Executive director function","Head of internal audit function","Chief finance function"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Executive director function is an FCA function, not a PRA function."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2012 who is responsible for the soundness of 'significant investment firms'?","options":["HMRC","FCA","PRA","FSA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The focus of the PRA is stability - the safety and soundness of deposit taking firms, insurers and systematically important investment firms."},{"question":"A firm is deciding which oversight body is directly accountable to the UK Government through HM Treasury for how it carries out its functions. Which body fits this description?","options":["The Prudential Regulation Authority","The Financial Policy Committee","The Bank of England","The Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Financial Conduct Authority is accountable to the UK Government via HM Treasury for how it carries out its functions. By contrast, the Prudential Regulation Authority is answerable to Parliament via the House of Commons Treasury Committee through the Bank of England, while the Financial Policy Committee is a Bank of England committee."},{"question":"Which of the following statements are correct?\nIThe FCA is responsible for ensuring consumers get a fair deal\nIIThe FCA is responsible for stability of the markets\nIIIThe PRA is responsible for ensuring consumers get a fair deal\nIVThe PRA is responsible for stability of the markets","options":["I and III","I and IV","II and III","II and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA is responsible for ensuring that financial markets work well so that consumers get a fair deal, whilst the focus of the PRA is on stability - the safety and soundness of deposit taking firms, insurers and systematically important investment firms."},{"question":"What is the maximum number of employees permitted to work in an authorised firm's compliance department?","options":["10","20","50","No maximum"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"There is no maximum (or minimum) limit to the number of employees permitted to work in a firm's compliance department."},{"question":"The Financial Conduct Authority is funded by contributions from which of the following?","options":["The Treasury indirectly through taxes","The LSE through a levy charged on transactions","Regulated firms authorised under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","The FCA through its own activities on the market"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Although the FCA is answerable to the Treasury, it is in fact a limited liability company. It is also funded by the financial services industry - businesses authorised under the FSMA 2000."},{"question":"Which of the following is not a conduct rule which must be followed by ALL accountability regime persons?","options":["You must act with integrity","You must observe proper standards of market conduct","You must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers","You must act with due skill, care and diligence"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This conduct rule (COCON Rule 6) is only applicable to SMCR roles at firms within scope of the Consumer Duty. Employees of out-of-scope firms would follow COCON Rule 4 instead - 'You must pay due regard to the interests of customers and treat them fairly'."},{"question":"Who is responsible for the regulation of individual savings accounts (ISAs)?","options":["Department of Business Innovation and Skills","HM Revenue and Customs","The Prudential Regulation Authority","The Stock Exchange"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"HM Revenue and Customs is responsible for the regulation of Individual Savings Accounts (ISAs). ISAs are tax efficient savings and investments."},{"question":"For which of the following actions can a firm NOT appeal to the Tax and Chancery Chamber of the Upper Tribunal?","options":["Refusal of Part 4A permission","Imposition of penalties under the Market Abuse Regime","Refusal of listing on the LSE","Imposition of penalties under the Criminal Justice Act"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A firm can make an appeal to the TCCUT in response to decisions made by the Regulator. The courts decide the penalties under the Criminal Justice Act. The Regulator decides on Part 4A permission, market abuse and listing on the LSE. The Regulator also decides on approved person status and the Regulator's disciplinary measures."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a specified investment?","options":["Floating rate note","Forward rate agreement","National savings certificates","Contract for differences"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"National Savings Certificates are not tradable financial instruments and therefore are not specified investments."},{"question":"Under the FCA's Senior Managers regime which of the following is ALWAYS performing a senior management function?","options":["Audit manager","Chairman of the Board of Directors","Compliance manager","Administration manager"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Any director is, by definition, ALWAYS a senior manager under the Senior Managers Regime. The other three roles mentioned may, or may not, be Senior Managers."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true of the Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC) regime implemented by the FCA?","options":["It is based on six outcomes for firms to achieve","Once implemented the firms have achieved their objectives","Firms should set reasonable expectations about the product performance","It looks to create a positive culture in the financial services industry"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FTOC is seen as a continuous process where firms continually update their policies and behaviours to meet the objectives. The purpose is to embed a culture of fair treatment of customers in the financial services. N.B. Since the advent of the Consumer Duty, FTOC only applies to firms that are out-of-scope of the Duty."},{"question":"In the Supervision sourcebook (SUP) the FCA sets out the roles within a firm that require approval. Which of the following is not included as a senior manager function?","options":["Director","Partner","Non executive director","Investment adviser"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An investment adviser is performing a certification function."},{"question":"The purpose of the Competition and Markets Authority is to ensure healthy competition between companies, and make markets work well, for the benefit of whom?","options":["UK Government","The FCA","Consumers and the economy","Companies"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Competition and Markets Authority helps to ensure healthy competition between companies in the UK for the ultimate benefit of both consumers and of the economy."},{"question":"When the Regulator receives an application for a senior manager to become an approved person and finds some problems, they will return the application:","options":["To the individual and stop the process","To the individual and wait for clarification or amendments before considering their decision","To the firm and stop the process","To the firm and wait for clarification or amendments before considering their decision"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Applications for senior manager status involve the firm applying to the Regulator."},{"question":"Which one of the following is not a senior manager function?","options":["Director function","Head of key business area","Chief Risk Officer","Significant harm function"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Significant harm functions are also known as 'certified functions'."},{"question":"Which of the following is true of designated professional bodies (DPB)?","options":["All UK professional bodies have DPB status under FSMA 2000, giving members the opportunity to conduct a range of regulated activities","Membership of a DPB gives authorisation to conduct the regulated activities prescribed in the Regulated Activities Order","Exemption via membership of a DPB is only allowable where the activities are incidental to the main business of the professional","DPBs, having exempt status, do not need to follow the FCA rules when conducting regulated activities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"If the regulated activities are incidental and the professionals do not receive separate remuneration, they are exempt from the need for authorisation. Only lawyers, accountants, actuaries, conveyancers and chartered surveyors may receive an exemption when they are are members of a DPB. Membership of a DPB does not give authorisation for regulated activities and does not exclude them from the rules."},{"question":"Which is not a specified investment?","options":["A short term bank deposit","An option on physically delivered gas oil","A gilt repo","A forward rate agreement"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"As gas oil options are physically delivered, they are not a financial instrument."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a regulated activity under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Managing a futures fund","Advising on share portfolios","Dealing in US dollars","Administering mortgages"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Dealing in currencies, including US dollars, is not a regulated activity under the FSMA 2000."},{"question":"An employer with one employee has not considered automatic enrolment because it believes the rules apply only to larger businesses. Under the Pensions Act 2008, what is its position?","options":["It must comply because any employer with at least one employee has a legal duty","It must comply because all employers irrespective of employees have a legal duty","It need not because only employers with at least five employees have a legal duty","It may defer compliance because only employers with an existing scheme have a legal duty"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Any employer with at least one employee has a legal duty to comply with automatic enrolment requirements under the Pensions Act 2008. The other options introduce thresholds or conditions not stated as the legal test."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is a statutory objective of the FCA?","options":["Ensuring the UK financial markets are competitive worldwide","Providing services that allow a firm to manage and control their risks","Implementing and monitoring financial legislation","Securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"This is one of the statutory objectives of the FCA."},{"question":"A firm wants to identify the main purpose of automatic enrolment under the Pensions Act 2008. What does this require employers to do?","options":["Offer salary sacrifice arrangements to all employees","Contribute to a pension scheme alongside eligible employees","Provide financial advice on pension transfers to all employees","Guarantee a minimum retirement income for eligible employees"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The main aim of the Pensions Act 2008 was to introduce automatic enrolment, requiring employers to automatically enrol eligible employees into a pension scheme and contribute towards it. The other options describe obligations not set out in the source."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a specified activity?","options":["Acting as principal and end user when dealing in shares","Acting in relation to a collective investment scheme","Providing safe custody services in investments","Managing investments on behalf of others"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Transactions are not regulated if the investor is also the end user and no service is offered to others."},{"question":"Personal responsibility for senior manager functions is enforced by:","options":["A set of Conduct Rules backed by a duty of responsibility","Four general principles supplemented by a code of ethics","A code of practice which incorporates a range of principles","Seven principles and a code of conduct"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"There are ten conduct rules that apply to senior managers. In addition, prescribed responsibilities are laid out for each senior manager in a Statement of Responsibility, which is legally enforceable. This imposes a duty of responsibility."},{"question":"According to Section 19 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (the 'General Prohibition'), which of the following activities would require a firm to gain authorisation by the FCA/PRA?","options":["Investment advice in newspapers and magazine articles","Television, radio and cable broadcasts about investments","Professional bodies providing advice in the normal course of business where the advice is separately remunerated","Trustees who are not being separately remunerated"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The following activities are not regarded as regulated activities:\nInvestment advice in newspapers (providing a tip sheet however is a regulated activity)\nTelevision broadcasts and similar activities\nTrustees, nominees and personal representatives (not separately remunerated) \nMembers of designated professional bodies (accountants, solicitors or actuaries) carrying on certain incidental regulated activities. However, if they are separately remunerated for those activities they do need authorisation."},{"question":"Which statement is the correct aim of the FCA's Consumer Duty?","options":["To ensure that professional clients receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services","To ensure that retail customers receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services","To ensure that eligible counterparties receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services","To ensure that financial services firms receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The aim of the FCA's Consumer Duty is to ensure that retail customers receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services."},{"question":"Which of the following Conduct Rules apply to all SMCR functions?","options":["Observe proper standards of market conduct","Disclose appropriately any information of which the FCA or PRA would reasonably expect notice","Take reasonable steps to ensure the business is organised so that it can be controlled","Take reasonable steps to ensure the firm complies with relevant requirements and standards of the regulatory system"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"'Observe proper standards of market conduct' is the correct answer. The other options apply to senior manager functions only."},{"question":"Which of the following is a specified investment under the Regulated Activities Order?","options":["Options on crude oil","Shares in an unincorporated business","US dollars","Premium bonds"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Currencies and premium bonds are not classed as specified investments under the FSMA 2000, nor are options on commodities (other than options on precious metals). Shares in any business would be a specified investment."},{"question":"Which of the following is true of the training and competence rules?","options":["Authorised firms are required to ensure their employees are competent for their role in the firm under Principle for Businesses 3, Management and Control","Approved examinations must be obtained within one year of working for an authorised firm","An employee must have passed the regulatory module before dealing with professional clients","An overseas individual may be allowed to sit just the regulatory module if they have at least four years relevant experience outside the UK"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Approved examinations must usually be obtained within 30 months of working for an authorised firm.\nAn employee must have passed the regulatory module before dealing with retail clients.\nAn overseas individual may be allowed to sit just the regulatory module if they have at least three years' relevant experience outside the UK.\nNote that a firm must regularly review the competence of its employees."},{"question":"All of the following are specified by the FCA as a certification function EXCEPT:","options":["Material risk takers","Dealing directly with clients","Supervisors of Certification Functions","Information providers"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Certification functions are: CASS oversight, proprietary trader, significant management, functions requiring qualifications, managers of certification functions, material risk takers and client dealing."},{"question":"To whom do the PRA's rule-making powers extend?","options":["Only PRA-authorised persons","All authorised persons","All regulated activities","All regulated activities and also activities that are not regulated"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Part 9A of the the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 empowers the FCA to make rules that are legally binding on authorised firms concerning regulated activity and also activity that is not regulated. The PRA has similar rule-making powers, however the powers only extend to PRA-authorised persons."},{"question":"Which of the following investments is regulated by the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Debentures","Property","Metals","Gambling"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Tangible assets and gambles are not regulated (unless in the form of a CFD)."},{"question":"Assuming that the activity is to be performed in the UK, which of the following would require authorisation under s19 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, the 'General Prohibition'?","options":["Dealing as principal for your own account","Arranging currency options","Dealing in commodities","Newspapers giving incidental investment advice"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Dealing as principal, newspaper advice and dealing in commodities are excluded activities."},{"question":"Which one of the following is NOT a statutory objective of the FCA under the Financial Services Act 2012?","options":["Securing an appropriate degree of consumer protection","Protecting the integrity of the UK financial system","Promoting effective competition","Promoting the safety and soundness of authorised persons"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FCA has a single strategic objective, which is ensuring that the markets function well. In addition the FCA has 3 operational objectives: Securing an appropriate degree of consumer protection; Protecting the integrity of the UK financial system; and Promoting effective competition The PRA has been given a single general objective of promoting the safety and soundness of PRA-authorised persons."},{"question":"Which statement best distinguishes an industry body from a professional body in UK financial services?","options":["An industry body has statutory regulatory powers, while a professional body does not","An industry body usually represents broader sector interests, while a professional body focuses on individual competence and ethical standards","An industry body handles customer complaints, while a professional body supervises prudential risk","An industry body authorises firms, while a professional body approves senior managers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Industry bodies tend to be more generalist and support broader sector interests, while professional bodies focus on examinations, competence, knowledge and ethical behaviour of individuals. Neither type authorises firms, supervises prudential risk or operates the complaints scheme."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is not one of the Principles for Businesses laid down for firms in the financial sector?","options":["Financial prudence","Customers' interests","Conflicts of interests","Prevention of money laundering"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Prevention of money laundering is not one of the principles for businesses."},{"question":"From which point in time must senior managers and certification functions comply with the Conduct Rules under the FCA's Accountability Regime?","options":["Once their firm processes their annual appraisal","From the moment a firm files a certificate with the regulator","All relevant staff must comply with the Conduct Rules from appointment","From the moment an affected employee is certificated/approved as a fit and proper person"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"All relevant staff must comply with the Conduct Rules from appointment."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is not true of the Principles for Businesses?","options":["All authorised firms must comply with the PRIN","Failure to comply with the PRIN can result in regulatory enforcement action for FCA firms","They only apply to firms authorised by the FCA","They represent a set of fundamental objectives backed by the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The PRIN are general statements of the fundamental obligations of FCA firms under FSMA 2000 and the regulatory system. They have been devised by, and are enforced by, the FCA. They apply to all authorised firms, PRA authorised firms also comply with the PRA's Fundamental Rules (FR)."},{"question":"All of the following are true of recognised investment exchanges, except:","options":["Membership does not confer authorisation as an investment business","It guarantees a market place where investors can always deal at fair prices","It provides an organised market framework within which transactions in investments can be effected","It will monitor the conduct of the market so as to prevent manipulation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Fair prices are not guaranteed, although they should be likely."},{"question":"Which of the following are not regulated investments, as defined by the Regulated Activities Order?","options":["ADRs and deep discounted bonds","Life assurance policies and certificates of deposit","Premium bonds and currencies","Bank deposits and exchange traded futures"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Premium bonds and currencies are not regulated investments. ADRs are American Depository Receipts and are specified investments."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, a person will not be carrying on a regulated activity if (s)he does which of the following?","options":["Establishes a collective investment scheme","Advises on foreign currency","Issues a tip sheet","Arranges deals in shares"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Foreign currency is not a specified investment."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2012, who is the prudential regulator for the UK insurance industry?","options":["His Majesty's Treasury","The Prudential Regulation Authority","Parliament","The Department for Business, Innovation and Skills"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA, or Prudential Regulation Authority, is the prudential regulator for the UK Insurance industry."},{"question":"Jo Wood has twice failed a key regulatory exam and the bailiffs have repossessed most of his belongings. He might not be considered fit and proper for which of the following reasons?","options":["Financially unsound and incapable","Incapable and dishonest","Dishonest and criminal","Criminal and financially unsound"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"He has no criminal convictions or dishonesty, but he is financially unsound and not competent or incapable because he has failed two key exams."},{"question":"Which one of the following statements concerning the Financial Policy Committee (FPC) is correct? The FPC meets","options":["Annually and produces an annual Financial Stability Report","Annually and produces a biannual Financial Stability Report","Quarterly and produces a biannual Financial Stability Report","Quarterly and produces an annual Financial Stability Report"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FPC meets at least four times a year and publishes a record of its formal meetings. It is also responsible for the Bank's biannual Financial Stability Report. Don't confuse the FPC with the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC), which meets monthly. Although the FPC does not meet 'quarterly', but 'at least 4 times a year', C is the best answer."},{"question":"A firm wishes to provide services in investments. According to the Regulated Activities Order, which of the following investments would NOT require the firm to seek authorisation from the FCA/PRA?","options":["Forward rate agreements","Commercial paper","Floating rate notes","Foreign exchange"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Foreign exchange (currencies) is not specified by the RAO."},{"question":"Which of the following does not reflect the range of sanctions that the PRA can impose on a firm breaching PRA rules?","options":["Maximum fine of £250,000","Varying Part 4A permission","Cancelling permission","Public censure"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The maximum fine is unlimited."},{"question":"Which types of directors are defined as senior manager functions in an FCA authorised firm?","options":["Both executive and non-executive directors, and the Chairperson","Executive directors only","Both executive and non-executive directors, but not the Chairperson","Non-executive directors only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Every member of the board of directors is defined as a senior manager in any authorised firm, irrespective of their title or status."},{"question":"Which of the following is responsible for funding the FCA?","options":["Authorised firms","His Majesty's Treasury","The Bank of England","His Majesty's Revenue and Customs"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA is a company limited by guarantee. The guarantor is the Treasury. However, on an ongoing basis, the FCA is funded by charging annual levies on its member firms."},{"question":"To whom is the FCA directly accountable?","options":["HM Treasury","Bank of England","UK Government","Its shareholders"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Although the FCA is ultimately accountable to the UK government on how it carries out its functions, it does so via HM Treasury. The FCA has no shareholders."},{"question":"A compliance officer is identifying the FCA sourcebook that applies prudential requirements to UK-regulated firms subject to MiFID, excluding banks and credit institutions. Which sourcebook applies?","options":["GENPRU","COBS","MIFIDPRU","IPRU-INV"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"MIFIDPRU contains prudential requirements that came into effect on 1 January 2022 for UK-regulated firms subject to MiFID that are not banks or credit institutions. GENPRU and IPRU-INV apply in different prudential contexts, while COBS is not a prudential sourcebook."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be considered acts of misconduct by a firm in the financial industry?","options":["A breach of the Financial Services and Markets Act","A breach of a Principle for Businesses","Failure to comply with instructions from an FCA inspector","Not immediately producing adequate information for an FCA inspection"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Failure to comply with an FCA statement of principle is an act of misconduct. This is also true for breaches of FSMA 2000, provision of false information and not complying with the instructions of an FCA inspector. Firms are permitted 'a reasonable time period' (generally taken to mean 48 hours, unless instructed otherwise) to produce adequate information for an FCA inspection."},{"question":"If an advisor carries on regulated activities without having sought Part 4A permission from the FCA, what is the effect on any contract with a client?","options":["It is void as from day one","It is void as from the date of the conviction of the advisor","It is voidable at the option of the client","It is voidable at the option of the firm"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Any agreements made by a person in contravention of the general prohibition are unenforceable by that person against the other party to the agreement. A consequence of a breach of the general prohibition is that certain agreements or contracts can be voidable, at the option of the client."},{"question":"When the FCA grants an exchange with recognised status, it:","options":["Confers authorisation to do investment business","Requires cooperation from the exchange with overseas regulators","Requires that the exchange must be fit and proper","Implies that all transactions will be settled in a timely manner"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In order to be recognised by the FCA, these exchanges and clearing houses need to be fit and proper for their purpose. Once recognised, the exchanges are referred to as recognised investment exchanges (RIEs), and the clearing houses are referred to as recognised clearing houses (RCHs). RIEs and RCHs are exempt persons in that they do not need to seek authorisation from the FCA to carry out regulated activities – they are, instead, recognised."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the FCA's attitude with regard to the enforcement of the Principles for Business?","options":["A firm can be prosecuted for failure to comply with the principles","The FCA will fine firms for violation of the principles","FCA firms can be disciplined because of a principle violation","The FCA doesn't enforce the principles"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A firm cannot be criminally prosecuted for breach of the Principles for Business but they can be disciplined by the regulator."},{"question":"Which of the following is MOST LIKELY to be a certified function?","options":["Head of internal audit","Head of financial reporting","Money laundering reporting officer","Financial planner"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A financial planner is a significant harm function, as if they fail to perform their job correctly this causes conduct risk for the consumer using their services. As such, they are always a certified function. MLROs and heads of functional areas in firms (such as the Head of Internal Audit and the Financial Controller (a.k.a. 'Head of financial reporting', this is the chief accountant reporting into the Chief Financial Officer, who is a director)) are ALWAYS Senior Manager Functions."},{"question":"The FCA is granted the power to enforce their rules and sanction and discipline those who do not follow them. Which of the following would the FCA not seek disciplinary action for?","options":["Failure to get a financial promotion approved","Not following FCA guidelines","Not following FCA rules","Not following FCA principles"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm may choose not to follow FCA guidelines (on cold calling hours for example) but failure to comply with rules and principles is an offence."},{"question":"Under the FCA's SM&CR, which of the following is most likely to be a senior management function for a UK bank?","options":["Compliance officer","Money laundering reporting officer","Customer advisor","Investment manager"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MLRO is always a senior manager function. All others are more likely to be certified functions."},{"question":"According to the Financial Services Act 2012, which one of the following is a statutory objective of the FCA?","options":["To balance the burdens and restrictions on firms with the benefits of regulation for consumers and the industry","The firm to use its resources in the most economic and efficient way","That individuals involved in the management of authorised firms must take appropriate responsibility","Protecting and enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The objectives are: Strategic objective: ensuring that the relevant markets function well Operational objectives are the following: securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers, protecting and enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system, promoting effective competition in the interests of consumers."},{"question":"According to the general prohibition (S19 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000), which of the following activities would NOT require a firm to gain authorisation by the Regulator?","options":["Purchasing ADRs on behalf of a private client","Advising on the purchase of Indian rupees","Managing a futures portfolio for a discretionary client","Broking shares for an execution only private client"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Advising on currencies, such as rupees, is not an authorised activity under FSMA 2000 as detailed in the Treasury Regulated Activities Order 2001."},{"question":"Which of the following does not require FCA/PRA authorisation?","options":["Sending dematerialised instructions in investments","Running a collective investment scheme","Dealing on behalf of others","Writing a newspaper column advising on investments"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Newspapers have a specific exemption."},{"question":"Which of the following Principles for Businesses requires firms to have appropriate risk management controls in place?","options":["Management and control","Integrity","Market conduct","Financial prudence"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Principle for Businesses 3: Management and control requires firms to take reasonable care to organise and control its affairs responsibly and effectively, with adequate risk management systems."},{"question":"Which of the following is not one of the Principles for Businesses?","options":["Skill, care and diligence","Management and control","Financial prudence","Senior management functions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Senior management functions are regulated via the Accountability Regime (SM&CR), not the Principles for Businesses."},{"question":"What is the maximum penalty for those operating an unauthorised firm carrying out regulated activities in the UK as stated in the Financial Services Markets Act 2000?","options":["Six months imprisonment and a £5,000 fine","Six months imprisonment and an unlimited fine","Two years imprisonment and a £5,000 fine","Two years imprisonment and an unlimited fine"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"If you are convicted of a breach of the General Prohibition (s19 FSMA 2000) in a Crown Court the maximum sentence is two years in jail and/ or an unlimited fine. The maximum sentence for cases heard in a magistrates court is six months and/ or a £5,000 fine."},{"question":"Which of the following are not specified investments as defined by the Regulated Activities Order?","options":["ADRs and deep discounted bonds","Life assurance policies and certificates of deposit","Premium bonds and currencies","Bank deposits and exchange traded futures"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"ADR is an American Depository Receipt.\nPremium bonds and currencies are not regulated investments."},{"question":"How would you describe a firm which has gained Part 4A permission?","options":["Authorised","Registered","Permitted","Regulated"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Part 4A permission authorises a firm to carry on regulated activities."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the objective of conduct risk regulation?","options":["Rules do matter, but outcomes for consumers and markets are the principal focus of regulatory concern","Prescriptive rules to manage the conduct of employees of financial firms are the only regulatory concern","Outcomes for consumers and markets do matter, but rules are the principal focus of regulatory concern","Outcomes for consumers and markets are the only regulatory concern"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA takes a balanced approach to the management of conduct risk. The rules do matter, but outcomes for consumers and markets are the principal focus of regulatory concern."},{"question":"Which one of the following is the FCA's strategic objective?","options":["Ensuring that the markets function well","Securing an appropriate degree of consumer protection","Promoting effective competition","Promoting the safety and soundness of authorised persons"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA has a single strategic objective, which is ensuring that the markets function well. In addition the FCA has 3 operational objectives: Securing an appropriate degree of consumer protection; Protecting the integrity of the UK financial system and Promoting effective competition\nThe PRA has been given a single general objective of promoting the safety and soundness of PRA-authorised persons."},{"question":"A Multilateral Trading Facility (MTF) is best described as:","options":["A system that brings together multiple parties that are interested in buying and selling financial instruments and which allows them to do so","A market place for eligible buyers and sellers who trade on a firm commitment basis but do not meet the minimum standards set out in MiFID","An exchange which is given a recognition order by the local competent authority to conduct its activities in a public forum","An account that meets the requirements set out by His Majesty's Revenue and Customs for a Minor's Trust Fund"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An exchange which is given a recognition order' is a recognised investment exchange and 'an account that meets the requirements of a Minors Trust Fund' is a distracter that refers to a child trust fund. 'A system that brings together multiple parties that are interested in buying and selling financial instruments and which allows them to do so' is the correct answer."},{"question":"A regulated firm is reviewing whether to strengthen its internal audit function. Which role best reflects internal audit's contribution to oversight within the firm?","options":["Developing an independent view of risks and reporting findings directly to the board","Setting prudential rules for authorised firms and monitoring market-wide stability","Approving financial promotions before they are communicated to retail clients","Investigating eligible complaints and issuing awards against financial businesses"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Internal audit is expected to develop an independent and informed view of the risks faced by a firm and be able to discuss findings directly with the audit committee and board. The other options describe functions of regulators, complaints bodies or approval processes rather than internal audit oversight 1."},{"question":"When the UK regulator allows a firm to undertake regulated activities, it grants which of the following?","options":["Authorisation","Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 prohibition","Regulatory exemption","Part 4A Permission"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Authorisation confers status, but activities must be permitted by Part 4A of the Act (Part 4A permissions)."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a type of work under the FCA supervision model?","options":["Proactive Firm Supervision","Reactive Supervision","Thematic","Proactive Intervention Framework"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Proactive Intervention Framework is a supervisory approach adopted by PRA and not the FCA."},{"question":"Provisions in the FCA’s Handbook indicated with the letter ‘R’ are binding upon whom?","options":["Approved persons","Authorised persons","The Financial Conduct Authority","Exempt persons"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Provisions in the FCA’s Handbook indicated with the letter ‘R’ are RULES – and these are binding on authorised persons (i.e. firms). If a firm contravenes a rule, it may be subject to discipline."},{"question":"Which of the following is/are considered in assessing the fitness and propriety of a firm?","options":["The competence and experience of the board","The ability to comply with the financial resources requirement regulations on an on-going basis","The specific activities to be performed","All are considered"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Firms must apply to the Regulator for authorisation under Part 4A of the FSMA 2000 and demonstrate that they are 'fit and proper'. In other words, the FCA has to satisfy itself that the firm has sufficient financial resources and management skills to undertake the relevant functions."},{"question":"What was the effect of Brexit on passporting for UK financial services firms?","options":["UK firms could continue passporting if they had FCA authorisation only","UK firms could continue passporting for wholesale business but not retail business","UK firms could no longer passport services throughout the EU without local authorisation","UK firms could no longer passport services unless they used an appointed representative"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"After Brexit, UK firms were no longer able to passport products or services throughout the EU without appropriate authorisation from the local regulator. The other options add conditions or distinctions not stated."},{"question":"Which of the following is not taken into account when the FCA assesses a firm's fitness and propriety?","options":["The ability of senior management to direct the business effectively and establish internal controls","Whether those seeking approval have suffered bankruptcy or defaulted on personal loans","The ability of the firm to meet its financial obligations on an ongoing basis","The market share the firm has within its sector of the financial services industry"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The fitness and propriety of a firm is rooted in the threshold conditions.\nThe ability of the senior management to control their firm is part of the suitability test.\nThe ability for an employee to pay their debts is part of the approved person fit and proper test, but it also plays a part in the adequate resources of a firm - employees are part of a firm's resources.\nThis condition also links with the firm's ability to meet its financial obligations.\nIt does not matter what market share the firm has."},{"question":"From which point in time must certification functions comply with the Conduct Rules under the SMCR regime?","options":["One year and no later from the certification of the employee","From the moment the firm files a valid certificate with the regulator","All relevant staff are immediately on appointment bound by the Conduct Rules","From the moment the employee receives their certificate from the RDC"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"All relevant staff must comply with the six individual Conduct Rules. This includes all staff except ancillary staff."},{"question":"If an applicant for approved person status has a criminal history covered by the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974 which of the following describes the right of the FCA to take this into account?","options":["It will not be taken into account","It might be taken into account if relevant","It would be taken into account if the offence occurred within five years","It would be taken into account if the offence occurred within ten years"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is no specified time limit here. The FCA reserves the right to consider any conviction."},{"question":"All of the following are specified by the FCA as SM&CR functions except:","options":["Executive director function","Compliance oversight function","Supervisor of certification functions","Health and safety function"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The list of senior managers is long and includes compliance oversight and directors. Certification functions also include supervisors of certification functions. health and safety is likely to be conduct staff or even ancillary staff."},{"question":"Where a firm operates under the Senior Manager and Certification Regime, which ONE of the following roles always requires approval from the Regulator? Somebody:","options":["Recruiting staff","Working in compliance","Acting as a non-executive director","With staff supervisory responsibilities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"All four could potentially require the regulator's approval as a Senior Manager Function, but only non-execs would always require such status."},{"question":"Who regulates UK banks?","options":["His Majesty's Treasury","The Prudential Regulation Authority","The London Stock Exchange","The European Central Bank"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA is the regulator - it is part of The Bank of England."},{"question":"The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 does NOT require businesses carrying out which of the following to be authorised?","options":["Managing a bond fund","Advising on currencies","Establishing a collective investment scheme","Arranging deals in investments"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Currencies are not specified investments."},{"question":"If the FCA is asked to pursue 'redress' it will:","options":["Consider the different methods of redress available to it","Impose a maximum penalty of £100,000","Refer the case to the FSCS","Follow the thresholds set out in the Claims Level Guidance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A is the best possible answer as the FCA could seek a restitution order or require an investor to contact the FSCS/FOS."},{"question":"According to the provisions under Senior Managers Regime, the responsibilities of directors must be:","options":["Clearly and appropriately apportioned","Reviewed by external auditors on an annual basis","Recorded and the records kept for twelve months from when the responsibilities change","Clear, fair and not misleading"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"As part of the application process, firms need to include a statement of responsibilities setting out the aspects of the firm’s affairs the person will be responsible for managing when performing the function. The regulators expect that each responsibility will be allocated to a single senior manager. Where a responsibility is shared between more than one senior manager regulators expect the division of responsibility and accountability for that responsibility to be clearly articulated."},{"question":"The FCA guidance on electronic communication with a client states that a firm should do all of the following, except:","options":["Be able to demonstrate that the client wishes to communicate using this type of media","If entering an agreement, make it clear to the client that a contractual relationship has legal consequences","Have in place arrangements to ensure secure transmission and receipt of the communication","Be able to demonstrate that it made it clear to the client that instructions communicated in this manner may not be acted upon immediately"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The firm should also be able to verify the authenticity and integrity of the communication, together with the date and time sent and received."},{"question":"Which of the following best reflects the 'thematic' work of the FCA?","options":["Pre-emptive identification of harm through review and assessment","It is remedial work to deal with problems that are emerging or have happened","Dealing with issues that are emerging or have happened to prevent harm growing","Wider diagnostic or remedy work where there is actual or potential harm across a number of firms"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Wider diagnostic or remedy work where there is actual or potential harm across a number of firms = Thematic\n\nIt is remedial work to deal with problems that are emerging or have happened = Reactive\n\nPre-emptive identification of harm through review and assessment = Proactive"},{"question":"Which of the following is not a sanction available to the FCA in relation to a breach of the conduct of business rules?","options":["Public censure","Fine","Prohibition order","Disqualification of directors"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Directors may only be disqualified through a court order."},{"question":"Which of the following roles should be performed by a Senior Manager Function according to SUP 10C and SYSC 24 of the FCA handbook?","options":["Working in compliance","Supervising other employees","Recruitment and training","Chair of the nominations committee"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Most chairperson functions are classified as senior manager functions and would require the individual to be approved.\nNote 1: Working in compliance is not necessarily a controlled function unless it is the compliance oversight function.\nNote 2: Only individuals supervising approved persons would need to be approved themselves.\n\nIn addition, do not be thrown by the use of specific Handbook references. Most questions that do this can still be answered without the Handbook reference: \"Which of the following roles should be performed by a senior manager function?\""}],"examFocus":["<strong>Gold, silver, currencies, and physical commodities are NOT specified investments</strong> — but options on precious metals ARE. This trips people up constantly.","<strong>Twin peaks:</strong> PRA = prudential (safety/soundness), FCA = conduct + prudential for solo-regulated firms. Dual-regulated firms answer to both.","<strong>PRA is accountable to Parliament via the Bank of England</strong> (Court of the Bank of England) — NOT directly to HM Treasury like the FCA.","<strong>FCA reports to HM Treasury</strong>; the Chancellor appoints the FCA's CEO and chair. Remember: HMT has overall responsibility for the UK financial system.","<strong>SM&CR:</strong> Senior Manager = regulator-approved (Form A). Certification = firm self-certifies annually. Conduct Rules apply to almost everyone from day one of appointment.","<strong>Threshold conditions</strong> (legal status, location of offices, effective supervision, appropriate resources, suitability, business model) are distinct from the <strong>Principles for Business</strong> — don't confuse the two lists in exam questions.","Intra-group dealing (dealing, arranging, managing, safeguarding investments <em>within</em> a company group) is <strong>excluded</strong> from needing Part 4A permission.","<strong>Non-executive directors and the Chairman are ALWAYS Senior Manager Functions</strong> — a very common exam question angle.","A breach of the General Prohibition (s19) makes contracts <strong>voidable at the option of the client</strong> — not automatically void.","<strong>FPC</strong> (systemic risk, meets 4x/year, biannual Financial Stability Report) vs <strong>MPC</strong> (interest rates, meets monthly/8x year) — these get mixed up on purpose in questions.","Guidance (the 'G' provisions in the Handbook) is <strong>not binding</strong>; Rules (the 'R' provisions) ARE binding on authorised persons."]},{"id":"reg-ch2","number":2,"title":"Conduct of Business and Client Assets","examWeight":"35 of 75 questions (~47%)","summaryHtml":"\n<p><strong>Chapter 2 is the single biggest topic in the exam (~35/75 questions).</strong> It covers COBS (Conduct of Business Sourcebook) end-to-end: who it applies to, how clients are categorised, what firms must tell clients, how promotions are policed, suitability/appropriateness, product disclosure, conflicts, inducements, dealing/execution, transaction reporting, and CASS (client assets). Precision on numbers is everything.</p>\n\n<h4>Application of COBS</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>COBS applies to UK firms doing MiFID business with UK clients from a UK establishment. Post-Brexit, COBS no longer applies to a UK firm's MiFID business from an EEA branch; it applies to non-UK (third-country) MiFID firms operating a UK branch.</li>\n<li>Exception: investment research and personal account dealing rules apply on a <strong>home state basis</strong> regardless of where business is conducted.</li>\n<li>ECP business disapplies large parts of COBS 2, 4, 6, 8A, 10, 11, 12 and 16.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Client categorisation (Section 3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Three categories: <strong>retail client</strong> (most protection), <strong>professional client</strong> (per se or elective), <strong>eligible counterparty</strong> (least protection).</li>\n<li>Large undertaking (non-MiFID) needs 2 of: balance sheet EUR12.5m, turnover EUR25m, 250 employees; OR called-up share capital/net assets >=GBP5m for 2 years. MiFID large undertaking: 2 of balance sheet EUR20m, turnover EUR40m, own funds EUR2m.</li>\n<li>Elective professional client: qualitative test (firm assesses expertise) plus quantitative test for MiFID business (2 of 3: 10 significant trades/quarter over 4 quarters; portfolio >EUR500,000; 1 year relevant professional experience).</li>\n<li>Local authorities are retail by default; can elect professional if portfolio >GBP10 million plus one other condition.</li>\n<li>Elective ECP: only per se professional clients (undertakings) that request it.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Communicating with clients / financial promotions (Section 5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Must be fair, clear and not misleading (Principles 6 and 7, or Principle 12/Consumer Duty).</li>\n<li>Approving a promotion for an unauthorised person requires the FCA \"gateway\" permission (2023).</li>\n<li>Past performance: cover minimum 5 years (or life of product if shorter), in complete 12-month periods.</li>\n<li>Cooling-off: minimum 24 hours for first-time high-risk investment customers.</li>\n<li>NMMIs banned to retail; RMMIs restricted with risk warnings, no incentives to invest.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Advising and selling (Section 7)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Suitability applies to personal recommendations and managing investments; requires knowledge/experience, financial situation (ability to bear loss), and objectives/risk profile.</li>\n<li>Suitability report timing: life policy before conclusion (or immediately after if oral/urgent cover); pension/stakeholder within 14 days if cancellation rules apply; otherwise before transaction concluded.</li>\n<li>Appropriateness applies to non-advised sales of complex products; not required for execution-only in non-complex instruments.</li>\n<li>Churning = excessive dealing for commission; switching = replacing one investment with another when not suitable in aggregate.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Product disclosure and cancellation (Section 8)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>KIID (UCITS), KID (PRIIPs, max 3 A4 pages), KFD (packaged products).</li>\n<li>Cancellation periods: life/pension products <strong>30 calendar days</strong>; everything else (cash ISA, non-life/pension advised or distance) <strong>14 calendar days</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Conflicts, inducements and research (Sections 9-10)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Disclosure of a conflict is a last resort, only when organisational arrangements cannot manage it; must be in a durable medium.</li>\n<li>Minor non-monetary benefits are allowed if disclosed, reasonable, and cannot impair duty to the client.</li>\n<li>Research must be paid for separately (own resources or RPA) unless: SME research (market cap under GBP200 million exemption), FICC research, open access research, or research from providers unconnected to execution services.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Dealing, execution and reporting (Sections 11-12)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Best execution = \"all sufficient steps\" (MiFID firms) vs \"all reasonable steps\" (AIFMs/residual CIS operators); factors: price, cost, speed, likelihood of execution/settlement, size, nature.</li>\n<li>Order execution policy reviewed at least annually.</li>\n<li>Transaction reporting (UK MiFIR): by close of next business day. Trade reporting (LSE): within 3 minutes. Post-trade transparency: 1 minute (equity), 5 minutes (non-equity). Short selling notification: 3.30pm next trading day.</li>\n<li>Periodic statements: at least quarterly (monthly if leveraged; annually if deal-by-deal confirmations given). 10% portfolio depreciation notification required for portfolio management (note: the requirement to notify clients of contingent liability/leveraged investment depreciation was removed from 7 June 2023).</li>\n<li>Deal confirmations: no later than the next business day.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Client assets (Section 13)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>CASS creates a statutory trust over client money (CASS 7). Bank acknowledgement letters must now be signed and returned before client money is placed (the old 20-business-day grace period was removed).</li>\n<li>DvP exemption unless settlement fails to occur within 3 business days.</li>\n</ul>\n","sections":[{"heading":"2.1 Application and General Provisions of COBS","html":"\n<p>The <strong>Conduct of Business Sourcebook (COBS)</strong> sits in the Business Standards block of the FCA Handbook. It shifts regulation towards outcomes rather than box-ticking, and implements MiFID's conduct-of-business provisions (high-level standards, not prescriptive detail). COBS also covers non-MiFID investment business.</p>\n<h4>1.1 Firms subject to COBS - geographic scope</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>General rule: COBS applies to firms carrying out relevant activities from an establishment (including that of an appointed representative) <strong>maintained in the UK</strong>.</li>\n<li>COBS generally applies where a UK firm does business with a UK client from an <strong>overseas establishment</strong>, unless the overseas person qualifies for certain exclusions.</li>\n<li>Post-Brexit: COBS no longer applies to a UK MiFID firm's business carried on from an <strong>EEA branch</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rules derived from MiFID apply to a <strong>non-UK (third-country) MiFID investment firm</strong> operating a UK branch; post-Brexit EEA firms with a UK branch are classed as third-country firms for UK MiFID business.</li>\n<li><strong>Exception</strong>: investment research and personal account dealing rules apply on a <strong>home state basis</strong> only - ie, they apply to UK firms regardless of where in the world the business is conducted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.2 Activities subject to COBS</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Designated investment business.</li>\n<li>Long-term insurance business relating to life policies.</li>\n<li>Accepting deposits - only in part (eg financial promotion rules, product information rules).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Eligible Counterparty (ECP) business disapplication</h4>\n<p>A range of COBS rules are disapplied for firms carrying on business with an ECP, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parts of COBS 2 (inducements, agent as client, reliance on others).</li>\n<li>Much of COBS 4 (fair, clear, not misleading; financial promotions; direct offer promotions; pre-service disclosure).</li>\n<li>COBS 6 (costs and charges information, compensation information).</li>\n<li>COBS 8A (client agreements).</li>\n<li>COBS 10 (appropriateness / non-advised services).</li>\n<li>Parts of COBS 11 (best execution, client order handling).</li>\n<li>Parts of COBS 12 (labelling of non-independent research).</li>\n<li>COBS 16 (reporting information to clients).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Members/participants of a regulated market do not need to comply with COBS 11.4 (client limit orders) between each other, but must comply when executing orders on behalf of a client.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.2 Appointed Representatives (ARs)","html":"\n<p>COBS applies to a principal firm in respect of the relevant regulated activities carried out on its behalf by its <strong>appointed representatives (ARs)</strong>. ARs are exempt persons who can carry out regulated activities under the principal's umbrella responsibility.</p>\n<p>Although a financial promotion communicated by an AR is technically an <strong>exempt promotion</strong> (so does not need separate approval), COBS makes clear that the <strong>principal firm remains responsible</strong> for the content of financial promotions communicated by its ARs.</p>\n<h4>2022 AR regime reforms (effective end of 2022, following a 2021 FCA consultation)</h4>\n<p>Two main areas of change:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Additional information on ARs and notification requirements for principals.</li>\n<li>Clarifying/strengthening principals' responsibilities and expectations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Information and notification requirements:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Principals must notify the FCA of a new AR appointment <strong>30 days before</strong> it takes effect.</li>\n<li>Principals must provide information on their business and their ARs, including the nature of regulated activities the ARs will undertake.</li>\n<li>Within <strong>60 days</strong> of the new rules coming into force, principals had to provide information on existing ARs to the FCA.</li>\n<li>Complaints and revenue data on ARs must be provided by principals.</li>\n<li>The FCA updates the Register with the regulated activities principals permit their ARs to undertake.</li>\n<li>Principals must notify the FCA whether they intend to provide (or cease providing) regulatory hosting services.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Responsibilities of principals / FCA expectations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enhanced oversight: adequate systems and controls, sufficient resources, monitoring growth.</li>\n<li>More effective responsibility: monitoring/assessing risk of harm; overseeing ARs to a standard comparable to direct employees.</li>\n<li>Clarity on when/how to terminate an AR relationship and assist an orderly wind-down.</li>\n<li><strong>Annual review</strong> of ARs' activities, business and senior management, including a self-assessment document prepared by the principal.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"heading":"2.3 Accepting Clients (Client Categorisation)","html":"\n<h4>3.1 Application of client categorisation rules</h4>\n<p>A firm carrying on designated investment business must categorise its clients. For mixed MiFID/non-MiFID business, MiFID categorisation applies throughout unless the two are conducted entirely separately.</p>\n<h4>3.1.1 Definition of a client</h4>\n<p>A client is someone to whom a firm provides, intends to provide, or has provided a service in carrying out a regulated activity, and (for MiFID/equivalent third-country business) anything that is an <strong>ancillary service</strong>. Includes potential clients and agents. For financial promotion rules, includes anyone communicated with, whether or not an actual client. Clients of a firm's AR/tied agent are also clients of the firm.</p>\n<h4>3.1.2 The client categories</h4>\n<table><tr><th>Category</th><th>Protection level</th><th>Key notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Retail client</td><td>Highest</td><td>Any client who is not professional or ECP. \"Customer\" = retail + professional clients combined.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Professional client</td><td>Medium</td><td>Per se or elective.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Eligible counterparty (ECP)</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Narrower list than per se professional; excludes large undertakings.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Per se professional clients</strong> include: entities required to be authorised/regulated (credit institutions, investment firms, other authorised financial institutions, insurance companies, CIS/management companies, pension funds/management companies, commodity/commodity derivatives dealers, other institutional investors); large undertakings; national/regional governments, public bodies, central banks, supranational institutions; institutional investors whose main business is investing in financial instruments.</p>\n<h4>Large undertaking thresholds</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MiFID/equivalent third-country business</strong>: meets any 2 of - balance sheet total <strong>EUR20 million</strong>; net turnover <strong>EUR40 million</strong>; own funds <strong>EUR2 million</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-MiFID business</strong> - a company is a large undertaking if:\n<ul>\n<li>Called-up share capital or net assets are/have been (in the past two years) at least <strong>GBP5 million</strong>; OR</li>\n<li>Meets 2 of: balance sheet total <strong>EUR12.5 million</strong>, net turnover <strong>EUR25 million</strong>, average <strong>250 employees</strong> in the year.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Partnership/unincorporated association: net assets at least GBP5 million (past 2 years) - for limited partnerships, calculated without deducting loans owed to partners.</li>\n<li>Trustee of a trust (other than pension schemes below): assets of at least <strong>GBP10 million</strong> (past 2 years).</li>\n<li>Trustee of occupational pension scheme/SSAS, or trustee/operator of personal/stakeholder pension: scheme has (or has had) at least <strong>50 members</strong> and assets under management of at least <strong>GBP10 million</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Eligible counterparties (ECPs)</h4>\n<p>Narrower than per se professional clients (excludes large undertakings): credit institutions, investment firms, other authorised/regulated financial institutions (EEA law or national law), insurance companies, UCITS CIS/management companies, pension funds/management companies, national governments/debt management offices, supranational organisations, central banks, and undertakings exempt from MiFID under Article 2(1)(k) or 2(1)(l).</p>\n<p><strong>ECP business</strong> = dealing on own account, execution of orders, or reception/transmission of orders (plus directly related ancillary services, and non-MiFID arranging) carried on <strong>with or for an ECP</strong>. If an ECP wants investment management or advice, it must be treated as a per se professional client for that business.</p>\n<h4>3.2 Agents</h4>\n<p>If a firm knows A is acting as agent for B, it should treat A as its client - unless (a) it has agreed in writing with A to treat B as the client, or (b) A's involvement is mainly to reduce the firm's duties to B, in which case B must be treated as the client regardless.</p>\n<h4>3.3 Recategorising clients - lower protection</h4>\n<p><strong>Elective professional client</strong>: retail client may be reclassified if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Qualitative test</strong>: firm assesses expertise, experience and knowledge and believes the client can make own investment decisions and understands the risks; AND (for MiFID business)</li>\n<li><strong>Quantitative test</strong> - any 2 of 3:\n<ul>\n<li>Average of <strong>10 significantly sized transactions per quarter</strong> over the last <strong>4 quarters</strong> on the relevant market.</li>\n<li>Financial instrument portfolio (cash deposits + financial instruments) exceeds <strong>EUR500,000</strong>.</li>\n<li>Works/has worked in the financial sector for at least <strong>1 year</strong> in a professional position requiring knowledge of the transactions envisaged.</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Process: client requests reclassification in writing (specifying products/services); firm gives a clear written warning of protections lost; client separately confirms in writing they are aware of the consequences.</p>\n<p><strong>Local authorities/municipalities</strong> (MiFID II) are retail clients by default. To become elective professional: qualitative test (COBS 3.5.3(1)) plus quantitative test (COBS 3.5.3B(2)): portfolio exceeds <strong>GBP10 million</strong>, AND either (a) 10 significant transactions/quarter average over 4 quarters, (b) the authorised person has 1+ year's relevant professional experience, or (c) it is an administering authority of the Local Government Pension Scheme acting in that capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Elective ECP</strong>: a professional client that is an undertaking, is a per se professional client (not merely because it's an institutional investor), and requests ECP treatment.</p>\n<h4>3.3.1 Recategorising clients - higher protection</h4>\n<p>Firms must allow professional clients/ECPs to request recategorisation to a higher-protection category, and may also do this on their own initiative. Recategorisation can be general or transaction-specific; a client can (in theory) be classified differently per instrument type, though most firms classify once for simplicity.</p>\n<p><strong>Protections lost on becoming elective professional (from retail)</strong>: fair/clear/not misleading communications and financial promotions; firm/services/remuneration info; suitability; appropriateness; dealing/carrying out orders; client reporting; remuneration/incentivisation rules; investor compensation (FOS/FSCS); exclusion of liability; share trading obligation; collateral transfer rules; some client money limitations.</p>\n<p><strong>Protections lost on becoming elective ECP (from professional)</strong>: client agreement; appropriateness; best execution; order handling; product governance; disclosure on instruments/costs/charges/remuneration/commissions; package products rules.</p>\n<h4>3.4 Notifications of client classification</h4>\n<p>New clients must be told how they have been classified, and (before services are provided) of their right to request recategorisation and any resulting limits on protection.</p>\n<h4>3.5 Policies, procedures and records</h4>\n<p>Firms need written internal policies/procedures for categorisation, and must retain records of each notice/agreement for the relevant period after ceasing business with the client.</p>\n<h4>3.6 Reliance on others</h4>\n<p>A UK MiFID firm receiving an instruction via another UK MiFID firm (or equivalent) can rely on client information and recommendations relayed to it - the third-party firm remains responsible for the accuracy/completeness of information and appropriateness of recommendations; the receiving firm is responsible for concluding the service/transaction. Reliance information should be given in writing. For non-MiFID business, it is generally reasonable to rely on written information from an unconnected authorised person/professional firm unless there are grounds to doubt its accuracy.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.4 Client Agreements and Information Provision","html":"\n<h4>4.1 Requirement to provide a client agreement</h4>\n<p>Applies to: designated investment business for a retail client; MiFID business and ancillary services (or equivalent third-country business) for a retail and/or professional client. <strong>Does not apply</strong> to insurance firms issuing life policies as principal.</p>\n<p>Must be a written basic agreement on paper or other <strong>durable medium</strong>, setting out essential rights/obligations. Must be provided, in good time before the client is bound (or before providing services, whichever earlier), together with information about the firm/services (COBS 6.1.4, COBS 6.1ZA.2) - authorised status, conflicts policy, etc. Exception: if the client requested distance communication and the firm follows the voice telephone rules, the agreement/information may be given immediately after the client is bound.</p>\n<p>Material changes to terms must be notified in good time before they take effect, in a durable medium.</p>\n<p><strong>Record retention</strong> - client agreement records kept for the longer of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>5 years</strong>;</li>\n<li>the duration of the relationship with the client; or</li>\n<li><strong>indefinitely</strong> for pension transfers, pension opt-outs, or free-standing additional voluntary contributions (FSAVCs).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2 Information requirements</h4>\n<p>COBS 2.2/2.2A: firms doing MiFID (or equivalent third-country) business must give retail and professional clients comprehensible information about the firm/services, designated investments/strategies (with risk warnings), execution venues, and costs/charges. Non-MiFID firms dealing with retail clients in derivatives, warrants or stock lending must also provide this.</p>\n<p><strong>Information about the firm</strong> (to retail, professional clients and ECPs where relevant): name/address/contact details; (MiFID business) languages of communication; communication methods including for orders; statement of authorisation and regulator's details; statement if acting via an AR/tied agent; nature/frequency/timing of performance reports (COBS 16); summary of the firm's conflicts of interest policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Managing investments</strong> - must establish an appropriate evaluation method (eg a benchmark). For retail clients, must also disclose (where applicable): valuation method/frequency; any delegation of discretionary management; specified benchmark; permitted investment/transaction types and limits; risk level reflected in the manager's discretion and any constraints.</p>\n<p><strong>Safeguarding investments/money</strong> - firms holding client money/investments (subject to MiFID custody/client money rules) must disclose: that a third party may hold assets/money; firm's responsibility for that third party's acts/omissions; consequences of third-party insolvency; risk if assets can't be separately designated overseas; that assets are subject to non-UK law and what this means for client rights; summary of protective steps taken (including compensation/deposit guarantee scheme details); details of any security interest/lien/set-off; and full information (durable medium, good time before) on the firm's obligations/risks.</p>\n<h4>4.3 Disclosure of costs</h4>\n<p>Firms must disclose to retail clients: total price (all fees, costs, charges, expenses, taxes via the firm), or basis of calculation if not known upfront; costs charged by the firm itemised separately; currency/conversion info if paid in foreign currency; statement that further costs/taxes not paid via the firm may apply, plus how paid/levied and compensation scheme info. ECPs/professionals can request cost details as a commercial (not regulatory) matter.</p>\n<p><strong>4.3.1 Timing/medium</strong>: good time before service provision, unless distance communication at client's request prevented this and voice telephone rules are complied with (then immediately after). Durable medium or firm's website (if website conditions met).</p>\n<p><strong>4.3.2 Keeping clients up to date</strong>: notify material changes in good time, in a durable medium if originally so provided.</p>\n<p><strong>4.3.3 Existing clients</strong>: repeat transactions in the same instrument type need not be treated as a new service each time, but the client must still receive relevant updated info (eg changed charges).</p>\n<p><strong>4.3.4 Compensation information</strong>: MiFID business firms must make available info identifying the relevant compensation scheme, amount/scope of cover; on request, conditions/formalities for claiming. Durable medium or compliant website.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.5 Communicating with Clients, including Financial Promotions","html":"\n<h4>5.1 Application of the rules</h4>\n<p>The 12 Principles for Businesses apply to regulated activities, dealing as principal, ancillary designated investment activities, home finance, insurance mediation, accepting deposits, and communication/approval of financial promotions. Firms subject to the <strong>Consumer Duty</strong> apply Principle 12 (plus cross-cutting rules/outcomes) instead of Principles 6 and 7; firms outside Consumer Duty scope remain subject to <strong>FTOC</strong> (Fair Treatment of Customers).</p>\n<p>MiFID (2007) introduced the general obligation that firms \"act honestly, fairly and professionally, and in accordance with the best interests of the client\" - implemented via COBS.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>FSMA offence</strong> to communicate a financial promotion unless the communicator is an authorised firm, or the promotion has been approved by an authorised firm. Exemptions from COBS 4.1.1 include: qualifying credit/home purchase/home reversion plans; non-investment insurance contracts; promotion of an unregulated CIS (never permitted to communicate/approve); credit agreements/consumer hire/credit-related regulated activities; authorised professional firms under COBS 18. These financial promotion rules generally do not apply where the client is an ECP.</p>\n<p><strong>FCA power</strong>: can direct removal/banning of a promotion without going through full enforcement; firm can make representations; FCA decides to confirm/amend/revoke and (if confirmed) publishes the direction, the promotion and its reasons.</p>\n<h4>5.1.2 Fair, clear and not misleading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Communications where capital is at risk must make this clear.</li>\n<li>Yield quotes must give a balanced short/medium/long-term impression.</li>\n<li>Complex products/charges/multiple remuneration elements communicated fairly and clearly, tailored to the audience's information needs.</li>\n<li>If FCA is named as regulator but some business is unregulated, this must be made clear.</li>\n<li>Third-party packaged/stakeholder products: give a fair impression of the producer/manager.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.1.3 Identifying promotions</h4>\n<p>Must be clearly identifiable as financial promotions (exceptions for professional clients/ECPs). Methods: real-time (face-to-face, phone, presentations); unsolicited real-time (\"cold calls\"); non-real-time (emails, letters, websites); direct offer promotions (invite a response).</p>\n<h4>5.1.4 Sustainability Disclosure Requirements / anti-greenwashing (2024)</h4>\n<p>Applies to all FCA-regulated firms making sustainability claims to retail or professional clients (regardless of where the communication originated, if distributed by an FCA firm). Claims must be correct/substantiated, clear, complete (no cherry-picking/omission), and support fair comparisons. Complements (does not override) existing PRIN/COBS/Consumer Credit fair-communication rules and the Consumer Duty.</p>\n<h4>5.2 Exceptions to the financial promotion rules</h4>\n<p>Disapplied for \"excluded communications\": aimed at high-net-worth individuals (FPO Article 48) and sophisticated investors (Articles 50/50A); exempt under the Financial Promotion Order (FPO) (unauthorised communicator, or originates outside UK with no UK effect); from outside the UK and would be FPO-exempt if a separate unauthorised person; subject to/exempt from the Takeover Code; one-off promotions that are not cold calls. <strong>Does not generally apply to UK MiFID business.</strong></p>\n<p>Nov 2023 HM Treasury changes (effective 2024): businesses must give their own details in exemption-based communications; \"certified\" removed from the high-net-worth individual exemption title; updated HNWI and self-certified sophisticated investor statements; changes extended to CIS-equivalent exemptions. Old investor statements are no longer valid.</p>\n<h4>5.3 Prospectus advertisements</h4>\n<p>COBS 4 does not apply where the Admissions to Trading on a Regulated Market (PRM) prospectus rules apply (PRM 12.1). A prospectus advertisement must: state a prospectus has been/will be published and where to obtain it; be clearly recognisable as an advertisement; be accurate/not misleading; be consistent with the prospectus; and (if written) carry a bold, prominent statement that it is not a prospectus.</p>\n<p><strong>Social media/influencers</strong>: FCA guidance requires balance and correct risk warnings; firms are responsible for all their promotions including via influencers; unapproved promotion by an influencer may be a criminal offence. FCA removed over 10,000 misleading adverts in one year (up from around 8,500 the year before).</p>\n<h4>5.4 Approval of financial promotions</h4>\n<p>SYSC 3/SYSC 4 require systems/controls for communicating or approving promotions. Since <strong>2023</strong>, a \"gateway\" permission from the FCA is needed before a firm may approve financial promotions for unauthorised third parties (firms without this permission may only approve their own promotions).</p>\n<p>Before approving, the firm must confirm compliance; if it later becomes non-compliant, approval must be withdrawn and known relying parties notified as soon as reasonably practicable. Firms may <strong>not</strong> approve real-time promotions (personal visits, phone calls, interactive dialogue). Firms cannot approve unregulated CIS promotions unless they could legitimately promote it themselves. Approval limited to disapplied-rule circumstances must state that limitation; communicating outside it is an offence.</p>\n<p><strong>5.4.2 Relying on another's approval</strong> (non-MiFID business): not a breach if the firm takes reasonable care to establish another authorised firm confirmed compliance, communicates only to the intended recipient type, and (as far as it should know) it remains fair/clear/not misleading and has not been withdrawn.</p>\n<h4>5.5 Communicating with retail clients</h4>\n<p>Rules: firm name included; accurate, balanced risk/benefit presentation; understandable to the average target recipient; no disguising/diminishing key warnings. Comparisons must be meaningful/fair/balanced (MiFID: cite data sources, key facts/assumptions). Tax treatment: explain it depends on individual circumstances and may change. Disapplied for third-party prospectuses, image advertising, and (non-MiFID) excluded communications.</p>\n<p><strong>High-risk investment promotion rules (2020, updated 2023)</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NMMIs</strong> (Non-Mass Market Investments) - banned to retail investors.</li>\n<li><strong>RMMIs</strong> (Restricted Mass Market Investments) - restricted to retail (eg no non-readily realisable securities, no peer-to-peer agreements).</li>\n<li>Risk warnings: could lose all money invested; prescribed risk summaries taking no more than <strong>2 minutes</strong> to read (firms may diverge for valid reasons).</li>\n<li>Ban on monetary/non-monetary incentives to invest (eg refer-a-friend bonuses).</li>\n<li>Minimum <strong>24-hour cooling-off period</strong> for first-time investors with a firm.</li>\n<li>Personalised risk warnings including the client's name.</li>\n<li>Retail clients must self-certify (with evidence) they meet criteria to receive high-risk promotions.</li>\n<li>Appropriateness test made more robust; firms cannot coach investors to pass it.</li>\n<li>Additional record-keeping on categorisation/appropriateness metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2023 guidance: approver's name and approval date must be shown; approvers need appropriate competence/expertise; must actively monitor ongoing compliance and appropriateness; enhanced conflicts requirements; must undertake preliminary suitability assessments before NMMIs are promoted to retail investors.</p>\n<h4>5.5.1 Cryptoasset financial promotions (final rules Sept 2023)</h4>\n<p>A \"qualifying cryptoasset\" is a cryptographically secured, transferable, fungible digital representation of value/rights (excludes e-money and existing controlled investments). Four legal routes to promote: (1) communicated by an authorised person; (2) made by an unauthorised person but approved by an authorised person (via gateway); (3) communicated by/for a cryptoasset business registered under the MLRs relying on the bespoke FPO exemption; (4) otherwise compliant with an FPO exemption. Cryptoassets classified as RMMIs: risk warnings, ban on incentives, positive frictions (24-hour cooling off, direct offer promotion rules, client categorisation - self-certified sophisticated investor category NOT available), appropriateness assessments.</p>\n<h4>5.6 Past, simulated past and future performance</h4>\n<p><strong>Past performance</strong>: not the most prominent feature; covers at least the preceding <strong>5 years</strong> (or whole life of investment/index/service if shorter), shown in complete 12-month periods; reference periods/sources shown; prominent warning that past performance isn't a reliable indicator of future results; currency stated with fluctuation warning if not sterling; gross figures must disclose effect of commissions/fees/tax/charges.</p>\n<p><strong>Simulated past performance</strong>: relates to an investment/index; based on actual past performance of the same/underlying investment(s)/indices; otherwise meets the past performance rules; prominent warning it is simulated and not a reliable indicator.</p>\n<p><strong>Future performance</strong>: not based on/referring to simulated past performance; based on reasonable assumptions supported by objective data; gross figures disclose charges; prominent warning that forecasts are not reliable indicators.</p>\n<h4>5.7 Direct offers or invitations</h4>\n<p>Must contain prescribed information: about the firm/services; management of investments (if relevant); safekeeping of investments/money; costs and charges; nature/risks of investments (incl. tax); statement that a prospectus has been published (Prospectus Regulation); description of combined-investment risk where relevant; sufficient detail on third-party guarantees. Can be omitted if the client can refer to another document. Must include a statement recommending the client seek personal advice if unsure of suitability.</p>\n<h4>5.8 Unwritten promotions and cold calling</h4>\n<p><strong>Unwritten promotions</strong> outside a firm's premises: appropriate time of day; identify self/firm and reason for contact at the outset; clarify if client wants to continue/terminate (and comply); give a contact point if an appointment is arranged.</p>\n<p><strong>Cold calling</strong> permitted only where: an existing client relationship exists and the call is expected; it relates to a generally marketable packaged product (not higher-volatility fund or linked life policy); or it relates to a controlled activity for limited investment types (deposits, readily realisable investments, excluding warrants/geared packaged products).</p>\n<p><strong>Overseas persons</strong>: firms may not approve/communicate overseas promotions unless the promotion states who approved/communicated it and explains (where relevant) that investor protection rules won't apply, the extent of UK compensation scheme cover (or its absence), and any overseas compensation/deposit protection details. The firm must have no doubt the overseas firm will deal honestly/reliably with UK retail clients.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.6 Retail Clients (Consumer Duty and FTOC)","html":"\n<p>This section cross-references Chapter 1, Section 2 for the detailed content on the <strong>Consumer Duty</strong> (four outcomes: products and services; price and value; consumer understanding; consumer support) and the <strong>Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC)</strong> initiative, which applies to firms outside the scope of the Consumer Duty.</p>\n<p>Key exam point: firms subject to Consumer Duty apply Principle 12 as the overarching standard (replacing Principles 6 and 7 for in-scope activities); firms not captured by Consumer Duty (no retail customers, out of scope, or exempt) remain subject to FTOC obligations and Principles 6/7.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.7 Advising and Selling Standards (Suitability and Appropriateness)","html":"\n<h4>7.1 Application of suitability rules</h4>\n<p>Apply when firms: make personal recommendations on designated investments (buy/hold/sell advice); manage investments for a retail client; manage assets of an occupational, stakeholder or personal pension scheme (outside MiFID/equivalent/optional exemption business). Basic advice rules may be applied instead for stakeholder products. Non-MiFID business: rules apply only to retail clients or pension scheme asset management (as above).</p>\n<p>Firms must obtain information on: knowledge and experience relevant to the specific investment type; financial situation, including ability to bear losses; investment objectives and current risk profile. Under UK MiFID, suitability extends to <em>all</em> decisions to trade, not just buy recommendations, including holding. Portfolio managers must assess suitability before a recommendation that requires altering the client mandate, and must keep client information up to date on an ongoing basis.</p>\n<p>FCA expectations on incentive schemes: check for spikes/trends in individual adviser sales patterns; monitor face-to-face sales conversations; manage risks in discretionary incentive schemes/balanced scorecards; monitor non-advised sales to prevent unauthorised personal recommendations; oversee AR incentive schemes; recognise that near-100% variable, sales-based pay raises misselling risk.</p>\n<h4>7.2 Information required for a suitability assessment</h4>\n<p>MiFID II requires firms giving investment advice or portfolio management to gather info on: knowledge/experience (types of service/transaction/investment familiar with; nature, volume, frequency, period of involvement; education/profession); financial standing (ability to bear losses); investment objectives/risk appetite. Firm must have a reasonable basis to believe the transaction meets objectives, risk is bearable, and risk is understood. Firms must not discourage clients from providing information, but can rely on it unless manifestly out of date/inaccurate/incomplete. <strong>If sufficient information cannot be obtained, the firm must not make a personal recommendation or take a trading decision for the client.</strong></p>\n<h4>7.3 Assessing suitability - professional clients</h4>\n<p>A firm may <strong>assume</strong> a professional client (per se and elective, for UK MiFID business) has the necessary knowledge/experience. For <strong>per se</strong> professional clients only, the firm may also assume the client can financially bear the related investment risks consistent with objectives. This assumption <strong>cannot</strong> be made for elective professional clients regarding financial ability to bear risk.</p>\n<h4>7.4 Churning and switching</h4>\n<p><strong>Churning</strong> = over-dealing/trading more frequently than necessary to generate fees/commission (relevant especially to discretionary portfolios). <strong>Switching</strong> = selling one investment and replacing it with another. Suitability guidance: a series of transactions each suitable in isolation may not be suitable overall if made so frequently as to be detrimental to the client (frequency vs the client's investment strategy).</p>\n<h4>7.5 Suitability report</h4>\n<p>Required whenever a firm makes a personal recommendation, covering (among others): equities, derivatives, structured products, unregulated CIS, acquiring/selling holdings in a regulated CIS/investment trust (via savings scheme or ISA promoted for that trust), pension contract actions (buy/sell/surrender/convert/cancel/suspend contributions), income withdrawal/short-term annuity elections, pension transfers/opt-outs, and life assurance recommendations.</p>\n<p><strong>Exceptions</strong> (no suitability report needed): investment manager's personal recommendation on a regulated CIS for a retail client; client habitually resident outside UK/not present in UK when consenting; friendly society recommendation on a small life policy with premium not exceeding <strong>GBP50/year</strong> (or <strong>GBP1/week</strong>); recommendation to increase a regular premium on an existing contract; additional single premium/contribution to an existing packaged product.</p>\n<p><strong>Timing</strong>: life policy - before contract concluded (unless info given orally or immediate cover needed, then durable medium immediately after conclusion); personal/stakeholder pension where cancellation rules apply - within <strong>14 days</strong> of concluding the contract; any other case - before the transaction is concluded (unless a distance marketing communication is used and client consents, then as soon as possible after execution).</p>\n<p>Content: client's demands and needs; possible disadvantages of the transaction; why the firm concluded the transaction is suitable, having regard to client-provided information. Telephone sale of a life policy (only contact by phone): must comply with the Distance Marketing Directive, be provided immediately after conclusion, in a durable medium.</p>\n<h4>7.6 Insistent clients</h4>\n<p>An <strong>insistent client</strong> is one where: the firm gave a personal recommendation; the client decides not to follow it (or wants something different); and the client wants the firm to facilitate the (unrecommended) transaction anyway. The firm must clearly, fairly communicate: that it did not recommend the transaction and it is not in line with its recommendation; the reasons the transaction is not in line with its recommendation; the risks of the client's proposed transaction; the reasons the firm did not recommend it.</p>\n<h4>7.7 Appropriateness (non-advised sales)</h4>\n<p>Applies to: MiFID investment services other than personal recommendations/managing investments; arranging/dealing in warrants and derivatives for retail clients responding to a direct offer promotion; firms assessing appropriateness for other UK MiFID firms.</p>\n<p>Firm must ask about the client's knowledge/experience relevant to the specific product/service type, to assess whether it is appropriate. May assume a professional client has necessary knowledge/experience for products/services within their professional classification. Firm can rely on client-supplied info unless known to be out of date/inaccurate/incomplete, and may infer knowledge from experience.</p>\n<p>If the product/service seems <strong>not appropriate</strong>, the firm must warn the client (can be standardised). If the client declines to give information, firm must warn it cannot assess appropriateness (also can be standardised); if the client insists on proceeding, it's the firm's judgement whether to proceed.</p>\n<h4>7.7.3 When appropriateness assessment is unnecessary</h4>\n<p>Not required if: the service is <strong>execution-only</strong> or reception/transmission of orders, for particular (\"non-complex\") financial instruments, at the client's initiative, and the client has been clearly told the firm won't assess appropriateness (losing that protection); AND the firm complies with its conflicts of interest obligations (Principle 8).</p>\n<p><strong>Non-complex financial instruments</strong> include: shares listed on a UK/EEA regulated market (or equivalent third-country market); money market instruments, bonds, other securitised debt without embedded derivatives; UCITS fund holdings; other instruments meeting the \"non-complex\" definition (not a derivative; sufficiently liquid; client liability doesn't exceed acquisition cost; publicly available comprehensive information exists) - see COBS 10A.4.1.</p>\n<p>No need to reassess for a series of similar transactions, but must reassess before a new type of service. Clients dealing before MiFID (2007) could be assumed to have necessary experience/knowledge (but not necessarily meet other criteria).</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.8 Product Disclosure and the Right to Cancel","html":"\n<h4>8.1 Purpose and nature of the rules</h4>\n<p>Ensures clients get sufficient/relevant information to decide whether to invest, whether recommended or self-selected.</p>\n<h4>8.1.1 KIIDs</h4>\n<p>Authorised fund managers (AFMs) of UCITS schemes must produce a <strong>Key Investor Information Document (KIID)</strong> - fair, clear, not misleading, consistent with the prospectus. Must cover: identification of the scheme; short description of objectives/policy; past performance or performance scenarios; costs and charges; risk and reward profile with guidance/warnings.</p>\n<h4>8.1.2 Key Features Documents (KFDs)</h4>\n<p>Required, in good time, for: each packaged product; cash deposit ISA; cash deposit CTF; a key features illustration for each packaged product produced. Also, nature/risk information on designated investments for MiFID business and for retail clients in certain other business.</p>\n<h4>8.1.3 PRIIPs and KIDs</h4>\n<p>PRIIPs Regulation requires a <strong>Key Information Document (KID)</strong> for packaged retail and insurance-based investment products - covers CIS and other packaged bank/insurer products, aiming to help investors compare features, risks, rewards and costs. UCITS KIID/KFD get a 5-year exemption from PRIIPs, extended to expire <strong>31 December 2026</strong>.</p>\n<p>Four PRIIP categories: investment funds; insurance-based investment products; retail-structured securities; structured term deposits.</p>\n<p>A KID is a stand-alone, standardised document, <strong>maximum 3 A4 pages</strong>, prepared by manufacturers (or published on their website); anyone advising on/selling a PRIIP to a retail investor must provide the KID in good time before the transaction. Applies to retail investment providers, life companies, discretionary managers, insurance-based investment firms, fund managers, stockbrokers/advisers on funds/structured products/derivatives, financial advisers, and platform operators.</p>\n<p>KID sections (fixed sequence): What is this product? / What are the risks and what could I get in return? / What happens if [manufacturer] is unable to pay out? / What are the costs? / How long should I hold it and can I take money out early? / How can I complain? / Other relevant information.</p>\n<h4>8.2 Cancellation and right to withdraw</h4>\n<p>Applies to: firms providing retail financial products based on designated investments/deposits; distance contracts with consumers for deposits/designated investments.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Product</th><th>Cancellation period</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Life and pension products</td><td><strong>30 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash deposit ISAs</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-life/pension products (advised, non-distance)</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-life/pension products (distance contracts)</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Example: an advised purchase of an AUT or OEIC has a 14-day cancellation right. The period starts from contract conclusion, or from receipt of contractual information if later.</p>\n<p>Firms must tell consumers (durable medium, good time before, or immediately after if not possible before) of: existence and duration of the right; conditions for exercising it (incl. any amount payable); consequences of not exercising it; practical details of exercising it (incl. address for notification). Not required if the consumer already has this information from elsewhere under COBS distance marketing/product information rules.</p>\n<p>Notification of cancellation is deemed observed if dispatched before the deadline; no reason needs to be given. Cancelling doesn't guarantee return of the full amount invested (eg CIS proceeds reflect price movement of underlying holdings).</p>\n<p><strong>Record-keeping</strong>: indefinitely for pension transfers/opt-outs/FSAVCs; at least 5 years for life policy/pension contract/personal pension/stakeholder pension; at least 3 years in any other case.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.9 Conflicts of Interest","html":"\n<h4>9.1 Principles and rules</h4>\n<p>Principle 8: \"A firm must manage conflicts of interest fairly, both between itself and its customers and between a customer and another client.\" Detailed rules are in SYSC, applying to common platform firms (MiFID business/ancillary services) and non-MiFID firms/business. Firms must take all appropriate steps to identify, prevent and/or manage conflicts between: the firm (incl. managers, employees, ARs/tied agents, connected parties) and a client; and one client and another.</p>\n<p>Conflict types: realising a gain/avoiding a loss at a client's expense; having an interest in an outcome distinct from the client's; incentive to favour one client over others; carrying on the same business as the client; receiving inducements beyond standard commission/fee.</p>\n<p>Firms must: maintain effective organisational/administrative arrangements to prevent conflicts harming clients; for externally-facing research, have information systems/controls and barriers preventing research information flowing to the rest of the firm.</p>\n<p>If a conflict cannot be managed, it must be <strong>disclosed</strong> to the client before undertaking business - letting the client decide whether to continue. Disclosure must be in a durable medium, with sufficient detail for an informed decision. <strong>Disclosure alone is not sufficient compliance</strong> - the firm's arrangements should be designed to prevent conflicts arising in the first place. Firms must: prepare/maintain/implement an effective conflicts policy; give retail (and potential retail) clients a description of the policy; keep records of activities where a conflict has arisen.</p>\n<h4>Information barriers (\"Chinese walls\")</h4>\n<p>Arrangements whereby information held in one part of the business is withheld from (or not used by) people in another part. Firm must: withhold/not use the information; permit staff in one part to withhold it from staff in another, but only where at least one part carries on regulated activity (or a connected activity). Firms must take reasonable steps to keep such arrangements effective and adequately monitored.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong>: corporate finance (one side of the wall) may have price-sensitive plans for a company; the equity research/market-making arm (other side) has no knowledge of these until public/acceptable to release. Not passing this on is not a breach of duty to clients.</p>\n<h4>9.2 Disclosing conflicts</h4>\n<p>Disclosure is required only where arrangements are <strong>not sufficient</strong> to prevent, with reasonable confidence, the risk of damage to the client's interests - ie, disclosure is a <strong>last resort</strong>. Must be in a durable medium with sufficient detail for an informed decision. Over-reliance on disclosure without adequate management of conflicts is not permitted (SYSC 10.1.8 vs SYSC 10.1.3).</p>\n<h4>9.3 Conflicts of interest policies</h4>\n<p>Firms need a written policy appropriate to the scale/nature of the business (and, for group members, considering group-wide conflicts). Policy must: identify circumstances giving rise to conflicts (especially where one person performs 2+ roles, eg research/advice, proprietary trading, portfolio management, corporate finance); and set out processes/procedures covering: control of information flow; separate supervision of conflicting-interest staff; removing direct remuneration links to conflict-prone activities; measures against inappropriate influence; measures controlling involvement in multiple conflicting activities. Corporate finance firms managing securities offerings need specific detail on allocation and pricing arrangements.</p>\n<h4>9.4 Investment research and research recommendations</h4>\n<p><strong>Investment research</strong>: information recommending/suggesting an investment strategy (with opinion on value/price), intended for public distribution, clearly labelled as research, presented as objective/independent, and must not constitute a personal recommendation. <strong>Investment recommendations</strong> (captured under UK MAR) are short-term trade ideas/strategies for one or more clients - a distinct concept.</p>\n<h4>9.4.1 Measures and arrangements (dealing ahead)</h4>\n<p>Firms producing research must, per SYSC 10.1.11R, prevent: financial analysts/relevant persons with knowledge of unpublished research from personally trading (or trading for others) ahead of publication (exceptions: eg execution-only client instructions, good-faith market maker activity); personal account transactions in the relevant investments without prior compliance/legal approval (exceptional circumstances only); accepting inducements from those with a material interest in the research subject matter; promising favourable coverage to issuers; anyone other than financial analysts reviewing draft research containing a recommendation/target price (except for compliance verification).</p>\n<p>Exemption if disseminating another party's research: that person is not in the same group; the firm doesn't substantially amend it; doesn't hold itself out as producer; verifies the producer is subject to conflicts rules (or has an equivalent policy).</p>\n<p><strong>Non-independent research</strong> is treated as a marketing communication (COBS 4 applies): must be clearly identified as a marketing communication and carry a clear, prominent statement that it wasn't prepared per independence requirements and isn't subject to dealing-ahead prohibitions.</p>\n<h4>Research recommendation standards</h4>\n<p>Firms preparing/disseminating research recommendations must: present fairly; disclose interests/conflicts; give firm name and regulator; give preparer's identity/job title; distinguish facts from interpretation/estimates/opinion; indicate reliability of sources; clearly label and explain assumptions behind projections/forecasts/price targets; be able to substantiate the recommendation to the FCA on request.</p>\n<p><strong>Fair presentation</strong> requires: material sources indicated (incl. issuer, and whether disclosed/amended pre-dissemination); valuation basis summarised; meaning of buy/sell/hold explained with time horizons, risk warnings, sensitivity analysis; planned update frequency/coverage changes referenced; date of first release and price dates given; substantive changes vs the last 12 months' recommendation clearly flagged.</p>\n<p><strong>Disclosure of interests/conflicts</strong>: relationships that may impair objectivity (incl. issuer relationships); relationships of persons involved in preparation (incl. remuneration links to investment banking deals); accessible interests prior to dissemination; shareholdings/financial interests over <strong>5%</strong> in the issuer; market maker/lead manager/investment banking service statements; conflict-prevention arrangements (incl. information barriers); price/date of acquisition if a preparer acquired shares.</p>\n<p><strong>Quarterly disclosure</strong>: proportion of recommendations that are buy/hold/sell; proportion of issuers (per category) that received material investment banking services in the prior 12 months.</p>\n<p>Disseminating third-party recommendations: state firm identity/regulator; ensure fair/clear/not misleading summaries; identify source and where third-party disclosures are accessible; make full disclosures as if it had produced the research itself. Materially amending a third-party recommendation: describe alterations (esp. direction changes, eg buy to sell) or comply with disclosure rules as though self-produced; maintain a written policy directing recipients to the original producer/recommendation/disclosures.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.10 Inducements and Payment for Research","html":"\n<h4>10.1 Inducements</h4>\n<p>The rules are essentially a <strong>payment prohibition</strong> unless expressly permitted. Apply to firms doing MiFID and non-MiFID designated investment business, but only regarding <strong>professional and retail clients</strong> - not ECP business.</p>\n<p><strong>Minor non-monetary benefits</strong> (permitted alongside independent advice, restricted advice, portfolio management to retail clients) must be: disclosed prior to the service; capable of enhancing service quality; of a scale/nature that could not impair the duty to act honestly, fairly, professionally in the client's best interests; reasonable, proportionate, unlikely to influence behaviour detrimentally; and consist of: generic/personalised product-related information/documentation; third-party written material commissioned/paid for by a corporate issuer to promote a new issuance; conference/seminar/training participation on a specific instrument/service; reasonable de minimis hospitality (food/drink at a meeting/conference/seminar).</p>\n<p>For MiFID business, firms cannot pay/accept fees, commissions or non-monetary benefits except: those paid to/by the client (or someone on their behalf, eg management fees); proper fees necessary for the service (custody, legal, settlement) that cannot give rise to conflicts; those paid to/by a third party, permissible only if they don't impair the client's-best-interests duty, are designed to enhance service quality, and are disclosed to a set standard before the service.</p>\n<p>Disclosure can be satisfied by: disclosing essential arrangements in summary form; undertaking to give further detail on request; and actually doing so on request. Full records of third-party payments must be kept for UK MiFID business. For non-MiFID firms/business, inducement rules aren't fully implemented: for third-party payments, only the \"does not impair\" test applies (not disclosure/enhancement tests). Retail non-MiFID packaged product sales are subject to inducement disclosure requirements; reasonable non-monetary benefits need not be disclosed in detail for non-MiFID business.</p>\n<h4>10.2 Inducements and research</h4>\n<p>UK regulated firms producing research must apply a <strong>separately identifiable charge</strong> when providing it to an entity not entitled to receive it free (ie a firm providing portfolio management, or independent/restricted advice to retail/professional clients). In-scope firms may only consume research if paid for via: (1) the firm's own resources; or (2) a <strong>research payment account (RPA)</strong> funded by a specific client charge.</p>\n<p>Doesn't apply to non-UK regulated research producers, or firms located outside the UK consuming research. The FCA \"gold-plated\" MiFID II by not applying the rules to AIFs/CISs whose core policy isn't investing in MiFID financial instruments (eg private equity, commercial property funds).</p>\n<h4>2022 rule changes (from 2021 FCA review)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SME research exemption</strong>: research on companies with market capitalisation below <strong>GBP200 million</strong> is exempt from the inducement rules (UK managers need not pay, though may choose to). Reviewed <strong>3 years</strong> after implementation (<strong>1 March 2025</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>FICC research exemption</strong>: fixed income, currency and commodity research based on underlying instruments, not an investment strategy primarily about FICC instruments; macro-economic research does <strong>not</strong> meet the FICC definition and must still be paid for.</li>\n<li><strong>Independent research providers exemption</strong>: research from providers not engaged in execution services and not part of a group including an execution/brokerage investment firm.</li>\n<li><strong>Openly available research</strong>: research openly available to firms and the public can be received free.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.3 Research Payment Account (RPA)</h4>\n<p>Funded only by a specific research charge agreed with clients, based on a research budget for third-party research. Must only pay for research, not other goods/services. NOT research (non-exhaustive): post-trade analytics; unanalysed price feeds/historical price data; seminar fees; corporate access services; order/execution management services; RPA administration.</p>\n<h4>10.4 Payment optionality (from August 2024)</h4>\n<p>UK buy-side firms (asset managers, pension schemes, CIS managers) may <strong>bundle</strong> payments for third-party research and execution services, provided relevant requirements around execution and research costs are met.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.11 Dealing and Managing","html":"\n<h4>11.1 Application</h4>\n<p>Covers: best execution (COBS 11.2, 11.2A, 11.2B, 11.2C); client order handling (COBS 11.3); client limit orders (COBS 11.4); record keeping (COBS 11.5A); personal account dealing (COBS 11.7); recording telephone/electronic communications (SYSC 10A). Applies generally to authorised firms, with variations by business nature/location; some provisions apply to non-MiFID firms as if rules. Personal account dealing rules apply to designated investment business of UK-established firms.</p>\n<h4>11.2 Best execution</h4>\n<p>COBS splits best execution into: <strong>COBS 11.2</strong> - AIFMs and residual CIS operators (must take <strong>all reasonable steps</strong>); <strong>COBS 11.2A</strong> - MiFID investment firms (must take <strong>all sufficient steps</strong> - a higher standard); <strong>COBS 11.2B</strong> - UCITS management companies. Applies where a firm owes contractual/agency obligations and acts on the client's behalf. <strong>Does not apply to ECP business.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Execution factors</strong>: price, costs, speed, likelihood of execution and settlement, size, nature, or any other relevant consideration. Relative importance depends on: the client (incl. categorisation); the client order; the financial instruments involved; the execution venues available. Best execution is not just best price - other factors can take precedence depending on circumstances.</p>\n<p><strong>The four-fold test</strong> (does the firm owe a duty of best execution, eg in OTC transactions - assessed cumulatively):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Did the investment firm initiate the transaction?</li>\n<li>Does the client only ask for a quote (not shop around)?</li>\n<li>Will the client be unable to identify relative price transparency levels in the market?</li>\n<li>Has the client confirmed reliance on the investment firm?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Role of price</strong> (retail clients): total consideration = price of the instrument + execution-related costs (venue fees, clearing/settlement fees, third-party fees).</p>\n<p><strong>Competing execution venues</strong>: firm must weigh its own costs and venue costs; commissions must not unfairly discriminate between venues (no differential commission/spread unless it reflects real cost differences).</p>\n<h4>11.3 Order execution policies</h4>\n<p>Firms need a policy to obtain (and demonstrate) best possible results. Explicit client consent is <strong>not required</strong> for the policy itself - it's provided at onboarding/on the website; continued dealing implies acceptance; if a client disagrees, they simply stop transacting with the firm.</p>\n<p><strong>MiFID</strong>: firms must disclose the order execution policy before undertaking MiFID business and obtain <strong>prior consent</strong> (general agreement or per-transaction). Must review the policy/arrangements at least <strong>annually</strong>, or on material change.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-MiFID</strong>: policy must cover, per instrument class, the execution venues used and factors affecting venue choice, ensuring consistently best results. More detail required for retail clients. Consent can be implied by continued trading after MiFID Terms of Business are provided. Review whenever a material event occurs, but at least annually; clients must be notified of material changes (FCA doesn't define \"material change\").</p>\n<p>Firms must monitor effectiveness of arrangements/policies and correct deficiencies; demonstrate compliance to clients on request. Portfolio managers must act in clients' best interests when placing orders; firms receiving/transmitting orders must act in clients' best interests when passing to brokers (considering execution factors, unless specific instructions given). Portfolio managers/receivers/transmitters must maintain execution policies (no consent needed) identifying the entities used per instrument class.</p>\n<h4>11.4 Specific client instructions</h4>\n<p>If a client gives a specific instruction, the firm must follow it and is deemed to have satisfied best execution <strong>only for the instructed part/aspect</strong> of the order; other aspects remain subject to best execution. Firms must not induce clients to give instructions that the firm knows will likely prevent obtaining the best possible result.</p>\n<h4>11.5 Client order handling</h4>\n<p>Firms must ensure prompt, fair, expeditious execution of client orders relative to other orders/the firm's own trading interests (links to avoiding conflicts). Comparable orders should be executed in the order received. Firms should ensure: prompt/accurate recording and allocation; sequential/prompt execution of comparable orders unless impracticable or client interests dictate otherwise; retail clients told immediately of material execution difficulties; prompt/correct delivery of assets/money where the firm oversees settlement. Firms must not misuse client order information (eg front-running).</p>\n<h4>11.6 Aggregation and allocation</h4>\n<p>Firms may only aggregate a client's deal with its own, or aggregate multiple clients' deals, if: unlikely to disadvantage any aggregated client; the possible disadvantage is disclosed; and a precise allocation policy exists covering volume/price effects and partial allocations. Partial execution of an aggregated order must be allocated per the policy. Own-account orders aggregated with client orders must not be allocated to clients' detriment - client orders take priority unless the firm can show less favourable terms would otherwise have resulted (then proportional allocation is allowed). Procedures must prevent detrimental reallocation of own-account transactions.</p>\n<h4>11.7 Client limit orders</h4>\n<p>Unless instructed otherwise, a firm receiving a client limit order for shares on a regulated market that cannot be immediately executed under prevailing conditions must make it <strong>public immediately</strong> (unless over normal market size), via a regulated market/MTF order book, or other means ensuring easy execution.</p>\n<h4>11.8 Personal account dealing</h4>\n<p>Firms must prevent \"relevant persons\" (directors, partners, ARs, or other staff involved in regulated activities, incl. via outsourcing) from: (1) entering a personal transaction contrary to UK MAR, involving misuse/improper disclosure of confidential information, or conflicting with the firm's duty to a customer; (2) improperly advising/procuring another to enter such a transaction; (3) improperly disclosing information likely to lead another to take such action (outside normal employment duties).</p>\n<p>Relevant provisions: rules on financial analysts' personal account transactions; rules on misuse of pending client order information. Firms must record all notified personal transactions and any authorisation/prohibition given.</p>\n<p><strong>Exceptions</strong> (rules do not apply to): deals under a discretionary management service with no prior communication between manager and relevant person about the deal; deals in units/shares of UCITS/OEICs/unit trusts where the relevant person is not involved in fund management; personal transactions in life policies. Staff (other than directors/partners/ARs) are subject only to the extent involved in investment/portfolio management services. Restrictions don't apply separately to each transaction in an ongoing series under a prior unchanged instruction (unless the instruction changes, or is withdrawn while acquired investments are simultaneously disposed of).</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.12 Reporting of Transactions and Reporting to Clients","html":"\n<h4>12.1 Reportable transactions</h4>\n<p>Covers: instruments admitted/traded (or applied for admission) on a trading venue; instruments with an underlying traded on a trading venue; instruments with an underlying index/basket of instruments traded on a venue. Applies regardless of whether execution was on a UK, Gibraltar or EU trading venue, or off-venue. Must be reported <strong>as soon as possible, no later than close of the next business day</strong>. UK investment firms determine whether their traded instrument is reportable. \"UK investment firm\" = person whose regular occupation/business is providing investment services to third parties, or performing investment activities professionally.</p>\n<h4>12.2.1 UK MiFIR</h4>\n<p>Transaction reporting gives regulators post-trade information to monitor market abuse (incl. insider dealing) and general market surveillance. The FCA's <strong>Transaction Monitoring Unit (TMU)</strong> oversees compliance; reports go via <strong>Approved Reporting Mechanisms (ARMs)</strong>, eg UnaVista, TRAX. Reporting is on a <strong>trade date + 1</strong> basis.</p>\n<p><strong>Trade reporting</strong> feeds market depth/liquidity/transparency data (single-sided - only the reporting institution's side). <strong>Transaction reporting</strong> can be dual-sided (both counterparties in scope).</p>\n<p>MiFID II post-trade transparency covers equities <em>and</em> non-equity instruments admitted to Regulated Markets, MTFs, OTFs. Reporting deadlines (unless waivers/deferrals apply): <strong>1 minute</strong> for equity transactions; <strong>5 minutes</strong> for in-scope non-equity instruments.</p>\n<h4>12.2.2 UK EMIR</h4>\n<p>All derivatives contracts must be reported to a <strong>trade repository</strong>. UK counterparties report under UK EMIR; EU counterparties under EU EMIR - even if one party is non-financial. Both counterparties must generally report (exceptions for mandated financial-counterparty reporting exist); by prior arrangement, one party can report for both (must be indicated). <strong>Non-UK entities have no UK EMIR reporting obligation</strong> (incl. UK branches of third-country firms, eg a Canadian bank's London branch). Reporting may be delegated to a third party (CCP, trading venue, counterparty bank).</p>\n<h4>12.2.3 Short Selling Regulation (SSR)</h4>\n<p>Private/public notification regime for net short positions. FCA intervention powers (adverse market conditions): extend notification/disclosure scope; require lenders to notify fee changes; restrict short selling/similar transactions; restrict/limit sovereign CDS transactions. Firms must notify the FCA (Electronic Submission System) by <strong>3.30pm</strong> the trading day after the position was reached; calculations made as at midnight on the day the position was reached. Market makers/authorised primary dealers can be exempt.</p>\n<h4>12.2.4 UK SFTR</h4>\n<p>Reporting for securities financing transactions (SFTs), similar to EMIR derivatives reporting, plus limits on collateral reuse. Reporting can be dual-sided; made to a trade repository, which submits to the FCA. Non-financial counterparties (corporates) are exempted by HM Treasury from SFTR reporting.</p>\n<h4>12.3 Reporting channels / responsibility</h4>\n<p>LSE systems: SETS (UK equities) and IOB (depositary receipts) - trade reporting is <strong>automatic</strong> for order-book trades. <strong>Off-order-book transactions</strong> (eg telephone trades) must be reported by the member firm as close to real time as possible, and in any case <strong>within 3 minutes</strong> of execution during the trade reporting period.</p>\n<p><strong>Trade reporting period</strong>: <strong>7.15am to 5.15pm</strong> each business day. If executed 7.15am-8.00am: report before 8.00am or within 3 minutes if later. If executed in the last 3 minutes of the period: report before 5.15pm. If executed outside the period: report before the start of the next trade reporting period.</p>\n<p>Responsibility rests with the <strong>more senior party</strong> (market maker member firm reports); if equal seniority, the <strong>selling</strong> member firm reports. Report must include: reporting member/counterparty identity; date/time; purchase or sale; dealing capacity (agency/principal); security and quantity; price; settlement due date; special conditions (eg ex-dividend).</p>\n<p>Exchange publishes reports incl. the <strong>Daily Official List (DOL)</strong> - closing prices, used for portfolio valuation. CREST settlement transaction reports: deadline <strong>8.00pm</strong> on trade day. International equities: Omgeo (DTCC subsidiary) online confirmation, both participants report by <strong>9.00pm</strong> on trade day.</p>\n<p>Post-Brexit: UK branches of EEA firms report to both the FCA and their home regulator; EEA branches of UK firms report to both home regulator and FCA. UK branches of third-country firms need not report to the FCA transactions executed outside the UK (exceptions may apply if a UK-based individual was involved in execution/pricing/client engagement).</p>\n<h4>12.4.1 Confirmation of transactions and periodic statements</h4>\n<p>Firms (other than those managing investments) executing a client order must: give essential information promptly, in a durable medium (for derivatives/options transactions); provide order status information on request; send a confirmation notice <strong>as soon as possible, no later than the next business day</strong> (or the business day after receiving confirmation from a third party).</p>\n<p>Confirmation notice contents: firm ID; client name/designation; trading day/time; order type; execution venue; financial instrument; buy/sell indicator; nature of order if not buy/sell (eg option exercise); unit price; total consideration; total commissions/expenses/mark-up-down; exchange rate (if currency conversion involved); client's settlement responsibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Record keeping</strong> for confirmations: UK MiFID business - at least <strong>5 years</strong> from dispatch; other business - at least <strong>3 years</strong>. Average pricing across tranches is acceptable for unit price calculation.</p>\n<h4>Periodic reporting</h4>\n<p>Investment managers must provide retail/professional clients with periodic statements (unless another party does so), containing: firm name; client's account name/designation; portfolio contents/valuation (each investment, value, cash balance start/end of period, performance); total charges (itemised: management fees vs execution costs, with note that a fuller breakdown is available on request); performance vs relevant benchmark(s); total dividends/interest/other payments received; corporate action information.</p>\n<p><strong>Frequency</strong>: at least <strong>quarterly</strong>; <strong>every 12 months</strong> if the client receives deal-by-deal confirmations and certain higher-risk investments are excluded; <strong>monthly</strong> if the client's portfolio is authorised to be leveraged. Deal-by-deal confirmations must still meet the transaction reporting content/timing rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Record keeping</strong> for periodic statements/execution notices: UK MiFID business - at least <strong>5 years</strong>; other business - at least <strong>3 years</strong> (exceptions for certain overseas customers and JISAs).</p>\n<p>Firms managing investments or operating accounts with uncovered open positions must report losses over a pre-agreed limit by the <strong>end of the business day</strong> the limit is breached (or the next full business day if breached on a non-business day). Portfolio management: must notify a client if the portfolio falls <strong>10% or more</strong> since the last valuation (and multiples of 10% thereafter), and similarly at investment level for contingent liability/leveraged holdings - <strong>but from 7 June 2023</strong> (following HM Treasury's 2022 Markets in Financial Instruments (Investor Reporting) (Amendment) Regulations) firms no longer have to provide this contingent-liability/leveraged-investment notification.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.13 Client Assets (CASS)","html":"\n<h4>13.1 Purpose of the client money and custody rules</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Client Assets Sourcebook (CASS)</strong> ensures firms take adequate steps to protect client money and client assets they are responsible for. CASS applies generally to every firm (subject to specific exemptions), and directly to activities for <strong>all client categories</strong> - retail, professional and ECP.</p>\n<p>Client money must be <strong>segregated</strong> from the firm's own money, so that on firm failure it is not available to the firm's creditors. Achieved by promptly placing client money in a separately designated client bank account, held by the firm on <strong>statutory trust</strong> (created under <strong>CASS 7.17.2R</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>CASS 6</strong> - custody rules: apply when a firm holds client financial instruments in the course of MiFID business, and when safeguarding/administering investments in non-MiFID business.</p>\n<p><strong>CASS 7</strong> - Client Money and Distribution Rules: apply to a UK MiFID investment firm when it holds client money in MiFID business (or any investment agreement with/for a client); when a non-MiFID firm opts into the MiFID client money rules; or when the firm holds money under CASS 5 (insurance mediation client money) and elects to apply CASS 7.</p>\n<p>Firms holding safe custody assets must: safeguard client ownership rights (esp. on insolvency); prevent client assets being used on the firm's own account without express client consent; introduce organisational arrangements minimising risk of loss/diminution.</p>\n<p>Firms must register correct legal title to assets in the name of the client, a nominee, or another authorised person, as appropriate. If a firm owns a nominee, it accepts the same responsibility as if it held the assets itself.</p>\n<h4>Client bank accounts</h4>\n<p>On opening a client bank account, the firm must notify the bank that client money must not be combined with the firm's own accounts; the account title must distinguish it from other firm accounts. Under the old rule, a UK bank had to acknowledge trustee status within <strong>20 (business) days</strong>, or the firm had to withdraw the money and place it elsewhere. <strong>Current rule</strong>: firms must obtain a signed acknowledgement letter for <strong>all</strong> client bank accounts (not just UK ones), and the <strong>20-business-day grace period has been removed</strong> - in effect, client money cannot be placed in the account until the signed acknowledgement letter has been returned.</p>\n<h4>Exemptions from the client money rules</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Money held in connection with a <strong>delivery versus payment (DvP)</strong> transaction - unless settlement fails to occur by close of business on the <strong>third business day</strong> following the delivery obligation's due payment date.</li>\n<li>Money that has become due and payable to the firm.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Firm failure - distribution of client assets</h4>\n<p>On firm failure, initial distribution of client assets is made on the basis of the firm's own records, from an <strong>initial client money pool</strong>. Any residual money after initial distribution goes into a <strong>second pool</strong>, distributed via the firm's claims process.</p>\n<h4>13.2 Exemptions from CASS</h4>\n<p>CASS does not apply to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Investment companies with variable capital (ICVCs).</li>\n<li>UCITS-qualifying schemes.</li>\n<li>A credit institution (bank) holding deposits with itself as banker (not as trustee).</li>\n<li>Coins held for the value of their metal.</li>\n<li>Money held in connection with a DvP transaction (unless payment fails to occur after 3 business days).</li>\n<li>Money due and payable to the firm.</li>\n<li>Cases where the firm carries on business in its own name but on the client's behalf, where required by the transaction's nature and the client agrees.</li>\n<li>CASS 6 and CASS 7 do not apply where a client transfers full ownership of a safe custody asset or client money to a firm to secure/cover present, future, actual, contingent or prospective obligations (title transfer collateral arrangements).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Specific individual rules within CASS may be disapplied depending on the nature of a firm's activities.</p>\n"}],"mcqs":[{"question":"A UK MiFID investment firm carries on MiFID business from a branch established in an EEA member state. Following Brexit, does the COBS Handbook apply to this business?","options":["Yes, COBS always applies to UK-incorporated firms regardless of location","No, the COBS Handbook no longer applies to a UK MiFID firm's business carried on from an establishment in an EEA state","Only the best execution rules apply","Only if the client is a retail client"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Following Brexit, COBS no longer applies to the MiFID business of a UK MiFID firm carried on from an establishment in an EEA member state. COBS does apply to third-country firms (including former EEA firms) operating a UK branch."},{"question":"Which two areas of COBS apply on a 'home state basis' to UK firms regardless of where in the world the business is conducted?","options":["Best execution and client order handling","Client agreements and suitability","Investment research and personal account dealing","Client money and custody"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The general geographic application rule for COBS has an exception for investment research and personal account dealing, which apply on a home state basis - ie, to UK firms wherever in the world they conduct that business."},{"question":"A firm wishes to communicate a new financial promotion on behalf of one of its appointed representatives (ARs). Which statement best describes the regulatory position?","options":["The AR's promotion needs no approval at all and the principal has no responsibility for it","The promotion is technically an exempt promotion not requiring approval, but the principal firm remains responsible for its content","The AR must personally seek FCA approval before the promotion is issued","ARs are prohibited from communicating any financial promotions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A financial promotion communicated by an AR is technically exempt and does not need approval, but COBS makes clear the principal firm is responsible for the content of financial promotions communicated by its ARs."},{"question":"Under the FCA's 2022 reforms to the appointed representatives regime, how far in advance must a principal notify the FCA of a new AR appointment?","options":["7 days","14 days","30 days","60 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Principals must notify the FCA of a new AR appointment 30 days before it takes effect. The 60-day period relates to principals providing information on existing ARs after the new rules came into force."},{"question":"Under the non-MiFID large undertaking test, a partnership must have net assets of at least what amount (at any time in the past two years) to qualify as a per se professional client?","options":["£2 million","£5 million","£10 million","£12.5 million"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A partnership or unincorporated association qualifies as a large undertaking (non-MiFID business) if its net assets are, or have at any time in the past two years been, at least £5 million (or currency equivalent)."},{"question":"For MiFID and equivalent third-country business, a company is a 'large undertaking' (and so a per se professional client) if it meets any two of which size thresholds?","options":["Balance sheet €20m, turnover €40m, own funds €2m","Balance sheet €12.5m, turnover €25m, 250 employees","Net assets £5m, turnover €25m, 50 members","Balance sheet €10m, turnover €20m, own funds €5m"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under MiFID/equivalent third-country business, a large undertaking must meet any two of: balance sheet total €20 million; net turnover €40 million; own funds €2 million. The €12.5m/€25m/250-employee test is the non-MiFID alternative test."},{"question":"A trustee of an occupational pension scheme wishes to qualify as a per se professional client (non-MiFID business). What is the minimum required combination?","options":["10 members and £5 million AUM","50 members and £10 million AUM","100 members and £20 million AUM","25 members and £2 million AUM"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A trustee of an occupational pension scheme, SSAS, or trustee/operator of a personal/stakeholder pension qualifies as a large undertaking if the scheme has, or has had, at least 50 members and assets under management of at least £10 million."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT part of the quantitative test for a retail client to become an elective professional client for MiFID business?","options":["Ten significantly sized transactions per quarter on average over the last four quarters","A financial instrument portfolio exceeding €500,000","At least one year's relevant professional experience in the financial sector","A minimum net worth of £250,000"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The quantitative test requires any two of: 10 significant trades/quarter average over 4 quarters; portfolio (cash + instruments) exceeding €500,000; and at least 1 year's relevant professional experience. There is no separate £250,000 net worth requirement in this test."},{"question":"Firm X provides services to person A, and knows that A is acting as agent for person B. In general, who should Firm X treat as its client?","options":["B, in all circumstances","A, unless the firm has agreed in writing to treat B as the client, or A's involvement is mainly to reduce the firm's duties to B","Whichever of A or B has greater net assets","The firm may choose either A or B at its discretion at any time"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The default position is to treat A (the agent) as the client, unless the firm has agreed in writing with A to treat B as its client, or A's involvement is mainly to reduce the firm's duties to B (in which case B must be treated as the client regardless)."},{"question":"Under COBS, for how long must a firm retain client agreement records, at minimum?","options":["3 years, or the duration of the relationship if shorter","5 years, or the duration of the relationship, whichever is longer (indefinitely for pension transfers/opt-outs/FSAVCs)","7 years in all cases","10 years for retail clients only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Client agreement records must be kept for the longer of 5 years or the duration of the relationship with the client, and indefinitely in the case of pension transfers, pension opt-outs, or free-standing additional voluntary contributions (FSAVCs)."},{"question":"A firm agrees a client agreement using a distance communication at the client's request, and complies with the voice telephone communication rules. When may it provide the agreement and required information to the client?","options":["It must always be provided before the client is bound","It may be provided immediately after the client is bound by the agreement","It never needs to be provided","Only within 30 days after the transaction"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where the client requested use of distance communications and the firm complies with the voice telephone communication rules, the firm may provide the agreement and information immediately after the client is bound, rather than beforehand."},{"question":"Which of the following is an example of an 'excluded communication' potentially exempt from the financial promotion rules?","options":["A direct offer promotion of a regulated CIS to retail clients","A promotion communicated to a high-net-worth individual under Article 48 of the FPO","A promotion relating to UK MiFID investment advice for a retail client","A cold call to a retail client with no existing relationship"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Excluded communications include those aimed at high-net-worth individuals (FPO Article 48) and sophisticated investors (Articles 50/50A). However, the exemption for excluded communications generally does not apply to UK MiFID business."},{"question":"Since 2023, before an authorised firm may approve financial promotions for communication by unauthorised third parties, what must it obtain from the FCA?","options":["A specific 'gateway' permission","Nothing - approval by any authorised firm is automatic","A letter of comfort from the client","Sign-off from the Prudential Regulation Authority"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In 2023 the FCA introduced a 'gateway' - firms wishing to continue approving financial promotions for third parties needed to apply to the FCA for permission. Firms without this permission may now only approve their own promotions."},{"question":"A firm's marketing materials include indications of past performance for an investment fund that has existed for eight years. What is the minimum period the past performance data must cover under COBS?","options":["1 year","3 years","5 years, shown in complete 12-month periods","10 years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Past performance information must cover at least the immediately preceding 5 years (or the whole period the investment/index/service has existed if shorter), and must show complete 12-month periods."},{"question":"Under the FCA's 2020 high-risk investment promotion rules, what is the minimum cooling-off period that must be applied to a first-time investor with a firm before they can invest in a high-risk product following a direct offer financial promotion?","options":["No cooling-off period is required","24 hours","48 hours","7 calendar days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA's rules introduced a minimum 24-hour cooling-off period for first-time investors with a firm, although the FCA encourages firms to apply higher standards than this minimum."},{"question":"Which category of high-risk investment is completely banned from being promoted to retail investors under the FCA's rules?","options":["Restricted Mass Market Investments (RMMIs)","Non-Mass Market Investments (NMMIs)","UCITS funds","Cash deposit ISAs"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The promotion of Non-Mass Market Investments (NMMIs) is banned to retail investors entirely. Restricted Mass Market Investments (RMMIs) may be promoted to retail investors but subject to restrictions (eg not permitted for non-readily realisable securities and peer-to-peer agreements)."},{"question":"A prescribed risk summary accompanying a high-risk investment promotion should, according to FCA guidance, take no longer than how long to read (absent valid reasons to diverge)?","options":["30 seconds","2 minutes","5 minutes","10 minutes"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Prescribed risk summaries should take no more than two minutes to read, though firms can diverge from this where there are valid reasons to do so."},{"question":"Under the FCA's cryptoasset financial promotion rules, which client categorisation option is NOT available for cryptoasset promotions, unlike other RMMI promotions?","options":["Restricted investor","High-net-worth investor","Self-certified sophisticated investor","Certified high-net-worth investor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"For cryptoasset promotions classified as RMMIs, the self-certified sophisticated investor category will not be applied - it is specifically excluded from the categorisation routes available for cryptoasset promotions."},{"question":"A firm wishes to cold call a retail client with whom it has no existing relationship, to promote a higher-volatility fund. Is this permitted under COBS?","options":["Yes, cold calling is always permitted for packaged products","No - cold calling is not permitted where it relates to a higher-volatility fund or a life policy linked to such a fund","Yes, provided the call takes place between 9am and 5pm","Yes, provided the client is informed of their right to cancel"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cold calls are permitted only in limited circumstances - eg where an existing relationship exists and the call is expected, or the call relates to a generally marketable packaged product that is NOT a higher-volatility fund or a life policy linked to such a fund."},{"question":"Under the suitability rules, what may a firm assume about a per se professional client (for UK MiFID business) that it may NOT assume about an elective professional client?","options":["That the client has sufficient knowledge and experience","That the client is able to financially bear the related investment risks consistent with their investment objectives","That the client wants to receive a suitability report","That the client is an eligible counterparty"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm may assume both per se and elective professional clients have the necessary knowledge and experience (for MiFID business). However, it may only assume the client's ability to financially bear investment risk for PER SE professional clients, not elective professional clients."},{"question":"A discretionary fund manager repeatedly trades a client's portfolio, generating substantial commission, in a pattern that is not justified by the client's investment strategy. This is best described as:","options":["Switching","Insistent client dealing","Churning","Aggregation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Churning is the activity of over-dealing or trading more frequently for a client than is warranted, in order to generate additional fees or commissions - most relevant where a firm manages a portfolio on a discretionary basis."},{"question":"A firm makes a personal recommendation to a retail client to take out a personal pension scheme, and the statutory cancellation rules apply. Within what period must the firm provide the suitability report?","options":["Before the contract is concluded, in all cases","Within 14 days of concluding the contract","Within 30 days of concluding the contract","No suitability report is required for pension contracts"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For a personal pension scheme or stakeholder pension where the cancellation rules apply, the suitability report must be provided within 14 days of the contract being concluded."},{"question":"A friendly society makes a personal recommendation in connection with a small life policy, with a premium of £45 a year. Is a suitability report required?","options":["Yes, always required for life policies","No - there is an exception for friendly society recommendations on small life policies with premiums not exceeding £50/year (or £1/week)","Only if the client is a retail client aged over 65","Yes, but only within 30 days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is a specific exception to the suitability report requirement where a friendly society makes a personal recommendation on a small life policy with a premium not exceeding £50 a year (or £1 a week if paid weekly). £45/year falls within this exception."},{"question":"A client rejects the personal recommendation given by their adviser and instead instructs the firm to execute a different, riskier transaction, which the firm agrees to facilitate. What is this client known as, and what must the firm do?","options":["A professional client; no further action is required","An insistent client; the firm must clearly communicate that the transaction is not recommended, the reasons why, and the risks involved","An elective ECP; the firm must refuse to execute the transaction","A vulnerable customer; the transaction must be referred to the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This describes an insistent client. The firm must communicate, clearly and fairly, that it has not recommended the transaction, why it is not in line with its recommendation, the risks of the proposed transaction, and why it did not recommend that transaction."},{"question":"Under the appropriateness rules, which of the following would generally be classed as a 'non-complex' financial instrument, meaning the appropriateness test need not be applied for an execution-only transaction?","options":["An exchange-traded derivative contract","A structured product with embedded derivatives","Shares listed on a UK regulated market","A warrant"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Shares listed on a UK or EEA regulated market (or equivalent third-country market) are treated as non-complex instruments. Derivatives, embedded-derivative products, and warrants are excluded from the non-complex category."},{"question":"A retail client declines to provide the information a firm needs to assess appropriateness for a non-advised transaction. What must the firm do?","options":["Refuse to proceed with the transaction under any circumstances","Warn the client that it is unable to assess appropriateness, then use its judgement on whether to proceed if the client still wants to","Proceed automatically without any warning","Refer the matter to the Financial Ombudsman Service"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If a client declines to provide the required information, the firm must warn the client that it cannot assess the appropriateness of the product/service (this warning may be standardised). If the client still asks to proceed, it is up to the firm's judgement whether to do so."},{"question":"What is the maximum length, in A4 pages, of a Key Information Document (KID) under the PRIIPs Regulation?","options":["1 page","2 pages","3 pages","5 pages"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A KID is a stand-alone, standardised document that can be up to a maximum of three A4 pages."},{"question":"UCITS schemes currently produce a KIID rather than a PRIIPs KID. Until what date does this exemption run?","options":["31 December 2024","31 December 2025","31 December 2026","31 December 2028"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"UCITS KIID/KFD were given a five-year exemption period following implementation of PRIIPs, which has been extended to expire on 31 December 2026."},{"question":"A retail client makes an advised purchase of units in an authorised unit trust (AUT) and later wishes to exercise their statutory right to cancel. Within how many days of the relevant trigger date must they do so?","options":["7 calendar days","14 calendar days","21 calendar days","30 calendar days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Non-life and pension products (whether advised, non-distance contracts, or distance contracts) carry a 14 calendar day cancellation period. Only life and pension products have the longer 30 calendar day period."},{"question":"What is the cancellation period applicable to life and pension products under the COBS cancellation rules?","options":["14 calendar days","21 calendar days","30 calendar days","60 calendar days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Life and pension products carry a 30 calendar day cancellation period - longer than the 14 calendar day period applicable to most other designated investments and cash deposit ISAs."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes when disclosure of a conflict of interest to a client is appropriate under SYSC/COBS?","options":["Disclosure should be the firm's first response whenever any conflict is identified","Disclosure is a last resort, to be used only when the firm's organisational arrangements are not sufficient to manage the risk of client detriment","Disclosure is never an acceptable method of managing a conflict","Disclosure is only required for eligible counterparties"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Disclosure of a conflict of interest is a last resort - firms should first have arrangements designed to prevent conflicts arising. Disclosure alone, without adequate management, is not permitted (SYSC 10.1.8 vs SYSC 10.1.3)."},{"question":"Under the rules on investment research, a firm's financial analyst becomes aware of the content of unpublished research recommending a 'sell' on a stock. What is the analyst generally prohibited from doing?","options":["Discussing the research with the compliance department","Undertaking a personal transaction in the relevant investment before clients have had a reasonable opportunity to act on the research","Attending internal research meetings","Reading the final published version of the research"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms must prevent 'dealing ahead' - financial analysts and other relevant persons with knowledge of unpublished research must not undertake personal transactions (or trade for others) until clients have had a reasonable opportunity to act on the research, subject to limited exceptions."},{"question":"What distinguishes 'non-independent research' from 'investment research' under the COBS conflicts of interest rules?","options":["Non-independent research is treated as a marketing communication subject to COBS 4, and must be clearly labelled as such","Non-independent research is exempt from all disclosure requirements","Non-independent research can only be produced by ECPs","There is no material difference between the two"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Non-independent research (not presented as objective/independent) is treated as a financial promotion/marketing communication and subject to COBS 4. It must be clearly identified as a marketing communication and disclose that it is not subject to the dealing-ahead prohibitions that apply to independent investment research."},{"question":"Under the inducements rules, which of the following is a permitted 'minor non-monetary benefit' that a firm may accept in connection with providing independent advice to a retail client?","options":["A cash payment from a product provider","Reasonable de minimis hospitality such as food and drink at a business meeting or conference","An all-expenses-paid overseas holiday","A percentage-based commission linked to sales volume"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Minor non-monetary benefits permitted include generic/personalised product information, third-party issuer-commissioned material promoting a new issuance, conference/seminar/training participation, and reasonable de minimis hospitality (eg food and drink at a meeting or conference)."},{"question":"A UK asset manager wishes to receive third-party investment research on a company with a market capitalisation of £150 million without paying for it separately. Is this permitted?","options":["No, all research must always be paid for separately regardless of market capitalisation","Yes - research on companies with a market capitalisation below £200 million is exempt from the inducement rules","Only if the company is listed outside the UK","Only with prior written approval from the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA introduced an exemption from the inducement rules for research on companies with a market capitalisation of less than £200 million, meaning UK-based fund managers do not have to pay for this research separately (though they may choose to)."},{"question":"Under COBS, what standard must a MiFID investment firm meet when seeking to obtain the best possible result for its clients when executing orders?","options":["All reasonable steps","All sufficient steps","Best efforts only","No specific standard is required"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MiFID investment firms (COBS 11.2A) must take 'all sufficient steps' to obtain the best possible result - a higher standard than the 'all reasonable steps' that continues to apply to AIFMs and residual CIS operators (COBS 11.2)."},{"question":"How often must a MiFID investment firm review the ongoing appropriateness of its order execution policy and arrangements, at minimum?","options":["Monthly","Quarterly","At least annually, or when material changes occur","Every 5 years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms must review the ongoing appropriateness of their order execution policy and arrangements at least annually, or when there are material changes affecting the firm's ability to continue obtaining the best possible result for clients."},{"question":"A client gives a firm a specific instruction relating to only one aspect of an order (eg the venue), leaving other aspects (eg timing) to the firm's discretion. What is the effect on the firm's best execution obligation?","options":["The firm is entirely relieved of its best execution obligation for the whole order","The firm is deemed to have satisfied best execution only in relation to the specifically instructed part of the order; the rest remains subject to best execution","The firm must ignore the client's instruction and apply its own execution policy throughout","The client's instruction is automatically void"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Following a specific client instruction satisfies best execution only for the part or aspect of the order covered by that instruction. Any other aspects of the order not covered remain subject to the general best execution obligations."},{"question":"A firm wishes to aggregate a client order with its own proprietary order. Under COBS, how must it allocate the trades if only some of the aggregated order is executed?","options":["The firm's own order must always be filled first","Client orders must be allocated in priority over the firm's own order, unless the firm can show that without including its own order, less favourable terms would have resulted","Orders must always be allocated pro-rata regardless of circumstances","The firm may choose to allocate however it wishes with no restriction"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where a firm aggregates its own-account orders with client orders, it must allocate client orders in priority over its own, unless it can show that without including its own order, less favourable terms would have resulted - in which case proportional allocation is permitted."},{"question":"A firm receives a client limit order for shares listed on a regulated market, which it cannot immediately execute given prevailing market conditions. What must it generally do, absent contrary client instructions?","options":["Cancel the order immediately","Make the limit order public immediately, unless it is over normal market size","Hold the order privately until it can be executed at any point in the future","Execute the order at a worse price to guarantee completion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Unless the client instructs otherwise, a firm receiving a client limit order that cannot be immediately executed must make it public immediately (via a regulated market/MTF order book or other accessible means), so it can be executed as soon as possible - except for orders over normal market size."},{"question":"Under the personal account dealing rules, which of the following transactions is generally EXEMPT from the restrictions?","options":["A discretionary management service deal where there was no prior communication between the manager and the relevant person about the deal","A direct personal instruction to buy shares in a company the relevant person's firm is advising on a takeover of","Disclosure of confidential information to a family member who then trades on it","A transaction that conflicts with the firm's duties to a customer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Personal account dealing rules do not apply to deals under a discretionary management service where there is no prior communication between the portfolio manager and the relevant person about the deal, deals in units of certain funds where the relevant person isn't involved in fund management, and personal transactions in life policies."},{"question":"Under UK MiFIR, by when must a UK investment firm report a reportable transaction to the FCA?","options":["Immediately, in real time","As soon as possible, no later than the close of the next business day","Within 5 business days","Within 1 calendar month"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Complete and accurate details of reportable transactions must be reported to the FCA as soon as possible, and no later than the close of the next business day (a 'trade date + 1' basis)."},{"question":"Under MiFID II post-trade transparency rules, within what deadline must an equity transaction generally be publicly reported (absent waivers/deferrals)?","options":["Immediately with no delay permitted","1 minute","5 minutes","15 minutes"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Unless waivers or deferrals apply, post-trade transparency reporting deadlines are 1 minute for equity transactions and 5 minutes for in-scope non-equity financial instruments."},{"question":"A UK branch of a Canadian bank (a third-country firm) executes a derivatives trade. Does it have a reporting obligation under UK EMIR?","options":["Yes, all branches operating in the UK must report under UK EMIR","No - non-UK entities, including UK branches of third-country firms, do not have a UK EMIR reporting obligation","Only if the trade exceeds £1 million in notional value","Only if the counterparty is a UK financial institution"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under UK EMIR, non-UK entities do not have a reporting obligation. This expressly includes UK branches of third-country firms, such as the London branch of a Canadian bank."},{"question":"Under the UK Short Selling Regulation (SSR), by what time must a firm notify the FCA of a net short position via the Electronic Submission System?","options":["9.00am on the same trading day","12 noon on the same trading day","3.30pm on the trading day after the position was reached","5.00pm on the trading day the position was reached"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms must notify the FCA by 3.30pm on the trading day after the day the position was reached, with calculations made as at midnight on the trading day the position was reached."},{"question":"Under UK SFTR, which category of counterparty is exempted by HM Treasury from the obligation to report securities financing transactions?","options":["UK financial counterparties","Non-financial counterparties (ie, corporate companies)","Central counterparties (CCPs)","Trade repositories"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under UK SFTR, non-financial counterparties (ie, corporate companies) are exempted by HM Treasury from having to report their securities financing transactions."},{"question":"An off-order-book equity trade is executed on the London Stock Exchange via telephone at 10.00am. Within how many minutes must the member firm report the trade, in normal circumstances?","options":["1 minute","3 minutes","10 minutes","30 minutes"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Off-order-book transactions must be reported by the member firm as close to real time as possible, and in any case within three minutes of execution, by reference to the LSE's trade reporting period (7.15am to 5.15pm)."},{"question":"By when must a firm (other than one managing investments) send a client a confirmation notice following the execution of a client order?","options":["Immediately, with no exceptions","As soon as possible, but no later than the next business day","Within 3 business days","Within 1 week"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm must send a notice confirming the deal details as soon as possible, but no later than the next business day; where confirmation is received from a third party, it must be passed on no later than the business day following receipt."},{"question":"How frequently must a firm managing investments send periodic statements to a retail client, as a general minimum requirement?","options":["Weekly","Monthly","At least quarterly","Annually"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Periodic statements must generally be sent at least quarterly. Exceptions: monthly if the client's portfolio may be leveraged; every 12 months if the client receives deal-by-deal confirmations and certain higher-risk investments are excluded."},{"question":"A firm carrying on portfolio management for a retail client must notify the client if the portfolio falls by what percentage since the last valuation?","options":["5% or more","10% or more (and multiples of 10% thereafter)","20% or more","25% or more"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm providing portfolio management must provide a depreciation notification if the client's portfolio falls 10% or more since the last valuation, and at each subsequent multiple of 10%."},{"question":"Following the Markets in Financial Instruments (Investor Reporting) (Amendment) Regulations, from what date do firms no longer need to provide a separate depreciation notification for contingent liability or leveraged investments at an individual holding level?","options":["1 January 2022","7 June 2023","31 December 2023","1 March 2025"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Following HM Treasury's 2022 Markets in Financial Instruments (Investor Reporting) (Amendment) Regulations, from 7 June 2023 firms no longer have to provide the depreciation notification at an investment level for contingent liability or leveraged investments."},{"question":"Under CASS, client money held by a firm is protected through which legal mechanism?","options":["A floating charge in favour of the client","A statutory trust created under CASS 7.17.2R","A personal guarantee from the firm's directors","An insurance policy underwritten by the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Client money is segregated and held on a statutory trust, created by the rules laid down in CASS 7.17.2R, ensuring that on the firm's failure the money is not available to repay the firm's general creditors."},{"question":"Under the current CASS rules, when may a firm place client money into a newly opened client bank account?","options":["Immediately upon opening the account, regardless of bank acknowledgement","Only after the bank has signed and returned the acknowledgement letter confirming trustee status - there is no grace period","Within 20 business days of opening the account, even without acknowledgement","At any time within 60 days of account opening"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The old rule gave banks 20 business days to acknowledge trustee status. The current rule removed this grace period: firms must obtain a signed acknowledgement letter for all client bank accounts before placing client money into them."},{"question":"Under CASS, the client money exemption for delivery versus payment (DvP) transactions ceases to apply if settlement has not occurred by when?","options":["Close of business on the same day as the transaction","Close of business on the third business day following the due payment date of the delivery obligation","Close of business on the fifth business day","There is no time limit on the DvP exemption"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The client money rules do not apply to money held in connection with a DvP transaction, unless the DvP does not occur by close of business on the third business day following the date of payment of a delivery obligation."},{"question":"On the failure of a firm holding client money, how is the initial distribution of client assets carried out?","options":["On the basis of the firm's own records, from an initial client money pool","Only after a full independent audit has been completed, which may take years","Equally among all clients regardless of their actual holdings","By the FCA directly, bypassing the firm's records entirely"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"On firm failure, the initial distribution of client assets is made on the basis of the firm's own records, from an initial client money pool. Any residual money after this initial distribution goes into a second pool for further distribution via the claims process."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What's the key difference between 'suitability' and 'appropriateness'?","back":"Suitability = advisory/discretionary services (firm must ensure the investment matches objectives, knowledge, financial situation). Appropriateness = non-advised services (execution-only/arranging) in COMPLEX products only — lighter-touch knowledge/experience check."},{"front":"When is NO suitability or appropriateness check required at all?","back":"Execution-only dealing in NON-complex products — only the client's name and address are needed."},{"front":"Does appropriateness apply to professional clients too?","back":"Yes — appropriateness protects BOTH retail and professional clients for non-advised complex-product business. (Suitability for retail clients covers all 3 factors; for per se professional clients under MiFID, the firm only needs objectives; for elective professional clients, objectives + financial situation — knowledge/experience is assumed.)"},{"front":"What are the 2 quantitative tests (need 2 of 3) for elective professional client status under MiFID?","back":"1) 10+ significantly-sized trades per quarter over the past 4 quarters 2) Portfolio > €500,000 (cash + financial instruments) 3) Worked in financial services professionally for 1+ year in a role requiring knowledge of the transactions."},{"front":"What extra test must be passed alongside the quantitative test for elective professional status (MiFID)?","back":"The QUALITATIVE test — the firm must independently assess the client has sufficient expertise/experience/knowledge. Both tests are required, not just the quantitative one."},{"front":"How does elective professional client status work for NON-MiFID business?","back":"Only the qualitative test + a written statement that protection will be lost — there is NO quantitative test for non-MiFID business."},{"front":"Are local authorities professional or retail clients?","back":"RETAIL clients under MiFID II — a very common trap since they 'sound' institutional. (The Bank of England, IMF, and pension funds ARE per se professional clients, for contrast.)"},{"front":"Can an individual ever be an eligible counterparty?","back":"NO — individuals can never be eligible counterparties, however experienced. Special purpose vehicles, per contrast, count as 'other institutional investors' (professional client, not ECP)."},{"front":"If an eligible counterparty asks for more protection, what happens?","back":"The firm re-categorises them as a PROFESSIONAL client (not straight to retail) unless the client specifically also asks to be treated as retail."},{"front":"What's the retail-client protection rule for best execution?","back":"Best result = total consideration = price + costs of execution (venue fees, clearing/settlement). Costs payable TO THE FIRM ITSELF are excluded from this specific assessment."},{"front":"If a client gives a specific instruction that conflicts with the order execution policy, what must the firm do?","back":"Follow the CLIENT'S specific instruction — doing so is deemed to satisfy best execution, even if the execution policy would have achieved a better result."},{"front":"When must an unexecuted client limit order be made public, and what are the exceptions?","back":"Must generally be made public immediately (e.g. via a regulated market/MTF) unless: (1) the client instructs otherwise, or (2) the order is larger than normal market size."},{"front":"Is order aggregation ever mandatory?","back":"NO — never mandatory. If used, the firm needs a written allocation policy and must believe it won't disadvantage clients overall. If a partially-filled aggregated order includes the firm's own order, the CLIENT'S allocation takes priority."},{"front":"What are the 14-day and 30-day cancellation rules?","back":"Standard packaged products / cash ISAs: 14 calendar days. Life policies and most pensions: 30 calendar days. Distance-marketing life policy contracts also get 30 days."},{"front":"How long must trade confirmations and periodic statements records be kept?","back":"MiFID business: 5 years from date of DISPATCH. Non-MiFID business: 3 years from date of dispatch. (Not from date of trade — a common trap.)"},{"front":"How often must periodic statements be sent to a retail client by default?","back":"Every 3 months (quarterly). Exception: leveraged/geared portfolios need MONTHLY statements. If the client gets deal-by-deal confirmations, periodic statements can go out just annually."},{"front":"By when must a trade confirmation reach a retail client?","back":"No later than the end of the NEXT business day (trade date + 1)."},{"front":"What qualifies as a 'durable medium' for client agreements, and what famously does NOT?","back":"Paper, email, CD-ROM/DVD, hard drive of recipient's computer — all count. WEBSITES DO NOT qualify as a durable medium by themselves."},{"front":"When is a client agreement NOT required?","back":"Where the client enters an insurance contract with the firm acting as PRINCIPAL — the one MiFID-business exception. Also not needed for advice-only services with no resultant deals (non-MiFID)."},{"front":"Client money — key rule about segregation and interest.","back":"Client money must be segregated from the FIRM'S money (applies to ALL client types, not just retail). Money due to the firm itself is not client money. If a firm pays interest on client money, that interest itself becomes client money too."},{"front":"How long does a delivery-vs-payment (DvP) transaction avoid client-money-rule treatment?","back":"Up to 3 business days — if DvP hasn't settled by close of business on day 3, the money must then be treated as client money."},{"front":"What is a Chinese wall, and is it mandatory?","back":"A physical/procedural information barrier to prevent conflicts of interest — a RECOGNISED method, but the FCA never mandates it by name. Firms just need adequate arrangements to manage information flow."},{"front":"What must a firm do about a conflict of interest it CAN manage vs one it CANNOT manage?","back":"CAN manage: manage it and continue dealing (no need to disclose). CANNOT manage: disclose the general nature/source to the client in a durable medium BEFORE acting."},{"front":"Define churning vs switching.","back":"Churning = excessive/unjustified trading in a client's portfolio (relevant to discretionary management) to generate commission. Switching = unjustified movement between similar packaged products."},{"front":"When is cold calling permitted? (3 conditions)","back":"1) Existing client relationship who would envisage/expect the call 2) Generally marketable, NON-geared, non-high-volatility packaged products 3) Readily realisable securities (excluding warrants). Emails are NEVER cold calls (only unwritten/real-time communications count)."},{"front":"What must a non-written financial promotion caller always do at the outset?","back":"Identify themselves and their firm, and make the purpose of the call clear — plus give a contact point if an appointment is arranged."},{"front":"Non-real-time vs real-time financial promotions — which needs prior compliance sign-off?","back":"Only NON-real-time promotions (adverts, websites, mailshots) must be checked/confirmed in advance by someone with appropriate expertise. Real-time promotions (calls, meetings) are spontaneous and can't be pre-approved."},{"front":"Can an authorised firm approve a promotion for an UNAUTHORISED firm?","back":"Yes, but only NON-real-time promotions (e.g. a magazine advert, website) — never telephone calls or other interactive/real-time promotions, since these could easily be changed after 'approval'."},{"front":"What must past-performance data in a promotion always include?","back":"Reference period(s) clearly stated; must NOT be the most prominent feature; must cover at least 5 years or the life of the product (whichever is shorter); a warning that past performance isn't a reliable indicator of future results; and a currency-fluctuation warning if not in the client's home currency."},{"front":"Can simulated past performance be used to project FUTURE performance?","back":"NO — simulated past performance can be shown (with a prominent warning it's simulated), but you cannot use it to project future performance."},{"front":"What is a durable-medium disclosure obligation that applies to ALL written retail communications?","back":"Firm's name included; fair & prominent indication of risks alongside benefits; understandable by the average member of the target group; and must not disguise/diminish important warnings."},{"front":"Inducements — what's generally allowed vs what's specifically prohibited?","back":"Allowed: minor non-monetary benefits (e.g. attending a seminar on a specific instrument), proper fees (custody, settlement, legal), rebates paid TO a client. Prohibited: general office equipment/software, office rent — anything the firm would normally pay for anyway."},{"front":"Research payment rules — what's the £200 million market-cap exemption?","back":"Research on companies with market capitalisation below £200 million is exempt from the inducement/unbundling rules — UK fund managers don't have to pay separately for it (from 1 March 2022)."},{"front":"Front-running rule for investment research — what must a firm wait for?","back":"After issuing research to clients, the firm must wait until clients have had a REASONABLE TIME to react before dealing on the firm's own account (no fixed number of days)."},{"front":"Personal account dealing — who does it apply to, and what's the core requirement?","back":"Applies to 'relevant persons' involved in providing investment/portfolio management services. Requires prior permission before dealing, and the firm must be informed promptly of any personal transaction."},{"front":"Name 2 transactions specifically EXCLUDED from personal account dealing rules.","back":"Life policies, and units in a UCITS scheme where the employee has no management relationship with that scheme."},{"front":"CASS record-keeping — how long?","back":"5 years for records of custody assets and client money reconciliations."},{"front":"What must firms disclose about client assets held by a third party (e.g. overseas custodian)?","back":"That a third party may hold them; the firm's responsibility for that third party's acts/omissions; what happens if the third party becomes insolvent; and a summary of protective steps taken. They do NOT have to guarantee against losses if the third party becomes insolvent."},{"front":"Which document must UCITS scheme managers produce for investors, and what must it include?","back":"A Key Investor Information Document (KIID) — includes scheme identification, objectives/policy, past performance or performance scenarios, costs/charges, and a risk/reward profile."}],"cisiMcqs":[{"question":"A buy-side firm wants to bundle payment for third-party research with execution services. What do the rules permit?","options":["Bundling is prohibited because research must always be paid from the firm's own resources","Bundling is permitted if the firm meets the relevant requirements on execution and research costs","Bundling is permitted only where the research relates to small-cap equity issuers","Bundling is prohibited unless the research provider is outside the firm's financial group"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA introduced payment optionality allowing UK buy-side firms greater flexibility, including bundling payments for third-party research and execution services, provided relevant requirements around execution and research costs are met. The other options contradict or narrow the rule incorrectly."},{"question":"All of the following statements regarding Chinese walls are true except:","options":["They are a method by which a firm can avoid a conflict of interests","They may be physical or artificial barriers preventing the flow of information","The FCA specify that Chinese walls must be implemented in all asset management firms","They may be operated by firms with several departments"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA defends the use of Chinese walls but does not require Chinese walls to be put in place in all firms without exception."},{"question":"The following are true of investment advertisements sent to retail clients except that they must:","options":["Not use any past performance data","Be fair, clear and not misleading","Indicate that the value of shares can go down as well as up","Warn that past performance is no guarantee of future performance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Past performance data may be used, provided a 'wealth warning' is given."},{"question":"The FCA Conduct of Business rules apply to all the following firms EXCEPT:","options":["An adviser recommending life policies","A company operating a stakeholder pension scheme","A bank providing retail broker services","A building society offering FX transactions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FX transactions are not regulated activities and are not covered under the conduct of business rules."},{"question":"Which of the following is a 'personal recommendation' under MiFID?","options":["Advising a client how to fill in an application form","Discussing company news or announcements","Explaining the risks of a particular investment instrument","Suggesting the purchase of a particular investment instrument"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The others are not 'personal recommendations'. They are general advice and, as far as MiFID is concerned, not business."},{"question":"Churning relates to:","options":["The overtrading of investments only","UK clients only","Retail clients only","Changing between packaged products only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Churning is the activity of dealing in securities with excessive frequency for a client in order to generate additional fees/commissions for the firm. It is relevant when, for example, a firm manages a client's portfolio on a discretionary basis."},{"question":"In relation to providing investments, the rule on inducements in the Conduct of Business rules, which ONE of the following is TRUE?","options":["The rules apply only when conducting business with retail clients","The rule does not prohibit the firm from paying an inducement to the client","The rule prohibits any non-monetary benefit, no matter how minor it may appear to be","The inducement must not be disclosed to the client until after the service is provided"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The rule \"\"A firm must not pay to, or accept from, a person other than the client, any fee, commission or non-monetary benefit\"\" allows payment to, and from, the client.\n\nThe inducement rules apply to business conducted with all customers, i.e. professional clients and retail clients.\n\n\nDisclosure of the inducement must be made to the client before providing the service. The prohibition does not apply to 'minor non-monetary benefits'."},{"question":"A firm needs to assess the appropriateness of an investment or service for a retail client when the service is:","options":["Based on shares that trade on a regulated market","Only the reception and transmission of the client's orders of a bond","Provided at the initiative of the client","To arrange the purchase of a contingent liability instrument"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Assessing appropriateness is generally needed when the firm provides services other than recommendations and managing investments, such as introducing a client to an investment opportunity. However, it does not apply simply to execution only trades.\nIt is also required for arranging deals in derivatives and warrants for retail clients and assessing appropriateness for another MiFID firm.\nAlthough services on regulated markets that fall under the above requirements are covered, it is not only those markets that are covered."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT taken into account when reclassifying a retail client as a professional client?","options":["Knowledge and understanding","Frequency of previous deals","Financial standing","Integrity and reputation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"When determining if a retail client can be classified as a professional client (an 'expert' customer) a firm has to consider the following factors:\nThe client's knowledge and understanding of the relevant designated investments and markets, and the inherent risks.\nHow long the client has been active in these markets.\nHow frequently the client has been dealing in the markets.\nThe extent to which a client has relied on advice on investments.\nThe size and nature of the transactions undertaken by the client."},{"question":"To whom should a firm produce a document about compensation claims when carrying on MiFID business?","options":["Professional clients only","Retail and professional clients only","Retail clients only","All clients"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A firm which carries on MiFID business must make available to any client who has used or intends to use those services, the information necessary for the identification of the compensation scheme or any other investor compensation scheme of which the firm is a member or any alternative arrangement provided for in accordance with the investor compensation scheme."},{"question":"A firm is outside the scope of the Consumer Duty. Which requirement continues to apply to its treatment of customers?","options":["Principle 12 requiring good outcomes for retail customers","Principle 6 requiring due regard to customers' interests and fair treatment","A duty to apply retail customer outcomes to its products and services","A duty to oversee the actions of firms in the distribution chain"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms not subject to the Consumer Duty must continue to adhere to Principle 6 and Principle 7. Principle 6 requires firms to pay due regard to the interests of customers and treat them fairly, which underpins Fair Treatment of Customers."},{"question":"Assuming that the client has approached the firm to request recategorisation, which one of the following could not be considered an elective eligible counterparty?","options":["The management company of a CIS","An investment firm based overseas","A commodity firm that trades for commercial purposes only","An individual trading for their own account with 10 years of professional experience"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An individual and other institutional investors cannot choose to be an elective eligible counterparty."},{"question":"When must a firm disclose charges to a retail client?","options":["Before providing the service","Along with the first contract note","After completion of the first deal","At the discretion of the customer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A firm must notify RETAIL CLIENTS of the amount or basis of charges IN WRITING, BEFORE any work is done. The FCA is comfortable with professional clients agreeing the basis of charges in arrears of any dealings, but this arrangement is not appropriate for retail clients."},{"question":"With regard to the approval of a non-real time financial promotion, which of the following is true? A firm:","options":["Can use any third party to approve the financial promotion but it remains the responsibility of the firm","Cannot use a third party to approve the financial promotion","Can use any third party authorised to approve the financial promotion and the promotion remains the responsibility of the firm","Can use any third party authorised to approve the financial promotion and the promotion becomes the responsibility of the third party"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In approving a financial promotion, the approving firm takes on responsibility for the promotion."},{"question":"Which one of these is not an excluded communication under the financial promotion rules?","options":["An unapproved financial promotion by an unauthorised firm","A financial promotion that is exempt under the FPO","A one-off financial promotion that is not a cold call","A personal quotation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is not an excluded communication, and would be considered a criminal offence, as it would in fact be a breach of s21 FSMA."},{"question":"A firm that manages a client's investments must ensure that which of the following occurs?","options":["Portfolio are benchmarked against the FTSE All Shares Index","Portfolio performances are benchmarked at least monthly","Any index they use to benchmark performance is specified in advance","Any deviation from the benchmark triggers a benchmark review"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The first benchmark (FTSE All Shares) might be comparable in some cases but not all and so is not the best answer here. The timing of performance benchmarking is not specified in the COBS. The firm should also include an outline of any designated investments that may be included in the portfolio, not just those in which it is currently invested."},{"question":"If providing execution only services in a non-complex product, the only information a firm requires is the client's:","options":["Name and address","Financial situation","Experience and expertise","Investment objectives"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The choices available reflect the rule on suitability or appropriateness:\n- Client's knowledge\n- Client's financial capability\n- Client's objectives\nThese are not required for execution only services in non-complex products. The only choice left is the name and address of the client. This makes logical sense, in that the firm needs to know to whom they must send the contract notes, etc."},{"question":"EMIR sets out requirements for reporting and risk management in the OTC derivative markets. Which firms are covered by this regulation?","options":["All EEA-incorporated and established entities","EEA fund management entities only","EEA financial entities only","EEA non-financial entities only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The rules apply to all EEA-incorporated and established entities, catching for the first time non-financial corporate entities and not just financial entities, such as banks."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is true with respect to cancellation rights on the sale to a retail client of a packaged product?","options":["None required","Cancellation rights should be offered","Cancellation and withdrawal rights of 21 days are compulsory","Cancellation rights may be offered for a minimum period specified by the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is the best answer. Depending upon the type of product, cancellation periods can vary between 14 and 30 calendar days."},{"question":"When considering best execution criteria for a retail client, which of the following would NOT be considered when obtaining the best possible result?","options":["The characteristics of the order","The characteristics of the investment","The costs incurred by the client payable to the firm","The costs incurred by the client payable to the execution venue"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The characteristics of the client, the order, the investment and the execution venue will all be taken into consideration. In addition for a retail client, costs directly relating to execution - including execution venue fees and clearing and settlement fees - must also be considered.\nCosts payable to the firm that the client is dealing with are not to be included in this assessment."},{"question":"When would an employee of an authorised firm MOST LIKELY be permitted to enter into a personal account transaction? If the transaction:","options":["It involves the use of confidential information acquired through the employees work","It might lead to a potential conflict of interest with the firm's clients","It involves the use of information about the transactions of one of the firm's clients","The employee is neither involved in a higher control function nor the provision of designated investment business"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In the three incorrect answers, there are obviously conflicts of interest or problems with client interests. The correct answer allows the person to not be considered as a relevant person in these circumstances."},{"question":"How long after a transaction must a confirmation be sent to the client?","options":["T+3","Not sent unless client requests it","Within three minutes","By the next business day"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Confirmations should be sent promptly (T+1)."},{"question":"A member of the compliance team is confirming the compliance of a financial promotion with FCA rules. Which of the following must be included in the promotion?","options":["Capital at risk products are clearly referred to as such","It labels the regulator as MiFID","It quotes a variety of expected returns","Reference to the British Bankers' Association Code of Conduct"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"MiFID is a directive, not a regulator. The expected returns figure should give a balanced view of the long and short term prospects of the investment instrument, but it is not something that must be included in the promotion."},{"question":"Which of the following sets of information is not required in a direct offer financial promotion to a retail client?","options":["The firm and its services","Costs and charges","Safekeeping of client money","Execution venues where trading will occur"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Execution venues will be in an order execution policy."},{"question":"Under the COBS, a professional client or an eligible counterparty can ask for a re-categorisation in which of the following cases?\nIIt is permitted on a trade by trade basis\nIIIt is permitted in general for all trades\nIIIIt is permitted on individual instrument classes\nIVIt is only available for complex financial instruments","options":["IV only","I, II, III","I and III","I and II"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The counterparty could ask or be offered the treatment in IV, but it is not limited to only complex financial instruments; it may be given for any product or service on either a trade by trade or general basis."},{"question":"When an FCA firm undertakes a promotion to a retail client, which is TRUE?","options":["Past performance can only be given for products for which there is five year performance history","The past performance must be the most prominent feature","Past performance data is mandatory when promoting to retail clients","There must be a warning that any performance data is not a reliable indicator of future performance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Past performance data can be included for less than five years, if it is for the life of the investment."},{"question":"The requirement for the firm to deliver the best possible result when executing client orders is expected for:","options":["Readily realisable instruments only","Money market instruments only","Securities, financial options and financial futures only","All types of financial instruments"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Best execution according to COBS applies to all types of financial instruments."},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, which of the following is permissible for the presentation of past, simulated past or future performance of a UK investment?","options":["The information must be a prominent part of the information presented to the client","The gross performance is presented","The figures are also stated in euros","The reference period and source of information are clearly stated"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The information may not be the most prominent feature of communication. If the performance is presented as gross, the commissions etc. must be disclosed. If the currency is in a non-home currency it must be stated that there is a possible currency impact."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be automatically given retail client protection by an FCA member firm under client categorisation?","options":["An independent person not authorised by the FCA","A trustee of a trust with assets of €5 million and funds of €1 million","A listed company with funds of €2 million and assets of €25 million","A body corporate with net turnover of €30 million and assets of €1 million"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A listed company with funds of €2 million or more and assets of €20 million or more would be considered a professional client. All of the others fail to meet the criteria required for professional categorisation and would be treated as retail clients."},{"question":"If a client can be considered as a professional client, then the firm may assume that they have sufficient knowledge of which risks?","options":["All designated investments offered by an authorised firm","Options on non-complex underlying assets","Designated investments for which they are classed as professional","Generally marketable packaged products"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Professional client status can be for particular investment services or transactions, so this would be the best answer.\n\nThere is no requirement that all designated investments are understood and the status does not specifically refer to options or packaged products (generally marketable or not)."},{"question":"Which personal transaction by a relevant person is exempt from the personal account dealing rules?","options":["A transaction in a listed bond","A transaction in an overseas equity","A transaction in a life policy","A transaction in an option"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Personal transactions in life policies are specifically excluded from the personal account dealing rules. The other investments are not identified as exempt categories in this context."},{"question":"Which of the following is not defined as a packaged product according to COBS?","options":["A regulated CIS","A personal pension scheme","An interest in an investment trust savings scheme","A government or public security"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A government or public security would be considered a readily realisable security, according to COBS."},{"question":"If the firm aggregates a client's order with a transaction for its own account, which of the following is true of the allocation policy?","options":["The firm's allocation of the order, if the order is partially allocated, must take priority","The client's allocation of the order, if the order is partially allocated, must take priority","The allocation of the orders must be in proportion with the order size","A retail client's allocation of the order, if the order is partially allocated, must take priority over a professional client's order"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"COBS states that the client must take priority on an aggregated order allocation. There is no stipulation as to whether the client needs to be a retail or professional client."},{"question":"Churning is the name given to:","options":["Dealing in derivatives","Unsuitable recommendations of packaged products","Dealing in advance of disseminating a research recommendation","Overtrading in securities"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Churning is the practice of over-dealing in a client's securities in an attempt to generate fees."},{"question":"Which of the following is least likely to give rise to a conflict of interest?","options":["Advising a client to sell when the firm has a client wanting to buy","Encouraging a client to buy when a firm has gone short","Advising a client to buy when the firm has a long over-exposure","Encouraging a client to sell when the firm's analyst rated the stock highly"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm that had gone short would be hoping for prices to fall. Encouraging a client to buy would not assist the firm in any way; it suggests that the firm has properly ignored its own position in giving advice."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is LEAST likely to cause a conflict of interest?","options":["Encouraging customer A to sell so B can buy","Recommending customer sells shares which an analyst rates highly","Customer instructs firm to buy shares when the firm holds a position","Advising both sides in a takeover situation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The question shows three situations where conflicts of interest could arise and one situation where it is unlikely. \n\n'Customer instructs firm to buy shares when the firm holds a position' is least likely to give rise to a conflict of interest.  The customer is instructing the firm to buy shares (i.e. execution only order). Remember, the question is not saying that the other situations will give rise to conflicts of interests in all cases, but that we should identify them as potential conflicts."},{"question":"Under the European Markets Infrastructure Regulation, which of the following is not a requirement on those that trade derivatives?","options":["Risk management procedures must be adopted for OTC derivatives","Certain OTC derivatives must be cleared through a qualifying central counterparty","To report all OTC derivative transactions to a trade repository","To ensure the orderly functioning of markets"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"To ensure the orderly functioning of markets is an objective of the regulator.\n\nEMIR focuses on the transparency and risk management of the OTC derivatives markets."},{"question":"A firm is preparing information for retail customers about a structured product. Which approach best reflects the Consumer Understanding Outcome?","options":["Providing technical product literature so customers can assess the full legal structure and complexity","Presenting key features, likely costs, risks and benefits so customers can make informed decisions","Delaying disclosure of product risks until they have become clear through customer interaction","Using the same detailed materials for retail and professional clients to ensure consistency"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Consumer Understanding Outcome requires firms to communicate in a way that enables customers to understand the features, likely costs, risks and benefits of products and services. Information should be clear, timely and appropriate for the target market, rather than overly technical or delayed."},{"question":"Which of the following are true with relation to a firm providing designated investment business to a retail client?\nIThe firm must pay due regard to the information needs of clients when communicating with them\nIIThe information should be sufficient to enable the client to understand the nature and risk of the service and investment\nIIIThe information communicated must be fair, clear and not misleading\nIVThe firm must provide appropriate information about itself to retail clients","options":["I and II","II and III","I, II and III","I, II, III, and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Note: the rules on information about the firm are an application of Principle of Business 7, 'Communications with Clients'."},{"question":"Which European regulation relates to the transparency and risk management in the OTC derivative markets?","options":["Markets in financial instruments directive","Capital requirements directive","Alternative investment fund manager directive","European markets infrastructure regulation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The European markets infrastructure regulation (EMIR) relates to the transparency and risk management in the OTC derivative markets. It requires trade reports to a trade repository, adequate risk management for trades, position management and collateral, and mandatory use of a central counterparty for some contracts."},{"question":"Which is FALSE in terms of the aggregation of orders?","options":["Firms must aggregate firm and client orders together","The firm must disclose its use of aggregation","Aggregation must be unlikely to operate to the disadvantage of clients","An order allocation policy must be in place"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Aggregation of trades is not mandatory."},{"question":"Where an investment bank is providing research to a fund manager a firm must apply a separately identifiable charge for this research. The FCA implemented changes under UK MiFID that fund managers do not have to pay if the research provided relates a company with a market capitalisation below:","options":["£50 million","£100 million","£200 million","£270 million"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"With respect to the provision of research under UK MiFID, the following new rules came into effect from 1 March 2022 under  the Inducement Rules for small or medium enterprises. Research on companies with a market capitalisation of below £200 million will be exempt – meaning that UK-based fund managers do not have to pay for this research. UK asset managers can continue to pay for this research if they so wish – due to operational limitations."},{"question":"Which of the following are not protected by the conduct of business rules sourcebook?","options":["Personal recommendations to a retail client","Pension transfers for retail client","Acting as investment manager for a retail client","Property purchases by a retail client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The purchase of property is not classed as designated investment business."},{"question":"A firm produces research with the sole intent of making this the focus of a recommendation to clients. When presenting such research recommendations, the firm must be considered as being bound by:","options":["Financial Promotion Rules","Distance Promotions Directives","Product Disclosure Rules","Client Asset Rules"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"All research recommendations are considered as being non-independent; research is to be considered as a marketing tool and so as a financial promotion."},{"question":"A firm plans to telephone a prospective client without prior appointment to discuss an investment. In which circumstance may the call be made under the cold calling rules?","options":["The recipient has an existing client relationship and would expect such a call","The recipient has seen the firm's website and may recognise the firm name","The recipient has previously invested elsewhere and understands investment risk","The recipient has been referred by another client and is likely to be interested"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A firm must not cold call unless one of the permitted conditions applies. One permitted condition is where the recipient has an existing client relationship with the firm and would envisage or expect to receive such a call."},{"question":"The financial promotion rules do not apply to marketing communications in respect of which one of the following?","options":["A predator company posting details of a takeover to shareholders","An authorised firm communicating details of derivative investments to clients","An independent financial adviser communicating its services in relation to pensions","An insurance company communicating a financial promotion regarding life policies"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Communications regarding takeovers are subject to the takeover code, not the FCA Financial Promotions Rules."},{"question":"A firm has just executed an order for a retail client. Within what period of time must the confirmation of trade be sent?","options":["Immediately","Within one business day","Within five business days","Within seven calendar days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The notice must be given as soon as possible and not later than one business day as specified in COBS."},{"question":"What must be disclosed in all written communication with a retail client?\nIThe name and business address of the firm making the communication\nIIThe fair and prominent indication of any relevant risks\nIIISufficient information that is clear to the average person likely to receive it\nIVInformation that is consistent with the information that is in the prospectus","options":["I and II","II and III","I, II, III","I, II, III, IV"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms must ensure that where they provide information about designated investment business, or issue and/or approve a financial promotion which is likely to be received by a retail client, they adhere to certain rules in COBS. These rules state that the:\n\n• firm’s name is included on the communication\n\n• information is accurate and does not emphasise potential benefits without also giving fair and prominent indication of any relevant risks\n\n• information is sufficient for, and presented in a way likely to be understood by, the average member of the group at whom it is directed or by whom it is likely to be received, and\n\n• information does not disguise, diminish, or obscure important items, statements or warnings."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a requirement for an elective professional client status according to the Conduct of Business rules?","options":["The client has carried out relevant trades at least ten times per quarter for the last four quarters","The value of the client's portfolio, including all financial assets, exceeds 500,000 euros","The client has been working in the financial sector in a professional capacity for at least one year","The client's cash deposits are in excess of 20,000 euros"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"There is no explicit requirement on the size of the client's cash deposits. The requirement is that the portfolio, including cash deposits must be greater than 500,000 euros."},{"question":"Limit orders not executed immediately must be made public to:","options":["ESMA","A Primary information provider","A regulated market","The FCA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Orders must be made public to a regulated/unregulated market or an MTF."},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, how many transactions per quarter (over the previous four quarters) must a client have carried out in order to be treated as an elective professional client?","options":["50","25","10","5"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"One of the criteria in the quantitative test is average frequency of transaction of ten per quarter."},{"question":"All of the following could be considered a professional client. Which of them is MOST LIKELY to have reached that status through election?","options":["Local authority","National body that manages public debt","Limited liability partnership","Designated investment exchange"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The others would all be considered per se professional clients. Local authorities are per se retail clients."},{"question":"An authorised firm communicates a financial promotion on a specific product containing past performance figures. Which of the following is true?","options":["Where the product does not have five years performance, the firm is not permitted to show past performance","A clear and prominent warning must assure investors the past performance is a reliable indicator of future performance","The past performance figures must be cited as gross and exclude the impact of fees and commissions","A warning about the effects of currency fluctuations must be included, if the figures are in a currency other than the client's domestic currency"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Firms must ensure that information including indications of past performance is such that:\n- the past performance is not the most prominent feature\n- it covers at least the immediately preceding five years (or the whole period for which the investment has been offered, and/or the financial index has been established, and/or the service has been provided if this is less than five years); in any event, it must show complete 12-month periods\n- reference periods and relevant sources are clearly shown\n- there is a clear and prominent warning that the data and/or figures refer to the investment's past performance and that past performance is not a reliable indicator of future results\n- if the figures are in a currency other than that of the EEA state in which the client is resident, that the currency is stated and there is a warning about the possible effects of currency fluctuations\n- if the performance is cited gross, that the effects of commissions, fees, tax and other charges are disclosed."},{"question":"A firm communicates a financial promotion for an overseas firm. Which additional statement may be required in the promotion?","options":["It may need to explain the extent to which UK compensation scheme arrangements will apply","It may need to confirm that all overseas promotions are approved by the FCA","It may need to state that UK conduct rules apply in full to the overseas firm","It may need to guarantee that retail clients will have identical legal protections"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A promotion for an overseas firm must, if relevant, explain the extent to which UK compensation scheme arrangements will be available and, if they will not, that fact. The other options overstate the position or impose requirements not set out in the rules."},{"question":"When can a retail client expect to receive information regarding their right to cancel an agreement to purchase units in an authorised unit trust?","options":["Before entering into the agreement at all times","After entering into the agreement at all times","Before and after entering into the agreement at all times","Before or after entering into the agreement depending on the situation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"If cancellation rights apply, firms must tell consumers of their rights in good time before (or where that is not possible, immediately after) the consumer is bound by the contract; and they must do so in a durable medium."},{"question":"What is the minimum amount of net turnover that a trust must have to be classified as professional client for MiFID business?","options":["€100 million","€75 million","€50 million","€40 million"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"MiFID requires at least €40 million in addition to at least one other of the quantitative tests.\nThe other quantitative tests are:\n- net assets €20m - own funds €2m\nThis is true of any undertaking, whether a company, charity or trust."},{"question":"Who performs the regulatory check of a mail-shot sent by an appointed representative?","options":["The compliance department of the appointing firm","The marketing department of the appointing firm","The overseer of the appointed representative who authorised the mail shot","The Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The general non-real time financial promotion rules require each promotion to be signed off by compliance or another employee with sufficient knowledge to do so."},{"question":"A firm plans an unsolicited real-time call to promote an investment product to a retail client. Which product may be promoted under the cold calling rules?","options":["A life policy linked to a higher-volatility fund","A generally marketable non-geared packaged product","A warrant traded on a recognised investment exchange","A readily realisable security issued by a public company"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm may cold call where the call relates to a generally marketable packaged product, provided it is neither a higher-volatility fund nor a life policy linked to such a fund. A warrant is excluded from the limited range of readily realisable investments, and the other products listed do not fall within the permitted categories for unsolicited real-time communications"},{"question":"A retail client who is habitually resident overseas does not wish to be sent a basic agreement letter for insurance business. The firm should:","options":["Report them to the FCA","Send them the client agreement regardless","Not send them a client agreement","Not do business with them"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Effecting insurance contracts is an exception to the rule requiring retail clients to be sent a client agreement. The location of the client is irrelevant."},{"question":"Under FCA rules on client categorisation, which of the following would ALWAYS be considered a per se professional client?","options":["Special purpose vehicle","A public company","A public body","An overseas individual"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Special purpose vehicles come under the definition of 'any other institutional investor'. The other choices would need to fulfil certain criteria before qualifying as a per se professional client."},{"question":"When communicating with retail clients, a firm producing information must ensure that:\nIThe name of the firm is included\nIIThe information does not emphasise any potential benefits of relevant business without risks being related\nIIIIt is presented in a manner that is likely to be understood by the average member of the target group\nIVIt does not obscure important items","options":["II only","I, II and III","I, III and IV","I, II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All of these are included in the General Rule of COBS."},{"question":"A firm provides personal recommendations to a retail client. After ensuring suitability, in which of the following situations would the firm need to provide the client with a suitability report?","options":["When a firm manages assets in a regulated CIS","When the firm recommends the investor increases their regular premiums to an existing pension policy","When the firm recommends the investor to invest in an ISA wrapped investment trust","When an investor purchases a \"small-life policy\" sold by friendly society with a premium no more than £50"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Suitability reports are needed for recommendations to deal in regulated collective investment schemes - unless the firm is acting as investment manager for the client - investment trusts (whether held in an ISA or not) and pensions."},{"question":"Which of the following will be included in a trade confirmation notice?\nIThe date on which the trade took place\nIIThe time at which the trade took place\nIIIThe total consideration paid/received in the transaction\nIVExecution venue","options":["I and II only","III only","I and IV","I, II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All of these are necessary."},{"question":"In order to gain favourable terms for its clients, a firm aggregates several client orders with its own order. During execution, only part of the order is filled. How must the order be allocated?","options":["In a pro rata fashion","On a first in, first out fashion","Based on the relative size of order","In line with the allocation policy"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The pro rata etc. might be part of the allocation policy."},{"question":"In the description of an investment within a financial promotion, in light of the 'fair, clear and not misleading' rule, the firm should always include which of the following?","options":["A clear representation of the long term yield figure of the investment alone","A clear representation of the short term yield figure of the investment alone","A single figure for the charges of the firm irrespective of their source","If fees cannot be specifically given, the basis on which these fees are assessed should be presented"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The firm should provide a yield figure that gives a balanced impression of both long and short term prospects. If the charging structure is complex then the fees should be explained in such a manner as to be fair, clear and not misleading to the recipient."},{"question":"Martin gives an instruction to a stockbroking firm to sell a holding of shares at a limit price much higher than the firm is willing to buy. Assuming that there have been no specific instructions beyond the order itself, which of the following best reflects what the firm should do next?","options":["Wait for the market to move to the limit price and then execute","Report Martin to the Regulator for market abuse","Execute immediately at the price it is willing to trade","Place the order on a regulated market"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"This question looks at the limit orders rule. Limit orders must be executed immediately or made public. As the firm is unwilling to execute the trade, they must place the order on a regulated market such as the LSE. The comment about \"assuming that there were no specific instructions\" is a reference to an exemption to this rule if the client has expressly stated that they do not want the orders to be made public: This exemption has not been triggered in this question."},{"question":"An authorised firm provides a service to a retail client. The FCA requires that the firm discloses its charges to that client. When should this disclosure occur?","options":["At the time of doing business","Before doing business","After a reasonable period of doing business","At the discretion of the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Charges must be made clear to clients before doing business or before the client is bound by the terms."},{"question":"In relation to the conduct of business rules in respect of disclosure of conflicts of interest, which ONE of the following is FALSE of the disclosure?","options":["It must be in writing","It can be made verbally","It must be made before acting on behalf of the client","It must be made in a durable medium"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The disclosure of a conflict of interest must be made in a durable manner and therefore cannot be made verbally.\nDetails of the conflict of interest must be disclosed to the client prior to acting on behalf of the client."},{"question":"If the information presented by a firm contains an indication of simulated past performance, then which of the following is appropriate?","options":["The simulated past performance must relate to a derivatives index that can react quickly to any movement in the underlying asset","The actual past performance of the investment should be ignored in order to avoid misleading the client","Simulated past performance is based on actual past performance of investments that are the same as the investments being simulated","A statement that the simulated past performance is a reliable indicator of the future performance of the instrument"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When firms give figures based on simulated (ie, notional, not having taken place in reality) past performance because the product or service does not have a track record, a firm must ensure that the simulated past performance figures:\n\n• relate to an investment or financial index (not necessarily a derivatives index)\n\n• are based on the actual past performance of one or more investments and/or indices which are the same as, or underlie, the investments being simulated\n\n• meet the rules set out in relation to past performance (except for the statement that they relate to that investment’s past performance, since they do not)\n\n• contain a prominent warning that they relate to simulated past performance and that past performance is not a reliable indicator of future performance."},{"question":"Which of the following statements regarding Chinese walls are false?","options":["They are a method by which a firm can avoid conflicts of interests","They may be physical or artificial barriers preventing the flow of information","The FCA specifies that Chinese walls must be implemented by investment firms","They may be operated by investment firms with several departments"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FCA regulations require that companies implement systems and procedures to prevent conflicts of interest. Yet the use of Chinese walls is not mandatory. This is because in certain situations (e.g. a sole trader) Chinese walls would not be necessary."},{"question":"If a firm is aware that Bob is acting as an agent for Bill, then Bill will be the firm's client if:","options":["Bob and the firm have agreed in writing to treat Bill as the client","Bill and the firm have agreed in writing to treat Bill as the client","Bill and Bob have agreed in writing that Bill will be treated as a client","Only if all three agree in writing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is given in COBS. It would also be the case if Bob were not a firm or overseas financial institution and the main purpose were to avoid the firm's responsibilities to Bill."},{"question":"The FCA rules on financial promotions expand which Principle for Businesses?","options":["Market Conduct","Financial Prudence","Customers' Interests","Clients' Assets"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"It is important that a firm has their clients' interests in mind when they communicate financial promotions."},{"question":"In which of the following situations would the firm need to apply the client money rules? If the money:","options":["Is paid by the client as part of a transaction due for settlement within one business day","Paid by the client is due to the firm for the firm's own account","Is interest paid on any holdings of client money","Has been paid to the client or authorised representative of the client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Client money rules govern money held by an authorised firm that belongs to a client, held in a client account. Money held by a firm is either 'client money' or 'firm's money'. \n\nWithin a delivery versus payment, if the money is due within three business days it is not necessary to count the money as client money. Once it is paid to a client then the money ceases to be client money. Although there is no obligation to pay interest on client money, if interest is paid, it needs to be treated as client money.\n\nIf money has been paid to a client, then it has left the firm's control and is now no longer client money"},{"question":"The COBS relating to financial promotions require an individual with appropriate expertise to check that the promotion is fair, clear and not misleading. For which of the following types of financial promotion is such a confirmation exercise required in advance of communicating the promotion?","options":["Real-time financial promotions","Non real-time financial promotions","All financial promotions","Real-time and non real-time financial promotions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Only non-real time financial promotions can be approved in advance of the communication.\n\nReal time financial promotions are spontaneous and it would be very difficult to approve in advance."},{"question":"Which clients need to have the most comprehensive risk explanation provided to them?","options":["A discretionary retail client selling shares to the firm","An execution only retail client selling warrants","A discretionary professional client engaged in derivatives","An execution only retail client buying options"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The risks should be explained to the client as specified by the client's knowledge etc. given that the first two are sales of assets pre-owned. A professional client would most likely not have a significant explanation of the risks given the requirements, as they are considered to have sufficient knowledge and experience."},{"question":"An investor opens a cash ISA with an authorised bank to benefit from the tax advantages offered. What is the minimum cancellation period the bank must offer?","options":["5 business days","10 business days","7 calendar days","14 calendar days"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The cancellation period for cancellable contracts is generally 14 days. However, for life policies and some pensions it is 30 days."},{"question":"If a firm communicates with a client solely by telephone, when must information on any agreement be provided?","options":["In good time before the consumer is bound by a distance offer or contract","Within seven calendar days after the conclusion of the contract","Within five business days after the conclusion of the contract","There would be no need to provide documentation for this type of communication"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In addition it must comply with the distance marketing rules and be in a durable medium."},{"question":"When a professional client is initially classified, which of the following terms is used?","options":["Person","Per se","Automatic","Elective"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A professional client will initially be classified as a per se client. They have to choose to be considered as an elective professional client."},{"question":"During the provision of a service to a professional client, a firm discovers a conflict of interest that it is unable to manage. What should the firm disclose?","options":["Nothing to the client, so as to respect client confidentiality","The general nature of any possible conflicts after the transactions were carried out","The general nature of the conflicts after the transactions, in a durable medium","The sources of the conflicts before any transactions proceed, in a durable medium"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The disclosure must also use sufficient detail to allow the client to make an informed decision with respect to the services provided."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the cancelation period offered to a client of the firm?","options":["Should be offered on all products","Optional on all products","Compulsory for certain products","Always a maximum of 14 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Reflection periods of 14 or 30 days have to be offered when the firm sells packaged products or cash deposit ISAs."},{"question":"A firm receives a client limit order in shares admitted to trading on a regulated market, but market conditions prevent immediate execution. What must the firm do?","options":["Send the order to the Financial Conduct Authority for immediate review","Record the order internally for later execution when prices improve","Make the order public immediately in a way accessible to other market participants","Aggregate the order with house business for execution at the next dealing point"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"An unexecuted client limit order in shares admitted to trading on a regulated market must be made public immediately unless the client has instructed otherwise. The other options do not satisfy the obligation to publicise the order."},{"question":"All are true in respect of churning and switching except:","options":["The objective is to earn commission income","Neither are generally considered in the interests of the client","The rules on churning and switching apply to retail clients only for advisory services and any client for discretionary services","Dealing may only take place if it would not be regarded as too frequent in the circumstances and is justified from the customer's point of view"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Churning and switching are still considered against the interests of the clients. The FCA provides them as guidance to the suitability rules, rather than rules themselves. They cover advisory and discretionary services for both retail and professional clients."},{"question":"Which of the following is client money?","options":["A transaction fee due to the firm","Money held by an approved bank","Settlement charge due to the firm","Money held in excess to a client's obligations"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Any money 'due to the firm' is not client money. Approved banks do not need to treat money deposited with them as client money.\nWhere a firm sells assets held as security against client obligations, the amount equal to the obligation is the firm's money. However, anything in excess of this obligation has to be returned to the client or treated as client money."},{"question":"To be treated as an elective eligible counterparty for MiFID business, a company must satisfy which conditions using the net turnover and/or funds criteria?","options":["Net turnover of at least €20 million","Company funds of at least €40 million","Net turnover of at least €20 million and company funds of at least €2 million","Net turnover of at least €40 million and company funds of at least €2 million"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"These are two of the quantitative requirements that must be met by a company to be an 'elective eligible counterparty'."},{"question":"Under the FCA Financial Promotions Rules a firm is permitted to disclose its opinion of the future performance of an investment. Which of the following is not permitted when taking advantage of this?","options":["A statement about the unreliability of forecasts","Projecting future performance from simulated past performance","Stating the assumptions on which the projection is based","Using past performance to predict the future performance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Indications of future performance cannot be based on simulated past performance."},{"question":"What must the firm's conflict of interest policy ensure?","options":["All groups within the firm are unable to discuss investment business with each other","All groups have sufficient guards on the information flow between each other","Measures to ensure that the relevant persons cannot find themselves on the wrong side of a Chinese wall","Chinese walls exist"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Clearly wall crossing is possible, but once it occurs it must be dealt with. Chinese walls are not a necessity: the FCA has them as a recommendation. The first choice (A) is suggesting that all information flows are impossible without conflicts of interest and this is excessive."},{"question":"Which one of the following, if unsolicited, would NOT be regarded as a cold call under the financial promotion rules?","options":["An email","A phone call","A personal visit","A conversation in the street"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A cold call is an unsolicited unwritten financial promotion. Emails are written communications - so cannot fall in the category of 'cold call'."},{"question":"Which one of the following would automatically be treated as a per-se professional client for MiFID business?","options":["A company with a balance sheet of €13m and a turnover of €30m","A Trust with a balance sheet of €22m and own funds of €1.5m","A company with own funds of €2.5m and a balance sheet of €12.5m","An authorised firm with a balance sheet of €18m and own funds of €32m"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All authorised/regulated firms are treated as per se professional for both MiFID and Non-MiFID business.\nThe quantitative tests for a large undertaking (company) to be treated as a per se professional for MiFID business are:\n(2 out of 3)\n- Minimum balance sheet: €20m\n- Minimum turn-over: €40m\n- Minimum own funds: €2m."},{"question":"A firm has searched through its client list, looking for those that have been inactive for some time. They plan to cold call these clients. If these clients are retail clients, which of the following products are permitted to be sold on the cold call?","options":["A generally marketable low volatility packaged product","A life policy linked to high volatility funds","A firm cannot sell any product on a cold call to a retail client","Shares in an inverse leveraged exchange traded fund"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The others are prohibited - either due to the increased risk through volatility or gearing, or the derivative aspects of the products. Where it comes to shares, they must be readily realisable. It is also worth noting that it is only the services relating to these shares that can be sold on the cold call, not the shares themselves."},{"question":"An authorised firm wishes to increase its retail client base by launching a cold calling campaign. Which of the following would be permitted?","options":["An insurance company marketing life assurance contracts with a high volatility element","A securities broker marketing warrants traded on the London Stock Exchange","A wealth management company marketing advisory services about main market shares","Marketing through cold calls is not permitted to retail clients under any circumstance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Cold calls can be used to sell services that relate to liquid securities. Listed shares would meet this definition. Note: the securities themselves could not be sold on the cold call."},{"question":"As an employee of an authorised firm, you are just about to make a personal recommendation to a client. Before doing so you must ensure that the investment:","options":["Has no risk","Is compatible with the client's level of experience and understanding of the risks involved","Contains only packaged products in the case of retail clients","Allows the client to take advantage of gearing opportunities whenever available"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Clearly portfolios may have risk. The opportunity of gearing opportunities is too strong in this statement. Retail clients are able to invest in more than only packaged products."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is true with respect to cancellation rights on the sale to a retail client of a packaged product? Cancellation rights:","options":["Are not required","Should be offered","Of 21 days are compulsory","May be offered for a minimum period specified by the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is the best answer. Depending upon the type of product, cancellation periods can vary between 14 and 30 calendar days."},{"question":"When a firm communicates initially with a retail client, COBS requires it to provide 'appropriate information', which includes all of the following except:","options":["The firm's name and address","A statement that it is FCA authorised or regulated","The name and status of the person they can contact","Who to refer complaints to if they cannot be resolved by the firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Reference to any complaints resolution body is not necessary."},{"question":"In relation to the following communication which ONE could an authorised firm approve on behalf of a firm which is NOT authorised?","options":["Personal visit","Magazine advert","Telephone conversation","A webcam conversation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An authorised firm cannot approve any communication which cannot be provided in advance i.e. telephone conversation, conversations during a personal visit or meeting or other interactive dialogues.\nAn authorised firm could approve a magazine advert as this can be approved in advance of it being communicated to the client."},{"question":"In the case of MiFID business, a firm must keep a copy of the periodic reports for:","options":["At least three years from the beginning of the relevant year","A period of five years from the date of dispatch","A period of three years from the date of dispatch","At least five years from the beginning of the relevant year"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MiFID generally requires records be kept for five years from the date of dispatch to the client."},{"question":"What is MOST likely to be covered by the FCA rules on financial promotions?","options":["One-off financial promotions","Promotion to investment professionals","Promotion aimed at private investors","Promotion to a sophisticated investor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Retail clients (of which 'private investors' are an example) are protected by the financial promotion rules contained within the COBS. One-off financial promotions, promotions to investment professionals, and sophisticated investors are normally exempt."},{"question":"A client requests an investment manager to carry out a trade in a listed security. Who is responsible for the transaction report?","options":["The client","Investment manager","London Stock Exchange","UKLA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"As a member firm the investment manager is required to file a transaction report."},{"question":"How do the conduct of business rules apply to an appointed representative of a firm?","options":["Directly","Indirectly","Not at all","Only regarding the financial promotion rules"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA cannot impose the COBS on appointed representatives directly, but obliges the firm whom they represent (their principal) to ensure that they behave appropriately."},{"question":"When a firm uses a website to promote a product and gives the facility for an online application this would be considered to be?","options":["Indirect real-time offer","Direct real-time offer","Direct non real-time offer","Indirect non real-time offer"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Website promotions are deemed to be non-real time financial promotions."},{"question":"From which of the following must a firm keep client money segregated?","options":["Money belonging to the firm","Money belonging to the firm and eligible counterparties","The firm and other retail client money","The firm and all other client money"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The aim of the rules is to keep client money segregated from that of the firm."},{"question":"What information must always be included in a firm's order execution policy for retail clients?","options":["The process the firm uses to specify relative importance of the execution factors","The exchange that the transaction will definitely be traded on","The price that the firm will get for the transaction","The foreign exchange rate that will be used in the transaction"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The firm will include a list of POSSIBLE venues... not necessarily the specific exchange. It will not have to specify the price nor the FX rate in the execution policy details."},{"question":"A firm has given a personal recommendation to a client. For which of the following would a suitability report NOT be required?","options":["A life policy for a retail client where a savings element is included","A transfer of, or opt-out from, an occupational pension scheme","Transactions in a regulated collective scheme for a professional client","A withdrawal by a retail client from short-term annuity"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Suitability reports are only required for retail clients in respect of investment products. There is an assumption that the members of occupational pension schemes are retail clients."},{"question":"A UK branch of a third-country firm executes a transaction outside the UK. Which statement best describes its obligation to report that transaction to the Financial Conduct Authority?","options":["It must report in every case by the next business day","It must report only if settlement occurs in the UK","It does not have to report because there is no UK effect","It does not have to report if the client is a UK professional client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Transactions made by UK branches of third-country firms do not have to be reported to the FCA when executed outside the UK. However, exceptions may apply depending on whether an individual based in the UK branch was involved in execution, pricing or client engagement."},{"question":"If an eligible counterparty requests to be dealt with for certain transactions as a professional client, what may the firm do?","options":["Ignore the request as an eligible counterparty is always treated as such","Treat them as a retail client for all the relevant transactions","Treat them as a professional client","Refer the eligible counterparty to the FCA for suspected market abuse"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is made explicit in COBS. The eligible counterparty is 'opting down' to professional client."},{"question":"A technology firm sells its shares to the public via the internet without the need to produce a prospectus. Which of the following rules would govern this offer?","options":["AIM rules for growth companies","COBS financial promotions rules","FCA prospectus regulation rules","Recognised investment exchange rules"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Normally a company offering new shares would be subject to prospectus regulation rules. However, this only applies if a prospectus is required. In this case, the COBS financial promotion rules will apply."},{"question":"According to the conduct of business rules and in relation to MiFID business, a firm must have in place a client agreement when it carries on:","options":["Designated investment business for a retail client only","Eligible counterparty business","Any regulated activity for a retail client and a professional client only","Designated investment business for a retail client and MiFID business for a professional client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Designated Investment Business is made up of MiFID and non-MiFID business. In respect of MiFID business, both retail and professional are protected. RC get protected for all designated investment business.\n\nNOTE: whilst designated investment business includes MiFID and non-MiFID business NOT all non-MiFID business is designated investment business. For example, bank deposits and pure protection insurance is not designated investment business."},{"question":"Which of the following could be classified as communicating a financial promotion?\nIInternet website\nIIMailshot\nIIICall centre making calls\nIVSales aid","options":["I, II and III","I, II, III and IV","II and IV","II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"All could be used for communicating financial promotions."},{"question":"According to COBS 4, the financial promotion rules always apply to a firm in relation to the communication of a financial promotion:","options":["Regarding a home purchase plan","Directed to an eligible counterparty only","In respect of designated investment business","Promoting a non-investment insurance contract"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The rules in COBS Chapter 4 apply to firms communicating with clients regarding their designated investment business and communicating or approving a financial promotion exemptions apply:\n• for qualifying credit, a home purchase plan or a home reversion plan\n• a promotion for a non-investment insurance contract, or\n• the promotion of an unregulated CIS which it is not permitted to approve.\n\nThe majority of the financial promotion rules do not apply to eligible counterparties."},{"question":"A firm wishes to execute a client order outside a regulated market or multilateral trading facility. When would this be permitted under the FCA conduct of business rules?","options":["This would never be permitted","It is always permitted","If there is a written notification sent to the client","If there is prior consent from the client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In order for there to be an extra-regulated market transaction, the firm must get the prior consent from its client."},{"question":"The firm is holding money belonging to clients. When would it NOT need to comply with the client money rules? If the money is:","options":["Coins held for the value of their metal","Held in a central bank","Interest paid by a Financial Conduct Authority authorised firm","Being held in a money market deposit account"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A firm does not need to apply the client money rules to coins held for the value of their metal on behalf of clients."},{"question":"A firm outsources part of its investment service to another provider. Which control is required for personal account dealing under the outsourcing arrangement?","options":["The outsourcing firm must maintain a record of personal transactions and provide it promptly on request","The outsourcing firm must seek annual approval from the Financial Conduct Authority for its monitoring procedures","The outsourcing firm must disclose all personal transactions to affected clients at the end of each quarter","The outsourcing firm must prohibit all relevant persons from dealing in designated investments"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Where outsourcing takes place, the arrangements must ensure the outsourcing firm keeps a record of personal transactions undertaken by any relevant person and provides it promptly on request. The other options introduce requirements that are not part of the rule."},{"question":"Which of the following is not a requirement for recategorisation as a professional client according to conduct of business rules in relation to MiFID business?","options":["The firm has ascertained that the client has sufficient experience and understanding and passes the quantitative tests","The firm has given a written warning of protections that will be lost","The client has given written consent to the protections that will be lost","The client has stated they are of sufficient experience, have sufficient understanding and pass the quantitative tests"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The customer must give written consent after it has received a written warning from the firm about lost protection, which the firm will only send if it believes the customer has sufficient experience and understanding. The onus is on the firm to assess."},{"question":"When is it allowable for an unauthorised person to communicate a financial promotion?","options":["After the unauthorised person has fulfilled the compliance requirements under the relevant COBS rules","At any time as long as the promotion is in relation to designated investment business","Takes reasonable care that it communicates it to any type of recipient regardless of was intended at the time of the confirmation","After reasonable care has been made to establish that an authorised firm has confirmed that it complies with the rules"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An unauthorised firm is not in breach of the rules if it communicates a financial promotion that has been produced by another party and:\n• takes reasonable care to establish that another authorised firm has confirmed that the promotion complies with the rules\n• takes reasonable care that it communicates it only to the type of recipient that was intended at the time of the confirmation\n• as far as it is (or should be) aware, the promotion is still fair, clear and not misleading, and has not been withdrawn by the other party."},{"question":"A firm is considering putting its own buy order on the same ticket as one of its client's buy orders. Which of the following is true before the firm does this?","options":["The firm has obtained consent from the client that this is acceptable","The firm truly believes that by aggregating the order, the terms of the trade will be improved","The firm has disclosed to the client that aggregation may operate to the disadvantage of clients","A firm may aggregate orders if it wishes"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A disclosure that aggregation may operate to the disadvantage clients is essential."},{"question":"When entering into a distance marketing contract for a life policy, how much time must a retail client be given to cancel the contract if they wish to do so?","options":["14 calendar days","14 business days","30 calendar days","30 business days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Cancellation is usually 14 calendar days, but for life policies (and pensions) it is 30 calendar days."},{"question":"A firm receives an order from a retail client, but there are problems in carrying out the instruction. When must a firm inform the client about this problem?","options":["Immediately","Within one business day","Within the expected settlement period","With the trade confirmation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The COBS specifies that retail clients must be informed of any material difficulty in the prompt execution of their order,\nimmediately on the firm becoming aware of this."},{"question":"Where a communication by a firm contains information about past performance, assuming an existing product that has been in existence for 25 years, this must cover a minimum period of:","options":["One year","Two years","Five years","Ten years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Any performance figures used in a financial promotions must cover at least the immediately preceding five years (or the whole period for which the investment has been offered, and/or the financial index has been established, and/or the service has been provided if this is less than five years).\n\nFor a product that is 25 years old, the minimum performance data that needs to be shown is 5 years."},{"question":"A firm may treat a client as an elective professional client for non-MiFID business if:","options":["The qualitative test alone is passed","The quantitative test alone is passed","Both the quantitative and qualitative tests are passed","The qualitative test is passed and there is a statement that protection will be lost"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The quantitative test is only used for MiFID business. The qualitative test and a statement of protection loss are always required. The client must state that it wishes to be treated as a professional client, the firm must warn the client that the loss of protection might occur and the client has to state, in writing, separate to any contracts, that it is aware of this loss of protection."},{"question":"The website of a firm shows two products; details on one are available only by post and of the other only online. Which of the two is a written financial promotion?","options":["Both","Neither","The one available by post only","The one available online only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Both products are considered written financial promotions."},{"question":"In which of the following situations would a firm need to perform appropriateness checks?","options":["For all services in all products provided to both retail and professional clients","For non-advised services in all products provided to both retail and professional clients","For non-advised services in complex products provided to both retail and professional clients","For non-advised services in complex products provided to retail clients only"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Appropriateness rules protect all customer (both retail and profession) in respect of non-advised services in complex products. Complex products are best thought of as derivatives and other contingent liability investments or highly illiquid or opaque investment."},{"question":"A firm has received several execution only client orders for purchases in Company A. The firm also has an order to purchase shares in that company. Which ONE of the following statements is FALSE in respect of the execution of these orders?","options":["The firm may aggregate the orders if it is unlikely that the aggregation will work to the disadvantage of any client","The firm can only rely on aggregation if the orders cannot be executed sequentially","If aggregation occurs, the firm must disclose to the client that the effect of aggregation may work to their disadvantage","The firm should aggregate the trade if it is in the client's best interests"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Client orders can be aggregated with each other or with the firm's orders provided that certain criteria are met.\nA firm is not required to perform aggregation."},{"question":"Which of the following communications would not have to follow the COBS promotions rules?","options":["A telephone communication requested by an existing client to promote readily realisable securities","An advert in a national newspaper communicating the logo of the firm, a contact point and the regulated activities provided","A video presentation sent out to potential clients promoting investments in UK government gilts","A direct offer communication to promote investment in derivatives"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"All would need to follow the communications rules except the one simply showing the firm and its services. These general types of promotions are often referred to as image advertising."},{"question":"When a firm trades with an eligible counterparty, which one of the following rules does not apply?","options":["Selling and advising","Personal account notices","Client assets","Client classification"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"COBS have a limited application to eligible counterparties and the advising and selling regulations do not apply."},{"question":"A firm is approached by an authorised collective investment scheme (CIS) in relation to designated investment business. Which of the following best represents the conditions under which the CIS will get classified as a professional client?","options":["Turnover of €40 million and own funds of €2 million","Turnover of €40 million and a balance sheet of €15 million","It is always classified as a professional client","It can never be classified as a professional client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Authorised collective investment schemes are per se professional clients."},{"question":"According to client asset rules (CASS), a firm may deposit client money in which of the following?","options":["A central bank","A credit granting institution unauthorised by the BCD","A non-EEA registered credit granting institution","A money market instrument"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under the Client Asset Rules, client money can be deposited in a central bank, a BCD credit institution (one that is authorised through the Banking Consolidation Directive), a qualifying money market fund or a bank authorised in a third country."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following needs to be included in a firm's order execution policy?","options":["Standard settlement instructions","Costs and charges","Prices","Execution venues"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The firm must include information about the different execution venues that it uses.\nThe firm must include details of the execution venues that consistently achieve the best possible results for their clients."},{"question":"Eligible counterparty status is not available to:","options":["An insurance company","A supranational organisation","A credit institution","An expert individual client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The others are per se eligible counterparties. Individuals cannot become eligible counterparties, no matter what their knowledge and experience."},{"question":"When providing which of the following activities must a retail client be assessed for appropriate levels of knowledge by the firm?","options":["An execution only share transaction","Executing a deal on an equity warrant","Recommending a foreign exchange spot transaction","Arranging a transaction on a corporate bond"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Appropriateness needs to be assessed and risks need to be highlighted for non-advised services in complex products. Non-advised services include execution only and arranging. Complex products include derivatives and warrants."},{"question":"To whom do the client money rules apply?","options":["All clients","All customers","Retail clients only","Professional clients only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Client Money Rules are part of the Client Asset Rules and they apply to all clients."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true of an eligible counterparty?","options":["They are only categorised as such for certain business","They can be individuals who have opted up from an elective professional client","They can be offered the same protection under COBS as a retail client","Many of the COBS rules are disapplied for eligible counterparties"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Only large undertakings can opt up from professional clients. Eligible counterparties are only considered as such for eligible counterparty business. It is possible for an eligible counterparty to request the same protection under COBS as a retail client."},{"question":"A firm designs a new investment product for first-time retail investors. Which action best demonstrates the Products and Services Outcome in practice?","options":["Targeting the product at clearly identified customer groups with matching needs and objectives","Offering the product to all client categories to maximise product awareness and understanding","Reviewing the product when the regulator requests evidence of compliance or customer harm","Promoting the product through generic materials ensuring target market is omitted for confidentiality"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Products and Services Outcome requires products and services to be designed to meet the needs and objectives of the target group. Product providers must identify a target market and carry out regular reviews, while distributors must market consistently with that target market."},{"question":"An analyst at an authorised firm makes a trade for their personal account in the units of a UCITS collective investment scheme. According to the FCA rules, how soon should they contact their compliance team to inform them of any increase in the number of units they hold?","options":["Immediately irrespective of the analyst's relationship with the CIS","Within one business day if the analyst has no relationship with the CIS","Immediately if the analyst has no relationship with the CIS","There is no need if the analyst has no management relationship with the CIS"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"So long as the person is not involved with the management of the UCITS CIS undertaking then the personal account dealing rules do not apply."},{"question":"If a person associated with the firm wishes to make a cold call, they must:","options":["Call between 9am and 5pm","Intend to discuss geared packaged products","Give a contact point for the client during the call","Not contact an existing client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The calls must be made at an appropriate time of day. Geared products are not considered appropriate of cold calls and existing clients can be called if they envisage a call. The client must be given a contact point, the identity of the individual and the firm and be allowed to terminate the conversation at their request."},{"question":"A firm discovers that an elective professional client's portfolio has reduced in size to less than €150,000 and the number of trades has been reduced to an average of four per quarter over the last 12 months. What actions should the firm take?","options":["None. The individual is a professional client and can be dealt with as such","Reclassify the individual as a retail client and notify the client of the action","Discuss the rationale for the change in behaviour and hold off reclassification for one quarter","Suggest that client increases their trading frequency or asks to be reclassified"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA states that the firm must take appropriate action if the client no longer meets the tests set down for opting up. The firm must, however, notify the client of this change in categorisation."},{"question":"If a financial analyst (a relevant person) is providing investment research for dissemination to its clients in particular financial investments, when would the analyst be permitted to deal on that information?","options":["They would never be able to deal on that information","They would always be allowed to deal on that information","They would be permitted to deal on the information if they submitted all confirmations of transactions to the firm's compliance","They would be permitted to deal on the information if granted approval by the firm's compliance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Although the analyst would be permitted to deal in this circumstance, it would be an exceptional circumstance for the firm's compliance to grant approval."},{"question":"The requirements for record keeping in respect of a firm conducting MiFID business is:","options":["Six years","Five years","Three years","Indefinitely"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MiFID explicitly states that firms must keep records for a period of five years.\nIf the record sets out rights and obligations of the investment firm and the client, it should be kept for the duration of the relationship with the client. Competent authorities may require firms to keep these records for up to five years after that relationship ceases."},{"question":"When is the FCA's Conduct of Business Rule relating to suitability required? When the firm is performing:","options":["Discretionary MiFID services on behalf of retail and professional clients","Discretionary MiFID services on behalf of retail clients only","Execution only MiFID services on behalf of retail clients only","Execution only MiFID services on behalf of any customer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Principle 9 requires firms to take reasonable care etc. for discretionary and advisory clients. In practice, this is achieved through compliance with the suitability rule. Suitability is required to be assessed for discretionary and advisory business, but not for execution-only business. The rule covers 'all customers' for MiFID business, and so therefore A is the best answer."},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, how long must a firm retain records of trade confirmations sent to a client?","options":["Three years from date of trade","Three years from date of dispatch","Five years from date of dispatch","Five years from date of trade"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In relation to MiFID business, trade confirmation records have to be kept for five years from date of dispatch."},{"question":"When carrying out client orders the firm must ensure:","options":["That professional clients must be informed of any material difficulties in executing the order","The order of execution is based on an ad-hoc policy","The order must be recorded within one business day","Comparable orders must be carried out sequentially except in specific conditions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Retail clients must be informed of problems. The execution policy must not be generated 'on the fly'. Orders must be promptly and accurately recorded - there is no specific timeframe given."},{"question":"An existing client approaches their firm and requests that they give him a lower level of protection. Which of the following would be appropriate action for the firm to take? The firm must:","options":["Offer this level of protection based on specific client instructions","Only offer this level of protection if the client in question is already deemed a professional client","Not offer this level of protection as if it were appropriate to do so it would have categorised the client as such already","First assess the client and then, if necessary, warn them of the additional risks"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A lower level of protection would involve a client asking for 'elective' status - or to opt up to the next category of client. This requires assessment by the firm involved before a decision is made."},{"question":"When a person is communicating a non-written financial promotion on behalf of an authorised firm which of the following must they always do?","options":["Identify himself and the regulator of the firm","Ensure the conversation is recorded and the tape kept securely","Ensure the call is made during office hours","Identify himself and the firm he represents"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The requirements for a non-written financial promotion are:\n1. Only do so at appropriate time of day - there is no requirement that this is made during office hours 2. Identify himself and the firm he represents at the outset and make clear the purpose of the call 3. Clarify that the client can continue with or terminate the communication at any time that he requests it 4. Give a contact point to any client with whom he arranges an appointment."},{"question":"A retail client would normally expect to get a periodic statement of their non-leveraged security based account at least:","options":["Annually","Every six months","Every three months","Whenever a transaction is executed"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"MiFID II has amended this rule. A retail client will generally get a periodic statement every three months. If the portfolio is leveraged then the periodic statement is at least monthly."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes Chinese walls? An arrangement that requires:","options":["Paperwork held by a person in the course of carrying on one part of its business to be withheld from, or not to be used by, persons with or for whom it acts in the course of carrying on another part of its business","Information held by a person in the course of carrying on one part of its business not to socialise with other persons with or for whom it acts in the course of carrying on another part of its business","Information held by a person in the course of carrying on one part of its business to be withheld from, or not to be used by, persons with or for whom it acts in the course of carrying on another part of its business","Information held by a person in the course of carrying on one part of its business not to talk to persons with or for whom it acts in the course of carrying on another part of its business"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is the definition given in the FCA's glossary of terms."},{"question":"All of the following may be considered \"proper fees\" under the FCA inducements rules EXCEPT:","options":["Costs for safekeeping and administration","Legal fees","Charges for the settlement of transactions","The cost of general office software"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Proper fees necessary for the provision of the business (e.g. custody costs, settlement fees and legal fees) are permissible under the MiFID II inducement rules."},{"question":"A firm is assessing whether a benefit may be accepted as a minor non-monetary benefit under the inducements rules. Which benefit is specifically listed as acceptable?","options":["Participation in seminars on the features of a specific financial instrument","Corporate access services arranged for the firm by an execution broker","Administration services for the operation of a research payment account","Analysis of historical price data sourced through an information provider"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Participation in conferences, seminars and other training events on the benefits and features of a specific financial instrument or investment service as an acceptable minor non-monetary benefit. The other options are listed in the source as goods or services that the FCA does not consider to be research for RPA purposes, and they are not identified here as acceptable minor non-monetary benefits."},{"question":"According to COBS, which of the following is not considered an execution venue?","options":["Systematic internalisers","Retail broker","Multilateral trading facility","Regulated market"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For the purposes of the provisions relating to best execution in COBS and in COLL, execution venue means a regulated market, an MTF or OTF, a systematic internaliser, or a market maker or other liquidity provider or an entity that performs a similar function in a third country to the functions performed by any of the foregoing."},{"question":"In which of the following circumstances may a firm aggregate a client's order with an own account order?","options":["It is likely that the aggregation will not work to the disadvantage of each of the clients concerned","Whenever aggregation is required","The firm has disclosed orally or in writing to clients, either specifically or in the client agreement, that the effect will work to their advantage","Aggregation is never allowed"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The firm should believe that aggregation will not work to the disadvantage of the clients. However, they should also notify the clients that a disadvantage is possible. Firms should also have an aggregations policy."},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, what minimum value must be exceeded by the portfolio of an elective professional client?","options":["250,000 euros","500,000 euros","1m euros","5m euros"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The portfolio has to be worth at least 500,000 euros."},{"question":"A firm provides portfolio management to a professional client and wishes to consume third-party research. Which payment method is permitted under the rules?","options":["Paying for the research directly from the firm's own resources","Receiving the research free where the provider also supplies execution services","Charging the cost through general commission with no separate identification","Recovering the cost through de minimus hospitality agreed with the research provider"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In-scope firms may receive research only if they pay for it either directly from their own resources or from a research payment account. The other options do not meet the permitted payment methods set out in the research payment rules."},{"question":"If a client is unwilling to provide sufficient information for a firm to assess suitability in respect of a personal recommendation, what should the firm do next?","options":["Act for a professional client on a non-advised execution only basis as long as the client provides instruction for the trades","Send a suspicious transaction and order report on the client to the FCA for market abuse without delay","Make an appropriate recommendation or take a decision to trade for him provided the client has accepted a risk warning","Make an appropriate personal recommendation to the customer on a take it or leave it basis"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"There is no requirement to report the client to the FCA.\nIf the firm does NOT obtain sufficient information, it must not make a personal recommendation to the client or take a decision to trade for him.\nIt would not be possible for a firm to make a personal recommendation as it does not have sufficient information.\nThe firm can still act for a professional client on an execution only basis.\n\nThis question would work in exactly the same way if it was for a retail client instead"},{"question":"Which one of the following is not defined as the client of a firm?","options":["A client of an appointed representative of the firm","A person acting through an agent","A person to whom a financial promotion is communicated by the firm","A potential client"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The agent is considered to be the client of the firm unless agreed otherwise."},{"question":"Which of these is true in relation to comparisons provided in a promotion for a MiFID investment?","options":["A distinction must be made between absolute and proportional differences","The source of the data must be stated","The comparison must explain that the results depend on the individual circumstances of each client","The rules are most stringent for qualifying credit, home purchase plans or home reversion plans"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If comparisons are made, they must be meaningful and presented in a fair and balanced way. For MiFID business, the data sources for the comparisons must be cited, as must any key facts and assumptions used. If tax treatment is mentioned, firms must explain that this depends on the individual circumstances of\neach client and that it may be subject to change. Information included in financial promotions must be consistent with that given in the course of carrying on business. The rules do not apply for qualifying credit, home purchase plans or home reversion plans."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true of the order execution policy?","options":["A firm must be able to demonstrate on request that it has followed the policy","A firm must notify clients about any material changes to their order execution arrangements","A firm must monitor the effectiveness of the policy on a transaction by transaction basis","The clients of the firm must consent to the order execution policy before the firm can deal for them"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"It is true that the firm must monitor the effectiveness of the policy, but not on a trade by trade basis. The COBS states that it must be done 'regularly' and that the policy as a whole must be reviewed annually."},{"question":"A retail client buys an advised authorised unit trust and later decides to cancel. Within what period must the client exercise the cancellation right?","options":["Within 7 calendar days of receiving the cancellation notice","Within 7 business days of the transaction being executed","Within 14 calendar days of the transaction being executed","Within 14 calendar days of receiving the cancellation notice"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A client who receives a cancellation notice for an advised purchase of an authorised unit trust or OEIC must exercise the right to cancel within 14 days. The other time periods are not stated for this example."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is LEAST LIKELY to be a professional client?","options":["Bank of England","IMF","Pension fund","Local authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Bank of England, IMF and pension providers are all examples of 'per se' professional clients. Under MiFID II local authorities and municipalities are to be treated as retail clients."},{"question":"In relation to financial promotions, the guidance for a firm wishing to communicate or approve a financial promotion aims to amplify which Principles for Businesses?","options":["'Customers' interests' and 'Communication with clients'","'Communication with clients' and 'Customers: relationship of trust'","'Market conduct' and 'Customers' interests'","'Customers' interests' and 'Customers: relationship of trust'"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Principle 6 - Customers' interests and Principle 7 - Communications with Clients."}],"examFocus":["<strong>Suitability vs Appropriateness</strong> is the single most-tested distinction in this chapter: suitability = advisory/discretionary services; appropriateness = non-advised (execution-only/arranging) in COMPLEX products only.","<strong>Execution-only in non-complex products needs no test at all</strong> — just name and address.","Client categorisation numbers to memorise: elective professional needs 2 of 3 quantitative tests (10 trades/quarter over 4 quarters, portfolio > €500,000, 1+ year professional experience) PLUS the qualitative test.","<strong>Local authorities and municipalities are retail clients</strong> under MiFID II — a classic trap given they 'sound' institutional.","<strong>Individuals can never be eligible counterparties</strong>, no matter how experienced.","Cancellation periods: <strong>14 calendar days</strong> is standard; <strong>30 calendar days</strong> for life policies and most pensions.","Trade confirmation to a retail client: <strong>by the end of the next business day</strong>. Record-keeping: <strong>5 years for MiFID business, 3 years for non-MiFID</strong> (both from date of dispatch, not date of trade).","<strong>Best execution</strong> for retail clients = total consideration (price + execution costs) — but costs payable TO THE FIRM itself are excluded from that assessment.","Aggregation of orders is <strong>never mandatory</strong>; if a client's order is only partially filled after aggregation, the <strong>client's allocation takes priority</strong> over the firm's own.","Client money rules apply to <strong>all clients</strong> (not just retail) — a frequent trap answer choice.","Websites are <strong>never</strong> a valid 'durable medium' for client agreements — but email, paper, CD-ROM/DVD are.","Cold calling is only permitted for: (1) an existing client who envisages the call, (2) generally marketable non-geared packaged products, or (3) readily realisable securities (excluding warrants)."]},{"id":"reg-ch3","number":3,"title":"Enhancing Market Integrity","examWeight":"19 of 75 questions (~25%)","summaryHtml":"\n<h3>Market Abuse (Section 1)</h3>\n<p>Market abuse is a statutory offence under FSMA; the UK regime came into force in 2001. The <strong>UK Market Abuse Regulation (MAR)</strong> (originally EU MAR, effective 2016, \"onshored\" after Brexit) sets the civil regime; the FCA's Handbook only gives <strong>guidance</strong>, not binding rules. The Financial Services Act 2012 (ss89-91) creates three related criminal offences: misleading statements (s89), false/misleading impressions (s90), and misleading statements about benchmarks (s91), with three defences (reasonable belief; conformity with price stabilising rules; conformity with control of information/information barrier rules). Penalties (Financial Services Act 2021): summary conviction - fine and/or up to 12 months; on indictment - up to 10 years (offences after 1 Nov 2021) or up to 7 years (offences up to 31 Oct 2021). Firms must maintain systems to detect and report suspicious orders/transactions via a <strong>STOR</strong> (Suspicious Transaction and Order Report) without delay; records must be kept 5 years and reviewed at least annually.</p>\n<h3>Insider Dealing (Section 2)</h3>\n<p>Under UK MAR, insider dealing covers using inside information to deal, cancel/amend orders, or recommend/induce another to do so. The criminal offence is set out in <strong>Part V of the Criminal Justice Act 1993 (CJA)</strong>: Section 52 creates three offences (dealing, encouraging, disclosing); Section 56 defines inside information (precise, not public, price-sensitive, relating to specified markets/instruments). The CJA was updated by the Insider Dealing (Securities and Regulated Markets) Order 2023 (in force 15 June 2023). General defences include not expecting a profit/avoided loss, reasonable belief information was widely disclosed, or would have acted the same regardless. Special defences exist for market makers acting in good faith, market information (e.g., a client's block trade order), and price stabilisation. Issuers must maintain insider lists and disclose inside information via a Regulatory Information Service (RIS).</p>\n<h3>Market Manipulation (Section 3)</h3>\n<p>Four forms: manipulating transactions, manipulating devices, dissemination (false/misleading information), and benchmark manipulation. PDMRs (persons discharging managerial responsibilities) must notify transactions within 3 business days and are barred from dealing during a <strong>closed period</strong> of 30 calendar days before results announcements. Accepted Market Practices (AMPs) are set by the FCA - currently none exist.</p>\n<h3>Money Laundering and Financial Crime (Section 4)</h3>\n<p><strong>POCA 2002</strong> creates five offences: concealing (s327), arrangements (s328), acquisition/use/possession (s329) - all up to 14 years; failure to disclose (s330) - up to 5 years; tipping off (s333A) - up to 2 years. The three stages of laundering are <strong>placement, layering, integration</strong>. Firms apply risk-based CDD (identify, verify, understand purpose), Simplified DD for low-risk relationships, and Enhanced DD for high-risk scenarios including PEPs (Politically Exposed Persons - EDD ceases 1 year after leaving prominent public function). Every firm must appoint an MLRO (UK-based, approved under SM&CR) and a nominated officer (may be the same person) to receive internal disclosures and report externally to the NCA.</p>\n<h3>Record-Keeping (Section 5)</h3>\n<p>CDD/identity records: 5 years from end of relationship. Transaction records: 5 years from completion, capped at a maximum of 10 years' retention.</p>\n<h3>Bribery Act 2010 (Section 6)</h3>\n<p>Four offences: bribing (s1), being bribed (s2), bribing a foreign public official (s6), and the corporate strict-liability offence of failing to prevent bribery (s7). Maximum penalty: 10 years' imprisonment (unlimited fine for the s7 corporate offence). Defence: \"adequate procedures\" per six MoJ principles (proportionality, top-level commitment, risk assessment, due diligence, communication, monitoring/review).</p>\n<h3>Fraud and Economic Crime (Section 7)</h3>\n<p>The Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act (ECCTA) created a new \"failure to prevent fraud\" corporate offence (effective 1 September 2025) for large organisations (meeting 2 of: turnover >£36m; balance sheet >£18m; >250 employees). Defence: reasonable fraud prevention procedures (six similar principles).</p>\n<h3>Disclosure and Transparency Rules (Section 8)</h3>\n<p>UK notifiable shareholding threshold is <strong>3%</strong> (retained from pre-Brexit EU rules, rather than the EU's 5%/10%/15% etc. scale), then every whole percentage point above. Notify the company within 2 business days; the company informs the market (e.g., via RNS) by the end of the next business day - not the FCA. Section 793 of the Companies Act 2006 lets a public company demand disclosure of beneficial ownership from suspected nominee shareholders.</p>\n","sections":[{"heading":"3.1 Market Abuse","html":"\n<p>Market abuse is a <strong>statutory offence</strong> covering financial market manipulation and insider dealing, damaging investor confidence and market integrity. The UK's market abuse regime is an offence under the <strong>Financial Services and Markets Act (FSMA)</strong> and came into force in <strong>2001</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Implementation of the Market Abuse Regulation (MAR)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>Market Abuse Regulation (MAR)</strong> came into effect in <strong>2016</strong>, updating the EU Market Abuse Directive (MAD).</li>\n<li>It extended scope to instruments traded off regulated markets (bilateral/OTC), covered spot commodity and related derivative markets, and tailored rules for SME issuers.</li>\n<li>Following implementation, the FCA Handbook provides <strong>guidance only</strong> (not binding rules) on matters governed by MAR.</li>\n<li>Post-Brexit, the EU MAR was \"onshored\"/\"retained\"/\"assimilated\" into UK law largely unaltered and is now called <strong>UK MAR</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Market Abuse (Amendment) (EU Exit) Regulations 2019</strong> made three key amendments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No obligation to share information with EU authorities without a guarantee of reciprocity.</li>\n<li>The FCA took over the regulatory responsibilities previously held by ESMA and the European Commission (EC).</li>\n<li>Supplementary legislation is now subject to HM Treasury instead of the EC.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Civil and Criminal Regime</h4>\n<p>Insider dealing and improper disclosure are covered by the CJA 1993 (criminal) and by ss89-92 of the <strong>Financial Services Act 2012</strong> (misleading statements/practices), which replaced the former FSMA s397. The Financial Services Act 2012 created three offences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Making misleading statements (<strong>Section 89</strong>)</li>\n<li>Creating false or misleading impressions (<strong>Section 90</strong>)</li>\n<li>Making misleading statements in relation to benchmarks (<strong>Section 91</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Three potential defences to a charge under ss89-91:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Reasonable belief that the act/conduct would not create a false or misleading impression.</li>\n<li>Conformity with the FCA's <strong>price stabilisation rules</strong> (allowing banks to support a new issue price for a limited period, with mandated disclosures).</li>\n<li>Conformity with the FCA's <strong>control of information rules</strong> (statements made on the basis of limited information behind an information barrier).</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Penalties (Financial Services Act 2021)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Summary conviction: fine not exceeding the statutory amount and/or jail up to <strong>12 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>Conviction on indictment: jail up to <strong>10 years</strong> or a fine, or both, for offences committed <strong>after 1 November 2021</strong>.</li>\n<li>Offences committed up to <strong>31 October 2021</strong>: jail up to <strong>7 years</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The FCA's Market Conduct Sourcebook (\"MAR\") sets standards: <strong>MAR 1</strong> covers market abuse requirements; <strong>MAR 2</strong> covers price stabilising rules. Civil sanctions range from financial penalties to public censure. In deciding between a penalty and censure, the FCA considers: whether the breach was deliberate/reckless; duration/frequency; benefit gained/loss avoided; evidence of systemic control failures; impact on market orderliness; risk of loss to consumers; extent of financial crime facilitated; aggregate effect of smaller incidents; speed of self-reporting; cooperation; likelihood of recurrence; compliance with other regulators (e.g., Takeover Panel); accuracy of information provided; and previous disciplinary record. It may also weigh market sophistication and the impact of a penalty/censure on markets or consumers.</p>\n<p>Criminal prosecution decisions follow the <strong>Code for Crown Prosecutors</strong> and Enforcement Guide, considering seriousness, victim loss, market distortion, profits/losses avoided, repeat risk, prior record, cooperation, dishonesty/abuse of trust, and personal circumstances. The FCA operates a \"<strong>no two bites of the cherry</strong>\" policy - it will not impose market abuse sanctions where a person is being criminally prosecuted for the same conduct, or bring disciplinary proceedings after a prosecution for substantially the same offence.</p>\n<h4>Prevention and Detection of Market Abuse (1.3)</h4>\n<p>UK MAR applies to executed trades, <strong>unexecuted orders</strong>, and <strong>requests for quotes (RFQs)</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Market operators/investment firms operating trading venues must establish effective arrangements, systems and procedures to prevent and detect insider dealing and market manipulation (including attempts), and report suspicious orders/transactions to the competent authority of the trading venue without delay.</li>\n<li>Firms arranging/executing transactions off-venue must maintain proportionate arrangements to detect and report suspicious orders/transactions.</li>\n<li>Where a firm has reasonable suspicion, it must report to the FCA <strong>without delay</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Required arrangements/systems/procedures must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Be appropriate and proportionate to the scale, size, nature and complexity of the business.</li>\n<li>Be assessed at least via an <strong>annual audit and internal review</strong>, updated as necessary.</li>\n<li>Be documented in writing (including changes), retained for <strong>five years</strong>.</li>\n<li>Allow analysis of every transaction/order executed, placed, modified, cancelled or rejected, on and off venue.</li>\n<li>Produce alerts for further analysis and cover the full range of trading activities.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Suspicious Transaction and Order Reporting (STOR)</h4>\n<p>Where there is reasonable suspicion an order/transaction (on or off a trading venue) may constitute market manipulation or insider dealing (including attempts), the person must notify the FCA without delay. A STOR must include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identification of the submitter and their capacity (own account vs on behalf of third parties).</li>\n<li>Description of the order/transaction: type of order/trading (e.g., block trades), venue, price and volume.</li>\n<li>Reasons for suspicion.</li>\n<li>Means of identifying persons involved (including the person placing/executing and the person on whose behalf).</li>\n<li>Any other relevant information/supporting documents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Firms may alternatively submit a <strong>market observation</strong> for suspicious activity not meeting the full STOR criteria (e.g., where the firm is not itself involved and lacks complete information).</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.2 Insider Dealing","html":"\n<h4>Inside Information and Insider Dealing (UK MAR)</h4>\n<p>Under UK MAR, <strong>inside information</strong> comprises:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Precise, non-public information relating to a financial instrument which, if made public, would be likely to significantly affect its price (or related derivatives).</li>\n<li>For commodity derivatives: precise, non-public information relating to the derivative or related spot commodity contract, expected/required to be disclosed under legal/market rules.</li>\n<li>For persons executing client orders: information conveyed by a client about their own pending orders.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Information is <strong>precise</strong> if it indicates circumstances/an event (existing, or reasonably expected to occur) specific enough to draw a conclusion about the likely price effect.</p>\n<p>Insider dealing under UK MAR occurs where a person:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Possesses inside information and uses it to acquire/dispose of instruments (for self or a third party).</li>\n<li>Uses inside information to cancel/amend an order placed before possessing the information.</li>\n<li>Recommends or induces another to deal on the basis of inside information.</li>\n<li>Recommends or induces another to cancel/amend an order on the basis of inside information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A person is an <strong>insider</strong> if they know the information is inside information, knowingly acquired from an inside source:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are an inside source themselves (director, employee, shareholder of an issuer - not necessarily the company whose securities are traded).</li>\n<li>They have access via their employment, office or profession (e.g., auditor, legal or corporate finance adviser).</li>\n<li>They have access, directly or indirectly, from a person who was an inside source or had access via employment (e.g., a director's spouse who overhears confidential takeover information at home).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Public Disclosure and Insider Lists</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Issuers must inform the public <strong>as soon as possible</strong> of inside information directly concerning them, via a <strong>Regulatory Information Service (RIS)</strong>, enabling fast, complete, correct and timely access.</li>\n<li>Issuers (and those acting for them) must maintain <strong>insider lists</strong> of everyone with access to inside information (employees, advisers, accountants, credit rating agencies), promptly updated for changes. <strong>Separate lists</strong> must be kept for each transaction, plus one for permanent insiders.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Unlawful Disclosure of Inside Information</h4>\n<p>Sharing inside information other than in the \"normal exercise of an employment, a profession, or duties\" is unlawful disclosure - a criminal offence, punishable by unlimited fines and/or injunctions. Permitted disclosure exceptions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal duties.</li>\n<li>Market soundings (e.g., to raise capital).</li>\n<li>Confidentiality-protected disclosure to facilitate a transaction, seek advice, or fulfil a legal obligation.</li>\n<li>Professional advice (lawyer, auditor).</li>\n<li>Takeover bids (to facilitate/seek commitment under the Takeover Code).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Criminal Justice Act 1993 (CJA) - Part V</h4>\n<p>The UK's criminal law on insider dealing is set out in <strong>Part V of the CJA 1993</strong>, updated by the <strong>Insider Dealing (Securities and Regulated Markets) Order 2023</strong> (in force <strong>15 June 2023</strong>), which updated the list of Regulated Markets.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 52 CJA</strong> - three ways of committing the offence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dealing in the security.</li>\n<li>Encouraging another to do so.</li>\n<li>Disclosing inside information to another.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Section 56 CJA</strong> - inside information is information which: (a) relates to securities traded/admitted to trade (or subject to a request to admit) on a UK, EEA or Gibraltar Regulated Market, MTF or OTF; (b) relates to securities on Nasdaq, SIX Swiss Exchange or NYSE; (c) relates to other securities whose price depends on (a)/(b); (d) is specific or precise; (e) has not been made public; and (f) is price-sensitive.</p>\n<p>Since the 2023 update, \"security\" under the CJA aligns with the UK MiFID definition of \"financial instrument\", covering (among others): transferable securities; money-market instruments; units in CISs; derivatives on securities/currencies/rates/indices; cash-settled commodity derivatives; physically-settled commodity derivatives traded on a UK regulated market/MTF/OTF; credit risk transfer derivatives; financial contracts for differences; derivatives on climatic variables/freight rates/inflation; and emission allowances.</p>\n<p>Information is \"made public\" (non-exhaustive list) when it is: published per regulated market rules (e.g., via the LSE's RNS); contained in records open to public scrutiny (e.g., a shareholder register); or readily available to those likely to deal (even if it must be sought out with diligence/expertise, or paid for by subscription).</p>\n<h4>Market Manipulation and Market Soundings (intro)</h4>\n<p>UK MAR prohibits engaging or attempting to engage in market manipulation (detailed in 3.3). A <strong>market sounding</strong> is where an adviser/investment bank discusses a potential new issuance with investors to gauge appetite (coupon, size), on behalf of an issuer. Disclosing firms must: obtain consent to receive inside information; inform the recipient they are prohibited from dealing/encouraging dealing on it; inform the recipient of confidentiality obligations; keep records of information given and of recipients who declined consent; and advise recipients when information ceases to be inside information. Recipients must independently assess whether information received is genuinely \"inside information\".</p>\n<h4>General Defences to Insider Dealing</h4>\n<p>For dealing, or encouraging another to deal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Did not expect the dealing to result in profit (or avoided loss) because of the information.</li>\n<li>Reasonably believed the information had been so widely disclosed that no party would be prejudiced by not knowing it.</li>\n<li>Would have acted the same way regardless of possessing the information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For disclosing only:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Did not expect any person to deal, or</li>\n<li>Did not expect the dealing to result in profit/avoided loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Special Defences</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Market makers</strong>: not guilty if acting in good faith in the ordinary course of market-making business; a further defence applies if acting to facilitate an acquisition/disposal under negotiation, using market information arising directly from those negotiations.</li>\n<li><strong>Market information</strong>: not guilty if the information held was market information (e.g., a client's pending block order) and it was reasonable to act as they did, having regard to the content of the information, the circumstances/capacity in which it was first obtained, and the capacity in which the defendant now acts. Example: a broker who knows a client wishes to buy 10,000 shares in XYZ plc and deals on that instruction has a market information defence (size of order relative to market impact is relevant).</li>\n<li><strong>Price stabilisation</strong>: stabilising managers/agents may support the price of new issues for a limited period post-issue under FCA Market Conduct Sourcebook Chapter 2 rules - a defence to insider dealing/manipulation allegations.</li>\n<li><strong>Legitimate behaviour</strong>: mere possession of inside information is not itself evidence of use. Legitimate activities include: effective information barriers; acting as a market maker; executing third-party orders; orders concluded before possessing the information; orders to satisfy a pre-existing legal obligation; conduct in a merger/takeover where information is made public at approval; and stake-building based on one's own decision to acquire/dispose.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"heading":"3.3 Market Manipulation","html":"\n<h4>The Four Forms of Market Manipulation</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Form</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. Manipulating transactions</td><td>Transactions/orders not for legitimate reasons/not conforming to accepted practices, giving a false/misleading impression of supply, demand or price, or securing an abnormal/artificial price.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. Manipulating devices</td><td>Transactions/orders employing fictitious devices or any other form of deception or contrivance.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. Dissemination</td><td>Spreading information (by any means) giving a false/misleading impression, known or reasonably expected to be known as false/misleading by the disseminator - the form most harmful to investors.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. Benchmark manipulation</td><td>Transmitting false/misleading inputs relating to a benchmark, or any other behaviour manipulating a benchmark's calculation.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Other behaviour \"considered as market manipulation\" under UK MAR includes: securing a dominant position over supply/demand (alone or in collaboration); trading at market open/close to mislead investors relying on displayed prices; placing/cancelling/modifying orders (including via algorithmic/high-frequency trading) to obscure genuine orders or create false signals; and \"scalping\" - voicing an opinion on an instrument (via media) having previously taken a position, then profiting from the market impact without disclosing the conflict of interest.</p>\n<h4>Legitimate Behaviour and Accepted Market Practices (AMPs)</h4>\n<p>Legitimate behaviour = conduct carried out for valid commercial reasons, in line with accepted market practices, without intent to mislead/distort (e.g., genuine hedging or investment strategy). The <strong>FCA</strong> is responsible for assessing/establishing AMPs in the UK - key points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transactions must be for legitimate reasons and conform to an established AMP.</li>\n<li>The FCA has the authority to establish AMPs, but <strong>currently no AMPs have been established</strong> in the UK.</li>\n<li>The FCA must consult stakeholders (issuers, service providers, market participants) before establishing an AMP.</li>\n<li>Identities of practitioners and the party responsible for transparency must be disclosed.</li>\n<li>Criteria considered include market structure, instrument types, and extent of retail participation.</li>\n<li>The prohibitions on insider dealing/market manipulation do not apply to buy-back programmes or stabilisation activities meeting specific UK MAR conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>PDMR Transactions (Managers' Transactions)</h4>\n<p><strong>Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibilities (PDMRs)</strong> are members of an issuer's administrative/management/supervisory bodies (e.g., directors) or senior executives with regular access to inside information and power to make managerial decisions affecting the issuer's future. The definition extends to <strong>Persons Closely Associated</strong> (spouses, dependent children, entities controlled by the PDMR).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PDMRs must notify the issuer (or emission allowance participant) and the FCA of every personal-account transaction in shares, debt instruments or related derivatives (or emission allowances/derivatives).</li>\n<li>Notification must be made promptly, <strong>no later than three business days</strong> after the transaction, containing: name; reason for notification; issuer/participant name; instrument description/identifier; buy/sell (and whether from option exercise); date/place; price and size.</li>\n<li><strong>Closed period</strong>: PDMRs are restricted from dealing in company shares/related instruments for <strong>30 calendar days</strong> before announcement of the interim or annual financial report. Exceptions may apply for severe financial difficulty requiring immediate fund realisation, or transactions under employee share schemes with no change in beneficial ownership.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Investment Recommendations</h4>\n<p>Persons/firms producing or distributing investment recommendations or suggested strategies must present information <strong>objectively</strong> and disclose interests/conflicts of interest. An investment recommendation is distinct from MiFID \"investment advice\"/personal recommendation - it is a proposal/strategy on financial instruments provided to more than one recipient (fund managers, wealth managers), typically short-term trading ideas.</p>\n<p>Required disclosures at the point of the recommendation include: clear distinction of facts from interpretations/opinions; clear indication of material information sources; clear labelling of projections/forecasts/price targets and their assumptions; date/time of production; a summary of valuation basis/methodology and assumptions (and any changes); clear explanation of recommendation meaning (buy/sell/hold) and time horizon, plus risk warnings/sensitivity analysis; planned update frequency; relevant date/time of prices referenced; and (ongoing obligations) disclosure of changes from prior recommendations on the same instrument within the preceding <strong>12 months</strong>, and a list of all recommendations disseminated in the preceding 12 months (date, identity of natural person(s), price target/market price at dissemination, direction, validity period).</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.4 Money Laundering and Financial Crime","html":"\n<h4>Introduction</h4>\n<p><strong>Money laundering (ML)</strong> turns proceeds of crime into apparently legitimate funds. AML provisions are a frontline defence against drug dealing, organised crime, human/wildlife trafficking, and terrorist financing, monitored via the <strong>National Crime Agency (NCA)</strong>. Key legislative sources:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proceeds of Crime Act (POCA) 2002</strong> - defines criminal property (property is criminal only if the alleged offender knows/suspects it is); requires firms to report ML suspicions.</li>\n<li><strong>Serious Organised Crime and Police Act (SOCPA) 2005</strong> - amended POCA; addressed the \"Spanish bullfighter\" issue (conduct legal abroad but illegal in the UK would otherwise count as criminal property) by allowing a defence where the offender reasonably believed the conduct was not criminal where it occurred - subject to the Secretary of State's power to designate certain overseas conduct (e.g., serious tax evasion, drug cultivation) as still reportable.</li>\n<li><strong>Criminal Finances Act 2017</strong> - amends POCA; creates two corporate offences of failing to prevent the criminal facilitation of tax evasion (UK tax and foreign tax).</li>\n<li><strong>Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Transfer of Funds (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017 (MLR 2017)</strong> - implemented the Fourth EU Money Laundering Directive (MLD4); sets out firms' systems/controls, training and KYC requirements. The Fifth Money Laundering Directive was transposed in 2020. Post-Brexit, the UK chose <strong>not</strong> to transpose the EU's Sixth Anti-Money Laundering Directive, considering its domestic law already compliant.</li>\n<li><strong>SYSC Sourcebook</strong> - high-level FCA rules/guidance on AML systems and controls (principles-based, not prescriptive), referring firms to <strong>JMLSG</strong> guidance for procedure.</li>\n<li><strong>JMLSG Guidance</strong> - produced by UK trade associations; not mandatory but HM Treasury-approved, so courts treat adherence as evidence of compliance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>POCA 2002 - the Five Offences</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Offence</th><th>Description</th><th>Max Penalty</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Concealing (s327)</td><td>Concealing or disguising criminal property</td><td rowspan=\"3\">Fine and/or up to 14 years</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arrangements (s328)</td><td>Being concerned in an arrangement facilitating acquisition/retention/use/control of criminal property for another (can include giving transaction advice)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Acquisition, use and possession (s329)</td><td>Acquiring, using or possessing criminal property, knowing/suspecting its origin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Failure to disclose (s330)</td><td>Employee in the regulated sector fails to disclose knowledge/suspicion of ML/TF to a nominated officer as soon as practicable</td><td>Fine and/or up to 5 years</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tipping off (s333A)</td><td>Regulated-sector person discloses a SAR/investigation likely to prejudice it</td><td>Fine and/or up to 2 years</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Section 330 (failure to disclose) requires three conditions: (a) the person knows/suspects (or has reasonable grounds to know/suspect) another is committing an offence; (b) the knowledge/suspicion arose during business in the regulated sector; (c) no disclosure was made to a nominated officer (e.g., the firm's MLRO) as soon as practicable. The <strong>CPS updated its prosecuting guidance on 2 June 2021</strong>, allowing standalone s330 prosecutions regardless of whether an ML offence has been substantiated.</p>\n<p>A defence to the first three offences (concealing, arrangements, acquisition/use/possession) exists if the person made the required disclosure to the nominated officer/MLRO (or, if they are the MLRO, to the NCA).</p>\n<p>The \"reasonable grounds to know or suspect\" test in s330 is the <strong>objective test</strong> - would a reasonable person have suspected, irrespective of the individual's protestations.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 342 POCA (Part 7)</strong> - prejudicing an investigation (applies to anyone, not just the regulated sector): making a disclosure likely to prejudice an investigation, or falsifying/concealing/destroying relevant documents. Defences: no knowledge/suspicion of prejudice; disclosure made in performance of a POCA duty; legal adviser acting professionally (unless furthering a criminal purpose); no knowledge documents were relevant; no intent to conceal facts. Penalty: fine and/or up to 5 years.</p>\n<h4>The Three Stages of Money Laundering</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Placement</strong> - introducing criminal proceeds into the financial system (e.g., bank/building society account, bureau de change).</li>\n<li><strong>Layering</strong> - moving proceeds through complex transactions to obscure the link to source and beneficiary (rapid FX/securities trades, CISs, insurance-based products, cross-border transfers).</li>\n<li><strong>Integration</strong> - dirty money re-enters the legitimate economy appearing as clean funds.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>AML provisions target identification and suspicion-reporting mainly at placement/layering, and record-keeping to prevent reaching integration.</p>\n<h4>Systems and Controls (SYSC 6.3)</h4>\n<p>Firms must identify, assess, monitor and manage ML risk, considering customer/product/activity profiles, distribution channels, transaction complexity/volume, systems, and operating environment. Systems must include: staff training; annual MLRO report to senior management/governing body; documented risk policies; ML risk consideration in day-to-day operations and new product/customer decisions; and proportionate ID procedures that do not unreasonably exclude customers. A director/senior manager (often the MLRO) must hold overall AML responsibility.</p>\n<p>The <strong>MLRO</strong> receives/assesses internal suspicion reports and decides whether to report to the NCA; must be an FCA-approved Senior Management Function under SM&CR, based in the UK, with appropriate authority, independence, resources and access.</p>\n<h4>JMLSG Guidance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Part I</strong> - general guidance for all financial sector firms; international context and UK legislative framework.</li>\n<li><strong>Part II</strong> - sectoral guidance (retail banking, wealth management, stockbroking, wholesale markets).</li>\n<li><strong>Part III</strong> - specialist guidance (electronic/wire transfers; UK financial sanctions regime).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Guidance requires firms to: take a risk-based, proportionate approach (documented policy, kept current, available to the FCA); simplify identity verification for many customer types; allow reliance on other firms' verification; introduce definitions (beneficial owners, PEPs); set CDD measures on a risk-based approach; set out reliance rules; specify when SDD/EDD apply; require scrutiny of complex/unusually large/unusual transactions; and maintain systems to respond to regulator enquiries.</p>\n<h4>Customer Due Diligence (CDD)</h4>\n<p>Three aspects at the outset of a business relationship:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identifying the customer/beneficial owner (name, address, date of birth; beneficial owners for non-personal customers).</li>\n<li>Verifying that identity (e.g., passport/driving licence for individuals; Companies House checks for corporates).</li>\n<li>Obtaining information on the intended nature of the business relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Tier</th><th>When applied</th><th>Examples/notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Simplified Due Diligence (SDD)</td><td>Lower ML risk counterparties</td><td>Other regulated firms; listed companies; beneficial owners of pooled accounts held by notaries/legal professionals; UK public authorities; community institutions; low-risk products (life assurance, e-money, pension funds, CTFs)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Standard CDD</td><td>Default</td><td>As above three-part process</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD)</td><td>Higher-risk situations (risk-sensitive basis)</td><td>High risk identified by firm's own assessment/supervisor info; high-risk third country; client not physically present; correspondent banking; PEP relationships; false/stolen ID provided; complex/unusually large or unusual-pattern transactions</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>If SDD does not apply, satisfactory ID evidence must be obtained/verified as soon as reasonably practicable; where verification is delayed (e.g., non-face-to-face business), the relationship should be restricted (e.g., transaction/transfer limits) until complete. If identity cannot be verified, the firm should not proceed and should consider reporting to the NCA.</p>\n<p>EDD requires: appropriate risk-management systems to identify PEPs/family/close associates; senior management approval to establish/continue the relationship; measures to establish source of wealth/funds; and enhanced ongoing monitoring.</p>\n<p><strong>PEP definition</strong>: \"an individual who is or has, at any time in the preceding year, been entrusted with prominent public functions, other than as a middle-ranking or more junior official.\" The obligation to apply EDD ceases <strong>one year</strong> after leaving office (longer if the firm considers it appropriate). Family members include spouse/partner, children and their spouses/partners, and parents. EDD is no longer required for family members/close associates once the PEP has left the prominent function, regardless of the one-year period.</p>\n<h4>MLRO and Nominated Officer</h4>\n<p>All firms (except sole traders, general insurance firms and mortgage intermediaries) must appoint an <strong>MLRO</strong> under FCA rules. <strong>MLR 2017</strong> requires firms to appoint a <strong>nominated officer</strong> to receive internal ML disclosures and make external NCA reports (also receives disclosures under POCA and the Terrorism Act 2000). In practice, the same person usually performs both roles and is called the MLRO.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Aspect</th><th>MLRO</th><th>Nominated Officer</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Primary role</td><td>Oversees the firm's entire AML compliance</td><td>Designated internal contact for suspicious activity reports</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Legal duty</td><td>N/A specifically</td><td>Must report suspicions to the NCA - failure is a criminal offence</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Internal function</td><td>N/A specifically</td><td>Recipient/initial reviewer of internal SARs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reporting function</td><td>Makes the final report to the NCA after considering internal suspicions</td><td>-</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Broader responsibilities</td><td>Staff training, CDD oversight, policy implementation</td><td>-</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The MLRO's responsibilities span: policy development/maintenance; monitoring transactions and receiving/assessing internal reports; making external SARs to the NCA and maintaining a register of reports; conducting firm-wide risk assessments; developing/delivering AML training and fostering a compliance culture; liaising with regulators/law enforcement; and maintaining accurate AML records.</p>\n<h4>Disclosure of Suspicious Activities</h4>\n<p><strong>Section 24 MLR 2017</strong> requires firms to ensure relevant employees are made aware of ML/TF law and data protection requirements, and receive regular training on recognising/handling suspicious transactions; training records must be kept. A \"relevant employee\" is one whose role can contribute to identifying/mitigating/detecting ML/TF risk. Disclosure is a two-stage process: an employee discloses suspicion to the MLRO, who reviews and decides whether to escalate to the NCA. Reporting to the MLRO discharges the employee's legal responsibility; the MLRO's report to the NCA discharges theirs.</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.5 Record-Keeping Requirements","html":"\n<p>Firms must identify customers/beneficial owners, verify identities, and understand the purpose/nature of the relationship, obtaining sufficient information for a complete risk picture and ongoing monitoring - both at onboarding and throughout the relationship (to validate whether risk/beneficial ownership information remains current).</p>\n<p>Firms must keep copies of documents/information obtained for CDD/CTF purposes, and sufficient transaction records, for <strong>five years</strong> after:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the business relationship ends, or</li>\n<li>an occasional transaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Transactional records occurring within a business relationship need not be kept beyond a maximum of <strong>ten years</strong>. Where a firm is relied upon by others for due diligence checks, it must retain those records for the same period; a firm relying on another party's checks must be able to demonstrate why it can rely on them, and that records are retained for five years.</p>\n<p>The FCA Handbook (SYSC Sourcebook, Chapters 6 and 9) requires firms to take reasonable care to make and retain adequate records, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evidence of identity (a copy, or a record of where a copy can be obtained).</li>\n<li>A record of every transaction carried out with/for the client in regulated activity, including internal/external reporting actions taken.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Record type</th><th>Retention period</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Evidence of identity</td><td>5 years from the end of the relationship between firm and client</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transactions</td><td>5 years from the date the transaction was completed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Any other case</td><td>5 years from obtaining the information or creating the record</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Records must also include the dates AML training was given, the nature of the training, and the names of staff trained.</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.6 The Bribery Act","html":"\n<h4>Overview - Four Offences</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Offering, promising or giving a bribe to another person (<strong>Section 1</strong>).</li>\n<li>Requesting, agreeing to receive or accepting a bribe (<strong>Section 2</strong>).</li>\n<li>Bribing a foreign public official (<strong>Section 6</strong>).</li>\n<li>Corporate offence of failing to prevent bribery (<strong>Section 7</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Penalties</h4>\n<p>Maximum <strong>10 years' imprisonment</strong> for individual offences; the corporate s7 offence carries an <strong>unlimited fine</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Key Sections</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Section 1</strong> - bribery: offering/giving/promising a financial or other advantage in exchange for improper performance of a relevant function or activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 2</strong> - being bribed: requesting/accepting/agreeing to accept such an advantage in exchange for improper performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 3</strong> - defines \"relevant function or activity\" as any function of a public nature; any activity connected with a business, trade or profession; any activity in the course of employment; or any activity by/on behalf of a body of persons (corporate or unincorporated). Applies to both private and public sectors, and to activity performed outside the UK.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 4</strong> - \"improper\" performance = breach of the expectation of good faith/impartiality, or performance not as expected of a person in a position of trust.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 5</strong> - the standard is what a reasonable person in the UK would expect; for conduct occurring outside the UK, local practices/customs are disregarded <strong>unless</strong> they form part of the jurisdiction's written law (constitution, statute, or judicial opinion set down in writing).</li>\n<li><strong>Section 6</strong> - bribery of foreign public officials: promising/offering/giving a financial or other advantage (directly or via a third party) not legitimately due. A <strong>foreign public official</strong> (s6(4)) is an individual holding legislative, administrative or judicial posts, or performing a public function for a foreign country/its agencies, or an official/agent of a public international organisation. Applies only to the briber, not the official; covers bribery via intermediaries (\"through a third party\").</li>\n<li><strong>Section 7</strong> - failure of commercial organisations to prevent bribery on their behalf: a broad, strict-liability offence (no need to prove intention) applying to all commercial organisations with UK business, with vicarious liability for bribery by an employee, agent, subsidiary or other third party.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Hospitality and Facilitation Payments</h4>\n<p>Corporate hospitality is not automatically a bribe if it has a legitimate business aim and is reasonable, proportionate and appropriate. <strong>Facilitation payments</strong> are an offence <strong>unless</strong> permitted by the written law of the foreign official's country, or made under threat of violence.</p>\n<h4>Defence - Adequate Procedures</h4>\n<p>A firm has a defence to the s7 offence if it can show it had \"adequate procedures\" in place. The MoJ's six guiding principles:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Proportionality</strong> - action proportionate to the risks faced and size of business.</li>\n<li><strong>Top-level commitment</strong> - senior leadership visibly opposes bribery.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk assessment</strong> - understanding the firm's bribery risks (markets, counterparties).</li>\n<li><strong>Due diligence</strong> - knowing who the firm deals with.</li>\n<li><strong>Communication</strong> - policies/procedures communicated (including training).</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring and review</strong> - keeping procedures current as risks change.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"heading":"3.7 Fraud and Economic Crime","html":"\n<p>The <strong>Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act (ECCTA)</strong> targets economic crime and corporate transparency. It introduced:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A new regulatory objective in the Legal Services Act 2007 on tackling economic crime.</li>\n<li>Companies House reforms to prevent/shut down fraudulent companies.</li>\n<li>Reforms to prevent abuse of limited partnerships.</li>\n<li>Additional powers to seize/recover suspected criminal cryptoassets.</li>\n<li>A new \"failure to prevent fraud\" offence.</li>\n<li>Measures addressing Strategic Lawsuits Against Public Participation (SLAPPs).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Failure to Prevent Fraud</h4>\n<p>This new corporate criminal offence came into effect on <strong>1 September 2025</strong>. An organisation may be criminally liable where an employee, agent, subsidiary, or other \"associated person\" commits fraud intending to benefit the organisation, and the organisation lacked reasonable fraud prevention procedures.</p>\n<p>Applies to: all large incorporated bodies, subsidiaries and partnerships; large not-for-profit organisations (e.g., incorporated charities); and incorporated public bodies. A <strong>\"large organisation\"</strong> must meet at least <strong>two</strong> of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Turnover of more than <strong>£36 million</strong>.</li>\n<li>Balance sheet total of more than <strong>£18 million</strong>.</li>\n<li>More than <strong>250 employees</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The offence applies to \"base fraud\" offences under <strong>Schedule 13 ECCTA</strong>: fraud offences under Section 1 of the Fraud Act 2006 (fraud by false representation; fraud by failing to disclose information; fraud by abuse of position); participation in a fraudulent business; obtaining services dishonestly; cheating the public revenue (common law); false accounting; false statements by company directors; and fraudulent trading. Aiding, abetting, counselling or procuring any of these also qualifies. <strong>Money laundering is NOT a fraud offence</strong> for these purposes.</p>\n<p>The base fraud offence must be committed by an \"associated person\": an employee, agent, subsidiary, or a person providing services for/on behalf of the organisation (while providing those services). The benefit may be financial or non-financial, and the organisation need not actually receive any benefit - it is enough that the organisation was <strong>intended</strong> to benefit.</p>\n<p>It is a defence if the organisation had <strong>reasonable procedures</strong> in place to prevent fraud. The Home Office's six fraud prevention principles: top-level commitment; risk assessment; proportionate risk-based prevention procedures; due diligence; communication (including training); monitoring and review.</p>\n<p>The offence creates <strong>strict liability</strong> - even if senior management were unaware of the fraud, the firm can be held liable, making the \"reasonable procedures\" defence critical in practice.</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.8 The Disclosure and Transparency Rules (DTR)","html":"\n<p>The <strong>Disclosure and Transparency Rules (DTR)</strong> are part of the FCA Handbook. Disclosure Rules are implemented by the FCA as listing authority; Transparency Rules give effect to the (former EU) Transparency Directive; both apply to issuers of securities admitted to trading on certain markets.</p>\n<h4>Disclosure Rules</h4>\n<p>Purpose: promote prompt/fair disclosure of relevant information; set out when an issuer may delay public disclosure of inside information; and set requirements to keep such information confidential to protect investors and prevent insider dealing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Denying access</strong> - issuers must have effective arrangements to deny access to inside information to those who do not need it for their function.</li>\n<li><strong>Breach of confidentiality</strong> - issuers must be able to make prompt public disclosure via an RIS if confidentiality of legitimately-delayed inside information is compromised, including a <strong>holding announcement</strong> giving as much detail as possible, explaining why a full announcement cannot yet be made, and promising further details.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Transparency Rules - Notifiable Shareholdings</h4>\n<p>Purpose: ensure adequate transparency/access to information in UK financial markets. The EU Transparency Directive set notification thresholds of <strong>5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 25%, 30%, 50% and 75%</strong>. The <strong>UK retained its own threshold of 3%</strong> (and each percentage point above), pre-dating the Directive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An investor has a notifiable interest once holding <strong>3% or more</strong> of a public company's shares (including via voting rights attached to shares/financial instruments, not just direct share ownership).</li>\n<li>Above 3%, notification is required whenever the holding crosses a <strong>whole percentage point</strong> (e.g., 3.7% to 4.1% is notifiable; 3.7% to 3.9% is not; 5.4% to 4.9% is notifiable; 5.4% to 5.1% is not).</li>\n<li>A fall back below 3% must also be notified.</li>\n<li>Indirect holdings (e.g., via a proxy with discretion to vote) can also trigger notification.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Voting rights attached to shares are <strong>disregarded</strong> in six cases:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Shares acquired solely for clearing/settlement, within the period up to the third trading day after execution.</li>\n<li>Shares held by a custodian or bare nominee.</li>\n<li>Shares held (if less than 10%) by a declared market maker.</li>\n<li>Shares held (if not more than 5%) in the trading book of an investment firm/credit institution.</li>\n<li>Shares held as collateral.</li>\n<li>Shares acquired for stabilisation purposes, provided voting rights are not exercised.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Deemed knowledge of acquisition/disposal (triggering the notification clock) arises no later than <strong>two trading days</strong> after the transaction, or when it becomes unconditional (if conditional on an external event).</p>\n<p>Notifiable interests include shares held by <strong>connected parties</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The investor's spouse.</li>\n<li>The investor's children (under 18).</li>\n<li>Companies controlled by the investor (control assumed where the investor holds at least <strong>one third</strong> of voting rights).</li>\n<li>Concert parties (agreements between two or more persons to act together, e.g., voting together) - if the combined holding reaches 3%+, it is notifiable as if a single holding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Process</strong>: notify the company in writing within <strong>two business days</strong> of the 3% threshold (or subsequent percentage point) being reached; if listed, the company must then report to a primary information provider (e.g., LSE's RNS) by the end of the following business day. Notification is <strong>not</strong> made to the FCA. The company must maintain a register of notifications, available at its registered office.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemptions</strong>: fund managers/operators of regulated CISs (e.g., AUTs, OEICs) are non-beneficial holders exempt from the 3% rule; they are instead subject to disclosure at 5% and 10% (not the percentages between), then at every percentage above 10%. Market makers are exempt below 10%. Custodians are fully exempt from disclosure, provided they cannot control voting rights.</p>\n<h4>Companies Act 2006 - Section 793 Letter</h4>\n<p>Under <strong>Section 793 Companies Act 2006</strong>, a UK public company may send a written notice to any person it knows/suspects to be a shareholder (or to have been one within the last <strong>three years</strong>), requiring confirmation of their interest and, for past interests, the identity of any buyer if known. Usually sent to <strong>nominee companies</strong> (legal owner of shares on behalf of a beneficial owner) rather than individuals, to identify the true beneficial owner. A written response is required within a reasonable time; persistent non-response allows the company to apply to court to have the shares <strong>frozen</strong> (loss of voting rights, dividend entitlement, and ability to sell). The company must keep a record of Section 793 letters sent and responses received.</p>\n<h4>International Comparison - US</h4>\n<p>The US Corporate Transparency Act (CTA) 2021 requires companies to report beneficial ownership information to a registry maintained by the <strong>Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN)</strong> (US Treasury). Required information: full legal name; date of birth; current residential/business address; and a non-expired US passport/state ID/driving licence number, or valid foreign passport number. Changes must be reported within <strong>one year</strong>, including: ownership exceeding or dropping below <strong>25%</strong>; a substantial change in company ownership; or a change in the beneficial owner's contact details.</p>\n"}],"mcqs":[{"question":"In what year did the UK's market abuse regime, established as an offence under FSMA, come into force?","options":["1993","2001","2016","2019"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The UK's market abuse regime under FSMA came into force in 2001. The CJA (criminal insider dealing) dates from 1993, the MAR came into effect in 2016, and the EU Exit amendments were made in 2019."},{"question":"Following Brexit, which of the following is NOT one of the three key amendments made by the Market Abuse (Amendment) (EU Exit) Regulations 2019?","options":["There is no obligation to share information with EU authorities without a guarantee of reciprocity","The FCA took over the regulatory responsibilities previously held by ESMA and the European Commission","Supplementary legislation is now subject to HM Treasury rather than the EC","The FCA Handbook rules on market abuse became legally binding for the first time"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FCA Handbook has always provided guidance (not binding rules) on UK MAR matters - this did not change as a result of the 2019 EU Exit amendments. The other three options are the genuine amendments made."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2012, which section covers the offence of making misleading statements in relation to benchmarks?","options":["Section 89","Section 90","Section 91","Section 397"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Section 89 covers making misleading statements, Section 90 covers creating false or misleading impressions, and Section 91 specifically covers misleading statements relating to benchmarks. These sections replaced the former FSMA Section 397."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2021, what is the maximum custodial sentence on conviction on indictment for a misleading statements offence committed after 1 November 2021?","options":["2 years","5 years","7 years","10 years"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"For offences committed after 1 November 2021, the maximum sentence on indictment is 10 years. Offences committed up to 31 October 2021 carried a maximum of 7 years. Summary conviction carries up to 12 months."},{"question":"For how long must firms retain written documentation of their market abuse detection arrangements, systems and procedures under UK MAR, and how frequently must these be reviewed at a minimum?","options":["3 years; reviewed every 2 years","5 years; reviewed at least annually","7 years; reviewed every 3 years","10 years; reviewed biennially"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"UK MAR requires documented arrangements to be maintained for five years and assessed at least through an annually conducted audit and internal review."},{"question":"A compliance officer identifies a suspicious order that has not yet been executed but appears intended to distort the market. Under UK MAR, what is the correct course of action?","options":["No action is required because no execution has taken place","A STOR should be submitted, as the requirement extends to attempted market abuse","The order should simply be cancelled with no further reporting","The matter should only be escalated internally, not to the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The STOR requirement extends to attempts to commit market manipulation or insider dealing, even where no execution has taken place but there was intent to distort the market. The firm must notify the FCA without delay."},{"question":"Under the Criminal Justice Act 1993, Section 52 sets out three ways of committing the offence of insider dealing. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","options":["Dealing in the security","Encouraging another to deal","Disclosing inside information to another","Failing to report a colleague's suspected insider dealing"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Section 52 CJA creates three offences: dealing, encouraging another to deal, and disclosing inside information. Failure to report a colleague's suspected wrongdoing is a money laundering-related concept (POCA s330), not an insider dealing offence under the CJA."},{"question":"A director's spouse overhears confidential takeover information at home and buys shares in the target company. On what basis would they be considered an 'insider' under UK MAR?","options":["They cannot be an insider because they are not an employee of either company","They obtained the information indirectly from a person who was an inside source","They are automatically exempt as a family member","Only the director, not the spouse, can commit an offence"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A person obtains information from an inside source if they access it directly or indirectly from someone who was themselves an inside source or had access via their employment - as with a spouse who overhears confidential information from a director."},{"question":"The Insider Dealing (Securities and Regulated Markets) Order 2023, which updated the list of Regulated Markets for the purposes of Part V of the CJA 1993, came into effect on which date?","options":["1 January 2023","15 June 2023","1 September 2023","31 December 2023"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Insider Dealing (Securities and Regulated Markets) Order 2023 came into effect on 15 June 2023."},{"question":"A broker learns that a client wishes to buy a large block of shares before executing the client's buy order. Which special defence to insider dealing is most likely to apply?","options":["Price stabilisation","Market information","Legitimate behaviour via information barriers","Market maker good faith"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is the classic market information defence - the broker's knowledge of the client's pending order is 'market information', and acting on it to execute the client's instruction is a specific statutory defence, provided it was reasonable in the circumstances."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the general defences available to a defendant charged with insider dealing (or encouraging another to deal)?","options":["The defendant did not expect the dealing to result in a profit or avoided loss","The defendant reasonably believed the information had been widely disclosed","The defendant would have acted the same way regardless of the information","The defendant was acting on behalf of a regulated investment firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Acting on behalf of a regulated firm is not, by itself, a recognised general defence. The three genuine general defences are: no expectation of profit/avoided loss; reasonable belief of wide disclosure; and that the defendant would have acted the same way regardless."},{"question":"Which of the following is one of the four main forms of market manipulation under UK MAR?","options":["Insider list failure","Benchmark manipulation","Failure to disclose","Tipping off"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The four forms of market manipulation are: manipulating transactions, manipulating devices, dissemination, and benchmark manipulation. Failure to disclose and tipping off are POCA offences; insider list failure relates to MAR disclosure obligations, not manipulation itself."},{"question":"A PDMR (person discharging managerial responsibilities) executes a personal transaction in their employer's shares. Within how many business days must this be notified to the issuer and the FCA?","options":["1 business day","3 business days","5 business days","10 business days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"PDMR notifications must be made promptly and no later than three business days after the transaction."},{"question":"What is the length of the 'closed period' during which PDMRs are restricted from dealing in their company's shares before the announcement of an interim or annual financial report?","options":["7 calendar days","14 calendar days","30 calendar days","60 calendar days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The closed period is 30 calendar days before the announcement of the company's interim or annual financial report."},{"question":"Regarding Accepted Market Practices (AMPs) in the UK, which statement is correct?","options":["The FCA has established several AMPs covering common trading strategies","The FCA has the authority to establish AMPs, but currently none have been established in the UK","AMPs are established by the Bank of England, not the FCA","AMPs automatically apply to all algorithmic trading strategies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA has the authority under UK MAR to establish Accepted Market Practices after consultation, but as at the time of writing no AMPs have actually been established in the UK market."},{"question":"Under UK MAR investment recommendation disclosure rules, a firm changing its recommendation on a financial instrument must disclose the change and the date of the previous recommendation if it was disseminated within what preceding period?","options":["3 months","6 months","12 months","24 months"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Where a recommendation differs from a previous recommendation on the same instrument disseminated during the preceding 12-month period, the change and date of the earlier recommendation must be indicated - an ongoing disclosure obligation."},{"question":"Under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, which offence carries a maximum penalty of a fine and/or up to 14 years' imprisonment?","options":["Tipping off (s333A)","Failure to disclose (s330)","Acquisition, use and possession (s329)","Prejudicing an investigation (s342)"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Concealing (s327), arrangements (s328), and acquisition/use/possession (s329) are punishable by a fine and/or up to 14 years. Failure to disclose (s330) and prejudicing an investigation (s342) carry up to 5 years; tipping off (s333A) carries up to 2 years."},{"question":"An employee in the regulated sector suspects a client of money laundering but fails to report it to the firm's nominated officer. Which POCA offence, and what is the applicable test for liability?","options":["Section 333A tipping off; a subjective test only","Section 330 failure to disclose; an objective test (reasonable grounds to know or suspect)","Section 342 prejudicing an investigation; a strict liability test","Section 328 arrangements; a subjective test only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is a Section 330 failure to disclose offence. Liability can arise not only where the person actually knew or suspected, but also where there were reasonable grounds to know or suspect - the objective test, based on what a reasonable person would have suspected."},{"question":"Which piece of legislation addressed the 'Spanish bullfighter' issue, whereby conduct legal in its country of origin but illegal in the UK would otherwise count as criminal property under POCA?","options":["The Criminal Finances Act 2017","The Serious Organised Crime and Police Act (SOCPA) 2005","The Money Laundering Regulations 2017","The Bribery Act 2010"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SOCPA 2005 amended POCA to provide a defence where the alleged offender reasonably believed the conduct was not criminal in the country where it took place, subject to the Secretary of State's power to still designate certain overseas conduct (e.g., serious tax evasion) as reportable."},{"question":"What are the three stages of money laundering, in the correct order?","options":["Layering, placement, integration","Placement, layering, integration","Integration, layering, placement","Placement, integration, layering"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The three stages are placement (introducing proceeds into the financial system), layering (moving funds through complex transactions to obscure origin), and integration (funds appear as legitimate, clean money)."},{"question":"A firm is establishing a business relationship with a new customer who is a beneficial owner of a pooled client account held by a firm of solicitors. Which due diligence tier is most likely to apply?","options":["Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD)","Simplified Due Diligence (SDD)","No due diligence is required at all","Only source-of-wealth checks are required"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Beneficial owners of pooled accounts held by notaries or legal professionals are one of the categories where Simplified Due Diligence may be applied, reflecting a lower assessed ML risk."},{"question":"Which of the following would trigger a requirement for Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD) under MLR 2017?","options":["The customer is a UK public authority","The customer is a listed company","The transaction involves a correspondent banking relationship","The product is a child trust fund"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Correspondent banking relationships are one of the specific circumstances requiring EDD under MLR 2017. UK public authorities, listed companies and CTFs are examples where Simplified Due Diligence may instead apply."},{"question":"Under the PEP definition used in AML regulation, an individual who has left a prominent public function ceases to require automatic EDD treatment after what minimum period?","options":["6 months","1 year","2 years","5 years"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm's obligation to apply EDD to a former PEP ceases one year after they left office, or longer if the firm considers this appropriate given ongoing ML/TF risk."},{"question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the roles of the MLRO and the Nominated Officer?","options":["The Nominated Officer oversees the firm's entire AML compliance framework, while the MLRO only receives internal reports","The MLRO and Nominated Officer are legally required to be different individuals within the firm","The Nominated Officer is the designated recipient of internal suspicion reports with a legal duty to report to the NCA; in practice this role is usually combined with the broader MLRO function","Only firms with more than 250 employees are required to appoint a Nominated Officer"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The nominated officer (required under MLR 2017) is the internal recipient of suspicion reports with the legal duty to escalate to the NCA. The MLRO has the broader FCA-driven compliance oversight role. In practice, the same individual usually performs both roles."},{"question":"Which of the following is the corporate, strict-liability offence created by the Bribery Act 2010, carrying an unlimited fine rather than imprisonment?","options":["Section 1 - bribing another person","Section 2 - being bribed","Section 6 - bribing a foreign public official","Section 7 - failure of a commercial organisation to prevent bribery"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Section 7 creates the corporate offence of failing to prevent bribery by an associated person, carrying an unlimited fine. Sections 1, 2 and 6 are individual offences carrying up to 10 years' imprisonment."},{"question":"Under the Bribery Act 2010, when is a facilitation payment to a foreign official NOT an offence?","options":["Whenever the payment is below £100","When it is permitted by the written law of the official's country, or made under threat of violence","Whenever the payment is made through a third party","Facilitation payments are never permitted under any circumstances"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Facilitation payments are generally an offence, except where the written law of the foreign official's country permits the official to accept the advantage, or where the payment is made under threat of violence."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the Ministry of Justice's six principles for 'adequate procedures' as a defence to the Bribery Act Section 7 offence?","options":["Proportionality","Top-level commitment","Risk assessment","Mandatory annual external audit by a Big Four firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The six MoJ principles are proportionality, top-level commitment, risk assessment, due diligence, communication, and monitoring and review. There is no requirement for a mandatory external audit by a specific category of auditor."},{"question":"Under the Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act (ECCTA), an organisation is classified as 'large' (and therefore within scope of the failure to prevent fraud offence) if it meets at least two of which three criteria?","options":["Turnover >£10m; balance sheet >£5m; >50 employees","Turnover >£36m; balance sheet total >£18m; >250 employees","Turnover >£50m; balance sheet >£25m; >500 employees","Turnover >£1bn; balance sheet >£500m; >1,000 employees"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A 'large organisation' under ECCTA must meet at least two of: turnover of more than £36 million, balance sheet total of more than £18 million, or more than 250 employees."},{"question":"When did the new 'failure to prevent fraud' corporate offence under ECCTA come into effect?","options":["1 September 2023","1 January 2024","1 September 2025","1 January 2026"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The failure to prevent fraud offence came into effect on 1 September 2025."},{"question":"Which of the following is explicitly stated NOT to be a 'base fraud offence' for the purposes of the failure to prevent fraud offence under ECCTA?","options":["Fraud by false representation","False accounting","Money laundering","Fraudulent trading"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The manual explicitly notes that money laundering is not an offence of fraud for these purposes. The base fraud offences under Schedule 13 ECCTA include fraud by false representation, false accounting, fraudulent trading and others, but not money laundering."},{"question":"An investor's shareholding in a UK-listed company rises from 3.7% to 3.9%. Under the UK DTR, is this notifiable?","options":["Yes, because it exceeds 3%","No, because it has not crossed a whole percentage point","Yes, because all changes above 1% must be reported","No, notification is only required for decreases in holdings"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Once above the 3% notifiable threshold, further notification is only required when the holding crosses a whole percentage point. A move from 3.7% to 3.9% does not cross a whole percentage point (e.g., from 3% to 4%), so it is not notifiable; a move to 4.1% would be."},{"question":"What is the UK's notifiable shareholding threshold under the DTR, and how does it differ from the EU Transparency Directive's approach?","options":["5%, the same as the EU threshold","3%, retained by the UK rather than adopting the EU's 5%/10%/15% scale","10%, higher than the EU's 5% threshold","1%, lower than any EU threshold"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The EU Transparency Directive set thresholds of 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 25%, 30%, 50% and 75%. The UK retained its own pre-existing threshold of 3% (and every percentage point above) rather than adopting the EU scale."},{"question":"Once a notifiable threshold is crossed under the UK DTR, within how many business days must the investor notify the company, and by when must the company inform the market?","options":["1 business day to notify the company; same day to the market","2 business days to notify the company; by the end of the following business day to the market","5 business days to notify the company; within 5 business days to the market","There is no fixed timeframe; disclosure must simply be 'prompt'"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The investor must notify the company in writing within two business days of the threshold being reached; the company must then report to a primary information provider (e.g., the LSE's RNS) by the end of the following business day. Notification is not made directly to the FCA."},{"question":"For the purposes of connected parties under the DTR, at what level of voting rights is an investor assumed to control a company?","options":["10%","20%","One quarter (25%)","One third"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Control of a company is assumed for connected-party purposes if the investor holds at least one third of its voting rights."},{"question":"Under Section 793 of the Companies Act 2006, what can happen if a shareholder persistently ignores a company's written notice requesting disclosure of their shareholding interest?","options":["The company can automatically cancel the shares","The company can apply to court to have the shares frozen, removing voting and dividend rights and the ability to sell","The FCA will impose a fine directly on the shareholder","Nothing further can be done as compliance is voluntary"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If Section 793 notices are persistently ignored, the company can apply to court to have the shares held frozen - the shareholder loses voting rights, dividend entitlement, and the ability to sell the shares."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Market abuse vs insider dealing — 3 key differences.","back":"Market abuse: CIVIL offence, no need to prove intent, no imprisonment possible. Insider dealing: CRIMINAL offence (CJA 1993), requires knowledge/intent, CAN result in imprisonment."},{"front":"What instruments does insider dealing legislation (CJA 1993) cover — and famously NOT cover?","back":"Covers MiFID instruments (shares, gilts, debentures, related derivatives) traded on UK/EEA/Gibraltar regulated markets, MTFs, OTFs, NASDAQ, SIX, NYSE. Does NOT cover currencies or forward FX contracts."},{"front":"What are the 3 SPECIAL (specific) defences to insider dealing?","back":"1) Market makers acting in good faith 2) Price stabilisation rules 3) Recipients of market information (e.g. in takeovers)."},{"front":"What are the GENERAL defences to insider dealing (available to anyone)?","back":"'Would have dealt anyway' regardless of the information, and 'did not expect the recipient to deal' (only relevant to the disclosure offence, not dealing itself)."},{"front":"What are the 3 stages of money laundering, and where is each type of firm most vulnerable?","back":"Placement (introducing cash — banks most vulnerable) → Layering (obscuring the trail — investment firms most vulnerable) → Integration (giving legitimacy — investment firms also vulnerable here)."},{"front":"Who does the MLRO report to, and how often must they update senior management?","back":"Reports suspicions to the NATIONAL CRIME AGENCY (NOT the FCA). Must brief senior management/the board at least once every 12 months."},{"front":"What must an employee do if they suspect money laundering?","back":"Report to the firm's MLRO (or Nominated Officer if there's no MLRO) — NOT directly to the NCA or FCA, and NOT discuss it with the client (tipping off risk)."},{"front":"'Tipping off' vs other POCA offences — sentencing difference?","back":"Tipping off: max 2 years + unlimited fine. Most other POCA offences (arrangements, concealing, assisting, failure to report): up to 14 years + unlimited fine."},{"front":"What defines 'criminal property' under POCA 2002?","back":"Any benefit (money OR otherwise) arising from criminal conduct — where the alleged offender KNOWS OR SUSPECTS it is criminal property. Covers proceeds of ALL crime, not just drug trafficking."},{"front":"Is JMLSG guidance legally binding?","back":"NO — not mandatory, but courts/the FCA take adherence to it into account as evidence of compliance with the Money Laundering Regulations. Firms following it in good faith have a strong defence."},{"front":"How long must client identification/verification records be kept?","back":"5 years after the business relationship ENDS (or after an occasional transaction is completed). 10 years is the outer maximum for certain transactional records, not the standard rule."},{"front":"Is a Politically Exposed Person (PEP) automatically suspected of wrongdoing?","back":"NO — enhanced due diligence applies simply because of their VULNERABILITY TO CORRUPTION given their position, not presumption of guilt."},{"front":"What is the PDMR ('Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibility') closed period, and its main purpose?","back":"30 calendar days before an annual/interim (half-yearly) results announcement — directors are restricted from dealing in own-company shares. Purpose: protect directors from the APPEARANCE of insider dealing, keep the market fair and orderly (not literally prevent all dealing)."},{"front":"Name 2 exceptions that can lift the PDMR closed-period restriction.","back":"Severe financial difficulty requiring immediate realisation of funds, and transactions under employee share schemes where beneficial ownership doesn't change."},{"front":"How soon must a PDMR notify the issuer and FCA of a personal transaction outside a closed period?","back":"Promptly, and no later than 3 business days after the transaction."},{"front":"What are the recognised forms of market manipulation under the FCA's Code of Market Conduct?","back":"Manipulating transactions, manipulating devices, dissemination (spreading false/misleading information), and benchmark manipulation. ('Misleading impressions' is NOT one of these — that's a separate CRIMINAL offence under FSA 2012 s90.)"},{"front":"Misleading statements/impressions (FSA 2012 ss89-91) — criminal or civil, and what's the defence?","back":"CRIMINAL offence. Defence: acting in conformity with the FCA's price stabilisation rules. (Market-maker defence applies to insider dealing, NOT to misleading statements.)"},{"front":"What safe harbour exists for behaviour that would otherwise look like market manipulation?","back":"'Accepted market practice' — behaviour carried out for legitimate reasons that conforms with practices accepted by the FCA is not manipulation, even if it looks unusual."},{"front":"When must a firm notify the FCA of a reasonable suspicion of insider dealing/market manipulation under UK MAR?","back":"Without delay — brief checks to confirm 'reasonable suspicion' are fine, but a full investigation is NOT required before reporting."},{"front":"What must issuers do when sharing inside information with external advisers?","back":"Maintain an INSIDER LIST of everyone with access — maintained separately for each transaction, plus one for permanent insiders."},{"front":"Is disclosing inside information to a professional adviser (e.g. lawyer, auditor) permitted?","back":"Yes — disclosure in the normal course of business/duties is a PERMITTED disclosure. Disclosing to a friend/family member outside that normal course is UNLAWFUL disclosure."},{"front":"What's the scope of the Bribery Act 2010, and what's the corporate defence?","back":"Applies WORLDWIDE for UK-connected organisations/individuals. Corporate 'failure to prevent bribery' offence has a defence of having ADEQUATE PROCEDURES in place to prevent it."},{"front":"What is FATF, and is it legally binding in the UK?","back":"Financial Action Task Force — an inter-governmental body setting INTERNATIONAL standards for AML/CTF. NOT directly binding in the UK; its recommendations shape domestic legislation instead."},{"front":"EMIR — what does it require, and what is it NOT about?","back":"Requires trade reporting to a repository, risk management for OTC derivatives, and mandatory central counterparty clearing for certain contracts. It does NOT cover 'ensuring orderly functioning of markets' generally — that's a general regulatory objective, not an EMIR-specific requirement."},{"front":"What penalties are available for market abuse (a civil offence) — and what is NEVER available?","back":"Available: unlimited fines, public censure, restitution, prohibition orders, withdrawal of approval. NEVER available: imprisonment (it's civil, not criminal)."},{"front":"Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act — what does the 'failure to prevent fraud' offence require?","back":"Applies where an employee/agent commits fraud intending to BENEFIT the organisation, and the firm lacked reasonable fraud-prevention procedures. It's a STRICT LIABILITY offence — the firm can be liable even if senior management didn't know."},{"front":"What size thresholds define a 'large organisation' under the failure-to-prevent-fraud offence?","back":"Must meet 2 of 3: turnover > £36 million, balance sheet total > £18 million, more than 250 employees."},{"front":"Money laundering covers cash and assets from where?","back":"ANY criminal source — anywhere in the world — provided the underlying conduct would be a criminal offence under UK law."},{"front":"What's the aim of the JMLSG's 'risk-based approach'?","back":"Firms must identify the money-laundering/terrorist-financing risks THEIR OWN business faces, and design systems/controls proportionate to that risk — not a one-size-fits-all checklist."}],"cisiMcqs":[{"question":"Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding market abuse?","options":["Intention does not need to be proved","The offence only relates to dealings in securities traded on a UK market","The maximum penalty is ten years in jail plus an unlimited fine","It applies only to UK approved persons"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Market abuse is 'effects' based legislation and therefore intention does not need to be proved. It applies in relation to all qualifying investments traded on a prescribed market, i.e. RIE, ROIE and all EU regulated exchanges. Generally the FCA will normally only prosecute for offences in relation to UK markets. It is a civil offence and therefore does not carry a jail sentence. It applies to all persons, i.e. individuals and institutions, whether they are located in the UK or overseas."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true regarding the insider dealing legislation under the Criminal Justice Act?","options":["Offences can be committed by individuals but not by companies","Encouraging a third party to deal on the basis of inside information is an offence","UK equities, gilts and related derivatives are all covered by the legislation","Disclosure of price sensitive information to a third party outside the company affected is not an offence"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Only individuals can be guilty of the offences. Any securities traded on exchange are covered. If the price sensitive information is disclosed to a third party from outside the company affected, it is still an offence and that third party becomes a secondary insider."},{"question":"All of the following are covered by the UK insider dealing legislation except:","options":["Options on shares","ADRs","Debentures","Forward foreign exchange contracts"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Insider dealing applies to MiFID instruments."},{"question":"The MLRO must report to senior management at least:","options":["Once every 6 months","Once every 12 months","Once every 18 months","Once every 24 months"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The MLRO must report to senior management at least once every calendar year."},{"question":"According to the Money Laundering Regulations, which of the following is FALSE?","options":["Staff must be trained to recognise suspicious transactions","A firm must keep records for a minimum of three years","The firm must appoint a Money Laundering Reporting Officer","The rules can apply to insurance companies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The firm must keep records for five years."},{"question":"Which of the following is TRUE of the Money Laundering Regulations 2017?","options":["All firms subject to the regulations must appoint a Money Laundering Reporting Officer","A firm cannot be prosecuted under the regulations if a clear audit trail has been documented","Records of customer identification must be kept for three years after the person ceases to be a customer","Disciplinary sanctions under the regulations are determined by the FCA"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Technically, the MLR2017 requires the appointment of a 'nominated officer' - however, although the obligations of the MLRO under the FCA requirements are different from those of the nominated officer under POCA, the Terrorism Act or the MLR 2017, in practice the same person tends to carry on both roles, and is usually known as the MLRO. Although failing to keep proper records is an offence, the mere presence of a 'clear audit trail' does not stop the firm being prosecuted under any circumstances. The legislation and sanctions were written by HMT. Records must be kept for five years."},{"question":"Under the Money Laundering Regulations all of the following are considered high-risk factors EXCEPT:","options":["Correspondent banking transactions","Transactions related to tobacco products","Transaction related to items of archaeological significance","Transactions within a pooled client account"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Although transactions within a pooled account are no longer automatically afforded simplified due diligence (SDD), they are not, in themselves, a high risk factor.\n\nCorrespondent banking transactions relates to large value transactions between international entities/banks (NOSTRO account transfers)."},{"question":"Under which of the following is insider dealing a criminal offence?","options":["Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","Companies Act 2006","The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Criminal Justice Act 1993"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Note: FSMA 2000 gives the FCA the power to prosecute."},{"question":"Failure to comply with the FCA's rule on fair, clear and not misleading communications would also constitute a breach of which of the following?","options":["Criminal Justice Act","Order execution policy","Trust law","Misleading statements and impressions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Failure to comply with the FCA's rule on fair, clear and not misleading communications would also constitute a breach of FSA 2012 s89-91 Misleading Statements and Impressions."},{"question":"Which activity is the subject of the defence in relation to distorting market prices of 'acting in accordance with the rules of'?","options":["Market making","Price stabilisation","Underwriting","Stock lending"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Behaviour which conforms to the share buy back and stabilisation regulations will not amount to market abuse. This is known as a 'safe harbour'."},{"question":"Which of the following gives the FCA the power to prosecute insider dealing offences?","options":["Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","Criminal Justice Act 1993","The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Companies Act 2006"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The offence is contained within the CJA 1993, however the ability of the FCA to prosecute comes from ss 401-402 FSMA 2000."},{"question":"Which transaction may be permitted during a closed period for a person discharging managerial responsibilities?","options":["A discretionary purchase to increase exposure before expected strong results","A sale to a family member to improve personal liquidity before publication","A transaction under an employee share scheme where beneficial ownership does not change","A purchase funded by a bonus that has not yet been disclosed to the market"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The source states that the closed-period restriction may be lifted for transactions under employee share schemes or similar transactions where there is no change in beneficial ownership. The other options describe discretionary dealing that is not identified as an exception."},{"question":"Which of the following would NOT be covered by the insider dealing legislation?","options":["FTSE index option","A foreign exchange forward","Debenture","FTSE futures contract"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Insider dealing legislation (CJA 1993) does not cover foreign exchange forwards."},{"question":"Which one of the following is NOT a money laundering offence, as defined in the Proceeds of Crime Act?","options":["Tipping off","Failure to report","Assistance","Dealing on price sensitive information"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The answer to 'dealing on price sensitive information' is an offence under the INSIDER DEALING legislation."},{"question":"Which of the following is not covered by the insider dealing legislation?","options":["Building society deposits","Gilts","Options on UK listed companies","Gilt futures"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Insider dealing covers MiFID instruments."},{"question":"What is the aim of the money laundering regulations?","options":["To set out the different money laundering offences","To set out the different penalties for money laundering","To prevent those with criminal records from opening bank accounts","To prevent the proceeds of crime from entering the financial system"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Money laundering rules are part of the general fight against crime."},{"question":"Which are TRUE of money laundering?","options":["The JMLSG Guidance Notes have received Treasury approval","It is a criminal offence not to report a suspicion","It is a criminal offence not to have procedures in place","All are true"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The courts are required to take account of guidance approved by the Treasury, such as the guidance notes. All are correct."},{"question":"Which reform is identified as one of the aims of the Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act?","options":["Introducing a new retail conduct regime for insurance intermediaries","Creating a new authorisation gateway for appointed representatives","Reforming Companies House to prevent fraudulent companies being created","Establishing a new compensation scheme for victims of market abuse"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Act is aimed at tackling economic crime and improving transparency over corporate entities, including reforms to Companies House to prevent the creation of, and shut down, fraudulent companies. The other options are not listed reforms under the Act."},{"question":"Market abuse is different from the criminal offence of insider dealing in all of the following ways except:","options":["Market abuse is based on the civil burden of proof, i.e. the balance of probabilities","It should be easier for the FCA to demonstrate abusive behaviour","It should be easier for the FCA to impose a custodial sentence","It can apply to commodities as well as commodity derivatives"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The burden of proof is less difficult to establish than beyond a reasonable doubt and hence it should be easier to demonstrate abusive behaviour. However, only fines can be imposed. Market abuse is applicable across the board."},{"question":"If the FCA is leading an investigation into insider dealing, who can use the special defence of 'acting in good faith'?","options":["Director","Person who has market information","Market-maker","Person involved in a hostile takeover"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Being a market maker is a special defence against the charge of insider dealing."},{"question":"A firm ends a business relationship with a client after completing customer due diligence and several monitored transactions. For how long must the firm keep copies of documents and information obtained for anti-money laundering and counter-terrorist financing purposes?","options":["For three years after the business relationship ends","For five years after the business relationship ends","For seven years after the business relationship ends","For ten years after the business relationship ends"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms must keep copies of documents and information obtained to meet customer due diligence and counter-terrorist financing requirements, and sufficient transaction records, for five years after the business relationship ends or after an occasional transaction. Ten years is the outer limit beyond which transactional records in a business relationship do not need to be kept."},{"question":"A chair of the board has access to price sensitive information about his company's shares. In which of the following circumstances could a director in the company trade?","options":["The director would follow the PDMR rules","The director would need permission from the FCA","The director would not be permitted to trade","Only if the director can prove they do not have any material price sensitive information"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"What the chair knows is irrelevant to the director's dealings. The director would go through the normal procedures."},{"question":"Which of the following is a provision of the Money Laundering Regulations?","options":["Employees should report all suspicions to the National Crime Agency","There is no need to identify new business","The regulations are considered guidance and are not legally binding","Training must be given to employees in relation to money laundering prevention"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Employees report to the MLRO. There is a need to identify new business or customers. The regulations are a legal obligation."},{"question":"If a financial analyst is providing investment research for dissemination to its clients in particular financial investments, when would they be permitted to deal on that information?","options":["The financial analyst, as a relevant person, would never be able to deal on that information","The financial analyst, as a non-relevant person, would always be allowed to deal on that information","The financial analyst, as a relevant person, would be permitted to deal on the information if they submitted all confirmations of transactions to the firm's compliance","The financial analyst, as a relevant person, would be permitted to deal on the information if granted approval by the firm's compliance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The personal account dealing rules state that permission to trade must be granted by Compliance. Although the analyst would be permitted to deal in this circumstance, it would be an exceptional circumstance for the firm's compliance to grant approval."},{"question":"Which disclosure of inside information is permitted under UK Market Abuse Regulation?","options":["Disclosure to a journalist to test likely market reaction before an announcement","Disclosure to a professional adviser to obtain advice on the transaction","Disclosure to a family member to discuss the commercial implications privately","Disclosure to a client to improve order timing before the information is published"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Permitted disclosure includes sharing inside information with a professional adviser, such as a lawyer or auditor, and other legitimate disclosures made in the normal exercise of duties. The other disclosures are not identified as permitted in the provided material."},{"question":"Before conducting designated investment business for a new customer, the money laundering regulations require an authorised firm to evidence identity. Which of the following is not true?","options":["Unless satisfactory evidence of identity is obtained in a timely manner, the business must not proceed","To identify someone, an official document bearing a photograph should always be obtained, whether the new business is opened face-to-face or through the post.","The register of electors, recent utility bills and bank statements are valid methods of verifying the address of a prospective customer","Records of identity must be kept for five years"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Although photographic identification is favourable, it is not always necessary. The Joint Money Laundering Steering Group states:\n\"a government-issued document (without a photograph) which incorporates the customer's full name, supported by a second document … which incorporates: - the customer's full name and - either his residential address - or his date of birth.\""},{"question":"In relation to the insider dealing legislation, which of the following individuals would be considered to be an insider?","options":["An employee who bought shares in the company after he heard that the chairman had purchased shares","A shareholder who purchased shares following receipt of published financial statements","The human resources director who purchases shares after being appointed to the position","The audit partner who sells shares after reading the audit report prior to publication of the financial statements"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The audit partner would be deemed to be an insider, as he has access to information which is not publicly available."},{"question":"Sue becomes suspicious of a 'high number of sales' by her client to an unknown party. Sue should report her fears to:","options":["The client","The FCA","The NCA","The MLRO"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The MLRO is the Money Laundering Reporting Officer. Every authorised firm must have one. They act as a conduit for information relating to money laundering from inside the firm to the relevant authorities."},{"question":"In the context of market abuse which of the following circumstances would not be considered an offence?","options":["Short selling","Price positioning","Abusive squeezes","Artificial transactions"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Short selling is a common acceptable trading strategy. The rest would be considered market abuse."},{"question":"Where can the law relating to insider dealing as a criminal offence be found?","options":["The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","The Criminal Justice Act 1993","The Companies Act 2006"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 outlines criminal money laundering offences, the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 creates regulatory powers for the FCA and PRA, and the Companies Act 2006 allows companies to audit their shareholder register (amongst other things)."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true of insider dealing as defined under the Criminal Justice Act?","options":["It only covers UK regulated markets, UK MTFs and UK OTFs","Gilts and other government bonds are included in the law on insider dealing","The Financial Conduct Authority is responsible for prosecuting offences","A defence exists if the individual would have acted in the same way had they not had the information"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The legislation applies to MiFID instruments traded on UK/EEA/Gibraltar regulated markets, MTFs and OTFs, together with the NASDAQ, SIX and NYSE."},{"question":"With regards to money laundering, the FCA will check whether a firm is in breach of the rules on systems and controls with reference to the firm's adherence to:","options":["The conduct of business rules","NCA reporting rules","JMLSG guidance","The Proceeds of Crime Act"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Joint Money Laundering Steering Group provides guidance of how to implement strategies to mitigate the risks of receiving and processing money for the purpose of laundering. The FCA set out their own requirements and standards for authorised firms in their sourcebook covering senior management arrangements, systems and controls (SYSC). When considering a breach of these requirements the FCA will make reference to whether the firm has followed the JMLSG guidance."},{"question":"To whom must the Money Laundering Reporting Officer report suspicious activities?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Action Task Force","The board of directors","National Crime Agency"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Any suspicions must immediately be reported to the NCA. Any weakness in internal controls must be submitted to the firm's directors on an annual basis."},{"question":"Which of the following is an inter-governmental body which sets international standards that aim to prevent money laundering and terrorist financing activities and the harm they cause to society?","options":["Federation of Regulators Against Criminal Activities","Financial Action Task Force","European Securities Markets Authority","International Anti-Money Laundering Commission"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Financial Action Task Force (FATF) was established in 1989 to initially examine and develop measures to combat money laundering. The FATF is the global money laundering and terrorist financing watchdog. It is an inter-governmental body which sets international standards that aim to prevent these illegal activities and the harm they cause to society."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be considered a suspicious transaction under the Money Laundering Regulations?","options":["Customer services are asked to take payment from a different account than the account history shows is usual","Payment is from a debit card account with a different card holder name","The customer initiates several small transactions totalling less than €7,500; it is an unusual size for the customer's transaction history","The customer is unable to prove his identity through the usual means, but is willing to offer other forms of identification"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Any unusual behaviour exhibited by a customer that does not conform to their profile, characteristics or history may be deemed a suspicious transaction."},{"question":"Which of the following pieces of legislation cover money laundering?","options":["Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Regulated Activities Order 2001","Bank of England Act 1998","Financial Services Act 2012"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 is the legislation that defines the money laundering offences which are all punishable by a fine and/or a term of imprisonment."},{"question":"The Criminal Justice Act regarding insider dealing contains a special defence providing the person was acting in good faith. This defence applies to:","options":["Private investors","Market makers","Managers of unit trusts","ICVCs"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is a defence for market makers, providing they are acting in good faith in the ordinary course of business."},{"question":"If an employee notices a suspicious transaction, which of the following is the correct first course of action?","options":["Their line manager reports to the NCA","The MLRO reports to the NCA","The employee reports to the MLRO","The employee discusses the matter with the client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"You must report to the MLRO who then reports to the NCA."},{"question":"What do insider dealing and market abuse have in common?","options":["They are defined by the Criminal Justice Act","They can both involve the use of non public information","Intent must be demonstrated","They are criminal offences"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Market abuse is dealt with by the Market Abuse Regulation. Market abuse does not require the person to have intended to abuse the market. Insider dealing is a criminal offence, however market abuse is a civil offence."},{"question":"Under which piece of legislation is the FCA empowered to issue fines for money laundering offences?","options":["FSMA 2000","POCA 2002","CJA 93","Companies Act 1985"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 grants the FCA the power to prosecute for money laundering offences under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 and for insider dealing offences under the Criminal Justice Act 1993."},{"question":"Who would receive notification of a director trading shares in their own company?","options":["The Takeover Panel","Their company","The Bank of England","The Competition and Markets Authority"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Directors inform their company within three business days when they trade in their own company's shares."},{"question":"At which stages of the money laundering process are investment firms generally most vulnerable to being involved in money laundering activities?","options":["Placement and layering","Layering and integration","Placement and integration","None of these stages"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"As a general rule, investment firms are most vulnerable to being involved in money laundering activities during the layering and integration stages of money laundering. Note: banks and building societies are most vulnerable at the placement stage."},{"question":"The FCA will consult which of the following sources of information in relation to money laundering offences?","options":["The Criminal Justice Act","The Joint Money Laundering Steering Group","The FCA Handbook","The Financial Services and Markets Tribunal"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Guidance, produced by the JMLSG, is provided to firms on how they should interpret and implement the anti-money laundering provisions. They are not mandatory but do highlight industry best practice. They are also approved by the Treasury, which means that if a firm can show that it adhered to them, the courts will take this into account as evidence of compliance with the legislation."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the specified types of market abuse?","options":["Insider dealing","Manipulating transactions","Processing proceeds of crime","Dissemination"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Processing proceeds of crime is the offence of money laundering."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be considered market abuse?","options":["An employee of a firm trades on price-sensitive information that has been disclosed through an RIS","A firm sells a large holding of bonds, a transaction that significantly reduces the market price, and then repurchases their holding at the lower price","A firm unintentionally submits incorrect trade details, which leads to a significant move in the market","A firm controls a large percentage of a company's shares and takes out a large up-bet on those shares with a spread-betting firm"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Information disclosed through an RIS (regulatory information service), such as the LSE's Regulatory News Service, is considered to be public information. For B, the firm has engaged in a wash trade and D is an example of an abusive squeeze, both of which is a breach of the Market Abuse Directive. Market abuse does not need to prove intent, so C may also be a breach under the Market Abuse Regulation."},{"question":"In relation to money laundering, which of the following is FALSE?","options":["Money laundering offences are defined by the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Identification procedures are generally required for anyone wishing to engage in new business","The offences are not confined to the financial services industry","Firms have to report money laundering suspicions to the Financial Action Task Force (FATF)"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FATF is an inter-governmental policy making body. In the UK, suspicions are reported to NCA."},{"question":"Inside information could be which of the following?","options":["Information which, if made public, could significantly affect the share price","Specific information on a takeover which has been printed in a newspaper","General information that a company is doing well","Public information announced by a shareholder"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Information which is not public, but would affect the share price if it was, would be classified as inside information."},{"question":"The FCA is pursuing an investigation into insider dealing. Which of the following would provide the accused with a special defence under the Criminal Justice Act against an accusation?","options":["Did not expect the recipient to deal","Market maker acting in ordinary course of business","Believed the information was published","Would have dealt anyway"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The other three are defined as general defences against insider dealing."},{"question":"Insider trading legislation does not cover which ONE of the following?","options":["Bond derivatives","Contract for difference on equity","Debentures","Forward foreign exchange contracts"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Insider trading only covers equity, debt, related derivatives and contracts for difference."},{"question":"Which of the following is true regarding money laundering?","options":["Placement and integration are the two stages in the process","All suspicions should be reported by employees to the MLRO","All suspicions must be reported by the MLRO to NCA","The role of MLRO is a certification function"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The three stages include layering. The MLRO has discretion to decide which suspicions are reported by the firm to NCA. The MLRO is a senior management function."},{"question":"Which two of the following are FALSE of the Money Laundering Regulations?\nIFailure to report a suspicion is a criminal offence\nIIFirms must appoint a Money Laundering Reporting Officer\nIIINot reporting when there are 'reasonable grounds' to suspect money laundering exists is not an offence\nIVThe regulations only apply to drugs laundering operations","options":["I and II","I and III","III and IV","II and IV"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The requirement (under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002) to report when 'reasonable grounds' exist places an objective test of suspicion on the financial services industry."},{"question":"Which organisation would a Money Laundering Reporting Officer normally report suspicions of money laundering to?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","National Crime Agency","Joint Money Laundering Steering Group","Financial Action Task Force"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The MLRO must report any suspicions to the National Crime Agency."},{"question":"In relation to the insider dealing legislation which ONE of the following is a SPECIAL defence against insider dealing?","options":["The information was publicly available","The information was being passed on in the proper course of business","The person would have dealt anyway","The individual acted in accordance with the stabilisation rules"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The special defences are:\n1. Stabilisation rules\n2. Recipients of market information\n3. Market makers"},{"question":"If a chair of the board in a listed company wanted to deal in the shares of her own company, she would inform:","options":["The company","HM Treasury","The PRA","The ESMA"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"All directors must inform their company when they trade in their own company's shares. There is also a need to inform the FCA. This is a requirement of the MAR PDMR Regime."},{"question":"The MLRO is an officer of the firm with what characteristics?","options":["The authority to carry out the responsibilities","Complete independence from the company","Some information to allow a reasonable attempt at carrying out the relevant duties","The authority and sufficient independence within the firm to carry out the duty"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"'The authority to carry out the responsibilities' is partially true. Complete independence is not necessary for the MLRO and some information is insufficient.\n'The authority and sufficient independence within the firm to carry out the duty' is the best answer."},{"question":"Which of the following is least likely to supply information as an insider?","options":["A secretary","A technician","A director","A small shareholder"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All employees may be guilty of insider dealing. Major shareholders may also have access to inside, price-sensitive information, but small shareholders are unlikely to enjoy such access."},{"question":"Which of the following instruments is NOT within the scope of the Criminal Justice Act 1993 (Insider Dealing)?","options":["Building Society share account","Warrant","Equity future","Gilt"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A building society share account would not be covered as it is not a MiFID instrument."},{"question":"Which of the following is TRUE of the Money Laundering Regulations 2017?","options":["All firms subject to the regulations must appoint a Money Laundering Reporting Officer","A firm cannot be prosecuted under the regulations if a clear audit trail has been documented","Records of customer identification must be kept for three years after the person ceases to be a customer","Disciplinary sanctions under the regulations are determined by the FCA"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The legislation and sanctions were written by HMT.\nRecords must be kept for five years."},{"question":"Which statement correctly describes accepted market practices under UK Market Abuse Regulation in the UK?","options":["They are established by trading venues for each instrument without regulatory approval","They are established by issuers for liquid securities with regular institutional trading","They are established by investment firms where clients have given prior written consent","They are established by the Financial Conduct Authority, although none currently exist"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The source states that the FCA is responsible for assessing and establishing accepted market practices in the UK market, but currently no accepted market practices have been established. The other options assign that role to bodies not identified in the source."},{"question":"Why does a firm have a written money laundering policy?","options":["So that management can identify specific customer investment requirements","To ensure that there is a framework with which to deal with money laundering","Only where enhanced due diligence is required due to the sensitive nature of the firm's client","To set out specific details in relation to the legislation provided in the Criminal Justice Act"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms must have an anti-money laundering policy statement in place; this provides a framework to the firm and its staff, and must identify named individuals and functions responsible for implementing particular aspects of the policy. The policy must also set out how senior management undertakes its assessment of the money laundering and terrorist financing risks the firm faces, and how these risks are to be managed."},{"question":"Primarily what kind of offence is insider dealing?","options":["Civil","Common law","Criminal","Tort"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Insider dealing is primarily a criminal offence as defined under the Criminal Justice Act. Where the regulator is unable to prove criminality, it has the ability to fall back on the civil offence of Market Abuse."},{"question":"The purpose of the Joint Money Laundering Steering Group (JMLSG) is:","options":["To provide firms with guidance in respect of money laundering regulations","To provide mandatory strategies on how to apply the money laundering regulations","To specify to the courts the penalties for money laundering offences","To instruct the FCA how to apply European money laundering legislation in the UK"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The purpose of the committee is to provide guidance in respect of the money laundering regulation. It is not mandatory, but can be used as a safe harbour against prosecution."},{"question":"Which TWO of the following would constitute insider dealing?\nIDealing on unpublished, price-sensitive information\nIIA director dealing in company shares immediately after the annual report is published\nIIIEncouraging another person to deal on unpublished, price-sensitive information\nIVBuying shares on rumours of a takeover","options":["I and IV","II and III","II and IV","I and III"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Dealing on, or encouraging another person to deal on, unpublished, price-sensitive information can constitute insider dealing. Note: if the annual results have been published, the information would not be classed as 'unpublished' any more. Also, 'rumours' are unlikely to constitute specific or precise inside information."},{"question":"To which of the following must a Money Laundering Reporting Officer report money laundering suspicions?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Action Task Force","National Crime Agency","Joint Money Laundering Steering Group"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"NCA stands for the National Crime Agency - a body that liaises with the police and customs in the investigation of money laundering and other criminal circumstances."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be committing an offence under the Criminal Justice Act 1993 if they purchased shares in ABC plc?","options":["An auditor to ABC who resigned three months ago who has confidential information on the company's results","A secretary to the office services manager in ABC who has seen an advance copy of an announcement to repurchase shares","A non-executive director of ABC who resigned seven months ago who expects profits to show an unexpected rise when published","A friend of the director of ABC who has obtained price-sensitive information from the director"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The non-executive director is not an insider since he does not have inside information from an inside source."},{"question":"Which of the following are TRUE of money laundering?\nIThere are three stages: placement, layering and integration\nIIAssisting a money launderer, failure to report and tipping off are all offences\nIIIThe firm must appoint an appropriate person to handle suspected cases\nIVRecords must be maintained for five years under the Money Laundering Regulations","options":["I, II and III","I, II, III and IV","I and IV","I, II and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Note: other procedures include internal controls and training of staff."},{"question":"To whom must the Money Laundering Reporting Officer report suspicious activities?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Action Task Force","The Board of Directors","National Crime Agency"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Any suspicions must be reported to the NCA as soon as is reasonably practicable. Any weakness in internal controls must be submitted to the firm's directors on an annual basis."},{"question":"Which of the following would be committing an offence under the Criminal Justice Act 1993 if they purchased shares in ABC plc?","options":["A market maker who obtained price-sensitive information in the course of his business and the information is of a kind it would be reasonable to expect him to obtain","A director of ABC plc with price-sensitive information who undertook the transaction without intending to make a profit or to avoid a loss","A friend of a director of ABC plc who obtained information but was unaware that it was price sensitive","None of the above"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An insider must knowingly have inside information (i.e. price sensitive and not public) from a known inside source. If there is no intent, then there is no offence. Being a market maker is an acceptable defence."},{"question":"Which of the following is untrue of the FCA rules and the legislation on money laundering?","options":["As part of the FCA's operational objective to protect and enhance market integrity, it must make sure that firms take adequate steps to prevent and detect money laundering","A firm does not have to identify a client if the transaction is one off and for €30,000","A firm must provide training for its staff to recognise suspicious transactions","A firm is required to retain documentation for a period of five years from the date that the client ceases to be a client of the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"B is false. One off transactions above €7500 require identification."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is a defence against S89-91 of Financial Services Act 2012 - making misleading statements or impressions?","options":["Market maker acting in good faith","Adherence to Stabilisation Rules","Adherence to conflicts of interest guidance","Adherence to Takeover Code"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is a defence available if the statement is made in conformity with the price stabilisation rules of the FCA.\nActing in accordance with the Takeover Code would provide a defence against market abuse.\nThere is a market maker defence for insider dealing, but not for misleading statements and impressions."},{"question":"Which of the following are TRUE regarding insider dealing?\nIOffences are committed by individuals, not corporations\nIIEncouraging a third party to deal is an offence\nIIIUK equities and gilts and their derivatives are covered\nIVDisclosure of inside information, other than in the normal course of business, is an offence","options":["I, II, III and IV","I, II and IV","II and IV","I and III"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Insider dealing is a crime committed by INDIVIDUALS. Offences include dealing, encouraging and passing on information."},{"question":"Which of the following is FALSE with regard to the money laundering regulations?","options":["Failure to report suspicions is an offence under the regulations","A Money Laundering Reporting Officer is only required for large, complex organisations","Inadequate education of staff is an offence even if no money laundering transactions take place","Records of identification must be kept for five years after the termination of the relationship"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An MLRO is a required control function in all firms."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the role of the Joint Money Laundering Steering Group in the financial sector?","options":["A governmental body that produces statutory regulation for financial institutions on fulfilling their duties in relation to money laundering","The FCA division responsible for the monitoring of provisions for money laundering in financial institutions","An independent body that provides guidance for financial institutions on fulfilling their duties in relation to money laundering","A branch of the National Crime Agency responsible for investigating money laundering activities in financial institutions"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The guidance notes produced by the JMLSG are not statutory. However, they have been given approval by HM Treasury, so will be taken into account by any court prosecuting under POCA."},{"question":"With regard to money laundering, all suspicions should be reported by employees to the MLRO, who in turn reports to?","options":["FCA","NCA","FATF","JMLSG"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Each firm, no matter how small, must appoint a Money Laundering Reporting Officer (MLRO) to whom suspicions should be reported. The MLRO will then, if appropriate, speak to the relevant authority, which is the National Crime Agency (NCA).\nFATF stands for Financial Action Task Force. JMLSG stands for Joint Money Laundering Steering Group."},{"question":"Which of the following points is true in relations to the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002?","options":["All businesses must identify clients","It is secondary legislation","Money Laundering covers property related to all crimes","JMLSG takes precedence over POCA 02"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"POCA 2002 is widely drafted. It specifies that money laundering relates to criminal property – that is, any benefit (money or otherwise) that has arisen from criminal conduct."},{"question":"Graham has been accused of giving a misleading impression to the markets in the course of his professional activities. Which of the following would give him a defence under FSA 2012?","options":["Takeover rules","Price stabilisation rules","Stock lending rules","Underwriting rules"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This question is asking about the offence of misleading statements and impressions under FSA 2012. Price stabilisation (the artificial support of the share price) is a specific defence for these offences."},{"question":"A fund manager lets the market know that they are very keen to buy substantial quantities of shares in Benno plc, when actually they hold a smaller quantity of shares in Benno that they plan to sell. Which of the following criminal offences, under the Financial Services Act 2012, is the fund manager most likely to be guilty of?","options":["Manipulating devices","Misleading statements","Disclosing inside information","Tipping off"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The fund manager is giving a misleading statement. ss89 - 91 of the Financial Services Act 2012 covers misleading statements (s89) and impressions (s90) as well as misleading impressions in respect of benchmarks (s91).\n'Manipulating devices' is a type of market abuse (a civil offence), which is covered by the Market Abuse Regulation (MAR).\nInsider dealing is covered under s52 of the Criminal Justice Act 1993.\n'Tipping off' is a money laundering offence, covered under s333 of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT an offence under the insider dealing legislation?","options":["Passing on price-sensitive information","A director dealing in their own company's shares two months before announcement of results","Dealing on price-sensitive information","Encouraging someone to deal on price-sensitive information"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Directors are permitted to deal in their company's shares subject to:\n1. Proper disclosure.\n2. Not acting on inside information."},{"question":"For the purposes of money laundering if a client is acting on behalf of a third party, whose identity needs to be established?","options":["The client","The third party","Both the client and the third party","Neither the client nor the third party"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"It will be the responsibility of the client/agent to establish the identity of their own client."},{"question":"In respect of Money Laundering Regulations which of the following is NOT true?","options":["Once an employee has reported suspicions to their Money Laundering Reporting Officer they have a further duty of disclosure to the authorities","It is a criminal offence not to report suspicions of money laundering","It is a criminal offence not to have adequate monitoring and reporting systems to prevent money laundering","Money Laundering Regulations relate to all serious crimes such as drugs dealing, counterfeiting and extortion"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The money laundering officer, not the employee, has an obligation to report suspicions to the National Crime Agency."},{"question":"At which stage of the money laundering process are the proceeds of crime separated from their source?","options":["Layering","Integration","Identification","Placement"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Placement is the physical disposal of cash, layering separates proceeds from their source and integration provides apparent legitimacy to criminally derived wealth."},{"question":"Which of the following is a general defence to insider dealing?","options":["Stabilisation","Market makers acting in the normal course of business","Would have dealt anyway","Recipients of market information"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The other three are special or technical defences to insider dealing. These provide situations where individuals may legally possess price-sensitive non-public information. 'Would have dealt anyway' is a general defence (available to anyone)."},{"question":"Market abuse involving manipulating devices would cover:","options":["Life insurance","Private company shares","Unsecured personal loans","Shares in an investment trust"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Investment trust shares are secondary market traded and are therefore covered."},{"question":"For the Senior Management Arrangements, Systems and Controls with respect to money laundering, which one of the following is true?","options":["Appropriate training should be provided to employees who might be exposed to money laundering activities","The board can delegate their responsibilities for money laundering requirements to the MLRO","The board is required to monitor transactions for money laundering","A firm is required to provide the necessary systems and authority to the FCA appointed MLRO"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under SYSC, with respect to money laundering, there is a requirement that appropriate training be provided to staff.\nThe board appoints the MLRO, not the FCA."},{"question":"Market making is a specific defence against insider trading if the market maker:","options":["Has the permission of the FCA to trade","Acts in good faith","Does not expect to make a profit","Thought the information was publicly available"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Market makers acting in good faith is a specific defence.\nNot expecting to make a profit or believing the information was public are general defences.\n\nNOTE: the words special and specific are interchangeable."},{"question":"Which of the following is an insider according to the Criminal Justice Act 1993?","options":["A person who pays for inside information","An employee whose work involves inside information","A person who obtains inside information by observation","A person who obtains inside information by dealing in securities"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Answer D relates to a market maker in which there is a special defence. Answer C is not a breach of CJA 1993. Answer A is illegal but this does not make the buyer of the information an insider."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding market abuse?","options":["Intention does not need to be proved to impose disciplinary action","The offence only relates to dealings in securities traded on a UK market","The maximum penalty is ten years plus an unlimited fine","It applies only to UK approved persons"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"\"Market abuse is 'effects' based legislation and therefore intention does not need to be proved. It applies in relation to all qualifying investments traded on a prescribed market, i.e. RIE, ROIE, ISDX and all EEA regulated exchanges. Generally the FCA will normally only take action for offences in relation to UK markets. It is a civil offence and therefore does not carry a jail sentence. It applies to all persons, i.e. individuals and institutions whether they are located in the UK or overseas.\""},{"question":"A director of a company has recently informed a friend about a pending takeover of the company, telling them that since this information is not public, they can make a quick profit by buying the company's shares. The friend declines to trade on this information. Who has committed an offence under insider dealing legislation in the Criminal Justice Act?","options":["The director","The friend","Both","No one"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The friend did not trade on the information. Assuming that she/he did not pass on this information further, she did not commit a crime. The director committed a crime by disclosing insider information and encouraging someone to deal on that information."},{"question":"Under the Joint Money Laundering Steering Group Guidance Notes, which of the following would identify why enhanced due diligence is required for a politically exposed person?","options":["Due to their position, they are more vulnerable to corruption","They have the ability to withhold information","They live overseas","They work for an authorised firm"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The main factor used in identifying a politically exposed person (PEP) is their vulnerability to corruption. According to JMLSG Guidance this includes persons who are: heads of state, heads of government and ministers; members of parliaments; members of supreme courts, or of other high-level judicial bodies whose decisions are not generally subject to further appeal; members of courts of auditors or of the boards of central banks; ambassadors, and high-ranking officers in the armed forces; and members of the administrative, management or supervisory boards of State-owned enterprises.\nThese categories also include direct family members of the above, such as spouse, children and parents. They do not, however, include middle-ranking or more junior officials."},{"question":"Under the Money Laundering Regulations a firm must ensure that they have appropriate internal controls. One of these is the appointment of a money laundering reporting officer. Which of the following is not true of the MLRO?","options":["They fulfil a senior manager function within the firm","They are the internal and external point of contact for matters arising in relation to money laundering","They are responsible for taking reasonable steps to establish and maintain adequate arrangements for awareness and training","They are a member of the National Crime Agency"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The MLRO is an employee of the firm, and although they pass on suspicions to NCA they are not members of this agency."},{"question":"Inside information could be which of the following?","options":["Information which, if made public, could significantly affect the share price","Specific information on a takeover which has been printed in a newspaper","General information that a company is doing well","Public information announced by a shareholder"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Information which is not public, but would affect the share price if it was, would be classified as inside information."},{"question":"Which of the following are penalties available to the FCA for cases of market abuse?\nIRestitution\nIIUnlimited financial penalty\nIIIWithdrawal of approval\nIVIssue a public statement","options":["II only","I and II","II, III and IV","I, II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"As market abuse is a civil offence, prison sentences are not applicable, but all the regulatory actions mentioned are available to the FCA."},{"question":"Which of the following prosecutes insider dealers?","options":["London Stock Exchange","Department for Business and Trade","Treasury","Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FCA can bring prosecutions for insider dealing. The London Stock Exchange has an obligation to monitor transactions to identify potential cases of insider dealing."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding market abuse?","options":["Intention does not need to be proved","The offence only relates to dealings in securities traded on a UK market","The maximum penalty is ten years plus an unlimited fine","It applies only to UK approved persons"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Market abuse is 'effects' based legislation and therefore intention does not need to be proved.It applies in relation to all qualifying investments traded on a prescribed market, i.e. all EEA regulated exchanges. Generally the FCA will normally only take action for offences in relation to UK markets.It is a civil offence and therefore does not carry a jail sentence.It applies to all persons, i.e. individuals and institutions whether they are located in the UK or overseas."},{"question":"Which of the following are in the scope of the market abuse regime:\nIShares traded on AIM\nIICopper futures traded on the LME\nIIIShares on the Official List of the LSE\nIVGas oil futures traded on ICE Futures","options":["III","II, III and IV","I and III","I, II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Investments traded on recognised investment exchanges are covered under market abuse. LME, LSE and ICE are all recognised investment exchanges in the UK."},{"question":"Which of the following penalties are NOT available to the FCA for cases of market abuse?","options":["Restitution order","Unlimited financial penalty","A prison sentence","Prohibition orders"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"As market abuse is a civil offence, prison sentences are not applicable."},{"question":"Money laundering covers:","options":["All money","Only tangible assets derived from crime","All benefits of criminal activity","Only money from criminal activities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Proceeds of Crime Act (POCA) 2002 is widely drafted. It specifies that money laundering relates to criminal property – that is, any benefit (money or otherwise) that has arisen from criminal conduct. Property is only criminal property if the alleged offender knows or suspects it is criminal property."}],"examFocus":["<strong>Market abuse (civil) vs insider dealing (criminal)</strong> — market abuse never requires proving intent and can never result in imprisonment; insider dealing can.","Insider dealing legislation (Criminal Justice Act 1993) covers MiFID instruments — <strong>NOT currencies or forward FX contracts</strong>.","Special/specific defences to insider dealing: <strong>market makers acting in good faith, price stabilisation, recipients of market information (takeovers)</strong>. General defences (available to anyone): 'would have dealt anyway', 'no advantage was expected', 'did not expect the recipient to deal'.","Money laundering's 3 stages — <strong>placement → layering → integration</strong>. Banks are most vulnerable at placement; investment firms at layering/integration.","<strong>MLRO reports to the NCA</strong>, not the FCA, and must brief senior management/the board <strong>at least once every 12 months</strong>.","<strong>'Tipping off' carries up to 2 years; most other POCA money-laundering offences (arrangements, concealing, assisting) carry up to 14 years.</strong>","JMLSG guidance is <strong>not mandatory</strong> but following it is a strong defence — the FCA references it when assessing SYSC compliance.","<strong>Closed period = 30 calendar days before annual/interim results announcements</strong> under the PDMR regime — directors are restricted from dealing in own-company shares during this window (exceptions: employee share schemes with no change in beneficial ownership, severe financial hardship).","Chinese walls are a <strong>recognised, not mandatory,</strong> way to manage conflicts of interest — the FCA never requires them by name.","PEPs need <strong>enhanced due diligence because of vulnerability to corruption</strong>, not because they are presumed guilty of anything."]},{"id":"reg-ch4","number":4,"title":"Complaints and Redress","examWeight":"9 of 75 questions (~12%)","summaryHtml":"\n<h4>Chapter 4: Complaints and Redress — Night-Before Summary</h4>\n<p><strong>1. Consumer Rights (CRA 2015).</strong> The Consumer Rights Act 2015 consolidates consumer protection law in three parts: (i) rights/remedies for goods, digital content and services; (ii) unfair contract terms (the \"grey list\" is indicative, non-exhaustive); (iii) enforcement/miscellaneous. The FCA is a \"qualifying body\" under the Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999 and can seek undertakings or injunctions against unfair terms.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cancellation periods:</strong> life & pensions = 30 calendar days; cash ISAs, other non-life/pensions (advised or distance) = 14 calendar days.</li>\n<li>Firms must disclose cancellation rights in a durable medium before/at point of contract.</li>\n<li>Record retention: indefinite (pension transfers/opt-outs/FSAVCs); 5 years (life/pension products); 3 years (other).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Customer Complaints (DISP).</strong> Eligible complainants: consumers, micro-enterprises (&lt;10 staff, turnover/balance sheet ≤ €2m), charities (income &lt; £6.5m), trusts (net assets &lt; £5m), certain SMEs, guarantors. Firms must acknowledge, investigate, and send a final response or holding response <strong>within 8 weeks</strong> (15 business days, extendable to 35, for PSD/EMD complaints). Complainants have <strong>6 months</strong> from final response to refer to the FOS. Records: 5 years (MiFID/UCITS business), 3 years (other). Firms receiving 500+ complaints per 6-month period file enhanced returns.</p>\n<p><strong>3. FOS.</strong> Free to complainants; funded by firm levies and case fees. Compulsory jurisdiction (CJ) is automatic for authorised firms; Voluntary jurisdiction (VJ) is opt-in. <strong>Award limits (act/omission on/after 1 Apr 2025, complaint referred on/after 1 Apr 2025): £445,000</strong> (was £430,000 for acts from 1 Apr 2024); for acts before 1 April 2019, the limit is £200,000 (was £195,000). Limits are CPI-adjusted each 1 April. Super-complaints: FCA must respond within <strong>90 days</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>4. FSCS.</strong> Pays compensation when an authorised firm defaults. Key limits: <strong>deposits £110,000</strong> per person per firm (£220,000 joint; up from £85,000 from 1 Dec 2025); temporary high balances £1.4m for 6 months; <strong>investments £85,000</strong>; insurance 100% (compulsory/long-term/PI/death-incapacity) or 90% (other); pension provider failure = 100% uncapped; SIPP operator failure/bad pension advice = £85,000.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Data Protection.</strong> DPA 2018 replaced DPA 1998, implementing UK GDPR. 6 DPA principles: lawful/fair; specified purpose; adequate/relevant/not excessive; accurate; not kept longer than necessary; secure. Breach notification to ICO within <strong>72 hours</strong> of awareness where feasible; individuals informed \"without undue delay\" if high risk. ICO fines: higher maximum £17.5m or 4% global turnover; standard maximum £8.7m or 2%.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Whistleblowing.</strong> Based on PIDA 1998. Firms must appoint a <strong>whistleblowing champion</strong> (usually a NED, or senior manager/director for insurers), report to the board at least annually, and tell UK employees about FCA/PRA whistleblowing services. Certain firms (deposit-takers, Solvency II insurers) must appoint a champion under SM&CR.</p>\n","sections":[{"heading":"4.1 Consumer Rights","html":"\n<h4>4.1.1 Consumer Rights Act 2015 (CRA)</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Consumer Rights Act 2015 (CRA)</strong> is a UK law that consolidates consumer protection law for contracts involving goods, digital content and services. It gives consumers statutory rights and remedies, and reforms/consolidates the law on unfair terms in consumer contracts (Part 2). It aims to enhance consumer confidence and simplify enforcement.</p>\n<p>The Act has three parts:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Consumers' rights and remedies for supply of goods, digital content and services.</li>\n<li>Unfair terms in consumer contracts and notices.</li>\n<li>Miscellaneous/general provisions, including investigatory powers for consumer law enforcers and secondary ticketing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Under the CRA, the <strong>FCA has statutory power to challenge unfair terms</strong> in financial services consumer contracts. The FCA is a <strong>qualifying body</strong> under the Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999, meaning it can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>seek an undertaking from a firm to amend/remove an unfair term from future contracts, and</li>\n<li>apply to the court for an injunction to prevent a firm using/enforcing the term against existing customers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The unfair terms provisions protect consumers against unfair terms in contracts and notices, <strong>including individually negotiated terms</strong>. A term is unfair if, <strong>contrary to the requirement of good faith</strong>, it causes a <strong>significant imbalance</strong> in the parties' rights/obligations to the detriment of the consumer.</p>\n<p>The CRA contains an indicative, <strong>non-exhaustive \"grey list\"</strong> of terms that may be regarded as unfair. A term not on the grey list is not necessarily fair. Fairness is assessed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the nature of the subject matter of the contract, and</li>\n<li>all circumstances at the time the term was agreed and all other terms of the contract (or related contracts), including implied terms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The CRA requires terms to be <strong>transparent</strong>. A term failing the transparency requirement is not automatically unfair, but this must be assessed against all relevant factors, with the consequence that a term found unfair is not binding on the consumer.</p>\n\n<h4>4.1.2 Cancellation and Withdrawal Rights; Reflection Periods</h4>\n<p>Cancellation rights must be provided to retail customers transacting investment business. The rules apply to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>firms providing retail financial products based on designated investments or deposits, and</li>\n<li>firms entering into distance contracts with consumers relating to deposits or designated investments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Purpose: to give clients a chance to reconsider and cancel within a defined period. If exercised, the contract is terminated.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Product</th><th>Cancellation period</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Life and pension products</td><td><strong>30 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash deposit ISAs</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-life and pensions products (advised, non-distance)</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-life and pensions products (distance contracts)</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Example: an advised purchase of an authorised unit trust (AUT) or OEIC has a <strong>14-day</strong> cancellation right.</p>\n<p>The cancellation period starts from the day the contract concludes, or the day the client receives contractual information, if later.</p>\n<p>Before (or, if not possible, immediately after) the consumer is bound, firms must tell consumers, in a durable medium, of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the existence of the cancellation/withdrawal right</li>\n<li>its duration</li>\n<li>the conditions for exercising it (including any amount payable)</li>\n<li>what happens if not exercised, and</li>\n<li>practical details of how to exercise it (including the address for notification).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This disclosure is not required if the consumer already receives similar information under COBS distance marketing/product information rules. Notification of cancellation is deemed effective if dispatched before the deadline expires; no reason needs to be given.</p>\n<p><strong>Record-keeping retention:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Indefinitely</strong> — pension transfers, pension opt-outs, FSAVCs.</li>\n<li><strong>At least 5 years</strong> — life policy, pension contract, personal pension scheme, or stakeholder pension scheme.</li>\n<li><strong>At least 3 years</strong> — any other case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On cancellation of an investment (e.g. a unit trust/UCITS fund), the client may not get back their full original investment — they receive the sale proceeds of their holding, which reflects market price movement.</p>\n<p>Source: FCA Website (Conduct of Business Sourcebook).</p>\n"},{"heading":"4.2 Customer Complaints","html":"\n<h4>4.2.1 Eligible Complainants</h4>\n<p>Access to the FOS requires (a) the complaint falls within FOS jurisdiction, and (b) the complainant is an <strong>eligible complainant</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Consumers</strong> — any person using or contemplating using services of an authorised firm/appointed representative.</li>\n<li><strong>Micro-enterprises</strong> — fewer than <strong>10 employees</strong> and turnover/balance sheet ≤ <strong>€2 million</strong> at time complaint raised.</li>\n<li><strong>Charities</strong> with annual income &lt; <strong>£6.5 million</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trustees of a trust</strong> with net asset value &lt; <strong>£5 million</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Certain SMEs</strong> — annual turnover &lt; £6.5 million and balance sheet &lt; £5 million, or fewer than 50 employees.</li>\n<li>A person acting for purposes outside their business/profession, or a borrower under a buy-to-let credit agreement (may include ECP/professional clients in some cases).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The complaint must arise from a business relationship with the firm (customer, beneficial interest in a pension scheme, policyholder). Personal guarantors of business loans also have FOS rights. Other authorised firms are generally NOT eligible complainants, unless the complaint concerns an activity for which they hold no permission themselves. ECPs/professional clients (other than pension scheme trustees) are generally not eligible, unless the complaint concerns activity outside their ECP/professional classification.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2 Complaints Procedures (DISP)</h4>\n<p>Firms must have <strong>appropriate written procedures</strong> for handling expressions of dissatisfaction from eligible complainants (firms may extend these to other complainants voluntarily). Procedures apply whether the complaint is oral/written and justified/unjustified, as long as it relates to provision (or failure to provide) a financial service. Procedures must cover: receiving, acknowledging, responding to, and investigating complaints, and notifying complainants of FOS rights.</p>\n<p>The Complaint Handling Rules are in <strong>DISP 1 (\"Treating Complainants Fairly\")</strong> of the FCA Handbook.</p>\n<p><strong>Consumer Awareness Rules (DISP 1.2):</strong> firms must publish a summary of their internal complaints process and FOS referral rights; refer complainants in writing to these summary details at/immediately after point of sale; provide the summary in writing on request and when acknowledging a complaint.</p>\n<p><strong>Complaint Handling Rules (DISP 1.3):</strong> require effective, transparent procedures for reasonable/prompt handling, free of charge, allowing complaints via any reasonable means. Firms must identify/remedy recurring or systemic issues via:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identifying root causes of complaints (including by product/service)</li>\n<li>analysing complaints resolved within one business day of receipt</li>\n<li>considering whether root causes affect other processes/products</li>\n<li>correcting root causes and prioritising action where reasonable</li>\n<li>regular reports to senior management on recurring/systemic problems</li>\n<li>keeping records of the analysis and senior management's response.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Complaints Resolution Rules (DISP 1.4):</strong> firms must investigate competently, diligently and impartially, obtaining further information as needed, and assess fairly, consistently and promptly: the subject matter; whether to uphold; appropriate remedial action/redress; and whether another firm may be jointly/solely responsible. Relevant factors: all evidence/circumstances; similarities with other complaints; FCA/regulator/FOS guidance; analysis of FOS decisions. The firm must explain its decision fairly, clearly, and not misleadingly, and comply promptly with any accepted redress offer.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.1 Stages and Timings</h4>\n<p>On receipt of a complaint, a firm must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>send a <strong>prompt written acknowledgement</strong></li>\n<li>keep the complainant regularly informed of progress, and</li>\n<li>within <strong>8 weeks</strong>, send a written response — either a <strong>final response</strong> or a <strong>holding response</strong> (different timescales apply to PSD/EMD complaints — see 4.2.2.4).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.2 Record Keeping and Reporting</h4>\n<p>Records must capture: the complainant's name; the substance of the complaint; and the resolution measures taken.</p>\n<p><strong>Retention:</strong> minimum <strong>5 years</strong> for MiFID business/UCITS collective portfolio management complaints; <strong>3 years</strong> for all other complaints — from date of receipt.</p>\n<p>Exception (annual report only, instead of standard reporting) where a firm's permissions relate to: credit-related activities/operating an electronic lending system with revenue &lt; <strong>£5 million</strong>; or only regulated claims management activities or regulated funeral plan activities.</p>\n<p>Firms must report to the FCA: total complaints received; total closed; total upheld/outstanding at start of period; total redress paid.</p>\n<p>Firms receiving <strong>500 or more complaints</strong> in a 6-month reporting period must additionally report: cause of complaints; number closed/upheld within different time periods; redress paid (upheld vs not upheld); and contextual information.</p>\n<p>A complaint is \"closed\" when the firm sends a final response, or the complainant confirms in writing acceptance of an earlier response. Under the two-stage approach, a complaint is closed if the complainant does not respond to the written response within <strong>8 weeks</strong>. The FCA publishes quarterly statistical complaints data.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.3 Final Responses to Complainants</h4>\n<p>A final response must do one of: accept the complaint and offer redress/remedial action; offer redress/remedial action without accepting; or reject the complaint with reasons. It must also inform the complainant that if still dissatisfied, they may refer to the FOS within <strong>6 months</strong> of the final response, and include the FOS leaflet.</p>\n<p>If unable to send a final response within 8 weeks, the firm must send a written response explaining why and indicating when the final response will follow, inform the complainant they may now refer to the FOS, and include the FOS leaflet.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.4 PSD/EMD Complaints</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Payment Services Regulations 2017</strong> (implementing the PSD, via HM Treasury; FCA is the nominated regulator) introduced different timescales for PSD/EMD complaints, implemented into <strong>DISP 1.6.2A</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Final response due by end of <strong>15 business days</strong> after receipt.</li>\n<li>In exceptional circumstances: send a holding response by end of the 15th business day (explaining the delay and a deadline), then send the final response by end of the <strong>35th business day</strong> after receipt.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>EMD complaint</strong> definition (FCA Handbook): any oral/written expression of dissatisfaction from/on behalf of an e-money holder about provision/failure to provide a financial service or redress determination, alleging financial loss/material distress/inconvenience, concerning rights/obligations under Part 5 of the Electronic Money Regulations.</p>\n<p><strong>PSD complaint</strong> definition: similarly, concerning rights/obligations under Parts 6 and 7 of the Payment Service Regulations.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.5 Improving Complaints Handling</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Next business day\" rule: firms may handle complaints informally (no acknowledgement letter needed) if resolved within <strong>3 business days</strong> of receipt.</li>\n<li>All complaints must be reported to the regulator, including those resolved within 3 business days.</li>\n<li>A \"new complaints\" return sent <strong>twice yearly</strong>, detailing complaints received.</li>\n<li>A <strong>summary resolution communication</strong> raising FOS awareness, sent following resolution by the third business day.</li>\n<li>Consumer call costs capped at the maximum basic rate.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>4.2.3 The Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS)</h4>\n<p>Under FSMA, the FCA has power to make complaint-handling rules; the independent dispute resolution body is the <strong>FOS</strong> (operated by Financial Ombudsman Service Ltd). It provides a free, impartial, efficient alternative to the courts, aiming for fair and reasonable outcomes. The FOS does not replace the courts — complainants may still take civil action, unless they have accepted the FOS's final decision. It is free to complainants; firms pay an annual <strong>levy</strong> plus <strong>case fees</strong> per accepted/investigated case.</p>\n<p><strong>Jurisdictions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Compulsory Jurisdiction (CJ):</strong> complaints from eligible complainants against FCA-authorised firms regarding regulated (and ancillary) activities unresolved within the firm's own timescales. Authorised firms are <strong>automatically</strong> subject to CJ.</li>\n<li><strong>Voluntary Jurisdiction (VJ):</strong> covers complaints outside CJ; firms opt in via contractual arrangement, becoming \"VJ participants\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>FOS Awards — compensation limits (fair compensation, excludes interest on award/costs and interest on costs):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Date complaint referred to FOS</th><th>Limit</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Act/omission before 1 April 2019 (any referral date)</td><td>£195,000 (referrals before rise) / £200,000 (on/after 1 Apr 2025)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Act/omission on or after 1 April 2019, referral on/after 1 April 2024</td><td>£430,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Act/omission on or after 1 April 2019, referral on/after 1 April 2025</td><td>£445,000</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Key principle: the applicable limit depends on <strong>both</strong> the date of the act/omission complained about <strong>and</strong> the date the complaint is referred to the FOS. Limits are adjusted automatically each <strong>1 April</strong> in line with <strong>CPI</strong> for the preceding January. Once the FOS adjudicates, it asks the complainant to accept or reject; if accepted, the decision is <strong>final and binding on both parties</strong>.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.4 Super-Complaints and Mass-Detriment References</h4>\n<p><strong>Super-complaints:</strong> certain designated consumer bodies (designated by HM Treasury via primary legislation) may raise competition/consumer issues with the FCA where a UK market feature(s) appears to be significantly harming consumer interests. The <strong>FCA must respond within 90 days</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mass-detriment</strong> = large-scale damage, harm or loss to consumers. A <strong>mass-detriment reference</strong> can be made by the FOS or a regulated firm where either: (1) there appear to be regular regulatory failures causing/risking consumer loss remediable by legal proceedings; or (2) a firm has regularly acted in a way that, if complained about, would likely be upheld by the FOS with an award made.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.5 Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS)</h4>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> pays compensation to eligible claimants when an authorised firm defaults (insurance cover exhausted, firm insolvent/unable to pay claims). Funded by a levy on all authorised firms.</p>\n<p><strong>Excluded from claiming (not eligible claimants):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>other authorised firms; overseas financial institutions</li>\n<li>CISs, their trustees and operators</li>\n<li>some pension/retirement funds (subject to exceptions below)</li>\n<li>supranational institutions, governments, central administrative authorities</li>\n<li>provincial/regional/local/municipal authorities</li>\n<li>directors/managers of the defaulting firm and relatives (unless small mutual association with unsalaried directors, or a credit union)</li>\n<li>group companies of the defaulting firm (unless also a trustee of certain pension schemes)</li>\n<li>persons holding ≥5% of the defaulting firm's capital (or group firm's capital)</li>\n<li>auditors of the defaulting firm</li>\n<li>anyone who contributed to the firm's default</li>\n<li>large companies, large mutual associations, large partnerships</li>\n<li>persons convicted of money laundering in connection with the claim</li>\n<li>claims under the Third Parties (Rights against Insurers) Act 1930.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exceptions that ARE eligible</strong> (despite being authorised firms), if the claim arises from an activity for which they lack Part 4A permission: sole trader firms; credit unions; trustees/operators of stakeholder/personal pension schemes; small businesses (turnover &lt; <strong>£1 million</strong>). Trustees of small self-administered/occupational pension schemes (where employer is not large) are also eligible.</p>\n<p><strong>Protected claims relate to:</strong> deposits; contracts of insurance; protected investment business; claims against the manager/trustee of an AUT or the ACD/depositary of an ICVC (claim made by unit-holder). Claims against a UK-authorised unregulated CIS operator are eligible (operating an unregulated CIS is designated investment business).</p>\n<p><strong>Compensation limits (per eligible person, per authorised firm, per product category):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Product</th><th>Limit</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Deposits (banks/building societies/credit unions, failure after 1 Dec 2025)</td><td><strong>£110,000</strong> per person, per licence (£220,000 for joint accounts). Increased from £85,000 (1 Dec 2025).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Temporary high balances (e.g. property sale, inheritance)</td><td><strong>£1.4 million</strong> for 6 months (increased from £1m, 1 Dec 2025)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance — compulsory, long-term, professional indemnity, death/incapacity due to sickness/injury (failure after 8 Oct 2020)</td><td><strong>100%</strong> protected, no upper limit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance — all other kinds</td><td><strong>90%</strong> protected</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance brokers/financial advisers — compulsory general insurance (no time limit)</td><td><strong>100%</strong> protected</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance brokers/financial advisers — other general insurance</td><td><strong>90%</strong> protected</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Investments (failure after 1 Apr 2019)</td><td><strong>£85,000</strong> per person, per firm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pensions — pension provider fails</td><td><strong>100%</strong> of claim, no upper limit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pensions — SIPP operator fails</td><td><strong>£85,000</strong> per person, per firm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pensions — bad pension advice</td><td><strong>£85,000</strong> per person, per firm</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Compensation covers financial loss only — for investment claims, it aims to restore the individual to the position they would have been in had they not invested.</p>\n"},{"heading":"4.3 Data Protection","html":"\n<h4>4.3.1 The Six Principles of the Data Protection Act 2018</h4>\n<p>The original <strong>Data Protection Act 1998</strong> governed how personal data must be handled to protect individuals' rights (personal data = data relating to identifiable living individuals). Following the EU <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)</strong> in 2018, the DPA 1998 was repealed and replaced by the <strong>Data Protection Act 2018 (DPA 2018)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The DPA 2018 has a wider remit than the GDPR:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>covers processing outside EU law scope (e.g. immigration), applying adjusted GDPR standards;</li>\n<li>transposes the EU Law Enforcement Directive 2016/680 into domestic law — <strong>Part 3</strong> covers processing for criminal \"law enforcement purposes\";</li>\n<li>covers national security (outside EU law scope), based on Council of Europe Data Protection Convention 108, applying to intelligence services;</li>\n<li>sets out the <strong>Information Commissioner's Office (ICO)</strong>'s duties, functions, powers, and enforcement provisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The 6 DPA 2018 principles</strong> — personal data must be:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>processed lawfully and fairly</li>\n<li>processed for purposes that are specified, explicit and legitimate</li>\n<li>adequate, relevant and not excessive</li>\n<li>accurate and kept up to date</li>\n<li>kept for no longer than necessary</li>\n<li>processed in a secure manner.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>4.3.1a Purpose and the ICO</h4>\n<p>ICO mission: \"to uphold information rights in the public interest, promoting openness by public bodies and data privacy for individuals.\" This covers safeguarding/empowering individuals, promoting transparency, ensuring compliance with data protection/e-privacy law. <strong>Data controllers</strong> (entities that collect/process personal data) must register with the ICO.</p>\n\n<h4>4.3.2 UK GDPR and Data Subject Rights</h4>\n<p>Post-Brexit, GDPR requirements were retained in domestic law as the <strong>UK GDPR</strong>; high-level requirements are unchanged.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scope:</strong> UK GDPR applies to both controllers AND processors (DPA applies to controllers only). It also covers overseas controllers/processors whose activities relate to offering goods/services to, or monitoring the behaviour of, UK data subjects. Data may be exported outside the UK subject to UK GDPR conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Accountability:</strong> controllers must be able to demonstrate compliance.</li>\n<li><strong>DPOs:</strong> mandatory for certain organisations (e.g. public authorities, or bodies that regularly/systematically monitor data subjects on a large scale) to appoint a <strong>Data Protection Officer</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data protection principles (UK GDPR):</strong> lawfulness; fairness and transparency; purpose limitation; data minimisation; accuracy; storage limitation; accountability.</li>\n<li><strong>Consent:</strong> must be freely given, specific, informed, and an unambiguous indication of wishes (statement or clear affirmative action); purpose-limited; distinguishable in a written declaration and intelligible/plain language; must be as easy to withdraw as to give. Criminal convictions/offences are NOT considered \"sensitive data\" under UK GDPR.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key rights of individuals:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right to be Informed</strong> — about collection/use, purposes, categories of data, recipients, retention periods.</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Rectification</strong> — correct inaccurate/incomplete data.</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Erasure</strong> (\"right to be forgotten\") — deletion of personal data in certain circumstances (builds on the CJEU-established principle).</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Restrict Processing</strong> — e.g. while accuracy is verified, or where objecting to processing.</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Data Portability</strong> — obtain/reuse personal data in a commonly used, machine-readable format to transmit to another controller.</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Object</strong> — no general right to object, but can object to processing based on legitimate interests/public tasks.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights related to Automated Decision-Making</strong> — right to understand logic and contest decisions made solely by automated means.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Data Security:</strong> controllers/processors must implement appropriate technical/organisational measures proportionate to risk (e.g. pseudonymisation, encryption, confidentiality of systems, regular testing).</p>\n<p><strong>Fines under UK GDPR:</strong> up to <strong>4% of annual worldwide turnover or £17.5 million</strong> (whichever greater) for breaches of certain provisions; up to <strong>2% or £8.7 million</strong> (whichever greater) for other specified breaches.</p>\n\n<h4>4.3.3 Breach Notification and Reporting</h4>\n<p>The ICO can investigate, take enforcement action and impose penalties for data breaches. <strong>Part 3 of the DPA 2018</strong> requires organisations to report certain personal data breaches to the ICO <strong>within 72 hours</strong> of becoming aware, where feasible. If likely to result in high risk to individuals' rights/freedoms, firms must also inform affected individuals <strong>without undue delay</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Personal data breach</strong> = a breach of security leading to accidental/unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorised disclosure of, or access to, personal data (more than just losing data).</p>\n<p><strong>Notify the ICO only if</strong> the breach is likely to result in a risk to individuals' rights and freedoms (e.g. discrimination, reputational damage, financial loss, loss of confidentiality/other economic or social disadvantage).</p>\n<p><strong>Report to ICO must include:</strong> nature of the breach (categories/approx. numbers of individuals and records concerned); DPO/contact point details; likely consequences; measures taken/proposed to address and mitigate.</p>\n<p><strong>Informing individuals:</strong> required without undue delay if <strong>high risk</strong> (a higher threshold than for notifying the ICO). Not required if: appropriate technical/organisational measures were applied (e.g. encryption); subsequent measures mean high risk is no longer likely; or it would involve disproportionate effort (in which case a public communication or similarly effective alternative is required). Information may be restricted where necessary/proportionate to avoid obstructing legal inquiries, prejudicing criminal investigations/prosecutions, or to protect public/national security or others' rights and freedoms.</p>\n<p><strong>Individual notification must include:</strong> nature of breach; DPO/contact details; likely consequences; measures taken/proposed.</p>\n<p><strong>ICO factors favouring a financial penalty:</strong> nature of personal data; duration/extent of contravention; number of individuals affected; importance/value/extent of breach; public importance; whether deliberate/premeditated; whether the controller ignored relevant advice; inadequate procedures/policies/practices.</p>\n<p><strong>Factors against a penalty:</strong> controller already complied with another regulator's requirements; contravention caused by circumstances outside the controller's direct control.</p>\n<p><strong>Two tiers of ICO penalty:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Higher maximum:</strong> £17.5 million or 4% of total annual worldwide turnover (preceding financial year), whichever is higher — applies to breaches of data protection principles, individual rights, or international data transfers.</li>\n<li><strong>Standard maximum:</strong> £8.7 million or 2% of total annual worldwide turnover, whichever is higher — applies to infringements of other provisions (e.g. administrative requirements).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Breaches can also lead to <strong>criminal prosecution</strong>, potentially with unlimited fines.</p>\n"},{"heading":"4.4 Whistleblowing","html":"\n<h4>4.4.1 Legal and Regulatory Basis for Whistleblowing</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998</strong> protects individuals from retaliation when they inform regulatory authorities of workplace concerns (\"whistleblowing\"). In 2013, the <strong>Parliamentary Commission on Banking Standards (PCBS)</strong> recommended banks put in place internal mechanisms for raising concerns and appoint a senior person responsible for their effectiveness.</p>\n<p><strong>Key whistleblowing rules require firms to:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>appoint an individual of sufficient seniority as their <strong>whistleblowing champion</strong> (FCA expects most firms to appoint a <strong>non-executive director (NED)</strong>, or a senior manager if there is no NED; insurers should apportion the role to a director or senior manager)</li>\n<li>put in place internal whistleblowing arrangements able to handle all types of disclosure from all types of individuals</li>\n<li>include text in settlement agreements explaining workers' legal right to blow the whistle</li>\n<li>tell UK-based employees about the FCA and PRA whistleblowing services</li>\n<li>present a report on whistleblowing to the board at least <strong>annually</strong></li>\n<li>inform the FCA if the firm loses an employment tribunal brought by a whistleblower</li>\n<li>require appointed representatives/tied agents to tell their UK-based employees about the FCA whistleblowing service.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>4.4.2 Key Regulatory Requirements (FCA/PRA)</h4>\n<p>FCA/PRA whistleblowing rules require firms to establish, implement and maintain appropriate, effective arrangements to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>handle disclosures effectively, including where confidentiality/anonymity is requested, and allow disclosure via a range of communication methods</li>\n<li>ensure effective assessment and escalation of reportable concerns, including to the FCA/PRA where appropriate</li>\n<li>include reasonable measures to prevent victimisation of whistleblowers by anyone under the firm's control</li>\n<li>provide feedback to whistleblowers where feasible/appropriate</li>\n<li>maintain: appropriate records of reportable concerns and their treatment/outcome; and up-to-date written procedures accessible to UK-based employees</li>\n<li>prepare: an annual report to the governing body/board on the operation/effectiveness of whistleblowing systems and controls (maintaining confidentiality of whistleblowers); and prompt reports to the FCA/PRA about each employment tribunal case lost where the claim was based on detriment (s.47B Employment Rights Act 1996) or unfair dismissal (s.103A Employment Rights Act 1996) for whistleblowing</li>\n<li>provide appropriate training for: UK-based employees; managers of UK-based employees (wherever based); and employees operating the firm's internal arrangements.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Firms' written procedures may note other appropriate routes exist (e.g. grievance/complaints procedures), but internal whistleblowing arrangements (SYSC 18.3.1R(2)) can be used after alternatives are exhausted. Firms may act against those making false/malicious disclosures.</p>\n<p><strong>Training for all UK-based employees should include:</strong> a statement the firm takes reportable concerns seriously; how to communicate concerns; examples of triggering events; examples of firm actions post-disclosure (incl. confidentiality protection); sources of external support (e.g. whistleblowing charities).</p>\n<p><strong>Training for managers (wherever based) should ensure they know:</strong> how to recognise a disclosure of a reportable concern; how to protect whistleblowers/confidentiality; how to give feedback where appropriate; steps to ensure fair treatment of anyone accused of wrongdoing; sources of internal/external advice.</p>\n\n<h4>4.4.3 Appointed Representatives and Tied Agents</h4>\n<p>Firms are encouraged to invite ARs/tied agents to adopt internal whistleblowing procedures. Firms must ensure their ARs/tied agents inform their UK-based employees (as workers) that they can make protected disclosures to the FCA, or the PRA if PRA-regulated.</p>\n\n<h4>4.4.4 Link to Fitness and Propriety</h4>\n<p>Evidence that a firm acted to the detriment of a whistleblower is regarded as serious by the regulator. It could call into question the firm's continuing satisfaction of <strong>Threshold Condition 5 (Suitability)</strong>, or an approved person's/SMR or Certification Regime employee's fitness and propriety status.</p>\n\n<h4>4.4.5 Whistleblowers' Champion</h4>\n<p>Under the <strong>SM&CR</strong>, certain firms (banks with deposit-taking permission, and insurance companies subject to Solvency II) must appoint a <strong>whistleblowers' champion</strong>. This person is responsible for ensuring/overseeing the integrity, independence and effectiveness of the firm's whistleblowing policies and procedures, including protecting whistleblowers from victimisation. The champion must have sufficient authority and independence, including access to appropriate resources (e.g. independent legal advice, training) and information. They need not have a day-to-day operational role and can be based anywhere, provided they can perform the function effectively and meet Senior Managers Regime criteria.</p>\n"}],"mcqs":[{"question":"Under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 (CRA), the FCA is designated as a 'qualifying body' under which piece of legislation, giving it power to challenge unfair contract terms?","options":["The Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977","The Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999","The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","The Consumer Credit Act 1974"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The CRA confers on the FCA the status of a 'qualifying body' under the Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999, allowing it to seek undertakings or injunctions against unfair terms. The other options are separate pieces of legislation not referenced for this specific power."},{"question":"A retail client receives a cancellation notice following an advised purchase of an authorised unit trust (AUT). Within how many days must they exercise their right to cancel?","options":["7 calendar days","14 calendar days","21 calendar days","30 calendar days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Non-life and pensions products (including advised AUT/OEIC purchases) carry a 14 calendar day cancellation period. 30 calendar days applies to life and pension products, not unit trusts."},{"question":"For how long must a firm retain records relating to a pension transfer where the client exercised cancellation rights?","options":["3 years","5 years","10 years","Indefinitely"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Records relating to pension transfers, pension opt-outs, or FSAVCs must be kept indefinitely. Life policies/pension contracts require 5 years, and other cases require 3 years."},{"question":"Which of the following would NOT normally be classified as an eligible complainant able to refer a complaint to the FOS?","options":["A consumer using the services of an authorised firm","A micro-enterprise with 8 employees and turnover of €1.5 million","A charity with an annual income of £8 million","A trustee of a trust with net assets of £4 million"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Charities are eligible complainants only if annual income is less than £6.5 million; a charity with £8 million income exceeds this threshold. The micro-enterprise (fewer than 10 employees, turnover under €2 million) and the trust (net assets under £5 million) both meet the eligibility thresholds."},{"question":"A firm receives a written complaint from an eligible complainant. Under DISP, within how many weeks must the firm send a final response or a holding response?","options":["4 weeks","6 weeks","8 weeks","12 weeks"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"DISP 1.4/1.6 require firms to send a final response or holding response within 8 weeks of receiving a complaint. This is the standard timescale; PSD/EMD complaints have a shorter 15-business-day timescale."},{"question":"A firm receives a complaint that falls within the definition of a PSD complaint under DISP 1.6.2A. Assuming exceptional circumstances apply and a holding response is sent, by when must the firm send its final response at the latest?","options":["By the end of the 15th business day","By the end of the 20th business day","By the end of the 35th business day","By the end of the 8th week"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Where exceptional circumstances prevent a final response within 15 business days, the firm sends a holding response by day 15 and must then send the final response by the end of the 35th business day following receipt of the complaint."},{"question":"Following a firm's final response to a complaint, within what period must the complainant refer the matter to the FOS in order to preserve their right to do so?","options":["3 months","6 months","9 months","12 months"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The final response must inform the complainant that they have 6 months from the date of the final response to refer the complaint to the FOS."},{"question":"A firm must retain records of a complaint relating to MiFID business for a minimum of how long from the date the complaint was received?","options":["1 year","3 years","5 years","7 years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Complaints relating to MiFID business (or UCITS collective portfolio management) must be retained for at least 5 years; all other complaints require 3 years."},{"question":"Under DISP, what additional reporting obligation applies to a firm that receives 500 or more complaints during a six-month reporting period?","options":["It must appoint a whistleblowing champion","It must provide enhanced information including cause of complaints, timescales for closure/upholding, redress paid, and context","It must automatically refer all complaints to the FOS","It becomes subject to the Voluntary Jurisdiction of the FOS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms receiving 500+ complaints in a six-month reporting period must provide enhanced data to the FCA covering cause, closure/upholding timescales, redress paid, and contextual information — beyond the basic totals all firms must report."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the difference between the FOS's compulsory jurisdiction (CJ) and voluntary jurisdiction (VJ)?","options":["CJ applies only to complaints over £100,000; VJ applies to smaller complaints","Authorised firms are automatically subject to CJ for regulated activities; VJ covers complaints outside CJ and requires a firm to opt in contractually","CJ is funded by complainants; VJ is free","VJ decisions are binding while CJ decisions are not"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Authorised firms are automatically subject to the FOS's compulsory jurisdiction for regulated (and ancillary) activities. The voluntary jurisdiction covers complaints falling outside CJ, and firms must opt in by entering a contractual arrangement with the FOS, becoming 'VJ participants'."},{"question":"A complaint is referred to the FOS on 15 May 2025, concerning an act or omission that occurred on 10 June 2019. What is the applicable FOS maximum award limit?","options":["£195,000","£200,000","£430,000","£445,000"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The act/omission occurred on or after 1 April 2019 and the complaint was referred on or after 1 April 2025, so the applicable limit is £445,000. The lower limits (£195,000/£200,000) apply only where the act/omission occurred before 1 April 2019."},{"question":"Within how many days must the FCA respond to a super-complaint raised by a designated consumer body?","options":["30 days","60 days","90 days","120 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA must respond to a super-complaint within 90 days of it being raised. HM Treasury, through primary legislation, determines which consumer bodies may make super-complaints."},{"question":"Which of the following persons would generally be EXCLUDED from claiming compensation from the FSCS?","options":["A sole trader firm claiming in respect of an activity for which it has no Part 4A permission","A director of the defaulting firm claiming in a personal capacity","A small business with turnover under £1 million","A trustee of a small self-administered pension scheme where the employer is not a large company"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Directors and managers of the defaulting firm (and their relatives) are generally excluded from FSCS compensation, unless the firm is a small mutual association with unsalaried directors, or a credit union. Sole traders, small businesses under £1m turnover, and trustees of small self-administered schemes are eligible claimants."},{"question":"A bank fails on 15 December 2025. What is the maximum FSCS protection available to a single eligible depositor with that bank?","options":["£85,000","£110,000","£220,000","£1.4 million"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For failures after 1 December 2025, the FSCS deposit protection limit increased from £85,000 to £110,000 per eligible person per firm/licence (£220,000 for joint accounts)."},{"question":"A client deposits £1.2 million temporarily following the sale of their house. Under the FSCS temporary high balances rules (post 1 December 2025), how much of this is protected, and for how long?","options":["£85,000, for 6 months","£1 million, for 6 months","£1.4 million, for 6 months","The full £1.2 million, indefinitely"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Temporary high balances (e.g. from a property sale or inheritance) are protected up to £1.4 million for a period of 6 months, following the increase from £1 million effective 1 December 2025."},{"question":"A SIPP operator fails after 1 April 2019. What is the maximum FSCS compensation payable per eligible person, per firm?","options":["£50,000","£85,000","£110,000","100% of the claim, uncapped"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If a SIPP operator fails, FSCS compensation is capped at £85,000 per eligible person per firm — the same limit as applies to bad pension advice and general investment claims. Only where the pension provider itself fails is compensation 100% uncapped."},{"question":"Under the FSCS rules for insurance firms failing after 8 October 2020, what proportion of a claim relating to compulsory insurance is protected?","options":["75%","90%","95%","100%"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Compulsory insurance, long-term insurance, professional indemnity insurance, and claims arising from death/incapacity due to sickness, injury or infirmity are 100% protected. All other kinds of insurance are 90% protected."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six principles of the Data Protection Act 2018?","options":["Personal data must be processed lawfully and fairly","Personal data must be adequate, relevant and not excessive","Personal data must be transferred outside the UK wherever technically possible","Personal data must be kept for no longer than is necessary"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"There is no DPA 2018 principle requiring data to be transferred outside the UK — this is not a principle at all. The six genuine principles cover lawfulness/fairness, purpose limitation, data minimisation ('adequate, relevant and not excessive'), accuracy, storage limitation, and security."},{"question":"A firm becomes aware of a personal data breach. Within what timeframe must it report the breach to the ICO, where feasible?","options":["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","7 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Part 3 of the DPA 2018 requires organisations to report notifiable personal data breaches to the ICO within 72 hours of becoming aware, where feasible."},{"question":"A firm suffers a data breach but the affected personal data was securely encrypted, meaning the breach is unlikely to result in high risk to individuals. What is the firm's obligation regarding notifying the affected individuals?","options":["It must still notify individuals within 72 hours","It has no duty to notify affected individuals directly, as one of the exemptions applies","It must notify the FOS instead of the individuals","It must notify individuals within 30 days regardless of risk"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The duty to inform individuals does not apply where appropriate technical/organisational measures (such as encryption) were applied to the affected data, meaning high risk is unlikely to materialise. The firm may still need to notify the ICO if there is a risk to rights and freedoms."},{"question":"Under UK GDPR, what is the 'higher maximum' fine the ICO may impose for breaches of the data protection principles?","options":["£8.7 million or 2% of annual worldwide turnover, whichever is higher","£17.5 million or 4% of annual worldwide turnover, whichever is higher","£20 million or 4% of annual worldwide turnover, whichever is lower","£17.5 million or 4% of UK turnover only, whichever is higher"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The higher maximum penalty under UK GDPR is £17.5 million or 4% of total annual worldwide turnover in the preceding financial year, whichever is higher, applying to breaches of core principles, individual rights, or international data transfer rules."},{"question":"Under UK GDPR, which of the following is a key difference in scope compared with the DPA?","options":["UK GDPR applies only to data controllers, while DPA applies to both controllers and processors","UK GDPR applies to both data controllers and processors, and can cover overseas entities offering goods/services to or monitoring UK data subjects","UK GDPR applies only within the UK, while DPA has extraterritorial effect","UK GDPR does not require consent to be freely given, unlike the DPA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"UK GDPR applies to both controllers and processors (unlike the DPA, which applies to controllers only) and has extraterritorial reach, covering overseas controllers/processors whose activities relate to offering goods/services to, or monitoring the behaviour of, UK data subjects."},{"question":"Who is generally expected by the FCA to be appointed as a firm's whistleblowing champion?","options":["The Chief Executive Officer","A non-executive director (or a senior manager if the firm has no NED); insurers should apportion the role to a director or senior manager","The Head of Compliance only","Any employee nominated by staff vote"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA expects most firms to appoint a non-executive director as their whistleblowing champion, or a senior manager where there is no NED. Insurers are expected to apportion the role to a director or senior manager."},{"question":"How frequently must a firm present a report on whistleblowing to its board?","options":["Monthly","Quarterly","At least annually","Only when a disclosure is received"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms must present a report on whistleblowing to the board at least annually, covering the operation and effectiveness of the firm's whistleblowing systems and controls while maintaining confidentiality of individual whistleblowers."},{"question":"What legislation originally introduced protection for individuals from retaliation for reporting workplace concerns to regulatory authorities (whistleblowing)?","options":["The Employment Rights Act 1996","The Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998","The Financial Services Act 2012","The Bribery Act 2010"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998 introduced legal protection for whistleblowers against retaliation. The Employment Rights Act 1996 is relevant to detriment/dismissal claims (s.47B and s.103A) but PIDA is the originating whistleblowing legislation."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"FSCS payout limits — investments vs deposits.","back":"Investments: £85,000 per person per firm. Deposits: £120,000 per person per firm (£240,000 for a joint account). The two numbers get swapped in wrong-answer options constantly — memorise which is which."},{"front":"Can an authorised firm ever claim on the FSCS?","back":"NO — authorised firms can never claim from the FSCS, regardless of the type of business (investments, deposits, or general insurance)."},{"front":"Who funds the FSCS, and who is it primarily accountable to?","back":"Funded by levies on authorised firms. Primarily accountable to the FCA and PRA (and indirectly to HM Treasury through the regulators)."},{"front":"What's the general time limit to bring an FSCS claim?","back":"Normally within 6 years of the event giving rise to the claim."},{"front":"Name the 4 categories of FOS-eligible complainants.","back":"Consumers; micro-enterprises (<10 employees, turnover/balance sheet ≤ €2m); charities with annual income < £6.5m; trustees of a trust with net assets < £5m."},{"front":"Are large corporates or authorised firms ever eligible to complain to the FOS?","back":"NO — large businesses/multinationals and authorised firms are never eligible complainants for FOS purposes."},{"front":"Small/medium enterprise FOS eligibility test — what are the alternative thresholds?","back":"Turnover < £6.5 million AND balance sheet < £5 million, OR fewer than 50 employees (any one of these routes qualifies)."},{"front":"Can a professional client or eligible counterparty ever use the FOS?","back":"Yes — IF the complaint concerns an activity OTHER than the one for which they were classified as professional/ECP. Classification for one purpose doesn't automatically exclude eligibility for another."},{"front":"What is a 'mass detriment reference', and who can make one?","back":"A reference to the FCA when there's evidence of regular firm failures to comply with regulatory requirements — made by the FOS or by authorised firms themselves. The FCA must respond within 90 calendar days."},{"front":"Complaints record-keeping — MiFID vs non-MiFID business.","back":"MiFID business: 5 years from date of RECEIPT of the complaint. Non-MiFID business: 3 years from date of receipt."},{"front":"How often must a firm report complaints statistics to the FCA?","back":"Every 6 months (twice a year) — even if the firm received ZERO complaints in that period."},{"front":"What must a firm's internal complaints procedure guarantee about who handles a complaint?","back":"Complaints must be handled by someone with sufficient experience/authority who was NOT involved in the original matter — impartiality is required."},{"front":"When must a firm send its final response to a complainant?","back":"Normally within 8 weeks of receiving the complaint. An acknowledgement letter should be sent promptly before that."},{"front":"What must a firm do if it can't resolve a complaint itself?","back":"Inform the client of their right to refer the complaint to the Financial Ombudsman Service and provide the explanatory FOS leaflet."},{"front":"What are the core UK GDPR / DPA 2018 data protection principles?","back":"Lawful & fair processing; purpose limitation; adequate/relevant/not excessive (data minimisation); accurate & kept up to date; not kept longer than necessary; processed securely."},{"front":"Who does a data controller report a data breach to?","back":"The Information Commissioner's Office (ICO) — NEVER the FCA, even for a financial-services data breach."},{"front":"When does the duty to inform INDIVIDUALS about a data breach NOT apply?","back":"If the data was securely encrypted, if the firm has taken measures removing the high risk, or if informing would involve disproportionate effort. (Notifying the ICO is a separate, lower-threshold duty that still usually applies.)"},{"front":"What powers does the ICO have beyond issuing fines?","back":"Serving enforcement notices where a data controller has contravened data protection principles. It does NOT impose custodial sentences."},{"front":"What does the Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998 actually protect?","back":"Protects workers from RETALIATION when they inform regulatory authorities of genuine workplace concerns — it does NOT create an obligation to disclose, and does NOT protect disclosures to the press specifically."},{"front":"Does whistleblower protection depend on where in the world the wrongdoing happened?","back":"NO — protection applies regardless of where the wrongdoing took place, as long as the worker is UK-based (or their manager is, for training purposes)."},{"front":"Can a settlement agreement stop an employee from whistleblowing?","back":"NO — firms must include wording confirming workers retain a legal right to blow the whistle; that right cannot be contracted away."},{"front":"What must firms appoint under the whistleblowing rules, and what's required of that role?","back":"A 'whistleblowing champion' of sufficient SENIORITY to oversee the integrity/independence/effectiveness of the firm's whistleblowing arrangements — plus an annual report to the board on their effectiveness."},{"front":"What does the Consumer Rights Act 2015 empower the FCA to do?","back":"Challenge/force firms to amend or remove UNFAIR TERMS in financial services consumer contracts, or apply to court for an injunction. It does NOT let the FCA impose criminal sanctions on directors for this."},{"front":"What is the CRA 'grey list', and is it exhaustive?","back":"An INDICATIVE, non-exhaustive list of potentially unfair contract terms — a term absent from the list can still be judged unfair."},{"front":"Can an individually-negotiated, transparent contract term still be unfair under the CRA?","back":"YES — transparency and individual negotiation do NOT automatically make a term fair. Fairness depends on whether it creates a significant imbalance to the consumer's detriment, contrary to good faith, given all the circumstances when agreed."},{"front":"Who administers the Freedom of Information Act 2000, and how does it relate to the ICO's other role?","back":"The Information Commissioner's Office — the same body also enforces the Data Protection Act. FOI gives the public access to info held by PUBLIC authorities."}],"cisiMcqs":[{"question":"In what circumstances could a claim on the Financial Services Compensation Scheme be made?","options":["When an authorised firm becomes insolvent","Exclusively in response to claims by retail clients","Breach of principles of business","As an alternative to mediation by the Financial Ombudsman Scheme"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Financial Services Compensation Scheme was established to provide compensation where authorised persons and appointed representatives are unable to satisfy claims against them. Although the scheme mainly applies to retail clients, it can also apply to professional clients and eligible counterparties."},{"question":"Gina and Tobias open a joint deposit account in the UK. If they wish to ensure that they do not lose any money on the deposit, which of the following is the maximum deposit they could make and still ensure this?","options":["Up to £350,000","Up to £240,000","Up to £170,000","Up to £120,000"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"£120,000 is the amount is guaranteed by the FSCS per person. A joint account held by two people will receive protection on twice that."},{"question":"For how many years must a firm keep information on a complaint for MiFID business?","options":["Three years from the date it receives the complaint","Three years from the date it resolves the complaint","Five years from the date it receives the complaint","Five years from the date it resolves the complaint"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The firm must make and retain records for a minimum of five years from the date of its receipt of the complaint with regard to MiFID business."},{"question":"If the Financial Ombudsman Service makes an award it can be for:\nIActual financial loss\nIIPain and suffering\nIIIDamage to reputation\nIVDistress or inconvenience","options":["I, and II","I and III","I, II, III and IV","II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FOS can make awards for a range of reasons including financial loss, pain and suffering, damage to reputation and distress or inconvenience."},{"question":"Which of the following is true in relation to the Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS)?","options":["Deposits are not covered by the scheme","The maximum payout for investment business is unlimited","Claims must be made within a set time limit","All eligible counterparties are covered"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A claim must be brought to the FSCS within a set time (normally within six years of the date on which the event giving rise to the claim occurred). The maximum payout for a claim against an investment firm (i.e. in relation to protected investment business) is £85,000. Separate FSCS arrangements exist to protect deposit accounts at UK authorised banks and policy holders of UK authorised insurers."},{"question":"How is the Financial Services Compensation Scheme funded?","options":["By investment businesses authorised under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","By contributions from the European Central Bank","By the Treasury","By a small levy on all stock market transactions"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Compensation Scheme is funded by authorised investment business in the UK."},{"question":"All of the following are true of the Financial Services Compensation Scheme in relation to investments, except:","options":["It applies to business conducted through the appointed representative of an FCA firm","For the scheme as a whole, the total payout for compensation in any one year is unlimited","The scheme is funded partly by government funds and partly by levies charged on FCA members","Each claim has a maximum payout of £85,000"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Financial Services Compensation Scheme is funded by levies on the authorised community only. The maximum claim for investments is now £85,000."},{"question":"If an insurer becomes insolvent, who would a small partnership apply to for compensation in light of a general insurance contract?","options":["FOS","FSCS","FCA","Bank of England"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Small partnerships can claim off the FSCS."},{"question":"Which of the following statements about a firm's internal complaints procedure is FALSE?","options":["The procedure must detail the method of response to complaints","It must cover how complaints will be investigated","Complaints must be dealt with by someone involved in the subject matter of the complaint","The complaints procedure must be in writing"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"It is important that complaints are dealt with by someone impartial and not involved in the same area."},{"question":"With whom must a processor of personal information register?","options":["Information Commissioner's Office","Financial Conduct Authority","Competition and Markets Authority","Department for Business Innovation and Skills"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Data Protection Act requires registration with the information commissioner."},{"question":"A firm argues that a disputed term cannot be challenged because it does not appear on the grey list. What is the best response?","options":["The term is fair because only grey-list terms can be challenged under the Act","The term is presumed fair unless the consumer proves actual financial loss","The term may still be unfair because the grey list is indicative and non-exhaustive","The term is enforceable because the grey list applies only to non-financial services"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The grey list is indicative and non-exhaustive, so a term not appearing on it may still be unfair. Fairness is determined by considering the subject matter, the circumstances when agreed and the other terms of the contract."},{"question":"If an individual is awarded money in respect of restitution, to whom is it paid first?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Services Compensation Scheme","Her Majesty's Treasury","Financial Ombudsman Service"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The money will be paid to the FCA first and then passed on to the individual concerned.\nIf the money had been in respect of compensation following a liquidation it would be paid out by the FSCS."},{"question":"How often must an authorised firm report details concerning complaints received, closed and outstanding to the FCA?","options":["Every eight weeks","Every 25 business days","Every six months","Annually"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This report must be filed even if the firm has received no complaints against it."},{"question":"Which of the following is not an obligation on a regulated firm when ensuring complaints receive a proper response?","options":["Establish an internal procedure to handle complaints","Send a copy of all complaints to the FCA","Remedy any legitimate grievances","Inform the client of the Financial Ombudsman Service"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The regulator does not need to receive copies of all complaints. However, a summary report on how complaints were handled does need to be sent."},{"question":"A manager based overseas supervises staff in the United Kingdom. Under the whistleblowing training requirements, what must the firm ensure?","options":["Training is required only for managers physically based in the United Kingdom","Training is required only if the manager is an approved person under the Senior Managers and Certification Regime","Training is optional where local employment law already covers protected disclosures","Training is provided to managers of United Kingdom-based employees wherever the manager is based"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The rules require appropriate training for UK-based employees and for managers of UK-based employees wherever the manager is based. This reflects the focus on protecting disclosures made by UK-based staff rather than the manager's location."},{"question":"If a firm receives and deals with a complaint with respect to non-MiFID business, how long is it necessary to keep the records of the complaint?","options":["Five years from the date of the resolution of the complaint","Three years from the receipt of the complaint","Five years from the date of the complaint","Three years from the date of the resolution of the complaint"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For non-MiFID business, the records are kept for at least three years."},{"question":"A firm settles an employment dispute with a worker and includes wording preventing the worker from contacting regulators. Which statement is most accurate?","options":["The clause is acceptable if the worker receives independent legal advice","The clause is acceptable if the concern does not involve consumer detriment","The firm must include wording explaining that workers have a legal right to blow the whistle","The firm must notify the Financial Conduct Authority only if the worker later makes a disclosure"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The whistleblowing rules require firms to put text in settlement agreements explaining that workers have a legal right to blow the whistle. A clause attempting to prevent contact with regulators would be inconsistent with that requirement."},{"question":"Under Data Protection Principle 1, information must be processed:","options":["Accurately and timely","Accurately and lawfully","Fairly and timely","Lawfully and fairly"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"There are a total of six enforceable principles of good practice contained in GDPR."},{"question":"Which of the following is an eligible complainant under the FCA regulations?","options":["A company that was considered to be a professional client at the time of problem","A private individual who has never worked in the financial sector","A FTSE 100 company with group turnover of more than £102.5 million","A CIS with balance sheet totals of more than €20 million and net turnover of €42 million"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The others are not considered eligible complainants. An eligible complainant is a small retail customer."},{"question":"If a firm receives a complaint with respect to MiFID business and deals with it within two weeks, how long is it necessary to keep the record of the complaint?","options":["Five years from the date of the resolution of the complaint","Three years from the date of the receipt of the complaint","Five years from the date of the receipt of the complaint","Three years from the date of the resolution of the complaint"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"With MiFID business, the records must be kept for at least five years."},{"question":"What is the maximum claim on the Financial Services Compensation Scheme in the event of an insolvency of a firm where the investor has lost money through protected investment business?","options":["£85,000","£50,000","£37,500","£120,000"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An investor can claim up to £85,000 from the FSCS for investments."},{"question":"An authorised firm has made a mass detriment reference to the FCA, because it feels that if the customer complaint was referred to the FOS, that the FOS is likely to find in favour of the complainant.\nWhat action, if any, is the FCA required to make?","options":["The FCA must respond within 90 calendar days to the firm","The FCA must respond within 90 business days to the firm","The FCA has no obligation to respond at all, and can refuse to look at the matter","The FCA will refer the case to the FOS, giving its input on the matter"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA must respond within 90 calendar days to the party making a mass detriment reference."},{"question":"Which of the following is the requirement concerning the holding of personal data under Principle 3 of the Data Protection Act 2018?","options":["Extensive, relevant and adequate","Comprehensive, not excessive and relevant","Adequate, relevant and not excessive","Extensive, adequate and comprehensive"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The correct words from Principle 3 of the Data Protection Act are 'adequate, relevant and not excessive'."},{"question":"Which of the following does not have any rights under the Financial Services Compensation Scheme?","options":["A retail client with an ISA","Trustee of a scheme with assets of less than £1 million","A small business with regard to a general insurance contract","An authorised firm with respect to general insurance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Authorised companies are not able to claim for general insurance contracts under FSCS."},{"question":"Which of the following is true in relation to the FCA's power to seek redress for consumers?","options":["Does not apply to breaches by market counterparties","Does not apply to transactions below £5,000","Is subject to a 28 day limit for the FCA to assess a claim","Includes breaches by unauthorised persons"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In areas such as market abuse and money laundering the FCA have the power to seek redress from both authorised and unauthorised persons if their behaviour has an impact on consumers."},{"question":"The Consumer Rights Act 2015 gives the FCA the power to do which of the following?","options":["To enforce criminal legislation, such as the Criminal Justice Act","To challenge unfair terms in financial services consumer contracts","To enforce civil legislation, such as the Market Abuse Regulation","To challenge unauthorised firms conducting regulated activities"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Consumer Rights Act 2015 allows the FCA to challenge unfair terms in financial services consumer contracts. \n\nThe Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 gives the FCA the power to do the other options."},{"question":"Which statement about transparency under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 is correct?","options":["A term that is transparent is automatically fair in all circumstances","A term that lacks transparency is necessarily unfair and void in every case","Transparency is relevant, but fairness must still be assessed in light of all relevant factors","Transparency applies only to notices and not to terms in consumer contracts"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The source states that the CRA requires terms to be transparent, but a term that fails the transparency requirement is not necessarily unfair by that fact alone. Fairness must be determined in the light of all relevant factors."},{"question":"Which of the following is UNTRUE in respect of the procedures of an authorised firm for dealing with client complaints?","options":["Firms must have adequate internal controls put in place","Firms must refer all complaints to the regulator","The firm must remedy legitimate grievances","The firm has a duty to inform clients of other avenues of complaint"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Although it is a requirement for relevant firms to 'report' data and statistics regarding numbers of complaints to the FCA, it is not a requirement to 'refer' complaints to the FCA. Internal complaints procedures must be in writing and cover the receipt of complaints, methods of response and how they will be investigated. Complaints should be handled by a member of staff with sufficient experience and authority, and who was not involved in the matter.\nIf a complaint cannot be resolved, the firm must inform the client about their rights to complain to the Financial Ombudsman and provide the client with the explanatory leaflet about the Financial Ombudsman Service."},{"question":"Which power does the Financial Conduct Authority have under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 in relation to unfair terms in financial services consumer contracts?","options":["It can impose criminal sanctions on directors who approve unfair terms","It can require immediate compensation to all affected consumers without court process","It can seek an undertaking to amend or remove an unfair term from future contracts","It can cancel any consumer contract automatically once a term is found to be unfair"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA may seek an undertaking from a firm that it will amend or remove an unfair contract term from future consumer contracts. It may also apply to the court for an injunction, but the source does not state the broader powers described in the other options."},{"question":"If a complaint has not been resolved by a firm who should the complainant turn to next?","options":["FCA","Financial Ombudsman","FSCS","The Treasury"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Ombudsman is approached only if the firm has not resolved the complaint."},{"question":"What is the maximum payout that will be made for deposits under the FSCS?","options":["£48,000","£50,000","£85,000","£120,000"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FSCS currently pay out up to £120,000 for deposits."},{"question":"If a complaint has NOT been resolved by a firm who should the complainant turn to next?","options":["FCA","FOS","FSCS","The Treasury"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Financial Ombudsman (FOS) is approached only if the firm has not resolved the complaint."},{"question":"All of the following represent data protection principles under DPA 2018, except:","options":["Must not be unlawfully processed","Adequate, relevant and not excessive in relation to the purpose for which it is processed","Processed in a manner which is clear, fair and not misleading","Accurate and kept up to date"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Clear, fair and not misleading is part of the communications rules."},{"question":"Which person would generally be eligible to complain to the Financial Ombudsman Service, assuming the complaint arises from a relevant business relationship with the firm?","options":["A large partnership with annual turnover of £20 million","A charity with annual income of £5 million","An overseas financial institution using treasury services","An authorised firm complaining about an activity for which it has permission"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Eligible complainants include charities with an annual income of less than £6.5 million at the time the complaint is raised. Large partnerships are not listed as eligible complainants, and authorised firms are generally not eligible where the complaint concerns an activity for which they already have permission."},{"question":"All of the following must always be complied with in relation to customer complaints, except:","options":["The presence of written procedures in relation to investigating complaints","Always paying compensation","The creation of written procedures in relation to responding to complaints","Disclosure of the right to refer the complaint to the Financial Ombudsman Scheme"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Options A, C and D are compulsory in order to fulfil the FCA's requirements regarding customer complaints."},{"question":"Per the complaints procedures, how often are firms required to file a summary report of complaints received?","options":["Monthly","Quarterly","Semi-annually","Annually"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A firm is required to file a report with the FCA every six months, where a firm receives 500 or more complaints during a reporting period."},{"question":"Which of the following is not true regarding the Financial Services Compensation Scheme?","options":["Retail clients may claim in respect of all losses (subject to a maximum claim)","In certain circumstances professional clients may make a claim on the scheme","Eligible counterparties may never make a claim on the scheme","The scheme is funded by levies on authorised firms"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Retail clients are 'eligible claimants', and so may claim on the scheme in respect of all losses (subject to a maximum claim). Any client of an insurance firm (including some professional clients and some eligible counterparties, but not other insurance firms) may also claim in respect of insurance only."},{"question":"A consumer and a firm negotiate a term individually rather than using standard wording. How does the Consumer Rights Act 2015 treat that term?","options":["It is excluded from review because only standard terms can be unfair","It is protected from challenge if the consumer signed the contract voluntarily","It is outside scope unless the term appears on the grey list","It is still capable of being unfair despite being individually negotiated"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Unfair terms provisions protect consumers against unfair terms in contracts and notices, including terms that have been individually negotiated. Individual negotiation does not remove the possibility that a term may be unfair."},{"question":"Who must a data controller be registered with under the Data Protection Act?","options":["Treasury","Information commissioner","MLRO","PRA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Note: MLRO = Money Laundering Reporting Officer."},{"question":"If an eligible claimant wins a claim against a deposit placed in a UK based credit institution, what is the maximum payout that can be made?","options":["£85,000","€ 85,000","£120,000","€ 120,000"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The question is testing your knowledge of the Financial Services Compensation Scheme: Who can pay out on losses suffered by eligible claimants with regard to deposits, investments and insurance - subject to limits. The maximum payout for deposits is now £120,000."},{"question":"The FCA requires that firms maintain records of complaints for a period of three years from the date of the receipt of the complaint for non-MiFID business. These records need not include details of:","options":["Any correspondence between the firm and the complainant, including details of any redress offered by the firm","The number of phone calls taken place in an attempt to resolve the complaint","The substance of the complaint","The name of the complainant"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"These records are required for the purposes of monitoring by the FCA."},{"question":"To whom, in the first instance, should a consumer make a complaint?","options":["The Financial Ombudsman Service","The FCA","A magistrate","The firm itself"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Complaints should, in the first instance, be referred to the firm. If the dispute is not resolved, it may then be referred to the Financial Ombudsman Service."},{"question":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to:","options":["Exclude any contract terms in its documentation aimed at consumers","Define unfair terms in all consumer contracts in the United Kingdom","Force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms","Provide compensation for those subject to unfair contract terms"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms."},{"question":"A firm uses a term in existing consumer contracts that the Financial Conduct Authority considers unfair. What further action may the Financial Conduct Authority take?","options":["Apply to the court for an injunction to prevent the term being used or enforced","Require the Financial Ombudsman Service to reopen all complaints linked to the term","Direct the Competition and Markets Authority to rewrite the firm's consumer contracts","Suspend the firm's permissions until all affected customers consent to the term again"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA can apply to the court for an injunction to prevent a firm from using or enforcing the term against existing customers. The other actions are not identified in the provided material."},{"question":"In which of the following situations would the Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS) be called upon the make a compensation payment?","options":["Where the firm is unable to pay out to claimants","Where an official who is representing a customer’s complaint finds in favour of the client","Where the payment needs to be made to resolve a client’s pension dispute","Where the courts award a restitution order and there are insufficient funds to pay"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FSCS will pay out compensation when an eligible claimant sees their financial institution become insolvent or the firm itself is in default. Since none of these words is available, the definition of default is the inability of the firm to pay due the bankruptcy, therefore the best answer."},{"question":"John works in a Spanish branch of company XYZ. Barry is one of the directors in the UK office who has found out that John has been involved in a criminal activity. Should Barry blow the whistle?","options":["Yes; only if the offence has been committed in Europe","Yes; regardless of where the offence has been committed","No; Barry's responsibility does not include Europe","No; it is not a problem connected to the UK office"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Whistleblowing refers to the Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998. PIDA allows an employee to make a protected disclosure if they believe a breach of law has been committed or an attempt to conceal this breach has occurred. PIDA allows for protected disclosure regardless of where the act has been committed."},{"question":"If a retail client wishes to ensure that they do not lose any money in a deposit, which of the following is the most suitable deposit or investment?","options":["Any amount in a UK account","Up to £85,000 in a UK account","Up to £120,000 in a UK bank account","Up to £150,000 in a UK bank account"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"£120,000 is the amount guaranteed by the FSCS."},{"question":"Which contracts are specifically identified as being covered by the Consumer Rights Act 2015?","options":["Contracts for goods, digital content and services","Contracts for securities, derivatives and wholesale deposits","Contracts for pensions, insurance and investment research","Contracts for mortgages, taxation and insolvency services"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The source states that the Act gives consumers statutory rights relating to consumer contracts for goods, digital content and services. The other options list categories not stated as the three core areas covered in the cited description."},{"question":"In relation to a breach of data protection, to whom does a data controller report?","options":["The Information Commissioner","The Chief Compliance Officer","The Comptroller of Data","The Secretary of State for Trade and Industry"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The data controller is the firm responsible for controlling the personal data. Where a breach of data protection is detected or suspected it should report to the ICO."},{"question":"Harold has lost £70,000 through a failed investment scheme. How much will be paid if his claim to the compensation scheme is successful?","options":["50000","63000","70000","85000"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Investors can claim for up to £85,000 of losses with regard to investments and will receive a payout of 100% of that claim."},{"question":"Which statement about the Financial Ombudsman Service is false?","options":["It is operationally independent of the FCA","It arbitrates in situations whereby the authorised firm's complaints procedures have already been used","It is for authorised corporate clients as well as individuals","It can consider complaints and make awards"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Financial Ombudsman Scheme is for 'eligible complainants' - retail clients that are also individuals, and certain small businesses, small charities and small trusts. Authorised firms are never eligible complainants."},{"question":"To whom is the Financial Service Compensation Scheme (FSCS) PRIMARILY accountable?","options":["The Treasury","FCA and PRA","Lord Chancellor","The First Lord of the Treasury"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FSCS is accountable to the FCA and PRA primarily and to the Treasury indirectly through the regulators."},{"question":"A firm includes an individually negotiated term in a consumer investment contract allowing it to vary a key charge unilaterally after the contract is agreed. The term is transparent and does not appear on the grey list. Based on the Consumer Rights Act 2015, which conclusion is most accurate?","options":["The term is fair because transparency prevents any further fairness assessment","The term is fair because individually negotiated terms fall outside the unfair terms provisions","The term may still be unfair if, in the circumstances when agreed, it creates a significant imbalance contrary to good faith","The term is binding because only grey-list terms can be challenged by the Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A term can still be unfair even if it is transparent, individually negotiated and not listed on the grey list. Fairness is assessed by considering whether, contrary to good faith, the term creates a significant imbalance to the consumer's detriment, taking account of the contract's subject matter, the circumstances when agreed and the other relevant terms."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is false with regards to whistleblowing?","options":["FCA firms must adopt and communicate to workers appropriate internal procedures for handling workers' concerns","The FCA would regard as a serious matter any evidence that a firm had acted to the detriment of a worker because he or she had made a protected disclosure","Evidence that the firm is acting to the detriment of an employee would call into question the fitness and propriety of the firm","The rules allow the firm and an employee to breach rules on client confidentiality at all times"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The whistleblowing rules (SYSC18) do not allow employees to breach rules on client confidentiality 'at all times'. Generally client confidentiality rules should be followed, though there may be some limited circumstances where client confidentiality rules can be broken if required to make a relevant disclosure.\n\nThe rules on whistleblowing are found in FCA Handbook Block 1: High Level Standards in the Senior Management Arrangements, Systems and Control (SYSC) sourcebook. Note that the FCA can withdraw the firm's authorisation if it breaches these rules."},{"question":"If an eligible claimant puts in a claim with respect to a protected investment with the FSCS for £37,000 what would be the maximum payout?","options":["£85,000","£37,000","£33,500","£0"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Investors can claim for up to £85,000 of losses with regard to investments and will receive a payout of 100% of that claim."},{"question":"A firm subject to the whistleblowing rules wants to allocate oversight of its internal arrangements to a mid-level compliance manager with limited authority. Based on the rules, what is the main regulatory issue?","options":["The role may be inappropriate because the individual may lack sufficient seniority","The role is unsuitable because only the Money Laundering Reporting Officer may hold it","The role is prohibited because whistleblowing oversight must be outsourced externally","The role is invalid because the Financial Conduct Authority appoints the individual directly"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Firms are required to appoint an individual of sufficient seniority as whistleblowing champion. The champion must be able to ensure and oversee the integrity, independence and effectiveness of the firm's whistleblowing arrangements, which would be difficult if the person lacks adequate authority or resources."},{"question":"When must a firm normally send a final response to a complainant?","options":["Four weeks","Five weeks","Six weeks","Eight weeks"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An acknowledgement letter must be sent promptly, and a final letter within eight weeks."},{"question":"Which arrangement best meets the regulatory requirement for a firm's internal whistleblowing procedures?","options":["A process limited to named employees reporting concerns through line management only","A process able to handle all types of disclosure from all types of individuals","A process restricted to concerns supported by documentary evidence at the outset","A process available only to employees based in the United Kingdom"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The rules require firms to put in place internal whistleblowing arrangements able to handle all types of disclosure from all types of individuals. Limiting disclosures by channel, person or evidential threshold would not meet that requirement."}],"examFocus":["FSCS payout limits: <strong>£85,000 for investments; £120,000 for deposits (£240,000 for joint accounts)</strong> — the two numbers get swapped in wrong-answer options constantly.","<strong>Authorised firms can never claim on the FSCS</strong> — this applies across almost every FSCS question variant.","FOS eligible complainants: consumers, micro-enterprises (<10 employees, turnover/balance sheet ≤ €2m), charities with income < £6.5m, trusts with net assets < £5m. <strong>Large corporates and authorised firms are never eligible.</strong>","Complaint record-keeping: <strong>5 years for MiFID business, 3 years for non-MiFID</strong> — same pattern as the COBS record-keeping rule in Chapter 2.","Firms must report complaints data to the FCA <strong>every six months</strong>, even if they received zero complaints.","Data Protection principles (UK GDPR/DPA 2018): lawful & fair, purpose-limited, adequate/relevant/not excessive, accurate & up to date, not kept longer than necessary, secure — <strong>breaches are reported to the ICO, never the FCA</strong>.","Whistleblowing (PIDA 1998) protection applies <strong>regardless of where in the world the wrongdoing occurred</strong>; settlement agreements must explicitly preserve the right to blow the whistle.","Consumer Rights Act 2015: the FCA can force firms to amend/remove unfair terms or seek a court injunction — <strong>the 'grey list' of unfair terms is indicative, not exhaustive</strong>, so a term absent from it can still be unfair."]}],"chapterCount":4,"mcqCount":145,"flashcardCount":133,"glossary":[{"term":"Authorised firm","chapter":1,"definition":"A firm granted Part 4A permission by the FCA/PRA to carry on one or more regulated activities in the UK.","chars":["Gains status by applying under Part 4A FSMA 2000 and demonstrating it is 'fit and proper'","Must comply with the FCA/PRA Handbook rules (Principles, COBS, SYSC, etc.) on an ongoing basis","Contrast with exempt persons (eg RIEs, appointed representatives) who don't need their own authorisation"]},{"term":"General Prohibition (s19 FSMA 2000)","chapter":1,"definition":"The rule that it is a criminal offence to carry on a regulated activity in the UK by way of business without authorisation or exemption.","chars":["Max penalty: 2 years' imprisonment + unlimited fine (Crown Court); 6 months + £5,000 (magistrates)","Breach can make related contracts voidable at the option of the client (not automatically void)","Enforced via FSMA ss401-402, which give the FCA power to prosecute"]},{"term":"Regulated activity","chapter":1,"definition":"An activity specified in the Regulated Activities Order (RAO) 2001, carried out by way of business, that requires authorisation.","chars":["Must involve a specified investment (eg shares, bonds, units in a CIS, options on precious metals)","Currencies, gold bullion, premium bonds and tangible property are NOT specified investments","Excluded activities (eg newspaper tip sheets, unremunerated trustees) fall outside the regime even if they look similar"]},{"term":"Specified investment","chapter":1,"definition":"An investment type listed in the RAO 2001 that triggers the need for authorisation when dealt in, advised on, or arranged.","chars":["Includes shares, debentures, gilts, units in a CIS, options/futures on financial instruments","Excludes currencies, gold/silver bullion itself, and National Savings Certificates","Options on precious metals ARE specified investments, unlike the metals themselves"]},{"term":"FCA (Financial Conduct Authority)","chapter":1,"definition":"The conduct regulator for the whole UK financial services industry and prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms.","chars":["Independent private company, funded entirely by fees on regulated firms","1 strategic objective (markets function well) + 3 operational objectives (consumer protection, integrity, competition)","Accountable to UK Government via HM Treasury; Chancellor appoints the CEO and chair","Oversees the FOS and, jointly with the PRA, the FSCS"]},{"term":"PRA (Prudential Regulation Authority)","chapter":1,"definition":"The prudential regulator for banks, building societies, credit unions, insurers and major investment firms.","chars":["Part of the Bank of England (via the Prudential Regulation Committee, PRC)","Single objective: safety and soundness of PRA-authorised firms, plus an insurance objective","Not directly accountable to any government body — answers to Parliament via the Bank of England","Funded by fees on the firms it regulates, same as the FCA"]},{"term":"Twin peaks regulation","chapter":1,"definition":"The post-2013 UK model where two regulators (FCA and PRA) oversee the same larger firms from different angles.","chars":["PRA = prudential (financial safety/soundness); FCA = conduct and market integrity","Applies to dual-regulated firms (banks, insurers, major investment firms)","Solo-regulated firms (eg most asset managers, stockbrokers) only answer to the FCA, for both conduct AND prudential matters"]},{"term":"Financial Policy Committee (FPC)","chapter":1,"definition":"A Bank of England committee responsible for identifying, monitoring and acting on risks to the whole UK financial system.","chars":["Created 2013 under the Financial Services Act 2012","Meets at least 4 times a year","Publishes the Bank of England's biannual Financial Stability Report","Not to be confused with the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC), which sets interest rates and meets 8x/year"]},{"term":"HM Treasury (HMT)","chapter":1,"definition":"The UK government department with overall responsibility for the financial services sector.","chars":["FCA reports directly to HMT","Chancellor of the Exchequer appoints the FCA's CEO and chair","Sponsors the underlying legislation (FSMA 2000, Financial Services Act 2012, etc.)"]},{"term":"Principles for Businesses (PRIN)","chapter":1,"definition":"The FCA's 12 high-level, overarching rules that all authorised firms must comply with at all times.","chars":["Breach can lead to disciplinary action even without breaching a specific detailed rule","Cover integrity, skill/care/diligence, management and control, customers' interests, market conduct, etc.","Principle 12 (Consumer Duty) disapplies Principles 6 & 7 for in-scope retail business"]},{"term":"Consumer Duty","chapter":1,"definition":"An FCA outcomes-based standard requiring firms to deliver good outcomes for retail customers, live since 2023 (extended to closed products 31 July 2024).","chars":["Built around 4 outcomes: products & services, price & value, consumer understanding, consumer support","Replaces Principles 6 and 7 for in-scope retail business","Firms out of scope of the Duty still follow Principle 6 and the Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC) six outcomes"]},{"term":"SM&CR (Senior Managers & Certification Regime)","chapter":1,"definition":"The UK's individual accountability framework, extended to all authorised firms from 2016 onward.","chars":["3 pillars: Senior Managers Regime, Certification Regime, Conduct Rules","Senior Managers are regulator-approved (Form A, 90-day statutory clock, Statement of Responsibilities)","Certification is self-certified annually by the firm for 'significant harm' functions","Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators are excluded from the regime"]},{"term":"Senior Manager Function (SMF)","chapter":1,"definition":"A role requiring pre-approval by the regulator because the individual has overall responsibility for a key area of the firm.","chars":["All directors (executive, non-executive, Chairman) are always SMFs","Examples: CEO, Chief Risk function, Compliance oversight, MLRO","Subject to a Statement of Responsibilities and the Duty of Responsibility"]},{"term":"Certification Function","chapter":1,"definition":"A role the firm itself certifies (not the regulator) as fit to perform because it could cause significant harm to customers or markets.","chars":["Re-certified annually by the firm, not approved individually by the FCA/PRA","Examples: retail customer advisers, financial planners, material risk takers, client dealers","PRA's certification scope is narrower — Material Risk Takers only"]},{"term":"Conduct Rules","chapter":1,"definition":"Baseline standards of individual behaviour under SM&CR, binding from the moment of appointment.","chars":["5 individual rules apply to (almost) everyone: integrity, skill/care/diligence, proper market conduct, client interests, disclosure to regulators","4 extra rules apply only to Senior Managers","Rule 6 (deliver good Consumer Duty outcomes) replaces Rule 4 where the Duty applies"]},{"term":"Fit and Proper Test (FIT)","chapter":1,"definition":"The assessment the FCA/PRA uses to judge whether an individual is suitable to perform a controlled function.","chars":["3 pillars: honesty/integrity/reputation, competence/capability, financial soundness","Considers bankruptcy, criminal convictions, exam failures, refusal by trade bodies","No fixed time limit — a past conviction can still be taken into account if relevant"]},{"term":"Part 4A Permission","chapter":1,"definition":"The specific grant from the FCA/PRA that authorises a firm to carry on particular regulated activities.","chars":["Distinct from 'authorisation' itself — authorisation confers status, Part 4A permission defines what you can actually do","Firms must demonstrate they are fit and proper (adequate resources, competent management)","Can be varied or cancelled by the regulator"]},{"term":"Recognised Investment Exchange (RIE)","chapter":1,"definition":"An exchange granted recognised status by the FCA, exempting it from needing its own Part 4A authorisation.","chars":["Must be assessed as fit and proper by the FCA to gain recognition","Membership of an RIE does NOT itself authorise a firm to do investment business","Examples: London Stock Exchange, ICE Futures, LME"]},{"term":"Multilateral Trading Facility (MTF)","chapter":1,"definition":"A system that brings together multiple buyers and sellers of financial instruments, similar to an exchange but operated by a firm.","chars":["The operator does not take proprietary positions against client orders","Client orders are matched against each other under non-discretionary rules","Distinct from a Recognised Investment Exchange, which has separate recognised status"]},{"term":"Appointed Representative (AR)","chapter":1,"definition":"A firm or person carrying on regulated activities under the umbrella of an authorised 'principal' firm, without needing its own authorisation.","chars":["The principal firm takes full responsibility for the AR's regulated activities","COBS applies to ARs only indirectly, through the principal's obligation to ensure compliance","Excluded from the SM&CR regime"]},{"term":"Designated Professional Body (DPB)","chapter":1,"definition":"A professional body (eg for lawyers, accountants, actuaries) whose members can carry out limited regulated activities without full FCA authorisation.","chars":["Exemption only applies where the regulated activity is incidental to the main professional business","Members lose the exemption if separately remunerated for that activity","Membership does NOT itself authorise members, nor exempt them from FCA rules where the exemption doesn't apply"]},{"term":"Retail client","chapter":2,"definition":"The default, most-protected client categorisation under COBS — any client who is not a professional client or eligible counterparty.","chars":["Gets the fullest set of protections: suitability reports, cancellation rights, charges disclosure, etc.","Local authorities and municipalities are treated as retail clients under MiFID II","Can be reclassified 'up' to professional only after a firm assessment and written warning of lost protections"]},{"term":"Professional client","chapter":2,"definition":"A client presumed to have the experience and knowledge to make investment decisions without full retail-level protection.","chars":["'Per se' professional clients (eg large undertakings, authorised firms) qualify automatically","'Elective' professional clients opt up after passing a qualitative test (and, for MiFID business, a quantitative test too)","MiFID quantitative test: 10+ trades/quarter, portfolio > €500,000, 1+ year relevant professional experience (2 of 3 needed)"]},{"term":"Eligible counterparty","chapter":2,"definition":"The least-protected client category — typically other regulated firms, governments and large institutions dealing on a principal basis.","chars":["Most COBS selling/advising rules are disapplied for eligible counterparty business","Individuals can never be eligible counterparties, however experienced","An eligible counterparty may ask to be 'opted down' to professional or retail client protection"]},{"term":"Best execution","chapter":2,"definition":"The obligation on a firm to take all sufficient steps to obtain the best possible result for a client when executing orders.","chars":["Applies to all types of financial instruments, not just certain classes","For retail clients, 'best result' factors in total consideration: price + execution costs (but not the firm's own charges)","Firms must have, and review, an order execution policy setting out how execution factors are weighted"]},{"term":"Client money","chapter":2,"definition":"Money a firm holds on behalf of a client that must be kept segregated from the firm's own money under CASS.","chars":["Must be segregated from the firm's own money, but the client money rules apply to money owed to any type of client","Money due to the firm itself (eg fees) is not client money","Can be deposited with a central bank, an approved credit institution, or a qualifying money market fund"]},{"term":"Chinese wall","chapter":2,"definition":"An information barrier — physical or procedural — preventing the flow of confidential information between different parts of a firm.","chars":["A recognised method of managing conflicts of interest, not an FCA mandatory requirement","Firms must still have adequate arrangements to manage the flow of information even without formal walls","Common between, eg, corporate finance and equity research/sales desks"]},{"term":"Churning","chapter":2,"definition":"Excessive, unjustified dealing in a client's portfolio by a firm, primarily to generate commission.","chars":["Relevant mainly to discretionary management, where the firm controls trading frequency","Considered a breach of the suitability/customer's-interests principle, not a standalone rule violation","Related concept: 'switching' — unjustified movement between similar packaged products"]},{"term":"Cold calling","chapter":2,"definition":"An unsolicited, real-time (non-written) financial promotion — eg an unexpected phone call or doorstep visit.","chars":["Written communications (including email) are never classed as cold calls","Only permitted in limited circumstances: existing client relationship expecting the call, or certain low-risk packaged products","Caller must identify themselves and the firm, and give a contact point"]},{"term":"Financial promotion","chapter":2,"definition":"Any invitation or inducement to engage in investment activity, communicated in the course of business.","chars":["Can be real-time (calls, visits) or non-real-time (adverts, websites, mailshots)","Non-real-time promotions must be signed off in advance by someone with compliance expertise","Must always be fair, clear and not misleading — a standalone requirement under COBS 4"]},{"term":"Suitability","chapter":2,"definition":"The requirement that a personal recommendation or discretionary decision matches the client's objectives, financial situation and understanding of risk.","chars":["Applies to advisory and discretionary services — not execution-only","A suitability report is required for, eg, pension transfers, regulated CIS recommendations, and investment trust recommendations","If a client won't give enough information, the firm must not make the recommendation at all"]},{"term":"Appropriateness","chapter":2,"definition":"A lighter-touch check (versus suitability) confirming a client has enough knowledge/experience to understand the risks of a non-advised, complex product.","chars":["Applies to non-advised services (eg execution-only, arranging) in complex products such as derivatives and warrants","Applies to both retail AND professional clients","Not required for execution-only dealing in non-complex products (only name & address needed there)"]},{"term":"Inducement","chapter":2,"definition":"A fee, commission or non-monetary benefit paid or received in connection with providing an investment service.","chars":["Not banned outright — payments to/from the client itself are allowed","Must be disclosed to the client before the service is provided","'Minor non-monetary benefits' (eg attending a seminar on a specific product) are exempt from the general prohibition"]},{"term":"Conflict of interest","chapter":2,"definition":"A situation where a firm's (or its staff's) interests could compete with, or diverge from, a client's interests.","chars":["Firms must have a conflicts of interest policy managing information flow between business areas","If a conflict can't be managed, the firm must disclose its general nature and source to the client in a durable medium, before acting","Undisclosed conflicts are a common exam trap — eg advising to buy shares the firm itself is short"]},{"term":"Aggregation (of orders)","chapter":2,"definition":"Combining several client orders (or a client order with the firm's own) into a single transaction.","chars":["Not mandatory — firms may aggregate only if unlikely to disadvantage any client overall","Must be governed by a written allocation policy","If an aggregated order is only partially filled, the client's allocation takes priority over the firm's own"]},{"term":"CASS (Client Assets Sourcebook)","chapter":2,"definition":"The FCA rulebook setting out how firms must protect and segregate client money and custody assets.","chars":["Applies to all clients, not just retail","Requires daily reconciliations of client money held vs client money required","Breach can trigger some of the FCA's most serious enforcement action, given the direct risk to client assets"]},{"term":"Market abuse","chapter":3,"definition":"A civil offence covering behaviour that damages market integrity — eg insider dealing, unlawful disclosure, or manipulation — assessed on the balance of probabilities.","chars":["Intention does NOT need to be proved — it is 'effects-based' legislation","Applies to all persons (individuals and firms), UK or overseas, on prescribed/UK markets","Penalties: unlimited fines, public censure, restitution, prohibition orders — but never imprisonment (it's civil, not criminal)"]},{"term":"Insider dealing","chapter":3,"definition":"A criminal offence under the Criminal Justice Act 1993 of dealing in, encouraging dealing in, or disclosing price-sensitive information about, securities.","chars":["Requires an individual (not a company) to knowingly possess inside information from an inside source","Covers MiFID instruments (shares, gilts, debentures, related derivatives) — NOT currencies or physical commodities","Special defences: market makers acting in good faith, price stabilisation rules, recipients of market information","General defence: 'would have dealt anyway' regardless of the information"]},{"term":"Inside information","chapter":3,"definition":"Precise, non-public information which, if made public, would be likely to have a significant effect on the price of a security.","chars":["Rumours are generally not specific/precise enough to count","Information disclosed via a Regulatory Information Service (RIS) is treated as public","Sharing inside information with a professional adviser in the normal course of business is a permitted disclosure"]},{"term":"Market manipulation","chapter":3,"definition":"Behaviour that gives a false or misleading impression of supply, demand or price — a form of market abuse.","chars":["Includes wash trades, abusive squeezes, and disseminating misleading information","'Misleading statements and impressions' under FSA 2012 s89-91 is the related criminal offence (separate from civil market abuse)","Price stabilisation carried out under FCA rules is a recognised safe harbour, not manipulation"]},{"term":"Money laundering","chapter":3,"definition":"The process of making the proceeds of crime appear to come from a legitimate source.","chars":["3 stages: placement, layering, integration","Investment firms are most vulnerable at the layering and integration stages (banks: placement)","Covers proceeds of ALL crime, not just drug trafficking — defined broadly under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002"]},{"term":"MLRO (Money Laundering Reporting Officer)","chapter":3,"definition":"The individual every firm must appoint as the internal point of contact for money-laundering suspicions.","chars":["A Senior Manager Function — not a member of the National Crime Agency","Reports suspicions on to the NCA, and to the firm's board at least once every 12 months","Employees must report suspicions to the MLRO; only the MLRO has discretion over what gets escalated to the NCA"]},{"term":"Politically Exposed Person (PEP)","chapter":3,"definition":"An individual who holds (or has held) a prominent public position, requiring enhanced due diligence due to corruption risk.","chars":["Includes heads of state, ministers, senior judges, ambassadors, and their immediate family","Enhanced due diligence is required because of their vulnerability to corruption — not because they're automatically suspected","Defined under JMLSG Guidance Notes"]},{"term":"FATF (Financial Action Task Force)","chapter":3,"definition":"An inter-governmental body (est. 1989) that sets international standards to combat money laundering and terrorist financing.","chars":["Issues the '40 Recommendations' that shape national AML/CTF regimes, including the UK's","Not a UK body and its standards aren't directly binding in the UK — they're implemented via domestic legislation"]},{"term":"JMLSG (Joint Money Laundering Steering Group)","chapter":3,"definition":"An industry body producing AML guidance notes approved by HM Treasury.","chars":["Guidance is not mandatory, but courts take adherence to it into account as evidence of compliance","The FCA references JMLSG guidance when assessing a firm's systems and controls (SYSC)"]},{"term":"Bribery Act 2010","chapter":3,"definition":"UK legislation creating offences of bribing another person, being bribed, and a corporate offence of failing to prevent bribery.","chars":["The corporate offence has a defence of having 'adequate procedures' in place to prevent bribery","Applies extraterritorially to UK-connected organisations and their associates"]},{"term":"Disclosure and Transparency Rules (DTR)","chapter":3,"definition":"FCA rules requiring listed companies to disclose price-sensitive information promptly and to notify major shareholdings.","chars":["Aim to ensure fair and timely access to information that could affect a security's price","Include notification thresholds for significant shareholdings (major shareholding notifications)"]},{"term":"FSCS (Financial Services Compensation Scheme)","chapter":4,"definition":"The UK's fund of last resort, compensating eligible claimants when an authorised firm is unable to meet its liabilities (in default).","chars":["Investment claims: max £85,000 per person per firm","Deposit claims: max £120,000 per person per firm (£240,000 for joint accounts)","Funded by levies on authorised firms; primarily accountable to the FCA and PRA","Claims must generally be made within 6 years of the event giving rise to them"]},{"term":"FOS (Financial Ombudsman Service)","chapter":4,"definition":"An independent, free dispute-resolution service for eligible complainants once a firm's own complaints process is exhausted.","chars":["Operationally independent of the FCA","Can award compensation for financial loss, distress, inconvenience and damage to reputation","Available to individuals and certain small businesses/charities/trusts — never to authorised firms themselves"]},{"term":"Eligible complainant","chapter":4,"definition":"A person or entity entitled to bring a complaint to the Financial Ombudsman Service.","chars":["Individuals (consumers), regardless of financial sophistication","Small businesses, charities (income < £6.5m) and trusts below defined size thresholds","Authorised firms are never eligible complainants for FOS purposes"]},{"term":"Data Protection principles (UK GDPR / DPA 2018)","chapter":4,"definition":"The core rules governing how personal data must be collected, used and stored.","chars":["Processed lawfully and fairly; adequate, relevant and not excessive; accurate and kept up to date","Data controllers must register with the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO)","Breaches are reported to the ICO, not the FCA"]},{"term":"Whistleblowing","chapter":4,"definition":"The protected disclosure of wrongdoing (breach of law, cover-up, danger) by a worker, under the Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998.","chars":["Protection applies regardless of where the wrongdoing took place (not limited to the UK)","Firms must appoint a whistleblowing champion of sufficient seniority","Settlement agreements must state that workers retain a legal right to blow the whistle — that right can't be contracted away"]},{"term":"Consumer Rights Act 2015","chapter":4,"definition":"Legislation giving the FCA power to challenge unfair terms in financial services consumer contracts.","chars":["The 'grey list' of potentially unfair terms is indicative, not exhaustive","A term can still be unfair even if transparent and individually negotiated","FCA remedies include seeking an undertaking to amend/remove a term, or applying to court for an injunction"]}],"mockExams":[{"id":"mock-1","title":"Mock Exam 1","mcqs":[{"question":"Provisions in the FCA’s Handbook indicated with the letter ‘R’ are binding upon whom?","options":["Approved persons","Authorised persons","The Financial Conduct Authority","Exempt persons"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Provisions in the FCA’s Handbook indicated with the letter ‘R’ are RULES – and these are binding on authorised persons (i.e. firms). If a firm contravenes a rule, it may be subject to discipline.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the objective of conduct risk regulation?","options":["Rules do matter, but outcomes for consumers and markets are the principal focus of regulatory concern","Prescriptive rules to manage the conduct of employees of financial firms are the only regulatory concern","Outcomes for consumers and markets do matter, but rules are the principal focus of regulatory concern","Outcomes for consumers and markets are the only regulatory concern"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA takes a balanced approach to the management of conduct risk. The rules do matter, but outcomes for consumers and markets are the principal focus of regulatory concern.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Aisha is going to pay an unregulated personal services company to carry out several transactions as her agent. If the company carries out each of the following transactions, charging commissions to Aisha each time, which transaction would involve a breach of s19 FSMA 2000 (‘the General Prohibition’)?","options":["Purchase of residential property for Aisha’s personal use","Acquisition of fine art for Aisha’s private collection","Buying shares in a publicly traded company","Investing in a friend's small business without any formal agreement"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Carrying out a ‘regulated activity’ (as defined under The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (Regulated Activities) Order 2001 (RAO)) requires authorisation from the FCA. If a company is not authorised when carrying out that activity, this is an offence under s19 FSMA 2000 (‘the General Prohibition’).\n\nThe Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (Regulated Activities) Order 2001 (RAO) lists various types of ‘regulated activity’ which come under the scope of financial regulation in the UK. This includes arranging deals in specified investments. Shares in a publicly traded company are considered a form of 'specified investment'. The other options listed, such as residential property, fine art, or a stake in a friend's small business, are not included in the list of ‘specified investments’ under the RAO.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A subsidiary company acts as broker for its parent company and arranges a deal in the shares of a public company. Which of the following statements is true?","options":["The subsidiary company is performing a regulated activity and requires Part 4A permission under the RAO","Arranging deals in the shares of public companies is not a regulated activity so no Part 4A permission is required","As the activity is conducted within the company group, it creates an exclusion from the need for Part 4A permission","As the activity is conducted within the company group, a breach of conflict of interest rules has occurred"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms undertaking intra-group dealing or activities, or where there are joint enterprise relationships, benefit from an exclusion of certain activities being considered regulated activities. These activities include:\n\nDealing in investments as principal or as agent;\nArranging deals;\nManaging investments; and\nSafeguarding and administering investments.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Rajesh is processing a variety of orders, both from external clients and from the firm’s own fund management team, for an FCA-regulated MiFID investment firm. According to the FCA's rules relating to client order handling (COBS 11), which statement is true in relation to Rajesh’s work?","options":["Rajesh can aggregate client orders, but only if it's in the best interest of the firm to do so","Rajesh is free to allocate investments based on his own discretion without any limitations","Rajesh must allocate investments in a manner that is fair to all clients","Rajesh cannot aggregate client orders under any circumstances"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Financial Conduct Authority's (FCA) Conduct of Business Sourcebook (COBS) requires that firms allocate investments fairly among clients. The allocation process must not result in unfairness or discrimination between customers. This rule is intended to prevent firms from favouring one client or group of clients over another.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is TRUE of an offence of 'receiving a bribe' under the United Kingdom's Bribery Act 2010?","options":["The receiver of the bribe must always be the person that requested the bribe","The offerer of the bribe can commit an offence even if the receiver of the same bribe does not","Requesting a bribe is an offence, regardless of whether or not the bribe is actually received","The accused must know that performance of the relevant activity by somebody else is improper"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under the Bribery Act 2010, it is an offence if you request, agree to receive or accept a financial or other advantage intending that, in consequence, a relevant function or activity should be performed improperly whether by yourself or another person.\n\nThe offence happens where you request, agree to receive or accept a financial or other advantage as a reward for the improper performance (whether by you or another person) of a relevant function or activity for yourself, or by another person at your request or with your assent or acquiescence. It does not matter whether you know or believe that the performance of the function or activity is improper.\n\nWhere another is performing the function or activity, it also does not matter whether that person knows or believes that the performance of the function or activity is improper.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A client approaches your firm wishing to engage you to provide a discretionary service on his account. In order to trigger the requirement to provide a client agreement before providing services the client must be:","options":["A retail client, as only retail clients receive client agreements for discretionary services","Either a retail client or professional client, as both are protected by this rule","A professional client, as retail clients would not be permitted to engage in discretionary services with a firm","Conducting their activities remotely from an overseas location"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Both retail and professional clients are protected under the client agreement rule.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is an eligible complainant under the FCA regulations?","options":["A company that was considered to be a professional client at the time of problem","A private individual who has never worked in the financial sector","A FTSE 100 company with group turnover of more than £102.5 million","A CIS with balance sheet totals of more than €20 million and net turnover of €42 million"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The others are not considered eligible complainants. An eligible complainant is a small retail customer.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following set out regulations ensuring firms control information to prevent the exchange of information that may harm the interests of one or more clients?","options":["Financial Stability Board","Financial Conduct Authority","Basel Committee","Competition and Markets Authority"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The control of information to prevent the exchange of information that may harm the interests of one or more clients is a requirement under the conflicts of interest rules set out by the FCA.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"If an authorised firm is conducting unauthorised regulated activities, this will be considered to be which ONE of the following?","options":["A criminal matter","A civil matter","A regulatory matter","A due diligence matter"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is a tricky question as, strictly, unauthorised regulatory activity can be dealt with in both the criminal and civil courts. However FSMA makes the breach of the general prohibition a criminal offence; therefore the criminal offence is the best answer.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Under the COBS rules, which one of the following rules would apply to eligible counterparties?","options":["Appropriateness","Client categorisation","Client agreements","Distance communications"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Of the four rules quoted, only the Client Categorisation COBS would apply to eligible counterparties.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Principle of Business 10 (client assets) applies to:","options":["Retail Clients and CIS's only","Retail Clients, Professional Clients and Eligible Counterparties","Retail Clients only","Retail Clients and Professional Clients only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"'Client' is defined as Retail Clients, Professional Clients and Eligible Counterparties.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is true of appointed representatives under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["They can be exempt from and authorised for the same activities","They can be exempt from certain activities and authorised for others","They cannot be both authorised and exempt regardless of activities","There is no distinction between exempt status and authorised status"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A person cannot be both authorised and exempt at the same time. For example, a firm cannot conduct some regulated activities as an authorised person and others in the capacity of an appointed representative of another firm.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A firm is able to execute a client order on venue A or venue B. If the spread associated with venue A is better, the firm must execute on:","options":["Venue A because the spreads are narrower","Venue B because the spreads are wider","Neither venue A or venue B because they use spreads","Either venue A or venue B depending on the other execution factors"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Different prices can only be charged for different venues, if the difference reflects the service provided. Firms should also consider how the other execution factors determine their choice of venue.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"When a firm provides designated investment business to a retail client, the examination requirement within the Training and Competency requirements do NOT apply for which of the following?","options":["Advising on certain investments","Managing certain investments","Dealing in certain investments","Arranging deals in certain investments"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The T&C examination requirements fall on a range of activities including advising, managing and dealing, as well as day to day oversight of certain operational functions. The requirements do not cover those who arrange deals without performing the deal themselves.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is not a threshold condition for a firm seeking Part 4A permission under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Location of offices","Effective supervision","Market conduct","Appropriate resources"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Market conduct is a Principle of Business, not a threshold condition. The threshold conditions are: - legal status - location of offices - prudent conduct - effective supervision (including close links) - appropriate resources - suitability - business model - appointment of a claims representative","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following does not have any rights under the Financial Services Compensation Scheme?","options":["A retail client with an ISA","Trustee of a scheme with assets of less than £1 million","A small business with regard to a general insurance contract","An authorised firm with respect to general insurance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Authorised companies are not able to claim for general insurance contracts under FSCS.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Under the regime created by the retail distribution review, which of the following is TRUE?","options":["Advisers must only charge an up-front fee for advice","Fees should be itemised, based on the product, and disclosed up-front","The charge can be ongoing and based on a percentage of the funds invested","Payment of commissions that are already in place for previous advice must continue"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Advisers must move to fee-based charging. This can be an upfront fee or an ongoing fee based on a percentage of the funds invested (as long as an on going service is provided). Fees should be itemised and disclosed up front, but this should be based on the service provided not the product.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Graham accepts regular deposits from the director of a small overseas company. Which of the stages of money laundering could Graham find himself under suspicion of under the Money Laundering Regulations?","options":["Integration","Segregation","Layering","Placement"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"These transactions would fall under 'placement' within the regulations.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which one of the following would be required to seek authorisation under S19 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Newspapers giving financial advice","Personal representatives","Certain overseas persons","Advising on stakeholder pensions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The general media, personal representatives (trustees) and certain overseas persons are excluded.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A financial promotion to which of these groups does not require approval prior to communication?","options":["Eligible counterparty","Existing client","Retail client","Corporate business"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Eligible counterparties are excluded from these protections.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"The rules on front-running of investment research require a firm to wait for how long before conducting own account trades following the publication of research to its clients?","options":["Five business days","Ten business days","Until the client has had a reasonable time to react to the research","There is no requirement for a firm to delay their trades provided that it is stipulated in the client agreement"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Following the issue of investment research to a client, a firm must wait until its clients have had a reasonable time to react before conducting own account trades.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An issuer is preparing a confidential takeover announcement and several external advisers are given access to inside information. What must the issuer do under UK Market Abuse Regulation?","options":["Maintain an insider list covering those persons with access to the inside information","Notify the Financial Conduct Authority once the transaction has been completed","Publish the names of those persons through a Regulatory Information Service promptly","Obtain annual declarations from those persons before any inside information is shared"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Issuers and persons acting on their behalf must maintain an insider list of all persons who have access to inside information, including advisers, accountants and credit rating agencies. The other options do not reflect the stated UK MAR requirement.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Under the Proceeds of Crime Act if you made an illegal disclosure in respect of an investigation into money laundering, you would be guilty of which of the following offences?","options":["Assisting","Concealing","Tipping-off","Possession"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is the offence of 'tipping-off' and it carries a maximum jail sentence of 2 years and/or an unlimited fine.\nThe other offences carry a maximum jail sentence of 14 years and/or an unlimited fine.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"In respect of the Conduct of Business Rules which of the following does NOT qualify as a 'durable medium'?","options":["Paper","CD-ROMs","Email","Internet websites"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Examples of a 'durable medium' are:\nPaper\nFloppy disks\nCD-ROMs\nDVDs\nHard drive of a recipient's computer (e.g. email)\nInternet websites do NOT qualify as a 'durable medium'.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following functions captured under the SM&CR regime is not classified as a senior manager function?","options":["Investment adviser","Head of internal audit","Chief executive","Money laundering reporting officer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An investment adviser is an example of a certification function which is the only category of function not classified as a senior manager function.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"To whom is the PRA directly accountable?","options":["HM Treasury","Court of the Bank of England","UK Government","FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Although the FCA is ultimately accountable to the UK government on how it carries out its functions, it does so via HM Treasury.\nLikewise the PRA is accountable to the court of the Bank of England.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following is false in relation to the sale of cash deposit ISAs sold via a distance contract and without advice?","options":["A firm must offer at least a 14 calendar day reflection period","Client has a right to cancel","The maximum period of reflection is 30 days","The firm should send a post sale notice detailing the customer's rights"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The reflection period for cash deposit ISAs is 14 days where sold via distance contract (e.g. on a website) and with no advice from the firm. The FCA sets down the minimum requirements for cancellation.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Using which of the following methods would it not be possible for a firm to provide a professional client with a basic written agreement?","options":["DVD","Email","Letter","Website"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"‘A basic written agreement’ is referring to a client agreement, which has to be on paper or other ‘durable medium’ (COBS 8A). Information conveyed on a web page does not automatically meet the requirements for a durable medium.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"The Joint Money Laundering Steering Group (JMLSG) advocates a risk-based approach. Which of the following outlines this approach?","options":["It is guidance where firms must identify the risks which their own business faces in conducting financial transactions and implementing arrangements, as well as the systems and controls to mitigate against the risk that the firm will be used for financial crime.","It is high level rules where firms must identify the risks which their own business faces in conducting financial transactions, as well as the systems and controls to mitigate against the risk that the firm will be used for financial crime","It is high level rules and guidance which is embedded within the FCA senior management arrangements, systems and controls (SYCS) sourcebook which would apply only to firms subject to PRA or FCA authorisation.","It is statutory legislation which emphasises risk as a priority in financial crime risk mitigation, and which drives the production of the Money Laundering Regulations"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The JMLSG Guidance for firms sets out how authorised firms should manage their risk in terms of money laundering and terrorist financing. The Guidance is essentially a risk-based approach; firms must identify the risks which their own business faces in conducting financial transactions and implementing arrangements, systems and controls to mitigate against the risk that the firm will be used for financial crime.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Who has overall responsibility for the UK's financial system, including the institutional structure and the legislation that governs it?","options":["FCA","PRA","HM Treasury","Bank of England"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Her Majesty's (HM) Treasury has overall responsibility for the UK's financial system, including the institutional structure and the legislation that governs it.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A financial adviser makes a personal recommendation on a retail investment product to a retail client. For which of the following reasons would the adviser NOT need to send out a suitability report? The recommendation relates to:","options":["Transactions in a regulated CIS","The surrender of a stakeholder pension","Making withdrawals from a short-term annuity","A small life policy from a friendly society"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Recommendations in relation to small life policies from a friendly society are excluded from the requirement to provide a suitability report. Other exclusions are recommendations relating to:\nA regulated CIS where the firm is acting as investment manager; The client is habitually resident outside the EEA; Increases to a regular premium in an existing contract; and An additional single contribution to a product where a single contribution has already been paid.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of these is a senior manager function?","options":["Complaints manager","Line manager","Chair of Risk Committee","Risk assessor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Of the four choices given, 'Chair of Risk Committee' is the only choice that is always that of a senior manager, since that committee of the Board would be chaired by a non-executive director.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Assuming abusive effect was identified, which of the following is not considered market abuse?","options":["Behaviour based on information not generally available","The person concerned did not observe standards of behaviour reasonably expected of them","Following the conduct of business rules","Behaviour occuring in relation to a financial market"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The conduct of business rules provide a safe harbour against accusations of market abuse.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which one of the following is ALWAYS a specified activity under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Acting as a currency dealer","An accountant offering investment advice","Operating a stakeholder pension scheme","Acting as a trustee for a trust"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A firm operating a stakeholder pension scheme is always a specified activity. Currency options and currency futures would be specified, but not spot or forward deals. Investment advice provided by an accountant will not be specified provided it is incidental to his main accountancy services. Acting as a trustee is not a specified activity provided that the trustee is not paid for his work.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the purpose of the 'Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibility' (PDMR) regime?","options":["It is designed to protect directors from the appearance of insider dealing","It requires directors to hold shares in their company for at least two years","It prevents directors from issuing shares in their company","It prevents directors from buying shares in their company"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is the best possible answer, as under the 'Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibility' (PDMR) regime directors are prohibited from buying shares prior to the announcement of results. Therefore D is only partially correct as directors are only prohibited from buying shares at certain times. Directors should hold shares for the long term, but there is no stated minimum holding period in the 'Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibility' (PDMR) regime.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following points is true in relations to the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002?","options":["All businesses must identify clients","It is secondary legislation","Money Laundering covers property related to all crimes","JMLSG takes precedence over POCA 02"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"POCA 2002 is widely drafted. It specifies that money laundering relates to criminal property – that is, any benefit (money or otherwise) that has arisen from criminal conduct.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which requirement applies under the rules governing high-risk investment promotion?","options":["The firm may offer a referral bonus if the client is experienced","The firm must provide a personalised risk warning including the client's name","The firm may promote non-mass market investments freely after a suitability check","The firm may waive risk summaries where the client is self-certified"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For high-risk investment promotions, clients must receive a personalised risk warning that includes the client's name. Promotions of non-mass market investments are banned to retail investors, and incentives to invest such as referral bonuses are prohibited.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A client could be at risk to either churning or switching in which of the following situations?","options":["Where a broker provides advisory services and waits for further instructions from the client","Where the firm manages a client’s portfolio on a discretionary basis","Where there is over reliance on mathematical algorithms in trading, eliminating the human element","Where a liquid market becomes more solid leading to irregularities in the economy"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"'Churning' is the activity of over-dealing and/or trading more frequently for a client in order to generate additional fees and/or commissions for the firm. It is relevant where, for example, a firm manages a client’s portfolio on a discretionary basis. 'Switching' is the activity of selling one investment and replacing it with another.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Several firms are involved in distributing a retail product. Which arrangement best shows the Consumer Duty outcome framework operating as intended?","options":["Each firm ensures the manufacturer takes sole responsibility for fulfilling the Customer Duty Outcomes","All firms take responsibility in line with its ability to influence product design, operation and distribution","Only the firm with direct customer contact monitors customer support and customer understanding","Wholesale firms building products are outside scope as long as they do not give personal recommendations"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where multiple firms are involved in a distribution chain, each firm must take responsibility for the outcome in a way that reflects its ability to influence product design, operation and distribution. A wholesale firm may still be in scope where it can determine or materially influence retail customer outcomes.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In which of the following situations would a firm need to perform appropriateness checks?","options":["For all services in all products provided to both retail and professional clients","For non-advised services in all products provided to both retail and professional clients","For non-advised services in complex products provided to both retail and professional clients","For non-advised services in complex products provided to retail clients only"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Appropriateness rules protect all customer (both retail and profession) in respect of non-advised services in complex products. Complex products are best thought of as derivatives and other contingent liability investments or highly illiquid or opaque investment.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Ibrahim is an asset manager at a UK domiciled fund management firm, using research concerning small and medium sized companies. What is the maximum company size by market capitalisation that would exempt Ibrahim from the need to pay for the research?","options":["£50 million","£100 million","£200 million","£500 million"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Research on companies with a market capitalisation of up to £200 million are exempt from the Inducement Rules – meaning that UK-based fund managers do not have to pay for this research. The exemption will be assessed by the FCA three years after the implementation of this exemption - this will be on 1 March 2025.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"In the course of MiFID business, in which one of these situations does the rule of appropriateness apply to a firm?","options":["Giving a personal recommendation to a client","Acting as investment manager for a client","Arranging a swap transaction for a client without giving any advice","Appropriateness has been assessed on behalf of the firm by another MiFID investment firm"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"If a firm is an investment manager or is providing recommendations, the firm needs to consider if the investment is SUITABLE.\n\nThis question tests appropriateness, which is for non-advisory services.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"In which of the following circumstances may an unauthorised firm make a financial promotion? It:","options":["Is never permitted to make a financial promotion","Is permitted to make a financial promotion only if the content has been approved by an authorised person","Is permitted to make a financial promotion only if the content has been approved by an approved person","Is always permitted to make a financial promotion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An authorised firm has a similar responsibility for a financial promotion that it approves as for one that it communicates. For example, a firm which approves a non-real time financial promotion must confirm that the financial promotion complies with the rules and be able to show that it has taken reasonable steps to ensure that the financial promotion is clear, fair and not misleading.\n\nNote: although it would actually be an individual that checks the promotion the ultimate responsibility lies with the authorised firm.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following Principles for Businesses is most closely related with the purpose of the Financial Promotions COBS?","options":["Consumer Duty","Market Conduct","Financial Prudence","Client Assets"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The purpose of the financial promotion rules is to ensure that promotions are identified as such, and that they are fair, clear and not misleading. Principle 12 – the ‘Consumer Duty’ Principle – says that 'a firm must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers'. If a promotion is ‘fair, clear and not misleading’, this will help to deliver the good outcomes required by Principle 12.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which one of these is not a per se eligible counterparty?","options":["Insurance company","UCITS scheme","Local authority","Supranational organisation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Local authorities are retail clients, and can never be treated as eligible counterparties.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"If an independent financial adviser (IFA) passes on the business of a client to a life assurance company, which of the following would be true? The life assurance company:","options":["Can rely on the information about the client provided by the IFA","Is responsible for assessing the suitability of clients referred to them by an IFA","Must assess appropriateness and the IFA must assess suitability","Would only assess suitability for execution only trades"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under the rule 'agent as client' the insurance company will treat the IFA as client, and therefore rely on the IFA to assess suitability or appropriateness. The fact that this question focuses on a life assurance company is irrelevant. It could easily refer to any firm conducting designated investment business.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under UK MAR where a person has reasonable suspicions regarding an order or a transaction in a financial instrument when placed on a trading venue, they should notify the FCA:","options":["immediately, once a full investigation has been completed to assess the nature of the event","after an external body has assessed the validity and nature of the event","after a 24 hour period has elapsed","without delay"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"MAR 16 requires firms to notify of reasonable suspicions relating to insider dealing, market manipulation, or attempted insider dealing or market manipulation to the FCA without delay. Firms may carry out brief checks to ensure the threshold of 'reasonable suspicion' is met before reporting; but this does not mean a full investigation must be completed first. The question states that the suspicion is 'reasonable', therefore we can assume that such checks have already taken place.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following FCA Principles for Businesses is disapplied for firms that are in-scope of the Consumer Duty?","options":["PRIN 2: Skill, care and diligence","PRIN 7: Communications with clients","PRIN 9: Customers: relationship of trust","PRIN 11: Relations with regulators"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For firms that are in-scope of the Consumer Duty, both PRIN 6: Customers' interests and PRIN 7: Communications with clients are replaced with PRIN 12: Consumer Duty.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"When considering the rule on product information to be disclosed before providing services, to whom would the firm need to make these disclosures?","options":["Retail clients only","Retail clients and professional clients","All clients and counterparties","Professional clients only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The product disclosure rules (i.e. KIDs) would only apply to retail clients.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A mortgage adviser gives advice to an individual who is looking to purchase a residential property with a 40% mortgage and the rest of the funds pertaining to illegal activities. Under which offence in the Proceeds of Crime Act could they be charged, leading to a possible 14 year jail sentence?","options":["Arrangements","Failure to follow procedures of informing the MLRO","Knowingly prejudicing an investigation","Concealing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Arrangements apply when a person assists someone else to keep the proceeds of crime. They must know or suspect that they are facilitating someone else to acquire, retain, use or control criminal property, and fail to make an authorised disclosure to the authorities. One such examples of arrangements is a financial professional who knowingly helps a person buy a house with criminal property money. Concealing is to conceal or hide criminal property, which a 14 year sentence. Knowingly prejudicing holds a sentence of 5 years.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A consumer is requesting access to more exotic product, faster transmission speeds and lower costs from an authorised firm. The firm has completed its due diligence for assessment of election for MiFID activities, all qualitative tests have been passed but the consumer cannot fulfil the requirements of the quantitative test. Which of the following would explain this?","options":["The firm does not believe that they have sufficient expertise in the area requested","The consumer has requested to be reclassified, listing which specific sectors and /or product but only in a durable medium","They have carried out on average eight significantly sized trades per quarter over the last year","The consumer has had over 5 years experience as a professional in the financial services sector"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The qualitative test has been passed,(therefore expertise is satisfied) we only need to concentrate on the quantitative tests for MiFID business. Any two of the following are true:\n\nThe client carried out, on average, ten significantly sized transactions on the relevant market in each of the past four quarters;\nThe size of the client’s financial portfolio exceeds €500,000 (defined as including cash deposits and financial instruments);\nThe client works or has worked as a professional in the financial services sector for at least a year on a basis which requires knowledge of the transactions envisaged.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A UK-regulated MiFID investment firm is explaining why prudential rules require it to hold capital and liquid assets. What is the main purpose of that requirement?","options":["To support orderly wind-down and reduce harm to consumers and markets","To guarantee positive returns and reduce investment risk for clients","To prevent operational failures and remove the need for supervision","To avoid regulatory intervention and maintain uninterrupted profitability"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Financial resources include capital and liquidity so a firm can meet liabilities as they fall due, and that the prudential regime is intended to reduce harm and support orderly wind-down. The other options describe outcomes not stated in the source.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Under article 19 of UK MAR, where an individual is subject to persons discharging managerial responsibilities (PDMR) and wishes to trade within 30 calendar days prior to the announcement of the company’s annual financial report, which of the following BEST describes the process?","options":["A closed period is a closed period and no trades by these individuals are permitted","The process of disclosure is only relevant to directors and the board, and might not be relevant","This would only be permitted if it involved sacrificing their employee shares","The company is required to make information on these trades public"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The closed period is defined as being 30 calendar days before the announcement of the company’s interim or annual financial report. The company is required to make these periods public as a condition of listing or under national law. This restriction may be lifted to permit a trade in the closed period in exceptional circumstances such as severe financial difficulty requiring the immediate realisation of funds. It also allows transactions under employee share schemes or similar transactions where there is no change in the beneficial ownership of those shares.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A fund manager is providing a discretionary management service to their clients. Halfway through their normal reporting period, the manager makes a large investment for the fund. When do they have to notify the client of the details of this purchase?","options":["Immediately","At the end of the year","In the trade confirmation","In the periodic statement"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Management groups providing investment management services for a client do not need to provide trade-by-trade reports, but will provide periodic statements at suitable intervals.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"LWQ Ltd is an FCA brokerage firm, executing trades in LSE listed shares for its clients. WXX plc is currently issuing shares to be listed on the LSE. Brian is a trader at LWQ, and Brian has been accused of the criminal offence of insider dealing by buying WXX plc shares immediately following their issue. How might Brian defend himself in court?","options":["Brian did not expect anybody to deal in WXX plc shares","LWQ Ltd expects no advantage from issuing WXX shares","LWQ Ltd is helping the underwriters to stabilise the price of WXX shares","There is a Chinese wall between LWQ Ltd and WXX plc"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Price Stabilising Rules allow stabilising managers and their agents to support the price of new issues of securities by buying the securities in the secondary market for a limited time after their issue. The rules therefore provide a defence to possible allegations of market abuse, such as insider dealing or price manipulation. The defence ‘did not expect a person to deal’ is only relevant to the offence of disclosing inside information, not to trading on inside information. The defence ‘no advantage was expected’ is relevant to an individual, not a firm – and besides, LWQ Ltd is not the underwriting firm. And the ‘Chinese Wall’ defence is a possible safe harbour to the civil offence of market abuse; it is not relevant to the criminal offence of insider dealing.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A market participant circulates false rumours online about a listed company, knowing the information is misleading. Which form of market manipulation does this most clearly illustrate?","options":["Manipulating transactions through false pricing activity","Benchmark manipulation through false market inputs","Dissemination through false or misleading information","Manipulating devices through false trading techniques"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Dissemination is the spreading of false or misleading information by any means where the person knew, or ought to have known, that the information was false or misleading. The other forms concern trading behaviour, deceptive devices or benchmark inputs rather than rumours.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which one of the following conduct rules applies to all persons captured by the SM&CR regime? You must:","options":["Take reasonable steps to ensure the business of the firm for which you are responsible complies with the relevant requirements and standards of the regulatory system","Take reasonable steps to ensure that the business of the firm for which you are responsible is controlled effectively","Disclose appropriately any information of which the FCA or PRA would reasonably expect notice","Observe proper standards of market conduct"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The individual conduct rules that apply to ALL SM&CR individuals are:\nYou must act with integrity\nYou must act with due skill, care and diligence\nYou must be open and cooperative with the FCA and PRA and other regulators\nYou must pay due regard to the interests of customers and treat them fairly\nYou must observe proper standards of market conduct","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Under the European Markets Infrastructure Regulation, which of the following is not a requirement on those that trade derivatives?","options":["Risk management procedures must be adopted for OTC derivatives","Certain OTC derivatives must be cleared through a qualifying central counterparty","To report all OTC derivative transactions to a trade repository","To ensure the orderly functioning of markets"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"To ensure the orderly functioning of markets is an objective of the regulator.\n\nEMIR focuses on the transparency and risk management of the OTC derivatives markets.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A broker is aware that his firm is involved in takeover proceedings involving company GHI plc. The broker receives a client order to sell all their stock 'At best'. According to the Market Abuse Regulation what should the dealer do?","options":["Take the order and execute it","Refuse to execute the order as the firm is involved in the corporate action","Inform compliance that there has been a breach in the Chinese wall","Take the order and postpone it until after the takeover is announced"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"With this question we have to assume that the order is an unsolicited execution only.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"To which one of the following activities do the conduct of business rules apply?","options":["Long-term insurance business in relation to life policies","Offering car breakdown insurance","Credit unions","Regulated mortgages"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The conduct of business rules also apply to designated investment business.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is ALWAYS required to be an approved person under the Accountability Regime rules?","options":["Trade input manager","Compliance assistant","Non-executive director","Management accountant"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A non-executive director is always required to be an approved person. Only the person responsible for the oversight of the compliance function is required to be an approved person.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In relation to the Client Asset Sourcebook rules, client money is money held separately from the:","options":["Firm's money","Firm's money and eligible counterparty's money","Firm's, eligible counterparty's and professional client's money","Firm's and professional client's money"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Client money is money which belongs to someone else.\nEligible counterparty's and professional clients' money will only NOT be considered client money if they have signed a written agreement opting out of client money protections.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"How often should an authorised firm report to the FCA in respect of customer complaints?","options":["Monthly","Quarterly","Twice a year","Annually"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms are required to report twice a year (i.e. every six months).","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"If the FCA requires inspection of documents during an investigation, what is the time limit for a regulated firm to produce them?","options":["A reasonable time period","12 hours","24 hours","7 business days"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The powers to inspect documents are given to the FCA under S165 FSMA 2000.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"According to the JMLSG's guidance notes, for what length of time should client verification records be kept?","options":["Five years from the date the account was opened","Three years from the date the account was opened","Three years from the date the account was closed","Five years from the date the account was closed"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The JMLSG issue guidance notes for financial institutions on the Money Laundering Regulations.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Steve is a director within a broking firm. Under the general requirements in the Senior Manager Arrangements, Systems and Controls Sourcebook, the FCA would expect Steve to have which of the following characteristics?","options":["An appropriate career history and qualifications","An ability to transcend the need to segregate duties within the firm","Sufficient remuneration to ensure he remains at the firm","He must be of good repute and sufficiently experienced"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under SYSC, persons who direct the business must be of good repute and sufficiently experienced to ensure the sound and prudent management of the firm. The FCA does not set out specific experience or qualifications for directors. There is requirement to segregate duties under SYSC.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"An adviser must disclose to a client the scope of their advice. Where a personal recommendation to a retail client concerning a retail investment product meets the requirements of independent advice, how is this defined in regulation under COBS 6.2B.11? Independent advice must be:","options":["be sufficiently diverse with regard to their type and product providers and not be limited to products issued or provided by the firm itself or others that have close links to the firm","be limited with regard to their type, and number product providers and not be extended beyond products issued or provided by the firm itself or other that have close links to the firm","be sufficiently diverse with regard to their type, and product providers but be limited to products issued or provided by the firm itself or provided by others that have close links to the firm","be limited with regard to their type, and product provider but not be limited to products issued or provided by the firm itself or others that have close links to the firm"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"COBS 6.2B.11R states independent advice must: 1. be sufficiently diverse regarding their: type, and issuers or product providers, to ensure the client’s investment objectives can be suitably met, and 2. not be limited to relevant products issued or provided by: the firm itself or by entities that have close links to the firm.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"According to COBS, a firm must take reasonable steps to manage any conflicts of interest with respect to independent research in which of the following cases?","options":["Market-making in good faith","Executing an unsolicited client order","Where there has been a reasonable opportunity for clients to act upon the research","The firm is making a recommendation to a client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All the others are reasons why the firm might be interacting with the market irrespective of research.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Firm XYZ is an authorised person in the UK and communicates a promotion from Firm JKL, an authorised overseas person. Which of the following is TRUE?","options":["There would be no compensation cover","As the promotion is being relayed through a UK firm the compensation scheme is available","The extent to which the compensation will or will not be available must be stated","The compensation cover follows home state rules"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Where a firm communicates or approves a financial promotion on behalf of an overseas person, the financial promotion must make clear which firm has communicated or approved it and (where relevant) must explain:\nThat the rules for the protection of investors will not apply\n\nThe extent to which the UK compensation scheme arrangements will be available (and if they will not, that fact), and\n\nIf the communicator wishes to do so, the details of any overseas compensation and/or the deposit protection scheme applicable.\n\nThe firm must not communicate and/or approve the promotion unless it has no doubt that the overseas firm will deal with its UK clients honestly and reliably.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"All of the following concerning general guidance notes issued by the FCA are true, except that they:","options":["Highlight and expand upon any issues surrounding the rules","Are binding on the firm","Allow firms to seek clarification on the rules, thereby reducing the likelihood of a breach","Are contained within the FCA Handbook and are denoted by the letter G"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Guidance notes are not binding on the firm and need not necessarily be followed to achieve compliance with the associated rule.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A firm is required to organise and control their affairs responsibly and effectively, and the FCA sets down high-level competence requirements for them to achieve this. Which of the following sourcebooks contained within the FCA Handbook outline these requirements for ALL firms?","options":["Senior Management Arrangements, Systems and Controls","Code of Market Conduct","Training and Competence","Fit and Proper Test for Approved Persons"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Senior Management Arrangements, Systems and Controls sets out the requirements for ALL firms.\nTraining and Competence Sourcebook sets out further requirements for RETAIL firms only.\nThe Fit and Proper Test applies to approved persons. Firms are also required to apply this to Certification Function.\n\nAll of the above form part of governance, ie, how a firm is managed and controlled. The clue in the question was 'organise and control'\n\nMarket conduct is not part of governance. See Principles for Business","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following statements is true in relation to the inducements rules?","options":["When a fee is paid that is of low monetary value it does not have to consider the best interests of the client","Custody costs, settlement and exchange fees, and legal fees are not permissible","In the case of non-MiFID business commission disclosure is never required","A rebate on a fee paid by a client is an acceptable payment by a firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In relation to designated investment business or, in the case of MiFID business, another ancillary service, a firm must not pay to, or accept from, a person other than the client any fee or commission (or provide or receive any non-monetary benefit).","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following best represents the impact of industry guidance?","options":["It is considered mandatory and a breach would result in disciplinary action","It is designed to plug gaps in the regulatory regime","Industry bodies are expected to monitor compliance","The FCA will not monitor or enforce compliance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"It is not mandatory and the FCA will not enforce or compel other bodies to enforce the guidance.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to:","options":["Exclude any contract terms in its documentation aimed at consumers","Define unfair terms in all consumer contracts in the United Kingdom","Force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms","Provide compensation for those subject to unfair contract terms"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms.","chId":"reg-ch4"}]},{"id":"mock-2","title":"Mock Exam 2","mcqs":[{"question":"Which European regulation relates to the transparency and risk management in the OTC derivative markets?","options":["Markets in financial instruments directive","Capital requirements directive","Alternative investment fund manager directive","European markets infrastructure regulation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The European markets infrastructure regulation (EMIR) relates to the transparency and risk management in the OTC derivative markets. It requires trade reports to a trade repository, adequate risk management for trades, position management and collateral, and mandatory use of a central counterparty for some contracts.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the below would ensure you offer best execution where you have no client specific instructions?","options":["Trade at the price offered on the SETS trading system","Act in accordance with the order execution policy","You cannot continue with the trade without specific client instructions on how to execute","Best execution is not owed where no specific client instructions are made"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The order execution policy sets out how a firm will achieve the best possible result for the client. Following this will ensure best execution.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An employee has noticed that one of his clients has started settling transactions in Australian dollars. There is nothing in the 'Know Your Customer' information that would suggest this is to be expected. Which of the following is the best description of the action that the employee should take?","options":["Contact the client and discuss the circumstances","Report the client to the Money Laundering Reporting Officer","Contact the National Crime Agency about the client","Report the client to the Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The best course of action would be for the employee to report these transactions to their MLRO. They should not contact the client as this may alert them that they are being investigated for money laundering.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of these are specified investments?","options":["Warrants","A building society accepting deposits","Property","Commodities"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is a tricky question. Property and commodities are tangible assets, so are not specified investments. Although deposits are specified investments, the full phrase is 'a building society accepting deposits' - this is a specified activity. Warrants are specified investments.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"How can the FCA demonstrate that it has conducted effective regulation within the financial services environment? Through:","options":["an annual report to the Treasury","a biennial report report to the Treasury","representations made to a select committee within the FPC","representations made to a select committee within the Ministry of Justice"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA is required to submit a report to the Treasury at least once a year – covering matters such as the way in which the FCA has discharged its functions, the extent to which its objectives have been met, and any other matters the Treasury may direct.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Two London Stock Exchange member firms execute an off-order book trade in a security. Which member firm is responsible for trade reporting?","options":["The buying member firm, unless both firms are market makers","The more senior member firm, or the selling member firm if both have the same seniority","The member firm acting as agent, unless the other firm is a retail broker","The member firm with the larger position, or the buying member firm if equal"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For off-order book transactions, trade reporting is required from member firms and responsibility rests with the more senior party to the trade. If the two parties are of the same seniority, the selling member firm reports the trade.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Rita is a trader at an investment firm, and she knows that research relating to one of the stocks she covers is about to be published by her firm. According to UK MAR, when is it permissible for Rita to trade on this research on her firm’s account?","options":["There is no requirement for Rita to wait for any particular length of time; she should trade as soon as she wishes","It is never permissible for Rita to perform own account trades in stocks covered by her firm’s research department","There is a minimum time period of three business days following publication of research before trading on her firm’s account for any stock covered by her firm’s research department","Rita must wait until clients affected by the publication of the research have had a reasonable time to react to the publication"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"UK MAR 20(1) states that, following the issue of investment research to a client, a firm must wait until its clients have had a reasonable time to react before conducting trades for the firm’s own account.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A firm wishes to apply for Part 4A permission to gain authorisation to conduct regulated activities. Due to the size and nature of a firm, it needs to become a dual-regulated firm. Which of the following is true?","options":["The firm will apply to the PRA who will take sole responsibility for granting Part 4A permission","The firm will apply to the PRA who will consult with the FCA before granting Part 4A permission","The firm will apply jointly to the PRA and the FCA who will take joint responsibility for granting Part 4A permission","The firm will apply jointly to the PRA and the FCA but the PRA takes responsibility for granting Part 4A permission"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If the firm is likely to be dual-regulated (ie, it is to be subject to both PRA and FCA supervision) and it wants to become authorised, the firm will make a single application to the PRA as its lead regulator. Although the PRA will make the final decision on the firm’s application, the PRA can only authorise the firm once the FCA has also consented to the firm’s authorisation.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Thomas works at a small bank. Over the last few months he has been accepting deposits from an individual who visits the UK regularly. This is most likely to be which stage in the process of money laundering?","options":["Placement","Layering","Scheme","Integration"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Accepting deposits is most likely to be the introduction of money into the financial system, which is part of the placement stage.\nThe other two stages are layering and integration. Scheme is a made-up answer.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"In respect of the Criminal Justice Act 1993, which of the following is most likely to be an insider?","options":["Someone who receives price sensitive information through a paid for subscription service","An employee who received price sensitive information in the normal course of business","Someone who acquires price sensitive information from a primary information provider","Someone who observes events that could have an impact on the price of a relevant security"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An employee who receives price sensitive information as part of his normal business is most likely to be an insider and would typically be held on an insider list.\nPaying for price sensitive information, for example research, through a subscription service would not make you an insider.\nInformation received via a primary information provider or obtained through observation is not inside information, so the person receiving it is not an insider. Information that can be gained through general observation is not considered inside information.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following is TRUE in respect of the conflicts of interest policy?","options":["The rules protect retail clients alone from the conflicts that could arise between clients of the same firm","It requires firms to maintain effective and administrative arrangements","Where a firm is part of a group, the policy should take account any potential conflicts arising from that group","The conflicts policy should be provided in full to retail clients"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is a tricky question.\nThe rules protect ALL types of client.\nA description of the policy should be provided to retail clients and potential retail clients.\nThe policy itself does not require firms to maintain effective and administrative arrangements, but shows how they will identify and manage conflicts.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is LEAST LIKELY to be a professional client?","options":["Bank of England","IMF","Pension fund","Local authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Bank of England, IMF and pension providers are all examples of 'per se' professional clients. Under MiFID II local authorities and municipalities are to be treated as retail clients.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of these is true in relation to COBS for a MiFID firm?","options":["They must follow COBS","They only need to follow COBS for non-MiFID business","They only need to follow COBS for MiFID business","COBS is amended for MiFID firms"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The general application rule states that firms are subject to the COBS Sourcebook if they carry on designated investment business, long-term insurance business in relation to life policies and activities relating to the above. This definition includes both MiFID and non-MiFID business.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"When disclosing past performance regarding a seven year old equity fund to retail clients, which of the following is required according to the COBS?","options":["Performance data for the life of the fund must be presented in the least prominent manner","Five years of performance data must be presented in the most prominent manner","The performance data must be presented gross of any fees and commissions","The reference periods for any performance data must be shown"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The past performance should not be the most prominent feature. The firm should aim to cover AT LEAST five years or the life of the investment - whichever is shorter. The data should not be the most prominent element and any reference periods must be clearly stated.\nTherefore, this fund should disclose at least five years of data, disclose the effects of fees and commissions.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"For what time period would a firm keep records on confirmation of trades for MiFID business?","options":["Three years from the date of trade","Three years from dispatch","Five years from the date of trade","Five years from dispatch"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A firm must retain a copy of any confirmation dispatched to a client for the following periods from the date of dispatch:\n- Five years - MiFID business\n- Three years - non-MiFID business","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A prospectus is required under Section 85 of FSMA and the issuer has decided to raise public awareness by issuing a prospectus advertisement. In relation, specifically, to this advert which of the following pieces of information needs to be clearly stated?","options":["It is clearly recognisable as image advertising and that past performance is not a determinant of future prospects","The prospectus has been (or will be) published, and where the investors can obtain it","That this advert holds all relevant information to allow the investor to make an informed decision on whether to subscribe","The name and address of the competent regulatory authority"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Image advertising is not a prospectus advertisement and is not subject to COBs. Where a prospectus is required under Section 85, where it relates to offers, admission to trading, of transferable securities. A prospectus advertisement may be used and is any type of communication (eg, TV, press, email, letter, text and brochure) which specifically promotes the offer to subscribe or acquire securities. An advertisement relating to such offers or admissions to trading must not be issued, unless: 1. it states that a prospectus has been (or will be) published, and where investors can obtain it 2. it is clearly recognisable as an advertisement 3. the information in it is not inaccurate, or misleading, and 4. it is consistent with the information that is, or is required to be, in the prospectus. Any written advertisement falling under these rules must also contain a bold and prominent statement to the effect that it is not itself a prospectus, but an advertisement, and that investors should not subscribe to any transferable securities which it refers to, except based on information in the prospectus.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, the client's best interests rule applies to:","options":["Retail clients only","All clients","Eligible counterparty business only","All customers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA handbook states: (1) A firm must act honestly, fairly and professionally in accordance with the best interests of its client (the client's best interests rule). (2) This rule applies in relation to designated investment business carried on: (a) for a retail client; and (b) in relation to MiFID or equivalent third country business, for any other client.\nThe Conduct of business rules provisions outlined above now apply to all clients, following the advent of MiFID II.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An investment firm is considering whether to rely on information supplied by a non-MiFID person. According to the FCA approach, when is such reliance generally reasonable?","options":["When the information is provided orally by a connected person with market expertise","When the information is provided in writing by an unconnected authorised person or professional firm","When the information is summarised informally by a client and confirmed later if needed","When the information is supplied by any regulated person without further assessment"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In relying on information by non-MiFID firms, e.g. firms authorised for non-MiFID business or professional firms, the receiving firm should take reasonable steps to establish that the other person providing written information is not connected with the firm and is competent to provide the information.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Under Senior Management Arrangements, Systems and Controls (SYSC), when a firm apportions its responsibilities amongst its directors and senior management it must ensure all of the following, except:","options":["That it is clear who has which responsibility","That the firm can be adequately monitored and controlled by directors, senior management and the firm's governing body","That details of apportionment and allocation of responsibilities are recorded and kept updated","That records of responsibilities are publicly available and sent to all clients before conducting designated business"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A firm must apportion responsibilities amongst its directors to ensure that each director is clear about their job functions and responsibilities. Apportionment of these responsibilities must be recorded and each version must be retained for six years after it has been replaced by a new version. However, there is no need to send copies of these to clients.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Who, under the Training and Competence rules, is responsible for setting out the standards of competence for employees of authorised firms?","options":["Her Majesty's Treasury, via the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","The Financial Conduct Authority","The Financial Standards Board","The authorised firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"It is ultimately up to the firm to define precise job roles and standards of competence and to conduct a proper assessment of competence.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"If a retail client has not advised the firm differently, how often must firms send periodic statements?","options":["Every month","Quarterly","Semi-annually","Annually"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Periodic statements should be sent every three months.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"According to the inducement provisions stated by the FCA, which of the following is not an appropriate payment by the firm?","options":["Custody fees paid by the firm to a third party on behalf of the client","Non-monetary benefits paid to a third party that do not impair compliance with the firm's duty to the client, enhance the quality of service and are properly disclosed","A third party providing general office equipment used for word processing and accountancy in the normal running of the firm's activities","Participation in conferences, seminars and other training events on the benefits and features of a specific financial instrument or an investment service"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"General office equipment is specifically prohibited by FCA regulations.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A defence against a charge of carrying out a regulated activity without Part 4A permission could be:","options":["They were managing the portfolio for their own benefit alone","They did not believe the activity they performed was a regulated activity","They had applied for authorisation and were awaiting confirmation","They dealt on their own account and held themselves out to others"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Dealing for yourself where no service is offered to others is an excluded activity.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which regulatory body safeguards the members of defined benefit pension schemes?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","Prudential Regulation Authority","Takeover Panel","The Pensions Regulator"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Pensions Regulator was formed in 2005 to safeguard members of work based pension schemes. The FCA is responsible for the activities of advisers and providers of personal pension plans.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In respect of the responsibilities of an authorised firm which of the following is TRUE?","options":["A firm does not have to adopt and communicate to workers' appropriate internal procedures for handling workers' concerns","The firm is not required to perform identity checks for money laundering purposes where the client is another authorised firm","A firm must ensure that staff have been provided with training in respect of identifying and investigating money laundering","A firm must take reasonable care to establish and maintain systems and controls that are appropriate to the business"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A firm must take reasonable care to establish and maintain systems and controls that are appropriate to the business. They should also adopt and communicate to workers' appropriate internal procedures for handling workers' concerns. Identification of other authorised firms is required, but it may be subject to simplified due diligence. Relevant staff should be training in identifying and reporting suspicions, but not investigating money laundering.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A firm provides services to both retail clients and professional clients. How should it apply the Consumer Duty requirements?","options":["It must apply Consumer Duty standards to all customers without exception","It must apply Consumer Duty standards only to retail customers in scope","It must apply Consumer Duty standards only where clients request retail treatment","It must apply Consumer Duty standards only to newly onboarded customers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where a firm serves clients both in and out of scope, it must comply with the relevant requirements for the products and services provided to the different customer types. The Duty applies to retail customers in scope, not automatically to all clients or only new ones.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which organisation falls within the scope of the failure to prevent fraud offence as a large organisation?","options":["An incorporated body with turnover of £35 million and 200 employees","An incorporated body with a balance sheet total of £20 million and 275 employees","An unincorporated partnership with more than 200 employees and a turnover of £40 million","A sole trader with balance sheet assets £15 million and a turnover or more than $35 million"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A large organisation must meet at least two of the stated criteria: turnover of more than £36 million, balance sheet total of more than £18 million and more than 250 employees. The other options do not reflect the stated threshold test.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Where money is held in connection with a delivery vs. payment transaction, the client money rules will not apply for:","options":["One business day only","Up to two business days","Up to three business days","Up to four business days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The rules will apply if DvP does not occur by close of business on the third business day.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Where a firm becomes aware of a conflict of interest that may damage a particular client's interests, which of the following procedures must the firm follow?","options":["Disclose relevant details in a durable medium to the client, allowing the client to decide","Disclose relevant details orally to the client, allowing the client to decide","Manage the conflict and continue to deal","Refuse to deal for the client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Where a firm identifies a conflict of interest they must manage it. Only if they are unable to manage the conflict are they required to disclose it.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is a general defence for insider dealing?","options":["A market maker acting in good faith","An authorised firm performing price stabilisation","A broker engaged in bid facilitation regarding a takeover","An individual who would have dealt anyway"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"\"Would have dealt anyway\" is a general defence. The other three answers are 'special' defences.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"What is the main purpose of the Capital Requirements Directive and the Capital Requirements Regulation framework?","options":["To create conduct standards and improve complaint handling arrangements","To create disclosure standards and improve client reporting arrangements","To create training standards and improve staff competence arrangements","To create prudential standards and minimise the risk of financial failure"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The CRD and CRR created a common framework for financial resources and risk management to minimise the risk of a firm's financial failure. The other options refer to conduct, reporting or training rather than prudential standards.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which one of the following is NOT regarded by the FCA as a reasonable inducement to be paid by a product provider to a firm in relation to the sale of packaged products?","options":["Key information documents","Office rent","Relevant legal expenses","Tea, coffee and biscuits at a meeting"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The inducement must be for the client and it must not be something that the firm would normally expect to pay, such as office rent. 'Minor non-monetary benefits', such as tea and coffee and biscuits at a meeting, are also allowed.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"For which one of these financial instruments is a firm required to assess appropriateness when performing execution only services?","options":["Money market instruments","Shares traded on a regulated market","Securitised debt embedding a derivative","Units in a UCITS scheme"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The other instruments are considered to be simpler financial instruments and therefore the appropriateness test is not required.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"After completing the sale of a personal pension to a retail client, the advisory firm must follow up with which of the following?","options":["A suitability report within 14 calendar days","Cancellation rights after 14 calendar days","A suitability report within 7 calendar days","Cancellation rights after 30 calendar days"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"When a personal pension is sold to retail clients, the advisory firm must provide cancellation rights at the point of sale.\nAfter this point, the firm should follow up with a suitability report within 14 calendar days, to confirm why the firm believes the pension is suitable.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"When complying with fair, clear and not misleading communications in respect of financial promotions, which of the following is true?","options":["Products or services that expose the client's capital to risk are not permitted","Promotions quoting yields should focus only on the long-term prospects of the investment","The FCA must not be named as the regulator in any promotion of financial services or products","Where the firm does not produce the product itself, it must give an impression of the producer"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Fair, clear and non-misleading promotions should be just that. This means:\n\nThose which deal with products or services where a client’s capital may be at risk should make this clear\nThose quoting yields give a balanced impression of short-, medium- and long-term prospects for the investment\nIf an investment product is, or services charges, are complex, or if the firm may receive more than one element of remuneration, this is communicated fairly, clearly and in a manner which is not misleading and which takes into account the information needs of the recipients\nIf the FCA is named as the firm’s regulator but the communication also includes matters not regulated by the FCA, that it is made clear that some aspects of the business relationship are not regulated by the FCA, and\nThose relating to packaged or stakeholder products not produced by the firm itself, give a fair, clear and non-misleading impression of the producer or manager of the product.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A broker receives an order from a professional client to sell 1,000 shares at a limit price of 100p via a firm-provided mobile phone. How long should the broker keep a record of this phone call?","options":["1 month","6 months","5 years","As long as necessary"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The COBS require good quality recordings regarding receiving, executing and arranging client orders to be made and kept for 5 years.\nThis includes communication via fax, email, instant messaging, and firm-provided mobile phones.\nThis applies to communication with any client.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"In which of the following circumstances would identification be required on establishing new business?","options":["Certain one-off transactions less than €7,500","A transaction with an EEA financial institution","A member of the Financial Action Task Force","A potential new client resident in the UK"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Identification procedures would not need to be put in place for most of these circumstances. Identification would, however, be necessary for a UK resident as described in the correct option. Client verification records must be kept for five years. Note that the limit for one-off transactions has recently been amended by the Fourth Money Laundering Directive.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"If a firm is providing investment research for dissemination to its clients in particular financial investments, when would the firm be permitted to deal on that information?","options":["The firm would never be able to deal on that information","The firm would always be allowed to deal on that information","The firm would be permitted to deal on the information if they submitted all confirmations of transactions to the firm's compliance","The firm would be permitted to deal on the information if granted approval by the firm's compliance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Although the firm would be permitted to deal in this circumstance, it would be an exceptional circumstance for the firm's compliance to grant approval.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"What is the main purpose of transaction reporting under UK Markets in Financial Instruments Regulation (UK MiFIR)?","options":["To provide investors with price transparency and support daily portfolio valuation","To provide the regulator with information for market abuse monitoring and market surveillance","To provide issuers with information for shareholder identification and voting analysis","To provide clients with information for settlement monitoring and reconciliation purposes"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Transaction reporting under UK MiFIR provides information to the regulator so transactions can be reviewed after the event. It is used to monitor for market abuse, including insider dealing, and to support general market surveillance.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Cancellation rights and the right to withdraw was offered and subsequently taken up in relation to a personal pension by a retail client on the 25th day. For how much longer must the firm keep a record of the pension contract drawn up in relation to this business?","options":["5 days","3 years","5 years","Indefinitely"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The record-keeping retention period is at least five years in relation to a life policy, pension contract, personal pension scheme or stakeholder pension schemes.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A trader places a series of orders that appear unusual but were entered to hedge a genuine commercial exposure. Under UK Market Abuse Regulation, when is this behaviour least likely to be market manipulation?","options":["When the behaviour was carried out for legitimate reasons and conformed with an accepted market practice","When the behaviour was carried out through a regulated market and recorded by the trading venue","When the behaviour was carried out by an authorised firm and reviewed in advance by compliance staff","When the behaviour was carried out in large size and disclosed immediately to selected counterparties"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"UK MAR recognises that conduct may not be market manipulation where it is carried out for legitimate reasons and conforms with an accepted market practice. The other options do not provide the stated defence in the source material.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"When may a senior manager, having applied for approval from the FCA, perform a senior manager function?","options":["As soon as the application is completed and sent out to the FCA","On receipt of confirmation that the FCA has received the application","As soon as the FCA grants approved status the manager may perform the function","After acknowledgement of approval and six months' grace under constant supervision"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"As soon as the FCA grants approved status the manager may perform the function.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"New legislation relevant to the Information Commissioner’s Office (ICO) has changed the approach and understanding of data protection. It brought in the ability for the ICO to bring in tougher enforcement penalties through fines. Along with these, what other powers does the ICO have? The power to:","options":["prevent firms from processing personal data","serve an enforcement notice","impose a custodial sentence of four years","to prohibit individuals the right to erasure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The ICO along with fines may still serve an enforcement notice if it is satisfied that a data controller has contravened or is contravening any of the data protection principles. The right to erasure is the right to be forgotten and the ICO promotes this not prohibits.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"When comparing conduct risk regulation with compliance with rules, which of the following is true?","options":["The conduct risk approach is retrospective, compliance with rules is forward looking","Compliance with rules is retrospective, the conduct risk approach is forward looking","Both the conduct risk approach and compliance with rules is retrospective only","Both the conduct risk approach and compliance with rules is forward looking only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Compliance with rules is generally a backward-looking philosophy. The question tends to be 'Did the firm comply?' The conduct risk approach, on the other hand, has a more forward-looking emphasis. 'Will the actions of the firm lead to poor outcomes for consumers or not?'","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"From which point in time must certification functions comply with the Conduct Rules under the SMCR regime?","options":["One year and no later from the certification of the employee","From the moment the firm files a valid certificate with the regulator","All relevant staff are immediately on appointment bound by the Conduct Rules","From the moment the employee receives their certificate from the RDC"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"All relevant staff must comply with the six individual Conduct Rules. This includes all staff except ancillary staff.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Where the regulator has seen fit to issue a section 56 prohibition order to an individual, which of the following observations is MOST correct? The prohibition order can relate to:","options":["a particular regulated activity or regulated activities generally. If the individual breaches the order, their authorisation is withdrawn under a civil action","a particular regulated activity or regulated activities generally. If the individual breaches the order, they are guilty of a criminal offence and liable to a fine","a particular regulated activity only. If the individual breaches the order, they are guilty of a civil offence and liable to a fine","a particular regulated activity only. If the individual breaches the order, they are guilty of a criminal offence and liable to a fine and a two year jail sentence"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The prohibition order can relate to a particular regulated activity or regulated activities generally. If the individual breaches the order, they are guilty of a criminal offence and liable to a fine","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"If a client is considered an eligible counterparty for the execution of orders, which of the following is true?","options":["The client receives regulatory protection for the execution of orders","The client will be classified as a per se professional client for advisory services","The firm must assess the client against large undertaking criteria","Neither firm nor counterparty may transact due to potential conflicts of interest"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Eligible counterparty (ECP) status only applies to eligible counterparty business, i.e. dealing activities. ECP status does not apply to advisory services. Any client considered an ECP for dealing will be a per se PC for advisory services.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which one of the following is NOT true about the information that must be disclosed by a firm to a client before providing services?","options":["The rules apply to business done for eligible counterparties","The firm must disclose its proposed investment strategies","The information must be in a format that is specific to each client","Execution venues must be disclosed"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The information may be provided in a standardised format.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following BEST describes the protections offered to 'whistle-blowers' as applied under the Public Interests Disclosure Act (PIDA)?","options":["It outlines the obligations on relevant persons to disclose information that is of public interest","It protects persons from retaliation where they inform the regulatory authorities of concerns that might come to their attention at their place of work","It protects persons from retaliation where they inform the press of any concerns that might come to their attention at their place of work","It outlines the obligations on relevant persons to disclose suspicions regarding money laundering"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"PIDA protects employees of the financial industry who reveal to a regulatory body that they have concerns over the way their company is organised and controlled. SYSC 18 reinforces the coverage of PIDA for financial services firms.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"What is the main purpose of cancellation and withdrawal rights for retail customers entering relevant investment transactions?","options":["To allow firms time to complete anti-money laundering checks after sale","To give clients an opportunity to reconsider within a specified period","To enable consumers to renegotiate charges if market conditions later deteriorate","To ensure all contracts can be terminated at any time without consequence"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The source states that cancellation and withdrawal rights are intended to ensure that clients have the opportunity to reconsider their decision within a certain period and cancel if they change their minds. The other options do not reflect the stated purpose.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"What must a firm do if it considers, on the basis of information received, that a particular product is not appropriate for a client?","options":["Refuse to proceed with the transaction","Advise the client of an alternative product (if any) that is appropriate","Obtain an indemnity from the client before proceeding with the transaction","Warn the client that, in its view, the product is not appropriate"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The firm must provide a warning to the client. The client can then decide whether to go ahead or not.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A broker is unable to execute a client order due to a trading halt on the exchange. This has led to the client order being postponed. In which of the following situations would the broker have to notify the client? If the order:","options":["Was based on a specific client instruction","Came from an eligible counterparty","Was based on a specific order execution policy","Came from a retail client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A firm may promote or postpone a client order in certain situations. Those situations could include market conditions, like a trading halt. In these cases, the retail client must be notified.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following bodies is responsible for promoting stable and prudent operation of the financial system through the effective regulation of deposit taking institutions, insurers and investment banks?","options":["Financial Services Authority","Prudential Regulation Authority","Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Policy Committee"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is the role of the PRA. Its aim is to take on the prudential supervision of investment firms and banks. The PRA is the Bank of England.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Any firm providing execution services for a client must produce an order execution policy. Which of the follow is not true with regard to a firm's order execution policy? It must:","options":["Contain an account of the relative importance the firm assigns to execution factors","Contain a list of the main execution venues used by the firm","Be assessed by a third party firm before being shown to clients","Be reviewed annually and regularly monitored for its effectiveness"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"There is no requirement for a third party firm to assess the policy. However, a firm must receive prior consent to this policy from its clients.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A professional client wishes to complain to the Financial Ombudsman Service about a matter unrelated to the activity for which it is classified as a professional client. What is the most accurate position?","options":["It is automatically excluded because all professional clients are outside scope","It may be eligible because classification does not always prevent eligibility for other activities","It is eligible only if the firm agrees to submit the complaint under voluntary jurisdiction","It is eligible only if the client opts down to retail client during the process"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Professional clients and eligible counterparties are generally not eligible complainants, but they may be eligible if the complaint concerns an activity other than that for which they are classified as a professional client or eligible counterparty. This requires careful application of the eligibility rule rather than simple client categorisation.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under which of the following circumstances do the conduct of business rules not require a firm to enter into a client agreement?","options":["The client is habitually resident outside the UK","The firm has established, by taking reasonable steps, that the client does not want to enter into such an agreement","The client is habitually resident outside the UK and the firm has established, by taking reasonable steps, that the client does not want to enter into such an agreement","The client enters into an insurance contract with the firm as principal"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The COBS are explicit in saying that MiFID business requires a client agreement for all customers, unless the firm acts as principal with an insurance contract.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"With which of the following are all staff bound by the Conduct Rules required to comply?","options":["You must take reasonable steps to ensure that the business of the firm for which you are responsible complies with relevant requirements and standards of the regulatory system","You must observe proper standards of market conduct","You must take reasonable steps to ensure that any delegation of your responsibilities is to an appropriate person and that you oversee the discharge of the delegated responsibility effectively","You must take reasonable steps to ensure that the business of the firm for which you are responsible is controlled effectively"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Conduct rule 5 (observe proper standards of market conduct) applies to both certified and senior manager functions. The other three options provided are senior manager conduct rules.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"What best describes the guidance notes from the Money Laundering Steering Group?","options":["Breaching them carries a penalty of six months imprisonment and an unlimited fine","They must be implemented by authorised firms","They provide an indication of how firms should interpret the laws on money laundering","Breaching them carries a penalty of an unlimited fine"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"As the name suggests, these are guidance notes. Therefore, answer C is the correct answer.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Who implements and administers the Freedom of Information Act 2000?","options":["Data Protection Office","The Information Commissioner's Office","Ministry of Justice","Department of Business, Information and Skills"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The ICO is responsible for both the Data Protection Act 1998 and its extension for public bodies, the Freedom of Information Act 2000","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Siobhan is employed by an FCA investment firm. She is executing orders in listed securities, both from external clients and from the firm’s own wealth management team. According to the FCA's client order handling rules (COBS 11), how must Siobhan treat client orders?","options":["Siobhan is not allowed to allocate investments under any circumstances","Siobhan can aggregate client orders, but only if it's in the best interest of the clients","Siobhan is permitted to allocate investments only to her firm’s most profitable clients","Siobhan is able to aggregate client orders without any restrictions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) has established guidelines for order execution, aggregation and allocation as part of its Conduct of Business Sourcebook (COBS 11.3). According to these rules, firms may aggregate client orders but they must reasonably believe that it is in the best interest of their clients and that it will not work overall to the disadvantage of any client whose order is to be aggregated. This is to ensure fair treatment and transparency, and to prevent firms from giving preferential treatment to certain clients or to themselves.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Money laundering relates to:","options":["Cash that has come from a criminal source","Cash and assets from a criminal source","Cash from a criminal source in the UK","Cash and assets from a criminal source in the UK"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The cash or assets could have come from a global location provided the action is considered a criminal action under UK law.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Ebony is discussing the importance of mitigating the impact of conduct risk with her staff. How should Ebony best describe the FCA’s approach on this topic?","options":["Prescriptive rules to manage conduct risk are the sole concern of the FCA","Outcomes for consumers and markets do matter, but prescriptive rules are the principal focus of the FCA’s concern","Prescriptive rules do matter, but outcomes for consumers and markets are the principal focus of the FCA’s concern","Outcomes for consumers and markets are the sole concern of the FCA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA is more concerned with the spirit than the details of the rules. The FCA takes a balanced approach, while the rules do matter, fair outcomes for consumers and market integrity is the main regulatory focus, although in reality, many of the detailed rules are aimed at the conduct of firms.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following are eligible to claim financial loss from the Financial Ombudsman Service?","options":["A private individual who was deemed professional client when they put in a claim","A charity with income of £250,000","A corporate client with assets of £19m","A trust with assets of £7m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Eligible complainants are:\n- consumers - any person using or contemplating using the services of an authorised firm or an appointed representative;\n- micro-enterprises - any person engaged in economic activity, irrespective of legal form, employing fewer than ten persons and having a turnover or balance sheet not exceeding €2 million at the time the complaint is raised;\n- charities with an annual income of less than £6.5 million at the time the complaint is raised;\n- a trustee of a trust with a net asset value of less than £5 million at the time the complaint is raised.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"What is the primary goal of the FCA's new Consumer Duty?","options":["To increase the profits of financial services firms","To ensure that good outcomes for consumers are at the heart of firms' business models and culture","To decrease the regulatory burden on financial services firms","To increase the number of financial products in the market"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA's new Consumer Duty is designed to ensure that financial services firms put the interests of consumers at the forefront of their business decisions. This includes ensuring that products and services are designed to meet the needs of consumers, that the firm acts in the best interests of consumers, and that communications are clear, fair, and not misleading.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following best reflects the reactive supervision work of the FCA?","options":["It is preventative work through structured conduct assessment of firms","It is remedial work to deal with problems that are emerging or have happened","It is intensive campaigns on sectors of the market or products posing risk to consumers","It is the system through which regulatory reports are made electronically to the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"It is remedial work to deal with problems that are emerging or have happened - this reflects \"reactive supervision work\".\nIt is preventative work through structured conduct assessment of firms - this reflects the \"firm systematic framework\".\nIt is intensive campaigns on sectors of the market or products posing risk to consumers - this reflects \"issues and products\".","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"If a retail client wishes to ensure that they do not lose any money in a deposit, which of the following is the most suitable deposit?","options":["Up to £120,000 in a UK bank account","Any amount in a UK account","Up to £35,000 in a UK account","Up to £50,000 in a UK bank account"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"£120,000 is the amount is guaranteed by the FSCS for deposits.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Liberty is pulling together a training course on UK MAR for traders at her stockbroking firm. Liberty should tell her staff that which of the following products or investments are OUT OF SCOPE of the UK MAR?","options":["Shares traded on AIM","Traded endowment policies","Copper futures traded on the LME","Auctioned products based on emissions allowances"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"UK MAR covers financial instruments, commodity derivatives and auctioned products based on emissions allowances (MAR 1). It does not cover life assurance products such as endowments, whether secondary market traded or not.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"To whom should a firm report if it is unable to obtain satisfactory corporate verification?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","Money Laundering Steering Group","National Crime Agency","Serious Fraud Office"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A firm should report to the National Crime Agency (NCA)\n\nClient identification problems suggest a money laundering risk which, alongside financing of terrorism, should be reported to the NCA and not the FCA. Money laundering rules (Proceeds of Crime Act 2002) attempts to criminalise the laundering of criminal proceeds from ANY crime (fraud, racketeering, drugs, robbery etc), which is why the NCA needs to be informed","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"If the Chairman of a UK listed company is aware of price sensitive information, but this is not known by anyone else, under what circumstances are other directors permitted to trade in their own-company shares?","options":["The directors can prove that they are not privy to the information","The directors obtain specific permission from the UKLA","The directors follow the rules of the PDMR regime","There are no dealing restrictions if it is during a closed period"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The 'Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibility' (PDMR) regime applies to directors carrying out own-company dealing.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A UK investment firm provides services into several jurisdictions. Why can overseas legislation affect how it carries out operational activities?","options":["Because many firms operate globally and local rules differ across products and services","Because many firms outsource globally and local tax rates differ across jurisdictions","Because many firms recruit globally and local employment laws differ across sectors","Because many firms market globally and local accounting rules differ across industries"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"UK financial services can be affected by overseas legislation because many firms operate globally and offer products and services subject to similar but different local rules. The other options refer to global business factors, but not the stated reason for the regulatory impact on operational activities.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is a breach of the 'Integrity' Conduct Rule?","options":["Failure to explain the risks of a derivative to a retail client, due to lack of understanding","Deliberately misleading a client about a transaction","Not sending out a client agreement to an eligible counterparty","Unknowingly, trading on inside information"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The first breach (Failure to explain the risks of a derivative) would be of skill, care and diligence. The second breach (Misleading a client about a transaction) would be a breach of integrity. Not sending out a client agreement to an eligible counterparty is not a breach, and trading on inside information is a breach of the Market Conduct rule.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following applies to a UK firm dealing in shares with an eligible counterparty?","options":["It is a fully regulated activity and must comply with all conduct of business rules","It is an eligible counterparty business and therefore has limited exposure to the conduct of business rules","Although the firm is UK based, the location of the client will affect whether the conduct of business rules apply","Share dealing is an exempt activity under the conduct of business rules"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When a UK firm deals with an eligible counterparty it is regarded as eligible counterparty business.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"If a company prepares semi-annual financial reports how long is the close period under the 'Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibility' (PDMR) regime of directors' dealings?","options":["One month","Two months","Three months","Four months"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"No trades being undertaken in the CLOSE PERIOD prior to the release of results. The close period is the 30 days prior to the announcement of the year-end, half-yearly (i.e. interim) results, or the announcement of quarterly results.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"What is the expectation on how the Bank of England deals with threats to financial stability?","options":["Through cross-party committees to discuss outcomes-based regulation","Through reporting to HM Treasury who deals with fiscal policy","Through delegating to the PRA to ensure firms are safe and sound","Through financial and non-financial operations both at home and abroad"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Maintaining financial stability involves protecting against threats to the whole financial system. The BoE’s surveillance and market intelligence functions detect such threats to the financial system. The threats are then dealt with through financial, and other, operations, both at home and abroad. In exceptional circumstances, the BoE may act as the lender of last resort by extending credit to banks, and other financial institutions, when no other financial institutions will.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"When a retail client has sought out advice in connection to a personal pension plan and cancellation rights apply, within which of the following number of days must a suitability report be provided to the client?","options":["At the date of inception","7","14","30"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In terms of timing, a suitability report must be provided in connection with a personal pension scheme or a stakeholder pension, if the cancellation rules apply, within 14 days of concluding the contract.","chId":"reg-ch2"}]},{"id":"mock-3","title":"Mock Exam 3","mcqs":[{"question":"Which of the following functions in an authorised firm is LEAST likely to require approval under the individual accountability regime?","options":["Chief Financial Officer","Manager responsible for certified persons","Money laundering reporting officer","Customer advisor on investments"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A role advising customers is the best answer here, as it is the most likely to require certification, not approval.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Under the SM&CR, which of the following would MOST LIKELY require certification from the employee's firm before they can conduct their activities?","options":["Client hospitality","Compliance oversight","Client adviser","A reporter working for a financial publication"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Certification function pose the risk of significant harm to the firm and its clients. Client advisers can do this.\n\nHospitality is an ancillary service and outside the scope of SM&CR.\n\nCompliance oversight is a senior manager function and would not need certification by the firm; they would need approval from the regulator.\n\nJournalists also fall outside the scope of SM&CR.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"What is the scope of the United Kingdom's Bribery Act 2010?","options":["Worldwide","The United Kingdom only","The European Union only","England and Wales only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Bribery Act 2010 is explicit in that the offences it covers can take place anywhere in the world, but be treated as though they had taken place in the UK for the purposes of investigation and prosecution. The requirement is that a person's acts or omissions are done or made outside the UK and would form part of such an offence if done or made in the UK, and that person has a close connection with the UK. In such circumstances, proceedings for the offence may take place anywhere in the UK.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which statement best describes the purpose of the Consumer Rights Act 2015 in relation to financial services consumer contracts?","options":["It replaces the Financial Conduct Authority conduct of business rule relating to fair agreements","It consolidates consumer protection law and reforms unfair terms in consumer contracts","It applies only to contracts for specified investments and excludes those for services","It creates a new liquidity rights for retail clients dealing with smaller investment firms"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Consumer Rights Act 2015 is described as consolidating consumer protection laws, setting statutory rights and remedies, and reforming and consolidating the law on unfair terms in consumer contracts. The other options misstate its scope and purpose.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Principle of Business 10 (client assets) applies to:","options":["Retail Clients and CIS's only","Retail Clients, Professional Clients and Eligible Counterparties","Retail Clients only","Retail Clients and Professional Clients only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"'Client' is defined as Retail Clients, Professional Clients and Eligible Counterparties.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following would not be able to take advantage of the FSCS?","options":["An individual who has money held in a cash deposit account wrapped in an Individual Savings Account","An authorised collective investment scheme investing in designated investments","A retail client dealing through an authorised intermediary","A small company with a qualifying insurance contract"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Authorised firms cannot claim from the FSCS.\nAn authorised intermediary would be a broker for example.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following is an eligible claimant under the Financial Services Compensation Scheme?","options":["Supranational institutions such as the World Bank","Trustees of a small self-administered pension scheme","Auditors of the investment firm that has become insolvent","An individual holding 6% of the defaulting firm's capital"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The role of the FSCS is to pay compensation to eligible claimants with a protected claim. Eligible claimants are typically anyone except those who could be classed as large undertakings, significant stakeholders or those who contributed to the failure of the firm.\n\nAlthough pension funds are generally excluded, trustees of small self -administered schemes or occupational schemes, where the employer is not a large company, large partnership or large mutual association, are also eligible claimants.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to:","options":["Exclude any contract terms in its documentation aimed at consumers","Define unfair terms in all consumer contracts in the United Kingdom","Force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms","Provide compensation for those subject to unfair contract terms"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"According to the Financial Services Act 2012, which one of the following is a statutory objective of the FCA?","options":["To balance the burdens and restrictions on firms with the benefits of regulation for consumers and the industry","The firm to use its resources in the most economic and efficient way","That individuals involved in the management of authorised firms must take appropriate responsibility","Protecting and enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The objectives are: Strategic objective: ensuring that the relevant markets function well Operational objectives are the following: securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers, protecting and enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system, promoting effective competition in the interests of consumers.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following statements regarding Chinese walls are false?","options":["They are a method by which a firm can avoid conflicts of interests","They may be physical or artificial barriers preventing the flow of information","The FCA specifies that Chinese walls must be implemented by investment firms","They may be operated by investment firms with several departments"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FCA regulations require that companies implement systems and procedures to prevent conflicts of interest. Yet the use of Chinese walls is not mandatory. This is because in certain situations (e.g. a sole trader) Chinese walls would not be necessary.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"What must the firm do when a specific instruction from a client would prevent a firm from following its order execution policy when carrying out the transaction?","options":["Decline to carry out the transaction","Ask the client to confirm the instruction in writing","Carry out the order as instructed","Ignore the instruction and follow its order execution policy"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is required by the Conduct of Business Rules.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"The purpose of the Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998 in terms of whistleblowing is:","options":["To render an employee's contract of employment invalid","To enable firms to ensure that employees do not talk to third parties about activities within the firm","To invalidate clauses in employment contracts that preclude protected disclosures","To enable the FCA to inform law enforcement agencies of criminal activity"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The act was designed to protect employees from retaliation if they inform regulators of their concerns.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"A client asks for a copy of a trade confirmation for a trade that was carried out six years ago. Which would be a valid response to the client?","options":["The firm is never required to provide copies of documentation","Records are only kept for five years from the date of dispatch","Records of trade confirmations are not kept by the firm","The client should contact the FCA for a copy"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Records are only required to be kept for five years.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An employer has failed to meet its legal responsibilities for automatic enrolment. According to the source, what action can The Pensions Regulator take?","options":["It can issue compliance notices, penalty notices and fines","It can issue prohibition orders, public censures and trading suspensions","It can issue winding-up orders, director bans and criminal confiscation orders","It can issue variation of permission notices, restitution orders and licence revocations"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"TPR can take enforcement actions such as compliance notices, penalty notices and fines if an employer does not comply with its legal responsibilities. The other options refer to powers or sanctions not stated in the provided source for TPR in this context.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Durable media are those which according to COBS:","options":["Can be accessed by a person and will last in its existing form for at least three years","Can be accessed by a person and which allows unchanged reproduction of the information stored on it","Must be provided for the use of the client in the event of any co-mingling of assets held by the firm","Must be generally and publicly accessible on the investment firm's website for the term of the relationship"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is no specification of the durability of the medium in terms of years. The reference is to the fact that it must be durable for sufficient time that is adequate for the information contained within the medium. It may be offered to the client, though there is no necessity of provision unless specified by a rule. Websites are not considered durable media.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following situations would require the firm to enter into a client agreement, with the production of such agreement in a durable medium?","options":["For all MiFID and ancillary services, when engaged in this business for any retail and professional client","For all regulated activities as defined under FSMA 2000 and for all clients","For all designated investment business and when engaged in this business with any type of client","For all ancillary MiFID services only and when engaged in this business for any retail and professional client"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The requirement to enter into a client agreement applies to designated investment business for a retail client and for MiFID business and ancillary services or equivalent third-country business carried on for a retail or professional client.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following would be a breach of the Conduct Rule 'Integrity'?","options":["Misleading a client by providing false information","Failing to take reasonable steps to apportion responsibilities clearly amongst those to whom responsibilities have been delegated","Permitting transactions without a sufficient understanding of the risks involved","Accepting implausible or unsatisfactory explanations from subordinates without testing the veracity of those explanations"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The full guidance on the conduct rules can be found in the Code of Conduct (COCON) Sourcebook.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, eligible counterparty status exists in relation to:","options":["Designated investment business only","Any regulated activity","Eligible counterparty business only","Any business agreed between the firm and the client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is the condition provided by the conduct of business rules. Eligible counterparty business includes own account dealing and dealing on behalf of a client.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Dealing on which of the following would be classified as an offence under the insider dealing legislation?","options":["Inside information when the person knows that it is inside information and knows that it is from an inside source","Generally available information when the person suspects that it is inside information and believes that it is from an inside source","Inside information when the person knows that it is general information and believes that it is from an outside source","Generally available information when the person knows that it is generally available information and knows that it is from an inside source"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A person will have inside information if they gain it through:\n- being a director, employee or shareholder or\n- having access to the information 'by virtue of their employment, office or profession' (e.g. auditors, printers, couriers)\nwhether the information is from a direct or indirect source.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Siti, a consumer, is opening a new cash deposit ISA account with Amina’s bank. Amina is unsure about how many days’ cancellation rights to offer. Under the Conduct of Business rules, what is the minimum cancellation period that must be provided to Siti following the sale of this product?","options":["7 days","14 days","21 days","28 days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"According to the FCA's Conduct of Business Sourcebook (COBS), a firm must provide a retail client with a 14-day cancellation period for a distance contract (or a contract concluded away from business premises) concerning most packaged products and cash deposit ISA accounts. This allows the retail client to cancel the contract without penalty and without giving any reason. This rule is designed to protect consumers by giving them sufficient time to reconsider their purchase.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which offence is specifically identified as not being a base fraud offence for the failure to prevent fraud regime?","options":["False accounting","False representation","Money laundering","Fraudulent trading"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The listed base fraud offences include false accounting, fraud by false representation and fraudulent trading, but money laundering is expressly stated not to be an offence of fraud. The other options are included within the base fraud offences.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A UK trade association body issues anti-money laundering guidance that is endorsed by HM Treasury but does not form part of the Financial Conduct Authority Handbook. Which body is this?","options":["The Financial Policy Committee","The Joint Money Laundering Steering Group","The Competition and Markets Authority","The Financial Ombudsman Service"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Joint Money Laundering Steering Group is a private sector body made up of leading UK trade associations in financial services. Its guidance is endorsed by HM Treasury, gives firms discretion in applying a risk-based approach and does not form part of the Financial Conduct Authority Handbook. The other bodies have different oversight roles and do not issue this guidance.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Who is allowed to make a mass detriment reference?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","HMT designated consumer body","Financial Services Compensation Scheme","FOS and Authorised firms"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FOS and Authorised firms can make mass detriment references to the FCA where it appears there have been regular failures by firms to comply with regulatory requirements. The FCA must publish a response within 90 days.\nHMT designated consumer bodies, such as the Citizen's Advice Bureau, are able to make super complaints.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"A firm recommends a packaged product to a retail client. What is the main purpose of the product disclosure rules in this situation?","options":["To ensure the client receives sufficient relevant information to make an informed investment decision","To ensure the client receives the same information for every type of financial product it invests in","To ensure the firm discloses its internal product approval records before allowing clients to invest","To ensure the client receives a guarantee that the product will meet its investment objectives"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The main purpose of the product disclosure requirements is to ensure clients receive sufficient and relevant information so they can decide whether to undertake an investment on an informed basis. The other options add requirements not stated in the COBS.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"When a firm is carrying out which type of business is the FCA's Conduct of Business Rule relating to suitability required?","options":["Advisory MiFID services on behalf of retail and professional clients","Advisory MiFID services on behalf of retail clients only","Execution only MiFID services on behalf of a retail client only","Execution only MiFID services on behalf of any client"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Suitability is required to be assessed for discretionary and advisory business. For MiFID services, this rule covers all customers. For non-MiFID designated investment business, the rule only covers retail clients.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"The rules on market abuse apply to:","options":["Regulated persons only","Regulated firms only","Members of the public","Regulated persons and members of the public"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Market abuse is a civil offence and applies to both regulated persons (i.e. both firms, and also approved and certified persons) and also generally to members of the public.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"In the event of a firm being unable to execute a client limit order immediately, the firm must make that order public in all but which of the following situations?","options":["The order relates to an investment that trades on a regulated market","The order is for a volume below the normal market size of the investment","The firm has a larger order for the same security that they wish to execute first","The client has informed the firm that they do not wish the trade to be made public"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"If the client instructs the firm not to make the trade public, the firm can follow this instruction.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A company with 42 employees, annual turnover of £5 million and a balance sheet total of £8 million wants to refer a complaint to the Financial Ombudsman Service. Which factor most strongly supports its eligibility as a complainant?","options":["Its balance sheet total is below £10 million and employee count below 50","Its turnover is below £6.5 million and balance sheet total is below £10 million","Its employee number is below 50 and the turnover is below £6.5 million","Its employee number is below 50, balance sheet total is below £10million and the turnover is below £6.5 million"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Certain small and medium-sized enterprises are eligible complainants if they meet the threshold of annual turnover below £6.5 million AND balance sheet below £5 million OR have fewer than 50 employees.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Which of the following is not a feature of the FCA's principles-based approach to money laundering in their Senior Management Arrangements, Systems and Controls (SYSC) Sourcebook? Firms should:","options":["Establish and maintain effective systems and controls for compliance with JMLSG","Be able to identify, assess, monitor and manage money laundering risk","Establish measures to ensure that money laundering risk is taken into account in their day-to-day operation","Delegate overall responsibility for the establishment and maintenance of effective anti-money laundering systems and controls to the MLRO"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Although adherence to the JMLSG guidance is taken into consideration by the FCA in any investigation, it is not a requirement of SYSC that firms do so.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A data breach is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of individuals. When would the duty to inform the individuals not apply?","options":["When the data taken was non-financial information","When the firm has informed the Information Commissionaer's Office","When the data taken had been securely encrypted","When the contravention was caused by circumstances outside the firm's direct control"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"If a breach is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of individuals, firms must inform those concerned directly without undue delay. In this context high risk means the threshold for informing individuals is higher than for notifying the ICO.\n\nThe duty to tell an individual about a breach does not apply if:\no firms have implemented appropriate technical and organisational measures which were applied to the personal data affected by the breach (for example the data has been securely encrypted)\no firms have taken subsequent measures which will ensure that any high risk to the rights and freedoms to individuals is no longer likely to materialise, or\no it would involve disproportionate effort.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"The main objective of the 'Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibility' (PDMR) regime is:","options":["To ensure the LSE is perceived to be a fair and orderly market","To prevent directors dealing in company shares","To restrict directors from holding shares in competitor companies","To ensure that confidence is maintained in foreign markets"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The 'Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibility' (PDMR) regime does not prevent directors' dealings, although it does attempt to restrict dealings during the closed periods (when there is a greater risk of insider dealing).","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"In which of the following situations would a firm need to perform appropriateness checks?","options":["Susan invests in shares listed on the London Stock Exchange through an execution only account","Martin buys a convertible bond from an authorised firm after receiving a personal recommendation","Gabriel makes his sixth investment in long calls through his execution only broker","Elias buys illiquid shares in an unquoted company brokered by an authorised firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Appropriateness applies to non-advised services in complex products. However, there is no need to reassess every time, if the client engages in a series of similar transactions. Susan invests in a non-complex product. Martin receives advice, so suitability would apply. Gabriel has invested in the complex product before (i.e. appropriateness has previously been assessed).","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"How long must records of custody assets be kept under the client asset rules?","options":["Three years after they were made","Five years after they were made","Three years after the end of the financial year in which they were made","Five years after the end of the financial year in which they were made"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CASS rules require records to be kept for five years.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"The MLRO must report to senior management at least every:","options":["6 months","12 months","18 months","24 months"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The MLRO must report to senior management at least once every calendar year.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following is not an appropriate action for avoidance of a conflict of interest?","options":["Disclosing an interest to a client","Relying on written conflicts of interest policy","Declining to act for a customer","Implementing Chinese walls in place of a personal account notice"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Personal account notices cannot be substituted with Chinese walls. The personal account dealing rules are mandatory.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is not one of the types of market abuse set out in the FCA's Code of Market Conduct?","options":["Misleading impressions","Manipulating devices","Dissemination","Benchmark manipulation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"'Misleading impressions' gives the spirit of MAR examples of market manipulation, but is not defined by the FCA's Code of Market Conduct as a type of market abuse. Misleading Impressions is a criminal offence under s90 FSA 2012.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Megan works in the customer relations department at an FCA-authorised firm, and is unsure as to which specific clients of the firm should be notified of the Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS) in the firm’s complaints handling literature. Which of the following would NOT be considered an eligible complainant for the FOS?","options":["A private individual","A business with a turnover of 1.2 million euros, and six employees","A charity with an annual income of £5.5 million","A non-financial multinational corporation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS) adjudicates complaints regarding authorised firms from a range of complainants including private individuals, micro-enterprises (businesses with a turnover or balance sheet that does not exceed 2 million euros, and fewer than 10 employees at the time the complaint is raised), and charities with an annual income of less than £6.5 million. However, large businesses such as multinational corporations are not eligible to use the FOS, making this choice the correct answer.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"A firm must assess the expertise of elective professional clients. This is known as:","options":["Client's best interests rule","Procedural requirement","Quantitative test","Qualitative test"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"To choose to become an elective professional client, for MiFID business the client must meet two tests - the quantitative and qualitative tests.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"For the purpose of the best execution rule, which one of the following is not an acceptable execution venue?","options":["Any over the counter arrangement","Any systematic internalisers","Any multilateral trading facility","Any regulated market"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Execution venues must be outlined in the firm's execution policy. Although some OTC markets are acceptable venues, it would not be an automatic choice and the firm would need to justify the choice. Of the four answers available, OTC is the best choice.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A retail customer contacts a firm after purchase to ask for help understanding ongoing servicing options. Which response best shows the Consumer Support Outcome?","options":["Directing the customer to the original terms in the initial disclosure document to ensure consistency","Providing support whenever the customer is looking to buy an additional service or product from the firm","Giving appropriate levels of service throughout the relationship to help the customer meet their objectives","Referring the customer to third party providers because post-sale support is outside the firm's responsibility"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Consumer Support Outcome expects firms to provide an appropriate level of service that meets customers' needs throughout their relationship. Support should help customers make informed decisions and communications must remain fair, clear and not misleading.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"In relation to a breach of data protection, to whom does a data controller report?","options":["The Information Commissioner","The Chief Compliance Officer","The Comptroller of Data","The Secretary of State for Trade and Industry"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The data controller is the firm responsible for controlling the personal data. Where a breach of data protection is detected or suspected it should report to the ICO.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"A firm completes an occasional transaction for a customer and no ongoing business relationship is established. For how long must the firm retain anti-money laundering and counter-terrorist financing records relating to that transaction?","options":["For three years after the transaction is completed","For five years after the transaction is completed","For five years from the end of the relationship","For eight years from the end of the relationship"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The source states that firms must keep copies of documents and information obtained to meet customer due diligence and counter-terrorist financing requirements, and sufficient transaction records, for five years after an occasional transaction. Ten years is the maximum period for transactional records within a business relationship, not the standard rule for an occasional transaction.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following is the best description of criminal property?","options":["Any benefit (money or otherwise) that has arisen from criminal conduct","Monetary benefits that have arisen from criminal conduct","Any property (money or otherwise) that is the property of a criminal","Money that is the property of a criminal"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Any benefit (money or otherwise) that has arisen from criminal conduct is criminal property. It is worth adding that the alleged offender must know, suspect or have reasonably suspected that the property is criminal property.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"According to the Conduct of Business Rules, when an appointed representative communicates a financial promotion, who ultimately bears responsibility for that communication?","options":["The appointed representative communicating the promotion","The authorised firm that approves the promotion","The product provider if different from the approving authorised firm","The person who confirms compliance of the financial promotion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Responsibility for the communication remains with the authorised firm, even if communicated by an appointed representative.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following arrangements for paying for investment research would NOT meet FCA requirements?","options":["Paying for research from the firm’s own resources, without applying a separate research charge directly to clients","Paying for research through a joint payment agreement, without clear controls to ensure the research cost is identifiable, justified and disclosed to clients","Paying for research through a separate research payment account, funded and operated in line with agreed client charging arrangements","Paying for research under arrangements that include regular assessment of its quality, value and effectiveness for clients"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA allows firms to pay for investment research in several ways. These include paying from the firm’s own resources, or through a separate research payment account. They are permitted to ‘bundle’ payments for third-party research and execution services, provided that they meet relevant requirements around the execution and research costs and was applied from August 2024. However, this is only acceptable where the firm has proper controls in place. The key issue in the answer is the lack of clear controls. The FCA expects firms to be able to identify the research cost, justify it and disclose it properly to clients. If those controls are missing, the arrangement would not meet FCA requirements","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which one of these is not an acceptable reason for a firm to fail to execute otherwise comparable orders sequentially?","options":["A retail client's order was given priority over other clients","Prevailing market conditions made it impracticable","It was in the interests of the client to delay execution","The characteristics of the client's order made it impracticable"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"All clients must be treated equally.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under the Securities Financing Transactions Regulation (SFTR) which of the following is true of the reporting obligations?","options":["Securities financing transactions do not need to be reported","Securities financing transactions need to be reported at initiation only","Securities financing transactions need to be reported at initiation and for lifecycle events","Securities financing transactions need to be reported on a daily basis"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The SFTR reporting obligation requires central reporting of SFTs, including life cycle events. It also requires disclosure of SFTs and reuse to investors.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An appointed representative is:","options":["An individual or a firm who has entered into a contract with an authorised firm to offer regulated activities, and takes legal responsibility for all future business with clients","An individual who has been appointed and authorised by an FCA firm to act as agent for specific or appointed clients, to ensure the clients receive the best possible service from the firm itself","An individual or a firm who has entered into a contract with an authorised firm to offer regulated activities, but the authorised firm retains legal responsibility and is seen to be the principal party with any prospective clients","An individual who is now subject to the Code of Conduct (COCON) and is responsible for appointed and managing those responsible for conducting regulated activities with clients"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"An appointed representative can be any type of person (i.e., a natural person or any other legal person) who has entered into a contract with an authorised person (the principal) for the purpose of conducting regulated activity. The authorised person becomes the principal and accepts legal responsibility for the regulated activity conducted by the appointed.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A director seeks to trade during a closed period. When can this restriction be lifted?","options":["When the trade is approved by the chair and reported before execution","When exceptional circumstances require the immediate realisation of funds","When the dealing is below the normal market size for that security","When the transaction is conducted by the spouse or child of the director"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The source states that the closed-period restriction may be lifted in exceptional circumstances such as severe financial difficulty requiring the immediate realisation of funds. The other options refer to conditions not stated in the provided material.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following is the best description of an outcomes-focused approach to regulation?","options":["More reliance on high level objectives and less attention to detailed rules","No specific rules and a greater onus on firms to use best judgement","A firm can rely on principles","A firm should rely on industry best practice and FCA guidance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A is the best description as principles based guidance relies on a regulatory framework and less detailed rules.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under anti-money laundering provisions, which ONE of the following is TRUE in respect of suspicious transactions? Employees should:","options":["Report the transactions to their compliance officer","Not report a suspicious transaction since that would constitute 'tipping off'","Ensure a report is made to their firm's MLRO","Never continue with the relationship once the transaction has been reported"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Not reporting a suspicious transaction is a criminal offence.\nEmployees should report suspicious transactions to their Money Laundering Reporting Officer (MLRO). This may not be the same as their compliance officer. The MLRO then reports to NCA.\nThe relationship may continue, but it will depend on what NCA says.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following set out regulations ensuring firms control information to prevent the exchange of information that may harm the interests of one or more clients?","options":["Financial Stability Board","Financial Conduct Authority","Basel Committee","Competition and Markets Authority"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The control of information to prevent the exchange of information that may harm the interests of one or more clients is a requirement under the conflicts of interest rules set out by the FCA.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"When must a firm gather more information from the client in order to assess appropriateness?","options":["Information already held by the firm was provided by the client and the firm knows it is complete","Information already held by the firm was provided by the client and the firm is aware it is out of date","Information already held by the firm was from previous transactions with the client","Information is not held by the firm but can be inferred from previous experience with the client"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm is entitled to rely on information provided by a client unless it is aware that the information is out of date, inaccurate or incomplete. A firm can use information it already has in its possession. A firm may satisfy itself that a client's knowledge alone is sufficient for them to understand the risk involved in a product or service. Where reasonable, a firm may infer knowledge from experience.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which ONE of these per se professional clients is not permitted to become an eligible counterparty?","options":["An institutional investor that is not an authorised or regulated person","A company with a turnover of 50 million euros and own funds of 5 million euros","The management group of a pension fund","A supranational body, such as the International Monetary Fund"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"According to the Conduct of Business Rules, an institutional investor that is not an authorised or regulated person cannot become an eligible counterparty. An example of this kind of investor would be a special purpose vehicle designed to create asset backed securities.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under COBS 4.5, which of the following is a requirement of the general rule on communication with retail clients?","options":["Assumptions must not be used","The content should be understandable by an average member of the the target group","The information should disguise or diminish important points, statements and warnings","It should emphasise relevant risk over the potential benefits"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The content should be understandable by an average member of the the target group.\n\nAssumption can be used (e.g. expected inflation) if they are clearly stated. The information should NOT disguise or diminish important points, statements and warnings. There is no requirement to make the risk more prominent than the benefits, but there must be a balance between the two.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Richard is a director of an FCA authorised firm. If Richard is made subject to a prohibition order, but continues working for the firm, and clients suffer losses, what is the likely result of this for the firm?","options":["The directors of Richard's firm could face up to two years’ imprisonment and/or an unlimited fine","Richard's firm will have its Part 4A permission varied in line with the offence","There is no consequence for Richard’s firm; only for Richard","Private persons can sue Richard’s firm for the losses that they suffer"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"s71 of FSMA 2000 provides for circumstances whereby a private person can sue a firm for damages if they suffer loss because the firm is found to be in breach of a statutory duty when:\nan individual within a firm carries out a function in breach of a s56 prohibition order,\nor an individual carries out a controlled function but without prior approval (s59).\ns71 also provides for clients who are non-private persons (eg, businesses, trusts) to sue for damages as a consequence of negligence concerning prohibition orders, in prescribed circumstances.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"An employee believes that a senior manager (an SMF) has been misrepresenting assets valuation and not fully representing the interests of the firm's clients. Under which piece of legislation would the employee be protected, if they raised their concerns appropriately?","options":["Parliamentary Commission on Banking Standards","Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998","UK Market Abuse Regulation","Proceeds of Crime Act (POCA) 2002"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998, which came into force in 1999, introduced legislation to protect individuals from retaliation, if they inform the regulatory authorities of concerns that might come to their attention at their place of work.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Under the fit and proper test (FIT), financial advisers must complete CPD. What does the abbreviation CPD refer to?","options":["Continuous personal development","Continuous psychological development","Continuous professional development","Continuous physical development"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Advisers will need to complete 35 hours of CPD per year.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"An individual acts as a supervisor to several employees who offer regulated financial advice specifically to retail clients only, activities that these employees have not yet been assessed as competent. Which of the following is TRUE of the supervisor?","options":["If the supervisor is performing their role for longer than 30 days, the firm must apply for approval from the regulator for the supervisor","The supervisor should ensure the employees have attained a full qualification within a period of 28 calendar months","That they, the supervisor, have attained an appropriate qualification relevant to the roles of those they supervise","The supervisor, has a statement of professional standing and has completed at 35 hours of structured learning"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms should ensure the supervisor has attained an appropriate qualification in this situation. It is a mandatory requirement for supervisors who oversee those individuals who advise on retail investment products and who have not yet been assessed as competent.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"How is UK MAR best described?","options":["UK MAR is a set of rules for the UK's foreign exchange market","UK MAR is intended to enhance the integrity of financial markets in the UK","UK MAR is a regulation that governs the UK's insurance industry","UK MAR is a law that oversees the operation of credit unions in the UK"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The intention of UK MAR is to prevent and detect market abuse. It applies to markets in financial instruments, but not to foreign exchange, insurance or deposit-taking markets. UK MAR helps the FCA discharge its ‘Market integrity’ statutory duty.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A relevant person completes a personal transaction in a designated investment. Which arrangement must the firm have in place to comply with the personal account dealing rules?","options":["It must obtain prior approval from the Financial Conduct Authority before settlement","It must ensure the transaction is reported promptly to the firm by notification","It must disclose the transaction promptly to all affected clients in a durable medium","It must ensure the transaction is reported to the market operator by the end of the day"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The firm's procedures must ensure it is informed promptly of a personal transaction, either by notification or by another method that enables it to identify the transaction. The other options add reporting or disclosure obligations that do not form part of the personal account dealing rules.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which reporting obligation applies where a firm contests but loses an employment tribunal case in which the claimant succeeds on a protected disclosure claim?","options":["It must report the case annually to the board only if the whistleblower remains employed","It must report the case to the Financial Ombudsman Service before notifying the regulator","It must report the case to the Information Commissioner's Office within 72 hours","It must report the case promptly to the Financial Conduct Authority or Prudential Regulation Authority, as applicable"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The rules require prompt reports to the FCA or PRA, as applicable, about each case the firm contested but lost before an employment tribunal where the claim succeeded wholly or partly on detriment or unfair dismissal linked to protected disclosure. The annual board report is a separate requirement.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Following the Brexit transitional period, UK transaction reporting diverged from mainland Europe with the implementation of UK MiFIR Transaction Reporting; in particular to securities financing transactions. Which of the following activities would a firm be required to disclose under the Securities Financing Transaction Regulation (SFTR)?","options":["Custodial activities involved in delivery verses payment instructions","OTC derivatives subject to the uncleared margin rules","Clearing house internal payments system","Stock borrowing or lending transactions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The UK MiFIR Transaction reporting requirements specifically exempt stock lending or borrowing transactions and repurchase agreements (repos) – these are often referred to as securities financing transactions. These are reportable under the Securities Financing Transaction Regulation (SFTR).","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under the CRD framework, what is the purpose of Pillar 2?","options":["To set additional capital for risks not covered in Pillar 1","To set disclosure standards for risks and capital to markets","To set minimum capital for credit, market and operational risks","To set conduct standards for firms dealing with retail clients"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Pillar 2 allows firms and supervisors to decide whether additional capital should be held for risks not covered in Pillar 1. Pillar 1 covers minimum capital requirements and Pillar 3 covers disclosure, while conduct standards are outside the three-pillar prudential framework.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","options":["Both the FCA and PRA are funded by the UK Government via HM treasury","The PRA is funded by fees paid by regulated firms and then funds the FCA","The FCA is funded by fees paid by regulated firms and then funds the PRA","The FCA is funded by fees paid by firms carrying out regulated activities"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FCA funded entirely from fees paid by the firms they regulate. The PRA is another name for the Bank of England, and the Bank of England is self-funding via its central bank operations and by fees on those financial institutions it regulates.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following is a principle for data protection?","options":["Personal data must be clear, fair and not misleading","Personal data must be kept for a maximum of six years","Personal data must be returned to the data subject","Personal data must be accurate and up to date"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The DPA 2018 Principles are: 1. processing must be lawful and fair 2. purposes of processing must be specified, explicit and legitimate 3. personal data must be adequate, relevant and not excessive 4. personal data must be accurate and kept up to date 5. personal data must be kept for no longer than is necessary, and 6. personal data must be processed in a secure manner.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Which statement best reflects the Consumer Duty cross-cutting rule on avoiding foreseeable harm to retail customers?","options":["Firms must protect retail customers from all foreseeable investment risk","Firms must ensure retail customers do not make poor financial decisions","Firms must remove product risk before offering services to retail customers","Firms must be clear and transparent to retail customers about foreseeable harm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The cross-cutting rules require firms to avoid foreseeable harm, but they do not require firms to protect customers from all foreseeable harm. The material also states firms must be clear and transparent and ensure customers understand and accept inherent product risks.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"How is the failure to prevent fraud offence characterised in relation to the firm's liability?","options":["It creates negligence-based liability requiring proof of weak supervision","It creates strict liability even if senior management were unaware of the fraud","It creates vicarious liability especially where directors approved the fraud","It creates personal liability for the firm's directors and compliance officer"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The offence creates strict liability for firms, meaning the firm can be held liable even if senior management were unaware of the fraud. The other options add requirements or limitations that are not stated.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which execution only client is MOST LIKELY to need to have the most comprehensive risk explanation provided to them?","options":["A retail client selling shares to the firm","A retail client selling warrants","A professional client engaged in derivatives","A retail client buying options"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The risks should be explained to the client as specified by the client's knowledge etc. given that the first two are sales of assets pre-owned. A professional client would most likely not have a significant explanation of the risks given the requirements, as they are considered to have sufficient knowledge and experience.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following would be considered a general defence against the accusation of insider dealing as defined under the Criminal Justice Act?","options":["Preventing the volatility of a share one week after an IPO","The trade was an execution only instruction","No other party was involved in the trade","Did not expect to gain a profit"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Where no advantage was expected is a general defence. This includes not expecting to make a profit. Preventing volatility in shares after an IPO is referred to as stabilisation. This is a specific defence. The other two choices would create no defence to an accusation under the Criminal Justice Act.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A UK MiFID firm receives a client instruction through another UK MiFID firm. On which basis may it rely on the third-party firm when providing the service?","options":["On client information and recommendations relayed by the third-party firm","On transaction records and complaints data retained by the third-party firm","On the third-party firm's capital resources and prudential reporting systems","On the third-party firm's approval of financial promotions for retail clients"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"For UK MiFID business, a firm may rely on information relayed about the client and recommendations provided by another UK MiFID firm or a firm subject to equivalent regulations. The other options refer to matters not identified as the permitted basis of reliance under this section.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is the correct interaction between the FCA regulations on money laundering and the JMLSG guidelines?","options":["JMLSG guidelines are statutory, the regulations are guidance","The regulations are statute, JMLSG guidelines are guidance","JMLSG guidelines overrule the regulations if money laundering is suspected","Firms cannot usually be disciplined for not following the regulations if they follow the JMLSG guidelines"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The JMLSG guidelines provide an indication of what is expected of financial sector firms and, when tailored by a firm to its own risk base and business profile.\nThe law is prescribed by the proceeds of crime act (POCA), which has nothing to do with the FCA. Also, do not confuse the Money Laundering Regulations 2017 (MLR 2017), which is also law, with FCA regulations, which is not law. FCA money laundering regulations (contained in SYSC) provide admin provisions for firms at risk - ie, they tell them what controls they should use in order to reduce their risk exposure. SYSC are regulations, rather than the law, ie they are not statute.\nJMLSG (guidance) then advises firms on how best to implement the FCA's money laundering regulations. Guidance is not 'binding'. This means that it is not mandatory and the firm does not have to follow it. However, these guidance notes have been accepted by HMT, so in following them you could not be considered in breach of the FCA's regulations. If the firm does not follow the guidance, this protection is lost.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"What is the aim of the FCA’s Financial Promotion COBS, and how do these rules relate to PRIN 12 ‘Consumer Duty’?","options":["The Financial Promotion COBS aim to protect consumers from misleading advertisements, while PRIN 12 requires firms to achieve good outcomes for consumers","The Financial Promotion COBS aim to reduce the number of financial promotions, while PRIN 12 is intended to reduce the number of poor quality financial products on the market","The Financial Promotion COBS aim to eliminate the use of financial promotions, while PRIN 12 requires firms to pay due regard to the information needs of its clients","The Financial Promotion COBS aim to increase the quality of financial promotions, while PRIN 12 requires firms to manage conflicts of interest fairly"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Financial Promotion Rules (COBS 4) are designed to ensure that financial promotions are clear, fair, and not misleading, protecting consumers from misleading advertisements. PRIN 12 of the Principles for Businesses, the ‘Consumer Duty' principle, complements this by ensuring that firms act in the best interests of consumers – and misleading financial promotions are obviously at variance with this intention. It is PRIN 7 that requires firms to pay due regard to the information needs of its clients; it is PRIN 8 that requires firms to manage conflicts of interest fairly.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A firm must retain records relating to an assessment of appropriateness for a minimum of:","options":["Two years","Three years","Five years","Six years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In this exam there will be an assumption that the rule applies to MiFID business. The requirement for MiFID record keeping is five years.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An authorised firm, acting on behalf of a client, has received an order to buy 5,000 shares in XYZ plc just before the market opens. The firm believes that the market will open with a sharp fall due to significant losses on the S&P 500 overnight. When should the firm execute the transaction?","options":["The order should be executed promptly and sequentially at all times","The transaction may be postponed, if the firm has obtained permission from the client","The execution may be delayed, if the firm believes it is in the best interests of the client","The transaction must be executed on market open to ensure best execution is achieved"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The rule states that a transaction should be executed as soon as is reasonably practicable. Transactions may be delayed if the firm has taken reasonable steps to ensure that the deal will not operate to the client's disadvantage.","chId":"reg-ch1"}]},{"id":"mock-4","title":"Mock Exam 4","mcqs":[{"question":"A firm engaging in a transaction triggered the COBS requirement to display proof of appropriateness for the transaction because the client:","options":["Was resident outside the UK","Engaged in execution only trades","Requested it","Had been categorised as a professional client"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Appropriateness is for non-advised trades and protects both professional and retail clients. The most likely factor to trigger the requirement to assess appropriateness is the execution only service, specifically when relating to a complex product.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"When assessing whether a term in a consumer contract is fair, which factor is specifically identified in the source?","options":["The profitability of the product over the life of the contract","The firm's market share in the relevant financial services sector","The consumer's investment performance after the contract was entered into","The circumstances existing at the time the term was agreed"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Fairness is determined by taking into account the nature of the subject matter and all the circumstances at the time the term was agreed, together with the other terms of the contract or related contract. The other factors are not identified in the provided material.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"To assess suitability for a retail client which one of the following, regarding the client, is the first thing that a firm should obtain from the client?","options":["Appetite for risk","Financial commitments","Knowledge and experience","Preferred investment types"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Although all four might be considered for suitability, the first thing to be assessed would be the client's level of knowledge and experience as this would then lead in to the other areas.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A MiFID firm receives a payment from a third party in connection with a service to a retail client. When is the payment permitted under the inducements rules?","options":["When the payment is minor in amount, paid to another authroised firm and recorded in the firm's internal register","When the payment is disclosed to the client, enhances the service and does not impair the client's best interests","When the payment is accepted by compliance, publicly disclosed on request and approved by the client","When the payment is paid after the service has been completed, disclosed in summary and linked to distribution"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For MiFID business, third-party payments are permitted only if they are disclosed to the client before the service, enhance the quality of the service and do not impair the firm's duty to act in the client's best interests. The other options omit or alter one or more of these required conditions.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Where a firm has an appointment with a prospective retail client to deliver an unwritten promotion outside of its premises, which of the following must it do prior to such a meeting?","options":["Send, in the post, all relevant documentation prior to meeting such as key information documents","Provide a statement that all publication to be provided has been produced in compliance with the Prospectus Regulation","Provide the client with a contact point in case of the need to cancel","Full disclosure that the information to be provided will allow the client to make an informed decision"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A firm must not initiate an unwritten promotion to a particular person outside its premises, unless the individual doing so gives the client a contact point if an appointment is arranged.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following could be categorised as an elective professional client?","options":["An investor with a personal portfolio worth €100,000 and on average 20 transactions per quarter","A clerk at a financial services firm with, on average, five transactions per year","The head of research at a fund management firm with a portfolio worth €700,000","An unauthorised firm with a balance sheet of €40m and turnover of €55m"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The quantitative criteria for being an elective professional client are: Average trade frequency of ten transactions per quarter over previous four quarters. Portfolio > €500,000. Works or worked in the financial sector for at least one year in a professional capacity. D would be treated as a per se professional client.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Where a firm is subject the Threshold Condition (TCs) and the minimum standards expected of being authorised by the Prudential Regulatory Authority, which of the following will be assessed only by the FCA under the FSMA (Threshold Conditions) Order 2013?","options":["Location of offices","Products and services","Conduct Issues","Capital adequacy"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA assesses applicants only for conduct issues, if they are dual-regulated firms, with the PRA assessing prudential issues. For single or solo regulated firms, the FCA will assess the applicant for both conduct and prudential issues.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A UK investment firm executes a transaction in a financial instrument traded over the counter, where the instrument is admitted to trading on a trading venue. How must the firm treat the transaction?","options":["It must treat the transaction as reportable and submit details by the close of the next business day","It must treat the transaction as non-reportable and submit details only if the venue requests them","It must treat the transaction as exempt and submit details only where settlement fails","It must treat the transaction as optional and submit details only where the client agrees"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A transaction is reportable if it involves a financial instrument admitted to trading on a trading venue, even where the transaction itself takes place outside a trading venue. Complete and accurate details must be reported to the FCA as soon as possible and no later than the close of the next business day.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following bodies oversees a firm's behaviour towards their clients and the markets on which they participate?","options":["Financial Services Authority","Prudential Regulation Authority","Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Policy Committee"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is the role of the FCA. The aims of the FCA are the closest to those of the FSA - protection of consumers and conduct in the markets.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In relation to an authorised firm placing reliance on others for information, which of the following is not a qualifying criteria?","options":["Information must be in writing","The information provider must be independent of the subject matter","The information provider must be based in the UK","The third party is competent to provide the information"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"There is no requirement for the information provider to be based in the UK.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"According to guidance in the FCA handbook which of the following would BEST indicate that an investment manager of a client portfolio was engaged in churning?","options":["Excessive activity in a client's portfolio containing shares","The investment manager acting in collusion with clients to secure a dominant position over the supply of or demand for an investment","The sale or purchase of an investment from the portfolio where there is no change in beneficial interest or market risk","Excessive activity in a client's portfolio containing packaged products"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Persons acting in collusion that secure a dominant position over the supply of or demand for an investment. A sale or purchase of an investment where there is no change in beneficial interest or market risk. Both are market abuse.\nExcessive activity in a client's portfolio containing packaged products is switching.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"The Appropriateness rule, in the FCA Conduct of Business rules, applies to:","options":["Advisory business on complex products with regard to all clients","Retail clients performing discretionary and advisory business in any investment","A professional client purchasing a capital at risk structured product with no advice","Any service provided to a retail client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Appropriateness applies to execution only services in complex products - capital at risk structured products are considered complex, as are derivatives. Advisory and discretionary services would need to ensure suitability, not appropriateness.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"The purpose of the prudential regulation is to ensure that firms maintain appropriate resources in excess of their:","options":["Capital resource requirements","Market risk exposure","Liquidity buffer","Rival firms resources"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The purpose of the prudential regulation is to ensure that firms have enough capital to operate safely. It does this by setting a capital resource requirement that firms must exceed.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An individual who is accused of insider dealing may not be able to use which of the following defence?","options":["Did not expect the recipient to deal","No advantage was expected","Would have dealt anyway","Dealt in the process of stabilisation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"This is a slightly vague question as it is not very clear if they are testing the general or specific defences. You would have to assume an individual could be anyone and therefore the question is testing general defences.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which set of measures is identified in Home Office guidance as informing an organisation's fraud prevention framework?","options":["Risk assessment, due diligence, communication including training, monitoring and review","Client categorisation, best execution, suitability testing, product governance and monitoring","Capital adequacy, liquidity monitoring, recovery planning, remuneration failures and controls","Market soundings, insider lists, delayed disclosure, transaction reporting and publication"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The guidance identifies six fraud prevention principles, including risk assessment, due diligence, communication including training and monitoring and review. The other options describe regulatory requirements from different subject areas.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Money that is proceeds of crime is placed on deposit in the UK. It is then transferred overseas in an offshore bank account. Which stage of money laundering does this transfer represent?","options":["Integration","Placement","Layering","Arrangement"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Layering involves moving the money around in order to make it difficult for the authorities to link the placed funds with the ultimate beneficiary of the money. This may involve excessive trading in currencies, shares or other investments. It can also mean moving the money from country to country.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"In relation to rules governing regulated and unregulated activities, Part 9A of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 empowers the FCA to do which one of the following?","options":["Make rules that are legally binding on authorised firms concerning regulated activity only","Suggest rules to HM Treasury that would be legally binding on authorised firms concerning regulated activity only","Create rules that are legally binding on authorised firms concerning regulated activity and also activity that is not regulated","Propose rules to HM Treasury that would be legally binding on authorised firms concerning regulated activity and also activity that is not regulated"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Part 9A of the FSMA empowers the FCA to make rules that are legally binding on authorised firms concerning regulated activity and also activity that is not regulated.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A firm wants to identify the body most concerned with macroeconomic and financial issues that could threaten the long-term growth prospects of the UK economy. Which body should it identify?","options":["The Bank of England Prudential Regulation Authority","HM Revenue & Customs","The Financial Policy Committee","The Competition and Markets Authority"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Financial Policy Committee is an official committee of the Bank of England and focuses on macroeconomic and financial issues that may threaten long-term UK growth prospects. This distinguishes it from the Prudential Regulation Authority, which focuses on prudential supervision of specific firms, and from HM Revenue & Customs and the Competition and Markets Authority, which have different remits.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Where a dual-regulated firm applies for Part 4A permission, which of the following threshold conditions is the sole responsibility of the Financial Conduct Authority?","options":["Effective supervision","Appropriate non-financial resources","Fitness and propriety","Prudent conduct"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Appropriate non-financial resources are the sole responsibility of the FCA. The PRA looks at prudent conduct. The others listed are the joint responsibility of both regulators.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Under the Market Abuse Regulation the FCA has the power to do which one of the following with respect to market abuse?","options":["Criminal prosecution","Civil enforcement","Collect information for the Crown Prosecution Service","Set the maximum fine for market abuse offences"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The question is asking about market abuse, which is a civil offence. Therefore, the FCA will sue the firm in the civil courts. The maximum fines are set out under the Market Abuse Regulation.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following methods of delivery would be permissible for a financial promotion to be approved by an authorised person?","options":["An unsolicited call from a company","A meeting or presentation","A website","Any interactive dialogue"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"We have to assume the authorised firm is not producing the promotion and is being asked to approve the promotion of another, unauthorised, entity. In these situations only a non-real-time communication is permissible","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In respect of the personal accounting dealing rules, the firm does not need to do which of the following?","options":["Ensure that the employee is aware of the restrictions on her personal transactions","When an employee undertakes a personal account transaction, the firm receives prompt notification or is otherwise able to identify the transaction","Ensure that the employee only uses the firm for whom she works for her personal account dealing transactions","Give permission before an employee deals for her personal account dealings in a firm for which it conducts designated investment business"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The employee may use another authorised firm to execute their transactions. That firm notifies the employee's firm of the transaction.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Under the Money Laundering Regulations all of the following are considered high-risk factors EXCEPT:","options":["Transactions with a prominent political figure","Transactions with no apparent economic purpose","Bilateral transactions with an authorised counterparty","Transaction related to items of scientific significance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Bilateral transactions with an authorised counterparty would be low-risk, although simplified due diligence would need to ba assessed on a case-by-case basis.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"In the event that a firm breaches the general prohibition under S19 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, which of the following is TRUE?","options":["All of the firm's contracts are void","Any agreements with the firm are unenforceable against the client","Contracts are voidable at the discretion of the court","Contracts are voidable at the discretion of the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In essence contracts are voidable at the discretion of the client.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Where a ‘Form A’ has been submitted by a firm for approval of an individual, which of the following could be a reason why they might fail to satisfy the criteria under financial soundness?","options":["A previous criminal conviction to do with fraud but not related to the financial services industry","They have not yet completed a satisfactory competence exam in financial management","That the person is of limited financial means","That they have had their assets sequestrated or have made arrangements with their creditors"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In assessing an applicant’s financial soundness, the regulator will have particular regard to whether the person: has been subject to any judgement to repay a debt or pay another award that remains outstanding or was not satisfied within a reasonable period, or has filed for bankruptcy, been adjudged bankrupt, had their assets sequestrated or made arrangements with their creditors. The fact that a person may be of limited financial means will not in itself affect their suitability to perform an SMF. Previous criminal convictions will come under ‘honesty, integrity and reputation’ not financial soundness.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A broker is unable to execute a client order due to a trading halt on the exchange. This has led to the client order being postponed. In which of the following situations would the broker have to notify the client? If the order:","options":["Was based on a specific client instruction","Came from an eligible counterparty","Was based on a specific order execution policy","Came from a retail client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A firm may promote or postpone a client order in certain situations. Those situations could include market conditions, like a trading halt. In these cases, the retail client must be notified.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"What is the MAIN purpose of the appropriateness requirement by the FCA?","options":["To ensure that advice given to retail clients is appropriate","To perform a duty of care for retail clients when providing discretionary services","To perform a duty of care for retail clients when providing execution only services","To ensure that all clients are given suitable and appropriate advice"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Appropriateness doesn't apply to advisory and discretionary services.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following would be a regulated activity and therefore those who offer it would need to be authorised?","options":["A bank providing finance to another person and accepting an instrument acknowledging the debt","A custodian, which is holding title documents to investments, dematerialised investments in its own name, and administers the collection of interest and or dividends","An individual who achieves direct membership of an exchange and trades derivatives only for their own account","A company that wishes to rationalise its capital structure and proceeds to buy back its own shares through the secondary market"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Safekeeping and administration of assets - i.e. acting as a custodian - is a regulated activity and needs to be authorised. There is no need to be authorised to conduct trades for your own account if you are the end user of those trades. A company issuing or buying back its own shares is has no need to be authorised. A bank providing finance to another person and accepting an instrument acknowledging the debt, for example a bank buying the debt instruments issued by a company, is also excluded.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Although a client provides a firm with specific instructions, the firm executes the trade in accordance with their own trade execution policy. What must the firm do?","options":["Advise the client by close of business on the next business day, stating the reasons for its action","Issue the client with a disclaimer","Report itself to the FCA, as it is potentially in breach of regulations","Provide the client with a copy of their trade execution policy as soon as reasonably possible"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"If a client gives specific instruction, the firm is obliged to act on them, and in doing so is deemed to have achieved 'best execution'.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of these would be exempt from the need for authorisation under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["A UK firm providing investment advice to overseas clients","A US firm providing execution services for a UK client","A French firm conducting an FX trade with a UK counterparty","A UK firm conducting MiFID business with a German client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The French firm will be excluded from the need for authorisation as the service being provided is not a regulated activity.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In the absence of agreement to the contrary, what must the firm do if a per se eligible counterparty asks to be subject to conduct of business protections?","options":["Re-categorise it as a retail client","Re-categorise it as a professional client","Recategorise it as an eligible counterparty","The firm has to refuse the request"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In this situation the client must be recategorised as a professional client and given more protection. If they request, they can also be classified as a retail client.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under the Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act, when can the firm be criminally liable?","options":["When the employee commits a fraud to benefit the firm and the firm lacked reasonable fraud prevention procedures","When the employee commits a fraud causing client loss and the firm lacked approved whistleblowing procedures","When the employee commits a fraud within the UK and the firm lacked a nominated money laundering officer","When the employee commits a fraud for personal gain and the firm lacked annual compliance training"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The offence applies where an employee, agent, subsidiary or other associated person commits fraud intending to benefit the organisation and the organisation did not have reasonable fraud prevention procedures in place. The other options introduce conditions not required for this offence.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"An authorised fund manager manages an Undertakings for Collective Investment in Transferable Securities (UCITS) scheme. Which document must it produce for investors?","options":["A key features illustration","A suitability report","A key investor information document","A cancellation notice"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Authorised fund managers managing UCITS schemes must produce a key investor information document. The other options relate to different disclosure requirements or apply in different circumstances.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following are not protected by the conduct of business rules sourcebook?","options":["Personal recommendations to a professional client","Pension transfers for retail client","Providing discretionary managed services for a professional client","Property purchases by a retail client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The purchase of property is not classed as a designated investment business.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A firm has received a sell limit order from a professional client. The order is the tenth order received from that client over a period of three months. The order cannot be executed immediately through the firm. Which of the following reasons would justify the firm not making the order public on the LSE?","options":["The frequency of trading is too high","The firm has the discretion to not make the order public","The client has instructed otherwise","The firm is required to make the order public"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Limit orders from all customers, not executed immediately, must be made public.\nThe only exceptions to this requirement is if the client instructs otherwise, or if the order volume is greater than normal market size.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"To whom would a firm report suspicions of money laundering?","options":["National Crime Agency","Tax and Chancery Chamber of the Upper Tribunal","Department for Business, Innovation and Skills","Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Firms would report any suspicions to the National Crime Agency.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true of insider dealing?","options":["It only applies to dealings via a regulated market","Gilts are included in the law on insider dealing","The FCA is responsible for prosecuting offences","An exemption exists if it can be shown that the individual would have acted in the same way without the information"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The legislation applies to dealings on: a UK, EEA or Gibraltar regulated market MTF or OTF; NASDAQ; SIX or NYSE; or by or via a professional intermediary.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following statements correctly distinguishes between criminal market manipulation (defined under ss89-91 FSA 2012) and civil offences of market abuse (defined in UK MAR)?","options":["Criminal market manipulation relates solely to the manipulation of market prices, while civil market abuse covers insider dealing only","Criminal market manipulation applies to all regulated markets, while civil market abuse only applies to securities markets","Criminal market manipulation and civil market abuse are essentially the same and carry exactly the same penalties","Criminal market manipulation is punishable by imprisonment, while civil market abuse can result in fines but not imprisonment"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Criminal market manipulation, prosecuted under sections 89-91 of the Financial Services Act 2012, can result in imprisonment upon conviction, reflecting its status as a criminal offence. In contrast, civil market abuse, regulated under the UK Market Abuse Regulation, typically results in financial penalties, such as fines, but does not carry the possibility of imprisonment since it is a civil rather than criminal matter.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following statements is true under the FCA's periodic reporting rules?","options":["The firm must send out a periodic statement every three months without exception","Investment managers must provide periodic statements even if another party provides them","Where a client receives deal-by-deal confirmations, no periodic statement is required","Where a client has a leveraged portfolio, the periodic statement must be sent out monthly"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"There are exceptions to the three-month rule. Where a client has a leveraged portfolio, the periodic statement must be sent out monthly. Where a client receives deal-by-deal confirmations, periodic statement can be sent out annually. Investment managers need not provide periodic statements if another party provides them.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under FCA regulations, which of the following is true of a written financial promotion?","options":["A firm may not arrange for a third party to carry out the confirmation exercise","A firm can arrange for a third party to carry out the confirmation exercise and the responsibility for the promotion lies with the third party","A confirmation exercise is not required for a non-real time promotion","A firm can arrange for a third party to carry out the confirmation exercise and the responsibility for the promotion remains with the firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Notice that there is a difference between confirmation and approval. Approval would lead to the approving firm taking on the responsibility. Confirmation, effectively proof reading, would leave the responsibility with the original firm.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following complies with COBS rule 4.8 on cold calls? Assume all calls are unsolicited by the client.","options":["A call to a prospective client who would envisage a call were they to become a client","A call that relates to a retail investment product, such as an investment trust share","A call to a retail client, where the investment being sold is a blue-chip stock","A call to a retail client, relating to a controlled activity in readily realisable securities"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"We can cold call:\nExisting clients who envisage a call - (not potential clients who would if they were)\nRegarding generally marketable packaged products (not retail investment products), which might include an investment trust saving scheme, but not direct investment in an investment trust shares.\nRegarding a controlled activity (e.g. advice) relating to deposits, readily realisable securities (other than warrants) or generally marketable packaged products (N.B. It is 'controlled activities' that can be provided, not the securities themselves, hence why C is incorrect).","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A person discharging managerial responsibilities plans to sell shares in their own listed company 20 days before the announcement of its semi-annual results. Under the rules, how is this normally treated?","options":["It is prohibited because the transaction falls within the 30-day closed period","It is permitted because semi-annual results announcements do not trigger a closed period","It is permitted if the sale is reported to the Financial Conduct Authority within three days","It is prohibited only if the person also possesses inside information at the time of sale"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The source states that persons discharging managerial responsibilities are restricted from conducting transactions in their company's shares or related instruments during the closed period, defined as 30 calendar days before the announcement of the company's interim or annual financial report. The other options either ignore the closed period or add conditions not stated in the provided source.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"All of the following statements regarding Chinese walls are true except:","options":["They are a method by which a firm can avoid a conflict of interests","They may be physical or artificial barriers preventing the flow of information","The FCA specify that Chinese walls must be implemented in all asset management firms","They may be operated by firms with several departments"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA defends the use of Chinese walls but does not require Chinese walls to be put in place in all firms without exception.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"In relation to the provision of research under the Conduct of Business Sourcebook, a broker must do which of the following?","options":["Promise favourable coverage to the subject","Receive payments from parties connected to the subject","Receive payment from the recipients of the research","Provide generic comments regarding the subject only"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Research must be charged for, rigorous, and display original thought. Brokers must not promise favourable coverage or receive payments from close connections to the subject.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which does the Capital Requirement Directive require? All firms:","options":["Should have regulatory capital at least equal to that specified","Falling under the scope of MiFID should have less than the regulatory capital specified","Should have no more than the specified regulatory capital","Under the scope of MiFID should have more than the amount of regulatory specified capital"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"CRD requires MiFID investment firms within its scope to maintain capital resources that exceed the minimum requirement prescribed by their competent authority.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"The purpose of a transaction report is:","options":["To prevent financial crime","To allow the FCA to review transactions","To create transparency of prices and volumes","To allow the Takeover Panel to monitor takeovers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A transaction report is there for settlement and regulatory purposes. It allows the FCA, in retrospect, to investigate trades. Trade reports provide market transparency.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which additional condition must be satisfied for a person to qualify as an eligible complainant for Financial Ombudsman Service purposes?","options":["The complaint must be submitted within three business days of the event","The complaint must involve a firm that is already in insolvency","The complaint must arise out of a business relationship with the firm","The complaint must relate to a loss exceeding the Ombudsman's monetary limit"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Eligibility depends not only on the status of the complainant but also on the complaint arising out of a business relationship with the firm. Examples given include being a customer, a policyholder or having a beneficial interest in a pension scheme.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Which of the following would not be an acceptable method of managing a conflict of interest?","options":["Ensuring that employees abided by the firm's personal account dealing policy","Ensuring that employees abided by a written conflicts of interest policy","Implementing and monitoring Chinese walls","Ensuring that client trades are executed first when a conflict arises"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Executing client trades first would not necessarily ensure their fair treatment.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following products or investments is covered under the FCA’s enhanced restrictions on the financial promotion of high-risk investments?","options":["Commodity derivatives","Crypto assets","Corporate bonds","Cash ISAs"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA has brought in enhanced restrictions to prevent harm to retail clients from investing in higher-risk products and investments that they do not understand and that are unsuitable for them (see FCA review PS22/10). Amongst other things, these higher-risk investments include crypto assets.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A retail client wishes to opt up to a professional client. Which of the following is a requirement under the quantitative test?","options":["10 trades in the previous quarter in the investment in question","Expertise, experience and knowledge of the investment in question","Portfolio of investment assets above €500K","At least 3 years relevant professional experience"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The requirements of the quantitative test are two of the following: Average trade frequency ten per quarter over previous four quarters (the previous quarter is not enough) Portfolio > €500k Works or worked in financial sector for at least one year in professional capacity Expertise, experience and knowledge is part of the qualitative test.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A firm is considering whether it must publicise an unexecuted client limit order. In which circumstance is the firm exempt from that requirement?","options":["Where the order is larger than normal market size","Where the order is placed outside normal market hours","Where the order is received from a professional client","Where the order is transmitted by an appointed representative"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A firm is not required to make the order public where the order is over normal market size. The other circumstances are not stated as exemptions from the publication requirement.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is a statutory objective of the FCA?","options":["Reducing the incidence of money laundering","Protecting the integrity of the financial system","Regulating financial services firms","Contributing to the securing of an appropriate degree of protection for those who are or may become policyholders of insurance companies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"'Protecting the integrity of the financial system' is the correct answer.\nPart of the integrity objective is to reduce all financial crime, not just money laundering. The insurance company objective is a statutory objective of the PRA, not the FCA.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A wholesale firm helps design a retail product but has no direct relationship with end customers. When can the Consumer Duty still apply to it?","options":["When its actions could determine or materially influence retail customer outcomes","When it manufactures wholesale products that become traded on a regulated market","When the outcomes of the business affect professional clients and eligible counterparties","When it has a central role in distribution, design and client communications"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Duty can apply to wholesale firms involved in a distribution chain where their actions could determine or materially influence retail customer outcomes, such as in product design, distribution or communications. It is applied on a proportionate basis, so firms remote from retail customers may have more limited obligations.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A firm arranges an appointment during an unwritten promotion made outside its premises. What must it give the client in that situation?","options":["It must give the client a cooling-off notice before the appointment is confirmed","It must give the client a contact point if an appointment is arranged","It must give the client a suitability report before the meeting takes place","It must give the client a cancellation form when the meeting is scheduled"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The rules on unwritten promotions require the firm to give the client a contact point if an appointment is arranged. The other documents listed are not specified here as mandatory simply because an appointment has been made.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of these is true in relation to comparisons provided in a promotion for a MiFID investment?","options":["A distinction must be made between absolute and proportional differences","The source of the data must be stated","The comparison must explain that the results depend on the individual circumstances of each client","The rules are most stringent for qualifying credit, home purchase plans or home reversion plans"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If comparisons are made, they must be meaningful and presented in a fair and balanced way. For MiFID business, the data sources for the comparisons must be cited, as must any key facts and assumptions used. If tax treatment is mentioned, firms must explain that this depends on the individual circumstances of each client and that it may be subject to change. Information included in financial promotions must be consistent with that given in the course of carrying on business. The rules do not apply for qualifying credit, home purchase plans or home reversion plans.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under the FCA Training and Competency rules, who must be supervised?","options":["All staff performing any activity","All staff performing specified activities","Staff not yet deemed competent performing specified activities","Staff not yet deemed competent performing any activity"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under the Training and Competency rules, firms must not allow an employee to carry on any of the specified activities without appropriate supervision. Firms are required to ensure that employees are appropriately supervised at all times.\nThe specified activities include the following, when conducted with or for retail clients:\n- advising on/dealing in/managing investments\n- acting as broker fund adviser\n- acting as pension transfer specialist\n- safeguarding assets or client money\n- advising on regulated mortgages and home finance","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following is true of the Financial Action Task Force?","options":["Their mission focuses on anti-money laundering and combating terrorist financing only","It imposes legislation on its member countries and jurisdictions","It continuously adapts its standards to address new risks","It leads international prosecution against human trafficking and illegal drugs"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FATF continuously adapts its standards to address new risks. It standards are recommendations for its members rather than law, but it does encourage member to build legislation around the standards. FATF focus on more than just money laundering and terrorist financing, they also help authorities go after criminal dealings in illegal drugs, human trafficking and other crimes, but they do not lead the prosecution.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following gives the FCA the power to prosecute insider dealing offences?","options":["Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","Criminal Justice Act 1993","The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Companies Act 2006"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The offence is contained within the CJA 1993, however the ability of the FCA to prosecute comes from ss 401-402 FSMA 2000.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following is true with regard to the Training and Competence Sourcebook?","options":["Authorised firms are only required to ensure that their approved persons are competent for their role in the firm","FCA firm employees must have their competence regularly reviewed","Appropriate examinations must be obtained for relevant roles within three years of working in the financial services","All employees must take appropriate examinations"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under FCA Principle for Businesses 3, management and control, and SYSC 5, all employees must be competent. The approved examinations must generally be passed within 30 months. Appropriate examinations are only a requirement for certain functions, including most dealing and advising roles.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of these is an eligible claimant under the FSCS?","options":["Trustees of a CIS","Trustee of a pension fund","Overseas financial institution","A shareholder who owns < 5% of an organisation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Eligible claimants are certain persons who have a protected claim against an authorised person who is in default. This includes most clients of the defaulting firm, except:\n- Other authorised firms\n- Overseas financial institutions\n- Supranational institutions, governments and central administrative authorities\n- Provincial, regional, local and municipal authorities; and\n- Large companies or large mutual associations","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"A firm discovers that it can execute a trade more favourably than the specific client instruction it has received. According to the specific client instructions rules, how should the firm proceed?","options":["Execute using the alternative method","Consult with the firm's risk committee","Refuse to execute","Execute according to the client's instructions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The rules on specific client instructions state that whenever a firm receives a specific instruction from a client, it must execute the order as instructed. The firm will be deemed to have provided the best possible result, even if an alternative means of execution would have given a better result.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of these is an eligible complainant under the FOS?","options":["An individual who was a professional client at the time of the transaction","A trust with £7.5m of assets","A charity with £10m of income","A business with a turnover of €1.3m"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The following meet the requirements for an eligible complainant:\n- A consumer\n- An enterprise with fewer than ten employees and turnover or annual balance sheet not exceeding €2 million (a micro enterprise) at the time the complaint is raised\n- Charities with an annual income of less than £6.5m at the time the complaint is raised; or\n- A trustee of a trust with a net asset value of less than £5m at the time the complaint is raised","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"An unsolicited real-time communication is simply an elaborate regulatory description of a cold call, but what can be promoted via such a communication?","options":["Readily realisable securities which include warrants","A personal pension","Higher volatility fund","Geared packaged products"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm may only make a cold call in one of the following three circumstances:\n- The recipient of the cold call has an established existing client relationship with the firm and envisages receiving cold calls\n- The cold call relates to a generally marketable product (which is not a higher volatility fund)\n- The cold call relates to activities where the only investments involved are readily realisable securities (other than warrants) and generally marketplace non-geared packaged products.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A market maker has their trade for depository receipts executed with a broker on the International Order Book, which is an electronic order driven system. In relation to the trade report, which of the following statements is correct?","options":["The market maker is obligated to report the trade within three minutes or before 5.15 p.m.","Both parties will need to make the trade report to the FCA no later than the end of the next business day","The broker is obligated to send the trade report to the market place within five minutes","The trade will be automatically reported and no action is needed by either party of the trade"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"As the trade is performed on an electronic order-driven system, trade reports are generated automatically and there is no need for participants to report the trades manually.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"What must a firm do when a client has placed a limit order in respect of shares traded on a regulated market and the order is not immediately executed by the firm under prevailing market conditions?","options":["Make the order public with a view to having it executed as soon as possible","Immediately complete the transaction by acting as principal in respect of the unexecuted portion of the order","Use its best endeavours to match the order with another client order","Submit a report to the market operator by the end of the trading day"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Any limit order that cannot be immediately executed by the firm will normally be made public by putting it on an order book trading system. Exceptions to this rule are when the client instructs the firm not to do so, or where the order is significantly large (approximately 10% of the average daily volume).","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A director shares non-public price-sensitive information with a friend who has no role in the transaction. How is this most likely to be treated under UK Market Abuse Regulation?","options":["As permitted disclosure because the information was shared outside market hours","As permitted disclosure because the recipient was not acting for personal gain","As unlawful disclosure because the sharing was outside the normal exercise of duties","As unlawful disclosure only if the friend later trades in the relevant securities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under UK MAR, unlawful disclosure is the sharing of inside information with another person unless the disclosure is part of the normal exercise of an employment, profession or duties. It is not necessary, on the provided source, for the recipient to have traded before the disclosure is unlawful.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Arthur has telephoned his financial advisor, asking them to post him a copy of the suitability report relating to a stakeholder pension they recommended to Arthur four years ago. To comply with consumer duty obligations and all other relevant regulations, what should the financial advisor do next?","options":["Warn Arthur that since the original suitability report would have been read to Arthur over the phone, a copy won’t have been kept","Produce a paper copy of the report, and post this to Arthur’s home address","Apologise to Arthur and explain that since stakeholder pensions are a non-MiFID investment, the copy was only kept for three years before being destroyed","Give Arthur the contact details of the authorised firm managing Arthur’s stakeholder pension and direct Arthur to call them"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Copies of suitability reports in relation to stakeholder pensions are required to be kept by the advisory firm (not the pension management firm) for five years (COBS 9A). These must be communicated to retail clients via a durable medium (COBS 9A).","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Fatima's role involves dealing with customers, specified as a significant harm function by the regulator. Under the certification regime, which of the following is true?","options":["Fatima needs regulatory pre-approval by the regulator before performing the role","Fatima needs to be certified by her firm before performing the role only","Fatima needs to be certified by her firm before performing the role and annually thereafter","Fatima could perform the role without the need for an assessment of fitness and propriety"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In order to perform a significant harm function, Fatima must first be assessed as fit and proper by her firm. This fit and proper test will than be repeated on an annual basis.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"According to the FCA's client money rules (CASS 7), which of the following is considered ‘client money'?","options":["Money held by a firm as collateral for a client's margin obligation","Money owed by the client to the firm","Money received by the firm from a client in relation to a proposed transaction","Money invested by the firm on behalf of the client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"According to the FCA's CASS 7 client money rules, client money is any money that a firm receives or holds on behalf of a client in relation to the provision of designated investment business. This includes money received from a client in relation to a transaction, such as payment for a financial product or service. The rules are designed to ensure that client money is properly protected and segregated from the firm's own funds.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is the best description of those who qualify as an ‘eligible claimant’ for the Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS) in the UK?","options":["UK citizens aged 18 and above only","Retail clients who have lost money due to poor financial advice","Both individual and small business customers of UK authorised firms","All UK clients of authorised firms regardless of their financial status"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS) is designed to provide compensation to eligible claimants if a regulated financial services firm fails or is unable to meet its financial obligations. ‘Eligible complainants’ include both individuals and small businesses, but does not include many larger clients, such as large companies, government or local authorities, etc.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Which of the following requires immediate notification by the firm to the PRA?\nIWhen it has breached the financial resources requirement\nIIWhen it believes that it may shortly breach the financial resources requirement\nIIIWhen it receives a complaint from a client\nIVWhen a systems failure means that the firm is unable to produce accounting records which disclose the financial position of the company","options":["I and II","I and III","II and IV","I, II and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Complaints must be recorded by the firm but there is no requirement to notify the PRA of all complaints received.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A UK asset manager receives macro-economic research that is openly available to firms and the general public. How must that research be treated under the FCA rules?","options":["It may be received free because it is openly available to the public","It may be received free because it falls within the SME research exemption","It must be paid for because it does not meet the definition of FICC research","It must be paid for unless it is bundled with execution services after August 2024"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Openly available research can be received free of charge.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An authorised firm wishes to conduct business over the internet with its clients. Which of the following is NOT an obligation on the firm when considering this form of business?","options":["Check the client wishes to communicate electronically","Explain such communication is legally binding","Explain that orders received electronically may not be acted on immediately","Ensure the security of the system"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"All orders should be processed immediately, although actual execution may take longer.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following represents an acceptable inducement under COBS?","options":["Membership fees to professional bodies","Management fees","Office administration software","Travel expenses"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Allowable services are those that would be directly relevant to customers.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A firm is considering its reporting obligations for a derivatives contract under UK European Market Infrastructure Regulation (UK EMIR). Which statement is correct?","options":["Only the financial counterparty must report the trade unless the client is a professional client","Only the executing broker must report the trade unless the trade is exchange-traded","Both counterparties must report the trade unless one reports on behalf of both by prior arrangement","Both counterparties must report the trade only where both are authorised firms in the UK"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under UK EMIR, all trades in relation to derivatives contracts must be reported to a trade repository and both counterparties must report each trade. By prior arrangement, one party may report on behalf of both counterparties, provided that this is indicated in the report.","chId":"reg-ch1"}]},{"id":"mock-5","title":"Mock Exam 5","mcqs":[{"question":"A firm relies on a third-party firm for client information in relation to UK MiFID business. Which party remains responsible for the completeness and accuracy of that information?","options":["The receiving firm only","The executing venue only","The third-party firm","The client alone"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The third-party firm retains responsibility for the completeness and accuracy of client information and for the appropriateness of any recommendations it provides. The receiving firm instead takes responsibility for concluding the services or transactions.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Under the Criminal Justice Act, which of the following constitutes a special defence against an insider dealing prosecution?","options":["Did not expect to make a profit","Market information","Believed the information was widely known","Would have dealt anyway"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The other three would be general defences against an allegation.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Under the SM&CR set out by the FCA, which one of the following is least likely to be a senior manager function?","options":["Non-executive director","Customer advisor supervisor","Money Laundering Reporting Officer","Senior manager responsible for effecting contracts of insurance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A customer advisor supervisor is most likely to be a certified, not approved, person.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A graduate has just been employed by a bank to perform the role of a pension transfer adviser. Which of the following is required of the firm before they take up their position. The firm must ensure the individual has:","options":["completed every single module of a relevant exam and they have completed training on money laundering and recognising suspicious transactions","completed training on money laundering and recognising suspicious transactions only, as they can perform their role if supervised by an experienced colleague","been certified to perform their role by the regulator under the headings of the fit and proper test","been approved to perform their role by a relevant regulatory body"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"When acting a pension transfer specialist, the individual must have attained the full qualification before commencing the activity, even if under supervision. Relevant employees must be made aware of the law relating to money laundering and terrorist financing and be given regular training on how to recognise and deal with suspicious transactions.\n\nAs a role requiring examinations, the pension transfer specialist will require certification, but this is given by the firm not the regulator.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following would best describe criminal property?","options":["Property which is, or represents, a person's benefit from criminal conduct, where the alleged offender knows or suspects that it is such","Assets which are, or represents, a person's benefit from criminal conduct, where the alleged offender knows that it is such","Property which is, or represents, a person's benefit from criminal conduct, where the alleged offender knows that it is such","Cash which is, or represents, a person's benefit from criminal conduct, where the alleged offender knows that it is such"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"\"Criminal property - that is, any benefit (money or otherwise) that has arisen from criminal conduct. Property is criminal property only if the alleged offender knows or suspects it is criminal property.\"","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which one of the following does a firm NOT need to obtain information about in order to assess suitability?","options":["The investment objectives of the client","The client's investment experience","The identity of the client's existing investments","The client's financial ability to bear risk"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The firm is NOT required to obtain information about its client's existing investments.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Using which of the following methods would it not be possible for a firm to provide a professional client with a basic written agreement?","options":["DVD","Email","Letter","Website"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"‘A basic written agreement’ is referring to a client agreement, which has to be on paper or other ‘durable medium’ (COBS 8A). Information conveyed on a web page does not automatically meet the requirements for a durable medium.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which factor is identified as relevant when the Financial Conduct Authority decides whether to accept a market practice as an accepted market practice?","options":["The structure of the market, the instruments traded and the extent of retail participation","The profitability of the strategy, the holding period and the number of intermediaries used","The seniority of the trader, the venue rules and the issuer's dividend policy","The tax treatment of the instrument, the settlement cycle and the client's risk profile"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA takes into account criteria including the structure of the market, the types of instruments traded and the extent of retail participation when deciding whether to accept a market practice. The other factors are not listed in the provided source.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"What must a person discharging managerial responsibilities do after carrying out a personal transaction in the issuer's shares outside a closed period?","options":["Notify the issuer promptly and no later than the next business day following the trade date","Notify the issuer and the FCA promptly and no later than three days after the transaction","Notify the market through a Regulatory Information Service as soon as reasonably practicable","Notify the chair of the board and the company's registrar within two business days of the trade"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The source states that persons discharging managerial responsibilities must notify the issuer and the FCA of relevant personal transactions promptly, no later than three days after the transaction. The other options either omit the FCA, impose a different timing requirement or add recipients not identified in the source.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"The Training and Competency (TC) Sourcebook emphasises a high-level approach, where firms must employ personnel with the skills, knowledge and expertise necessary to discharge their responsibilities. If an employee, when offering services to retail clients, offers basic advice only, which of the following is TRUE?","options":["The customer is of limited understanding with regards to financial services and only simply language is allowed","The client has been subject to an extensive fact-find which includes the collection of both hard and soft facts","The product being advised upon is a stakeholder pension","The client would only like advice on products for which there is Key Information Document"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Where a firm is providing advisory services, which consists of basic advice, the only product to which this relates is a stakeholder pension.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which one of the following is overseen jointly by both the FCA and PRA?","options":["UKLA","FOS","CMA","FSCS"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FCA is also known as the UK Listing Authority (UKLA). It oversees the Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS) by itself. The Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) is an entirely separate government regulator.\nThe Financial Services and Compensation Scheme (FSCS) is overseen by both the FCA and PRA.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"What is the primary purpose of the personal account dealing rules?","options":["To prevent relevant persons from undertaking transactions that misuse confidential information or conflict with duties owed to clients","To prevent relevant persons from trading in overseas securities or complex derivatives without senior management approval","To prevent relevant persons from investing in designated investments during market hours when client focus should be applied","To prevent relevant persons from holding units in collective investment schemes or independently managed fund accounts"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The rules require firms to prevent relevant persons from entering into personal transactions that are contrary to UK Market Abuse Regulation, misuse confidential information or conflict with the firm's duties to a customer. The other options impose restrictions that are not stated as the purpose of the rules.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"The Senior Management and Arrangements, Systems and Control (SYSC) regulations encourage directors and senior managers of authorised firms to do all of the following, EXCEPT:","options":["Take appropriate responsibility for the apportionment of responsibilities","Take appropriate responsibility for the systems and controls of the firm","Take care to install avenues of disclosure for workers' concerns in relation to 'whistleblowing'","Take more time to identify transactions involving money laundering"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SYSC 2 - Apportionment of Responsibilities\nSYSC 3 - Systems and Controls\nSYSC 4 - Workers' concerns in relation to 'Whistleblowing'\nAlthough SYSC now includes rules on the responsibilities of senior manager with regard to money laundering, it does not dictate the time managers get to identify transactions relating to this offence.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A consumer is requesting access to more exotic product, faster transmission speeds and lower costs from an authorised firm. The firm has completed its due diligence for assessment of election for MiFID activities, all qualitative tests have been passed but the consumer cannot fulfil the requirements of the quantitative test. Which of the following would explain this?","options":["The firm does not believe that they have sufficient expertise in the area requested","The consumer has requested to be reclassified, listing which specific sectors and /or product but only in a durable medium","They have carried out on average eight significantly sized trades per quarter over the last year","The consumer has had over 5 years experience as a professional in the financial services sector"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The qualitative test has been passed,(therefore expertise is satisfied) we only need to concentrate on the quantitative tests for MiFID business. Any two of the following are true:\nThe client carried out, on average, ten significantly sized transactions on the relevant market in each of the past four quarters;\nThe size of the client’s financial portfolio exceeds €500,000 (defined as including cash deposits and financial instruments);\nThe client works or has worked as a professional in the financial services sector for at least a year on a basis which requires knowledge of the transactions envisaged.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"What sort of financial promotion may a firm not approve on behalf of an unauthorised firm?","options":["Telephone calls","Unsolicited mail shots","Online advertising","TV and radio marketing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An authorised firm cannot approve a non-written communication as it could easily be changed.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In the event that a firm breaches the general prohibition, which of the following is TRUE?","options":["All of the firm's contracts are IMMEDIATELY void","Any agreements with the firm are unenforceable against the client","Contracts are voidable at the discretion of the court","Any agreements with the client are unenforceable against the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In essence, contracts are voidable at the discretion of the client.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to:","options":["Exclude any contract terms in its documentation aimed at consumers","Define unfair terms in all consumer contracts in the United Kingdom","Force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms","Provide compensation for those subject to unfair contract terms"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Ibrahim is an asset manager at a UK domiciled fund management firm, using research concerning small and medium sized companies. What is the maximum company size by market capitalisation that would exempt Ibrahim from the need to pay for the research?","options":["£50 million","£100 million","£200 million","£500 million"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Research on companies with a market capitalisation of up to £200 million are exempt from the Inducement Rules – meaning that UK-based fund managers do not have to pay for this research. The exemption will be assessed by the FCA three years after the implementation of this exemption - this will be on 1 March 2025.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which statement best describes the role of the Financial Action Task Force (FATF) in relation to UK financial services regulation?","options":["It issues binding UK rules on anti-money laundering for authorised firms","It sets international standards that influence legislative and regulatory reform","It approves UK supervisory guidance on customer due diligence procedures","It enforces UK criminal sanctions for terrorist financing offences"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FATF is a global anti-money laundering and terrorist financing body that sets international standards and works to generate political will for national legislative and regulatory reforms. Its recommendations shape regulation, but it does not issue binding UK rules or enforce UK sanctions.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A fund manager sends out a mailshot blindly to several clients. The email contains information regarding the manager's products and his website. Which of the following is covered under the Distance Marketing Directive?","options":["The email but not the website","The website but not the email","Neither the email nor the website","The email and the website"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Since the email promotes the website, both would be covered under the directive.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A per se professional client asks an authorised firm for it to be categorised as a retail client for extra protection for MiFID business. Which is TRUE about the firm's response?","options":["A report of market manipulation should be made IMMEDIATELY to the FCA","Re-categorisation is possible without telling the client","The firm would gain client agreement regarding re-categorisation","Although the firm must do it, they can wait until the client has signed a risk warning"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Risk warnings would be signed if the client were opting-up. All clients are permitted to ask for extra protection by being re-categorised. This re-categorisation can be, generally, regarding specific services or regarding specific investments.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following identified personnel would be subject to the firm’s personal account dealing rules for both MiFID and non-MiFID business?","options":["An individual who is directly involved in the provision of investment management services on behalf of the firm","A non-executive director of a competitor, who intends to execute similar trades to those of the firm","An employee of the firm who performs a trade validation, verification and reporting role within the institution","Any employee who is employed in the wholesale business side of an authorised firm"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Personnel (other than directors or partners of a firm or its appointed representatives) are only subject to the personal account dealing rules to the extent which they are involved in the provision of the firm’s investment or portfolio management services.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following is responsible for regulating the capital adequacy of large investment firms in the UK?","options":["Prudential Regulation Authority","Financial Policy Committee","European Securities and Markets Authority","Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The PRA will regulate the capital adequacy of deposit takers, insurers and large investment firms. The FCA will regulate the conduct of these firms.\nThe FCA will regulate the capital requirements and the conduct of all other financial firms in the UK.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"As part of the requirements set out in SYSC, which of the following is TRUE?","options":["Responsibilities must be split equally among all directors","All firms must maintain clear apportionment of significant responsibilities among directors","Records of responsibilities must be held for a five-year period of time","Directors cannot be held liable for rule breaches by employees"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The split does not necessarily need to be equal, records must be held for six years, and directors are liable for all rule breaches in their area.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Who can make a mass detriment reference to the FCA?","options":["The Financial Ombudsman Service","HM Treasury","Certain consumer bodies designated by HM Treasury","The Financial Services Compensation Scheme"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Mass detriment references can be made to the FCA either by the Financial Ombudsman Service, or by firms themselves.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"What is the maximum that can be claimed from the FSCS by eligible claimants in respect of sterling deposit losses in a single institution?","options":["£85,000 per individual","£120,000 per deposit","£120,000 per individual","£85,000 per deposit"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The maximum payout is based on the default of a deposit provider of a value up to £120,000. An individual could have multiple sterling deposits in various institutions, and the FSCS would guarantee up to £120,000 in each case.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"At what time of day may a cold call be made?","options":["9:00am - 9:00pm","Normal business hours","Any time except on a Sunday","An appropriate time"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"There is no time restriction for cold calls provided they are made at an appropriate time of day.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An adviser must disclose to a client the scope of their advice. Where a personal recommendation to a retail client concerning a retail investment product meets the requirements of independent advice, how is this defined in regulation under COBS 6.2B.11? Independent advice must be:","options":["be sufficiently diverse with regard to their type and product providers and not be limited to products issued or provided by the firm itself or others that have close links to the firm","be limited with regard to their type, and number product providers and not be extended beyond products issued or provided by the firm itself or other that have close links to the firm","be sufficiently diverse with regard to their type, and product providers but be limited to products issued or provided by the firm itself or provided by others that have close links to the firm","be limited with regard to their type, and product provider but not be limited to products issued or provided by the firm itself or others that have close links to the firm"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"COBS 6.2B.11R states independent advice must: 1. be sufficiently diverse regarding their: type, and issuers or product providers, to ensure the client’s investment objectives can be suitably met, and 2. not be limited to relevant products issued or provided by: the firm itself or by entities that have close links to the firm.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"According to the Conduct of Business Rules, in which of the following situations would an authorised firm need to assess whether an investment or service is appropriate for a client?","options":["Providing advice on investments of a complex nature to an inexperienced client","Arranging a deal in a warrant for a retail client who has responded to a direct offer","Engaging in eligible counterparty business in relation to derivatives","Managing a retail client's portfolio on a discretionary basis"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Appropriateness needs to be assessed for business other than advice and investment management. It does not apply to eligible counterparties.\nAppropriateness needs to be assessed when a firm arranges or deals in relation to a warrant or derivative where the client has responded to a direct offer financial promotion.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A firm prepares a product disclosure document for an Undertakings for Collective Investment in Transferable Securities (UCITS) scheme. Which content must be included?","options":["A summary of the firm's conflicts policy and complaints procedure","A presentation of past performance or, where relevant, performance scenarios","A transcript of all portfolio manager meetings held during the year","A forecast of the exact return that the scheme will achieve"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The KIID must include essential information such as identification of the scheme, objectives and policy, past performance or performance scenarios, costs and charges and a risk and reward profile. The other options are not listed as required KIID content.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Siobhan is employed by an FCA investment firm. She is executing orders in listed securities, both from external clients and from the firm’s own wealth management team. According to the FCA's client order handling rules (COBS 11), how must Siobhan treat client orders?","options":["Siobhan is not allowed to allocate investments under any circumstances","Siobhan can aggregate client orders, but only if it's in the best interest of the clients","Siobhan is permitted to allocate investments only to her firm’s most profitable clients","Siobhan is able to aggregate client orders without any restrictions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) has established guidelines for order execution, aggregation and allocation as part of its Conduct of Business Sourcebook (COBS 11.3). According to these rules, firms may aggregate client orders but they must reasonably believe that it is in the best interest of their clients and that it will not work overall to the disadvantage of any client whose order is to be aggregated. This is to ensure fair treatment and transparency, and to prevent firms from giving preferential treatment to certain clients or to themselves.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"An employee belongs to a professional body and their employer has a less demanding internal code of conduct. Which standard should the employee follow?","options":["The employer's internal code because firm policies override external standards","Neither code unless the regulator confirms one or other is binding","The professional body's code because it takes precedence over the firm's code","The less detailed code because it reduces the risk of breaching conflicting rules"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Members of professional bodies are expected to comply with the professional body's code of conduct and that it takes precedence over the firm's internal code, even though this may create an ethical dilemma. The other options are inconsistent with that hierarchy.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"What is the expectation on how the Bank of England deals with threats to financial stability?","options":["Through cross-party committees to discuss outcomes-based regulation","Through reporting to HM Treasury who deals with fiscal policy","Through delegating to the PRA to ensure firms are safe and sound","Through financial and non-financial operations both at home and abroad"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Maintaining financial stability involves protecting against threats to the whole financial system. The BoE’s surveillance and market intelligence functions detect such threats to the financial system. The threats are then dealt with through financial, and other, operations, both at home and abroad. In exceptional circumstances, the BoE may act as the lender of last resort by extending credit to banks, and other financial institutions, when no other financial institutions will.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"When confirming whether a financial promotion complies with the financial promotions regulations, which of the following is TRUE?","options":["There is no need for authorised firms to confirm compliance","Confirmation of compliance must be made by a person with appropriate expertise","Confirmations of the promotions must be made by the communicating firm","Confirmations of the promotions must be made by somebody other than the communicating firm to ensure no conflict of interest"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The person checking the promotion must have appropriate expertise.\nIf a firm is not authorised, they must get the promotion checked by an authorised firm.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"When an appointed representative is communicating a promotion to retail clients, and making appointments, which of the following is not required?","options":["The detailed risks of the investment","Whether the client wishes to continue","The caller's name","A contact point to cancel the appointment"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The detailed risks of the investment can be discussed at a later date.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is TRUE regarding the conflicts of interest rules in the Conduct of Business sourcebook?","options":["The policy can be in writing or delivered orally","It must state how the firm will use Chinese walls to manage conflicts","The policy must specify procedures for managing the conflicts","It has to identify all existing conflicts"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The conflicts of interest policy must:\n- Be in writing - Identify potential conflicts (not all existing conflicts) - Specify procedures to manage conflicts - this does not have to include the use of Chinese walls.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following is not one of the types of market abuse set out in the FCA's Code of Market Conduct?","options":["Misleading impressions","Manipulating devices","Dissemination","Benchmark manipulation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"'Misleading impressions' gives the spirit of MAR examples of market manipulation, but is not defined by the FCA's Code of Market Conduct as a type of market abuse. Misleading Impressions is a criminal offence under s90 FSA 2012.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"For which personal account purchase would it be necessary for a relevant employee to obtain prior permission to deal, according to the personal account dealing rules?","options":["Shares in a UCITS scheme by the employee","A life policy","A fund managed by an independent broker","Shares purchased by the employee on behalf of her spouse"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All of the trades mentioned here are specifically excluded for personal account dealings, except the purchase of shares. The fact that the shares are purchased for the employee's spouse is irrelevant.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Where an individual has fulfilled the criteria for approval, which of the following is TRUE in respect of the continuing obligation of ensuring fitness and propriety of the those performing this function?","options":["The authorised firm is required to get the individual to provide a portfolio of evidence to demonstrate how they are able to display fitness and propriety at quarterly intervals","The competent regulatory authority is to satisfy themselves on at least an annual interview with the individual, that they display ongoing fitness and propriety","The annual assessment is only for certified persons; once in role, an approved person only needs to be reassessed when applying for further approval","The authorised firm are required to satisfy themselves on at least an annual basis that the individual displays ongoing fitness and propriety"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The obligation to demonstrate that an individual is fit and proper is not limited to the approval process. Under the SM&CR, authorised firms are required to satisfy themselves on at least an annual basis as to the ongoing fitness and propriety of those individuals for whom approval to perform an SMF has been granted.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"When must confirmation of a transaction be dispatched to a retail client?","options":["Immediately","No later than the end of that business day","No later than the end of the next business day","As soon as reasonably practicable"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The requirement for trade confirmations remains trade date +1 business day in the Conduct of Business Rules.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"The directors of a UK financial institution use an overseas agent who, it has been proved, has accepted and used bribes in their activities. Which of the following would be a valid defence against any of the four offences of the Bribery Act 2010?","options":["The directors are not responsible for the action of an agent","The agent's acceptance of bribes took place overseas","The directors could prove that adequate procedures are in place to prevent bribery","The directors can prove that they did not benefit directly from the proceeds of the bribes"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Full defence may be possible if there is proof of adequate procedures being in place to prevent bribery and these can be clearly demonstrated.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A Money Laundering Reporting Officer (MLRO) has not organised any money laundering training in the firm for the past 30 months. Two new employees, who joined the firm last month, have been found to have been involved in handling criminal funds. The criminals have since fled the UK. Which of the following parties are the FCA most likely to successfully prosecute?","options":["The two new employees who are personally liable","The MLRO who is personally liable","The two new employees and the MLRO who are personally liable","The firm which is liable as an entity"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"As an approved person, the MLRO will be prosecuted by the FCA for breach of the Money Laundering Regulations (MLR).\nThe MLRO is required to organise training at least every 24 months, and therefore the two new employees would have a defence against an accusation of money laundering. They have not received the necessary training, and thus did not know any better.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"The employee of a sponsoring financial services firm suspects that the company they are helping to list on the London Stock Excahge may be involved with money laundering. To whom would they report this information?","options":["NCA or FCA","MLRO or Nominated officer","PRA or Nominated officer","MLRO or FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Suspicion of money laundering should be reported to the MLRO by the employee that suspects. If the organisation does not have an MLRO it should be reported to the Nominated officer.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following situations would require the firm to enter into a client agreement, with the production of such agreement in a durable medium?","options":["For all MiFID and ancillary services, when engaged in this business for any retail and professional client","For all regulated activities as defined under FSMA 2000 and for all clients","For all designated investment business and when engaged in this business with any type of client","For all ancillary MiFID services only and when engaged in this business for any retail and professional client"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The requirement to enter into a client agreement applies to designated investment business for a retail client and for MiFID business and ancillary services or equivalent third-country business carried on for a retail or professional client.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"In which of the following circumstances would it be permissible to not give a client a client agreement?","options":["Retail and professional clients with regard to advisory MiFID business","Retail clients wanting advisory services regarding non-MiFID business","Retail clients paying for advice only with no resultant deals","Retail clients for all designated investment business"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Client agreements need to be sent out to all clients; that is retail clients for all designated investment business and professional clients for MiFID business only. Although client agreements would need to be sent to clients receiving advisory services (these assume deals are performed based on that advice), they would not be needed for advice only, where no resultant deals occur.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which legislation establishes a framework of rights and duties to safeguard personal data?","options":["DPA 2018","FIA 2000","PIDA 98","FSA 2012"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Data Protection Act 2018 is enforced by the Information Commisioner. The Freedom of Information Act 2000 provides public access to information held by public authorities. The Public Interest Disclosure Act provides whistleblowing protection.","chId":"reg-ch4"},{"question":"Where would a firm seeking authorisation to perform certain regulated activities find the statutory procedures for seeking permission to perform regulated activities?","options":["The Financial Services and Markets Act","The Criminal Justice Act 1993","The Financial Services Act 2012","The Companies Act 2006"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"FSMA Part 4A specifies the procedures for seeking permission to perform regulated activities","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which one of the following can NOT be a per se eligible counterparty?","options":["An investment bank authorised by the PRA","A central bank","Another regulated firm","A local authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All authorised or regulated firms are per se eligible counterparties.\nIn this particular question 'another regulated firm' is not to be confused with 'other institutional investors', which can only be treated no higher than per se professional client.\nNational governments are per se and not local.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A subsidiary commits a listed fraud offence intending to benefit its parent company, but no benefit is actually received. How is the position treated under the offence?","options":["The offence applies only if the parent company receives a financial benefit","The offence applies only if the fraud also causes a measurable client loss","The offence applies because intended benefit is sufficient even if no benefit is received","The offence does not apply because subsidiaries are exempt from the associated person test"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The benefit may be financial or non-financial, and an organisation does not need to actually receive any benefit because the fraud can be complete before any gain is received. The other options contradict the stated scope of the offence.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"If a firm is holding money belonging to clients, which of the following is a reason for the firm to be exempt from the CASS 7 client money rules?","options":["The money is coins held for the value of their metal","If the money is held in a central bank","The money is interest paid by a Financial Conduct Authority authorised firm","If the money is being held in a money market deposit account"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A firm does not need to apply the client money rules to coins held for the value of their metal on behalf of clients.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A financial services firm is assessing which external body may investigate mergers, anti-competitive behaviour and consumer protection issues affecting markets. Which body performs this role?","options":["The Competition and Markets Authority","HM Treasury","The Financial Conduct Authority","The Prudential Regulation Authority"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Competition and Markets Authority promotes competition for the benefit of consumers and investigates mergers, anti-competitive agreements, abuses of dominant positions and consumer protection issues. HM Treasury sets government policy, while the Financial Conduct Authority and Prudential Regulation Authority are financial regulators rather than the UK's general competition authority.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"For which of the following products is a firm required to judge appropriateness when approached by a retail client for execution only services?","options":["Small cap shares","Speculative grade bonds","Warrants","FTSE shares"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Deals in relation to derivatives or warrants for retail clients require the firm to judge appropriateness in terms of risk and understanding.\nDerivatives and warrants are known collectively as 'complex products'.\nIn contrast, execution only services in non-complex products do not require an appropriateness judgement by the firm.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A firm operates a research payment account. Which funding arrangement complies with the rules?","options":["Funding it through dealing commission collected across client transactions","Funding it through a specific charge agreed in advance with clients","Funding it through issuer contributions linked to new and follow-on issues","Funding it through execution rebates retained from the exchange by the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The research payment account must be funded only by a specific research charge to clients, agreed with clients and developed by the firm as its research budget. The other funding methods are not permitted by the source.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which of the following firms are subject to the capital adequacy rules under the Interim Prudential Sourcebook for Investment Businesses (IPRU-INV)?","options":["Credit institutions","MiFID investment firms","Lloyd's underwriting agents","Banks"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Credit institutions and banks are subject to CRD. MiFID investment firms are subject to MiFIDPRU. Everyone else is subject to IPRU-INV.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In relation to the insider dealing legislation which ONE of the following is a SPECIAL defence against insider dealing?","options":["The accused was acting on information that was publicly available","The information was being passed on in the proper course of business","The person would have dealt anyway","The individual acted in accordance with the stabilisation rules"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The specific (or special) defences are: 1. Stabilisation rules 2. Takeovers (recipients of market information) 3. Market makers","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"In order for an authorised firm to assess the suitability of a transaction in MiFID business for an elective professional client, the firm must assess investment objectives and:","options":["Nothing else","Financial situation","Investment knowledge and experience","Investment knowledge and experience, and financial situation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For an elective professional client, the firm must ascertain the investment objectives and financial situation of the client.\nIn the case of MiFID business conducted with professional clients, the firm is entitled to assume that they have the requisite investment knowledge and experience.\nIf the question had asked about a per se professional client, the firm would only have to ascertain information about the investment objectives of the client, and nothing else.\nFor retail clients the suitability rules apply to designated investment business and the firm is required to ascertain information about all three factors.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Whistleblowing procedures within an authorised firm should:","options":["Ensure all concerns are escalated to the responsible senior manager","Keep the progress on any investigation confidential, even from the whistleblower","Provide a single method of disclosure to be used by employees and other relevant staff","Include the preparation of an annual report on the effectiveness of the procedures"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Whistleblowing procedures should:\nEnsure concerns are assessed and escalated where appropirate\nProvide feedback to the whistleblower where feasible and appropriate\nAllow for disclosures to be made through a range of methods\nPrepare an annual report to the board on the effectiveness of the procedures.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Fred is accused of disseminating rumours in an attempt to induce investors to purchase an investment. Which of the following defences to Market Abuse could Fred use?","options":["The information disclosure complied with accepted market practices","Fred knew that the information was false and misleading","The false or misleading impression related to a qualifying investment","The information was communicated through an internet chat room"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The 'dissemination offence' rules refer to the market abuse legislation, where the behaviour consists of the dissemination of information by any means which gives, or is likely to give, a false or misleading impression as to a qualifying investment by a person who knew, or could reasonably be expected to have known, that the information was false or misleading.\nThe prohibition on market manipulation will not apply providing that the person carrying on any other behaviour establishes that it has been carried out for legitimate reasons considered accepted market practices.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which statement best reflects Principle 4 of the FCA's Principles for Businesses in relation to prudential standards?","options":["A firm must maintain adequate systems and controls at all times","A firm must maintain adequate financial resources at all times","A firm must maintain adequate disclosures to clients at all times","A firm must maintain adequate complaints records at all times"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Principle 4 states that a firm must maintain adequate financial resources, which include capital resources and liquidity resources. The other options refer to different regulatory obligations, not Principle 4.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which statement correctly describes how insider lists must be maintained under UK Market Abuse Regulation?","options":["They must be reviewed annually and retained within the firm's compliance manual","They must be maintained separately for each transaction and for permanent insiders","They must be submitted monthly to the Financial Conduct Authority for verification","They must be limited to employees and exclude external professional advisers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The source states that firms are required to maintain separate insider lists for each transaction and one for permanent insiders. The other options add requirements or exclusions that are not stated in the provided material.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"A firm chooses to comply with the client limit order rules by transmitting an unexecuted order elsewhere. Which destination would satisfy that obligation?","options":["To the client's custodian using a standard settlement instruction","To a regulated market operating an order book trading system","To the firm's compliance department using an internal escalation process","To the issuer's registrar using a written notification procedure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Transmitting the order to a regulated market or multilateral trading facility operating an order book trading system is a permitted way to make the order public. The other destinations do not make the order public in the required manner.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the cross-cutting rules of the FCA Consumer Duty?","options":["Act in good faith towards retail customers","Avoid causing foreseeable harm to retail customers","Enable and support retail customers to pursue financial objectives","Any advice is suitable and takes account of the retail customer's circumstances"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"This is a tricky question, requiring knowledge of the exact wording of the Consumer Duty cross-cutting rules. 'Any advice is suitable and takes account of the retail customer's circumstances' is very similar to outcome 4 of the Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC) objectives, which have been replaced by the Consumer Duty cross-cutting rules for in-scope firms. Whilst in-scope firms would certainly need to consider suitability of advice under the Consumer Duty, this is not one of the cross-cutting rules.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A firm wishes to make an unwritten promotion to a particular person outside its premises. Which action is required at the outset of the communication?","options":["It must explain the complaint process and compensation arrangements in full","It must identify the caller and the firm and make the reason for contact clear","It must provide a written summary of risks before continuing the conversation","It must confirm the client category and appropriateness status before proceeding"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For an unwritten promotion outside the firm's premises, the caller must identify themselves and their firm at the outset and make the reason for the contact clear. The other options are not stated as required opening steps for the communication.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"The FCA has found that a firm's structured product contains unacceptable risks to consumers. Under the powers granted by the Financial Services Act 2012, which of the following steps can the FCA take to manage these risks?","options":["The FCA can ban the product for up to six months without consultation with the firm","The FCA can ban the product for up to 12 months, but only after consultation with the firm","The FCA can ban the product for up to six months, but only after consultation with the firm","The FCA can ban the product for up to 12 months without consultation with the firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FCA has been given new enforcement powers by the Financial Services Act 2012 (FSA 2012). These powers include the ability to ban financial products posing unacceptable risks to consumers. The ban can last for up to 12 months without consultation.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Firms holding client money or investments for retail clients, professional clients and ECPs must provide all of the following information, EXCEPT:","options":["The investments/money may be held by a third party on the firm’s behalf","Where investments are held overseas, the extent to which overseas laws will apply","A summary of steps the firm has taken to protect the client's money and/or investments","That the firm will guarantee any losses should the third party become insolvent"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Although a firm need to illustrate how it will guard against losses and explain what will happen if the thrid party becomes insolvent, there is no requirement for them to guarantee any losses.\nInformation should include:\n1. that the investments/money may be held by a third party on the firm’s behalf\n2. what the firm’s responsibility is for any acts and/or omissions of that third party\n3. what will happen if the third party were to become insolvent\n4. if the investments cannot be separately designated in the country in which they are held by a third party, what this means for, and what the risks are to the client\n5. that the investments are subject to the laws of a non-UK jurisdiction and what this means for their clients’ rights over them\n6. a summary of the steps the firm has taken to protect the client’s money and/or investments, including details of any relevant investor compensation or deposit guarantee schemes available to them\n7. if the money or investments are subject to any security interest, lien or right of set-off, full details of this fact and the terms of it, and\n8. full and clear information, in a durable medium, in good time before entering into securities detailing clearly what the firm’s obligations are with regard to those investments and what the risks may be.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Chinese walls have been breached and one particular employee has received sensitive information with regards to a firm takeover. What should the firm do?","options":["Send the employee on a holiday until the takeover is completed","Allow the employee to deal, ignoring what he knows","Restrict the employee from advising clients if related to the target company","Discipline the employee and consider removing their approved person status"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The firm needs to manage any conflicts of interest. Chinese walls are one method of doing so. A breach of these is not necessarily the fault of the employee, so discipline would be inappropriate, but other measures should be taken to make sure the employee does not allow the information to conflict with his duties to the client. The best solution to this would be to prevent the employee from advising clients if the content relates to the takeover target.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Where an authorised firm appoints an appointed representative to conduct regulated activities on its behalf, PERG 2.10 states that the appointed representative does not require Part 4A permission. Which of the following best indicates the reason for this?","options":["Appointed representatives pose no risk of harm to clients through the business they are permitted to conduct so do not need authorisation","The authorised person becomes the principal and accepts legal responsibility for the regulated activity conducted by the appointed representative","Appointed representatives, instead of authorisation, receive 'appointment' from the relevant regulator in order to conduct regulated activities","Appointed representatives are natural persons and, as such, are unable to apply for Part 4A permission from the regulators"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An appointed representative can be any type of person (ie, a natural person or any other legal person) who has entered into a contract with an authorised person (the principal) for the purpose of conducting regulated activity. The authorised person becomes the principal and accepts legal responsibility for the regulated activity conducted by the appointed representative. In this way, the appointed representative becomes an exempt person under FSMA for the conduct of permitted regulated activity.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"In relation to the best execution rules in the conduct of business sourcebook, is it acceptable for a firm to charge different amounts for conducting the same trade on TWO different execution venues?","options":["Yes - provided disclosure is made to the client","Yes - provided the difference between the charges represents differences in trading costs on the execution venues","No - it is never acceptable","No - it is not acceptable when there is only a choice of two venues"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The COBs state that the firm must not structure its charges or its commissions in such a way as to discriminate unfairly between execution venues.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Following Brexit, what happened to existing direct EU legislation that applied in the UK financial services sector?","options":["It ceased to apply until replaced by FCA Handbook rules","It was converted into UK law by the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018","It continued to apply only where endorsed by ESMA and the EBA","It was replaced automatically by equivalent PRA supervisory statements"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018 converted existing direct EU legislation into UK law and preserved existing UK laws implementing EU obligations. The other options misstate the mechanism or the institutions involved.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"The Financial Conduct Authority's Principles for Businesses do NOT include which of the following statements? A firm must:","options":["Deal with its regulators in an open and cooperative way","Pay due regard to the interests of its customers and treat them fairly","Operate an appropriate and effective complaints procedure","Take reasonable care to organise and control its affairs responsibly and effectively"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Complaints are not covered. They are dealt with in a separate block of the FCA Handbook.","chId":"reg-ch1"},{"question":"A person buys an unusually large number of a particular share near the end of the day. Which of the following is most likely true in relation to market abuse?","options":["The person is attempting to drive the closing price for the share down","The result will give a more accurate impression of market values","The trade could lead to investors making the wrong decision","The person is disseminating false information to mislead the market"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"If a person buys a large number of a particular share near the end of the day, they may be aiming to drive the stock price higher to improve the performance of their investment. The market price is pushed to an artificial level and investors get a false impression of the price of those shares and the value of any portfolio or fund that holds the stock. This could lead to people making the wrong investment decisions. Although it is misleading the market, it is not dissemination.","chId":"reg-ch3"},{"question":"Which of the following firms would be required to comply with the UK Conduct of Business rules?","options":["A UK subsidiary of a foreign firm writing life assurance business for UK clients","A Japanese bank arranging hedging strategies for a UK based company","A French company conducting business with a German company","A UK bank arranging a commercial loan for a client in the UK"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The COBS apply to all firms conducting designated investment business and issuing contracts of life assurance in the UK. The UK subsidiary offering services to UK clients will be covered by UK COBS. Hedging and commercial loans are not designated investments, so not covered by COBS. The French and German companies are clearly not bound by UK COBS.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"Which one of these statements is false in relation to a firm which holds itself out as giving independent advice on packaged products?","options":["It must require the client to pay for its advice with an up-front fee","Its scope of advice may cover the whole market","It must offer the client the opportunity to pay for its advice with an up-front fee","Its scope of advice may cover the whole of a sector of the market"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The client does not have to pay for the advice up-front only. The client can pay over the life of the investment instead.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"A firm is assessing whether an investment activity falls within the scope of the Consumer Duty. Which customer is within scope for investments?","options":["A customer who is not a professional client","A customer classified as an eligible counterparty","A customer who has elected to be a professional client","A customer dealing only in financial instruments over £50,000"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"For investments under COBS, the Duty applies to business conducted with a customer who is not a professional client. Customers who elect to be treated as professional clients are not in scope for the Duty, although the process for determining status is in scope.","chId":"reg-ch2"},{"question":"If client A is advised by firm B on his investments, and then client A asks firm C to execute a trade for him, then firm C should keep a recording of the conversation between whom?","options":["Between A and C covering the instruction to execute the trade","Between C and the administration department that processes the trade","Between C and the floor trader who executes the trade","Between Firm C's financial analysts, to avoid any potential conflicts of interest"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The question relates to record keeping and client orders. Of the four options, the conversations between C and its administration department and the floor trader can be immediately discarded. With regard to record keeping, the need is to record the order and the conversation between client A and firm C is the initiation of the order.","chId":"reg-ch2"}]}],"glossaryFlashcards":[{"front":"Authorised firm","back":"A firm granted Part 4A permission by the FCA/PRA to carry on one or more regulated activities in the UK. Key points: Gains status by applying under Part 4A FSMA 2000 and demonstrating it is 'fit and proper'; Must comply with the FCA/PRA Handbook rules (Principles, COBS, SYSC, etc.) on an ongoing basis; Contrast with exempt persons (eg RIEs, appointed representatives) who don't need their own authorisation."},{"front":"General Prohibition (s19 FSMA 2000)","back":"The rule that it is a criminal offence to carry on a regulated activity in the UK by way of business without authorisation or exemption. Key points: Max penalty: 2 years' imprisonment + unlimited fine (Crown Court); 6 months + £5,000 (magistrates); Breach can make related contracts voidable at the option of the client (not automatically void); Enforced via FSMA ss401-402, which give the FCA power to prosecute."},{"front":"Regulated activity","back":"An activity specified in the Regulated Activities Order (RAO) 2001, carried out by way of business, that requires authorisation. Key points: Must involve a specified investment (eg shares, bonds, units in a CIS, options on precious metals); Currencies, gold bullion, premium bonds and tangible property are NOT specified investments; Excluded activities (eg newspaper tip sheets, unremunerated trustees) fall outside the regime even if they look similar."},{"front":"Specified investment","back":"An investment type listed in the RAO 2001 that triggers the need for authorisation when dealt in, advised on, or arranged. Key points: Includes shares, debentures, gilts, units in a CIS, options/futures on financial instruments; Excludes currencies, gold/silver bullion itself, and National Savings Certificates; Options on precious metals ARE specified investments, unlike the metals themselves."},{"front":"FCA (Financial Conduct Authority)","back":"The conduct regulator for the whole UK financial services industry and prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms. Key points: Independent private company, funded entirely by fees on regulated firms; 1 strategic objective (markets function well) + 3 operational objectives (consumer protection, integrity, competition); Accountable to UK Government via HM Treasury; Chancellor appoints the CEO and chair; Oversees the FOS and, jointly with the PRA, the FSCS."},{"front":"PRA (Prudential Regulation Authority)","back":"The prudential regulator for banks, building societies, credit unions, insurers and major investment firms. Key points: Part of the Bank of England (via the Prudential Regulation Committee, PRC); Single objective: safety and soundness of PRA-authorised firms, plus an insurance objective; Not directly accountable to any government body — answers to Parliament via the Bank of England; Funded by fees on the firms it regulates, same as the FCA."},{"front":"Twin peaks regulation","back":"The post-2013 UK model where two regulators (FCA and PRA) oversee the same larger firms from different angles. Key points: PRA = prudential (financial safety/soundness); FCA = conduct and market integrity; Applies to dual-regulated firms (banks, insurers, major investment firms); Solo-regulated firms (eg most asset managers, stockbrokers) only answer to the FCA, for both conduct AND prudential matters."},{"front":"Financial Policy Committee (FPC)","back":"A Bank of England committee responsible for identifying, monitoring and acting on risks to the whole UK financial system. Key points: Created 2013 under the Financial Services Act 2012; Meets at least 4 times a year; Publishes the Bank of England's biannual Financial Stability Report; Not to be confused with the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC), which sets interest rates and meets 8x/year."},{"front":"HM Treasury (HMT)","back":"The UK government department with overall responsibility for the financial services sector. Key points: FCA reports directly to HMT; Chancellor of the Exchequer appoints the FCA's CEO and chair; Sponsors the underlying legislation (FSMA 2000, Financial Services Act 2012, etc.)."},{"front":"Principles for Businesses (PRIN)","back":"The FCA's 12 high-level, overarching rules that all authorised firms must comply with at all times. Key points: Breach can lead to disciplinary action even without breaching a specific detailed rule; Cover integrity, skill/care/diligence, management and control, customers' interests, market conduct, etc.; Principle 12 (Consumer Duty) disapplies Principles 6 & 7 for in-scope retail business."},{"front":"Consumer Duty","back":"An FCA outcomes-based standard requiring firms to deliver good outcomes for retail customers, live since 2023 (extended to closed products 31 July 2024). Key points: Built around 4 outcomes: products & services, price & value, consumer understanding, consumer support; Replaces Principles 6 and 7 for in-scope retail business; Firms out of scope of the Duty still follow Principle 6 and the Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC) six outcomes."},{"front":"SM&CR (Senior Managers & Certification Regime)","back":"The UK's individual accountability framework, extended to all authorised firms from 2016 onward. Key points: 3 pillars: Senior Managers Regime, Certification Regime, Conduct Rules; Senior Managers are regulator-approved (Form A, 90-day statutory clock, Statement of Responsibilities); Certification is self-certified annually by the firm for 'significant harm' functions; Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators are excluded from the regime."},{"front":"Senior Manager Function (SMF)","back":"A role requiring pre-approval by the regulator because the individual has overall responsibility for a key area of the firm. Key points: All directors (executive, non-executive, Chairman) are always SMFs; Examples: CEO, Chief Risk function, Compliance oversight, MLRO; Subject to a Statement of Responsibilities and the Duty of Responsibility."},{"front":"Certification Function","back":"A role the firm itself certifies (not the regulator) as fit to perform because it could cause significant harm to customers or markets. Key points: Re-certified annually by the firm, not approved individually by the FCA/PRA; Examples: retail customer advisers, financial planners, material risk takers, client dealers; PRA's certification scope is narrower — Material Risk Takers only."},{"front":"Conduct Rules","back":"Baseline standards of individual behaviour under SM&CR, binding from the moment of appointment. Key points: 5 individual rules apply to (almost) everyone: integrity, skill/care/diligence, proper market conduct, client interests, disclosure to regulators; 4 extra rules apply only to Senior Managers; Rule 6 (deliver good Consumer Duty outcomes) replaces Rule 4 where the Duty applies."},{"front":"Fit and Proper Test (FIT)","back":"The assessment the FCA/PRA uses to judge whether an individual is suitable to perform a controlled function. Key points: 3 pillars: honesty/integrity/reputation, competence/capability, financial soundness; Considers bankruptcy, criminal convictions, exam failures, refusal by trade bodies; No fixed time limit — a past conviction can still be taken into account if relevant."},{"front":"Part 4A Permission","back":"The specific grant from the FCA/PRA that authorises a firm to carry on particular regulated activities. Key points: Distinct from 'authorisation' itself — authorisation confers status, Part 4A permission defines what you can actually do; Firms must demonstrate they are fit and proper (adequate resources, competent management); Can be varied or cancelled by the regulator."},{"front":"Recognised Investment Exchange (RIE)","back":"An exchange granted recognised status by the FCA, exempting it from needing its own Part 4A authorisation. Key points: Must be assessed as fit and proper by the FCA to gain recognition; Membership of an RIE does NOT itself authorise a firm to do investment business; Examples: London Stock Exchange, ICE Futures, LME."},{"front":"Multilateral Trading Facility (MTF)","back":"A system that brings together multiple buyers and sellers of financial instruments, similar to an exchange but operated by a firm. Key points: The operator does not take proprietary positions against client orders; Client orders are matched against each other under non-discretionary rules; Distinct from a Recognised Investment Exchange, which has separate recognised status."},{"front":"Appointed Representative (AR)","back":"A firm or person carrying on regulated activities under the umbrella of an authorised 'principal' firm, without needing its own authorisation. Key points: The principal firm takes full responsibility for the AR's regulated activities; COBS applies to ARs only indirectly, through the principal's obligation to ensure compliance; Excluded from the SM&CR regime."},{"front":"Designated Professional Body (DPB)","back":"A professional body (eg for lawyers, accountants, actuaries) whose members can carry out limited regulated activities without full FCA authorisation. Key points: Exemption only applies where the regulated activity is incidental to the main professional business; Members lose the exemption if separately remunerated for that activity; Membership does NOT itself authorise members, nor exempt them from FCA rules where the exemption doesn't apply."},{"front":"Retail client","back":"The default, most-protected client categorisation under COBS — any client who is not a professional client or eligible counterparty. Key points: Gets the fullest set of protections: suitability reports, cancellation rights, charges disclosure, etc.; Local authorities and municipalities are treated as retail clients under MiFID II; Can be reclassified 'up' to professional only after a firm assessment and written warning of lost protections."},{"front":"Professional client","back":"A client presumed to have the experience and knowledge to make investment decisions without full retail-level protection. Key points: 'Per se' professional clients (eg large undertakings, authorised firms) qualify automatically; 'Elective' professional clients opt up after passing a qualitative test (and, for MiFID business, a quantitative test too); MiFID quantitative test: 10+ trades/quarter, portfolio > €500,000, 1+ year relevant professional experience (2 of 3 needed)."},{"front":"Eligible counterparty","back":"The least-protected client category — typically other regulated firms, governments and large institutions dealing on a principal basis. Key points: Most COBS selling/advising rules are disapplied for eligible counterparty business; Individuals can never be eligible counterparties, however experienced; An eligible counterparty may ask to be 'opted down' to professional or retail client protection."},{"front":"Best execution","back":"The obligation on a firm to take all sufficient steps to obtain the best possible result for a client when executing orders. Key points: Applies to all types of financial instruments, not just certain classes; For retail clients, 'best result' factors in total consideration: price + execution costs (but not the firm's own charges); Firms must have, and review, an order execution policy setting out how execution factors are weighted."},{"front":"Client money","back":"Money a firm holds on behalf of a client that must be kept segregated from the firm's own money under CASS. Key points: Must be segregated from the firm's own money, but the client money rules apply to money owed to any type of client; Money due to the firm itself (eg fees) is not client money; Can be deposited with a central bank, an approved credit institution, or a qualifying money market fund."},{"front":"Chinese wall","back":"An information barrier — physical or procedural — preventing the flow of confidential information between different parts of a firm. Key points: A recognised method of managing conflicts of interest, not an FCA mandatory requirement; Firms must still have adequate arrangements to manage the flow of information even without formal walls; Common between, eg, corporate finance and equity research/sales desks."},{"front":"Churning","back":"Excessive, unjustified dealing in a client's portfolio by a firm, primarily to generate commission. Key points: Relevant mainly to discretionary management, where the firm controls trading frequency; Considered a breach of the suitability/customer's-interests principle, not a standalone rule violation; Related concept: 'switching' — unjustified movement between similar packaged products."},{"front":"Cold calling","back":"An unsolicited, real-time (non-written) financial promotion — eg an unexpected phone call or doorstep visit. Key points: Written communications (including email) are never classed as cold calls; Only permitted in limited circumstances: existing client relationship expecting the call, or certain low-risk packaged products; Caller must identify themselves and the firm, and give a contact point."},{"front":"Financial promotion","back":"Any invitation or inducement to engage in investment activity, communicated in the course of business. Key points: Can be real-time (calls, visits) or non-real-time (adverts, websites, mailshots); Non-real-time promotions must be signed off in advance by someone with compliance expertise; Must always be fair, clear and not misleading — a standalone requirement under COBS 4."},{"front":"Suitability","back":"The requirement that a personal recommendation or discretionary decision matches the client's objectives, financial situation and understanding of risk. Key points: Applies to advisory and discretionary services — not execution-only; A suitability report is required for, eg, pension transfers, regulated CIS recommendations, and investment trust recommendations; If a client won't give enough information, the firm must not make the recommendation at all."},{"front":"Appropriateness","back":"A lighter-touch check (versus suitability) confirming a client has enough knowledge/experience to understand the risks of a non-advised, complex product. Key points: Applies to non-advised services (eg execution-only, arranging) in complex products such as derivatives and warrants; Applies to both retail AND professional clients; Not required for execution-only dealing in non-complex products (only name & address needed there)."},{"front":"Inducement","back":"A fee, commission or non-monetary benefit paid or received in connection with providing an investment service. Key points: Not banned outright — payments to/from the client itself are allowed; Must be disclosed to the client before the service is provided; 'Minor non-monetary benefits' (eg attending a seminar on a specific product) are exempt from the general prohibition."},{"front":"Conflict of interest","back":"A situation where a firm's (or its staff's) interests could compete with, or diverge from, a client's interests. Key points: Firms must have a conflicts of interest policy managing information flow between business areas; If a conflict can't be managed, the firm must disclose its general nature and source to the client in a durable medium, before acting; Undisclosed conflicts are a common exam trap — eg advising to buy shares the firm itself is short."},{"front":"Aggregation (of orders)","back":"Combining several client orders (or a client order with the firm's own) into a single transaction. Key points: Not mandatory — firms may aggregate only if unlikely to disadvantage any client overall; Must be governed by a written allocation policy; If an aggregated order is only partially filled, the client's allocation takes priority over the firm's own."},{"front":"CASS (Client Assets Sourcebook)","back":"The FCA rulebook setting out how firms must protect and segregate client money and custody assets. Key points: Applies to all clients, not just retail; Requires daily reconciliations of client money held vs client money required; Breach can trigger some of the FCA's most serious enforcement action, given the direct risk to client assets."},{"front":"Market abuse","back":"A civil offence covering behaviour that damages market integrity — eg insider dealing, unlawful disclosure, or manipulation — assessed on the balance of probabilities. Key points: Intention does NOT need to be proved — it is 'effects-based' legislation; Applies to all persons (individuals and firms), UK or overseas, on prescribed/UK markets; Penalties: unlimited fines, public censure, restitution, prohibition orders — but never imprisonment (it's civil, not criminal)."},{"front":"Insider dealing","back":"A criminal offence under the Criminal Justice Act 1993 of dealing in, encouraging dealing in, or disclosing price-sensitive information about, securities. Key points: Requires an individual (not a company) to knowingly possess inside information from an inside source; Covers MiFID instruments (shares, gilts, debentures, related derivatives) — NOT currencies or physical commodities; Special defences: market makers acting in good faith, price stabilisation rules, recipients of market information; General defence: 'would have dealt anyway' regardless of the information."},{"front":"Inside information","back":"Precise, non-public information which, if made public, would be likely to have a significant effect on the price of a security. Key points: Rumours are generally not specific/precise enough to count; Information disclosed via a Regulatory Information Service (RIS) is treated as public; Sharing inside information with a professional adviser in the normal course of business is a permitted disclosure."},{"front":"Market manipulation","back":"Behaviour that gives a false or misleading impression of supply, demand or price — a form of market abuse. Key points: Includes wash trades, abusive squeezes, and disseminating misleading information; 'Misleading statements and impressions' under FSA 2012 s89-91 is the related criminal offence (separate from civil market abuse); Price stabilisation carried out under FCA rules is a recognised safe harbour, not manipulation."},{"front":"Money laundering","back":"The process of making the proceeds of crime appear to come from a legitimate source. Key points: 3 stages: placement, layering, integration; Investment firms are most vulnerable at the layering and integration stages (banks: placement); Covers proceeds of ALL crime, not just drug trafficking — defined broadly under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002."},{"front":"MLRO (Money Laundering Reporting Officer)","back":"The individual every firm must appoint as the internal point of contact for money-laundering suspicions. Key points: A Senior Manager Function — not a member of the National Crime Agency; Reports suspicions on to the NCA, and to the firm's board at least once every 12 months; Employees must report suspicions to the MLRO; only the MLRO has discretion over what gets escalated to the NCA."},{"front":"Politically Exposed Person (PEP)","back":"An individual who holds (or has held) a prominent public position, requiring enhanced due diligence due to corruption risk. Key points: Includes heads of state, ministers, senior judges, ambassadors, and their immediate family; Enhanced due diligence is required because of their vulnerability to corruption — not because they're automatically suspected; Defined under JMLSG Guidance Notes."},{"front":"FATF (Financial Action Task Force)","back":"An inter-governmental body (est. 1989) that sets international standards to combat money laundering and terrorist financing. Key points: Issues the '40 Recommendations' that shape national AML/CTF regimes, including the UK's; Not a UK body and its standards aren't directly binding in the UK — they're implemented via domestic legislation."},{"front":"JMLSG (Joint Money Laundering Steering Group)","back":"An industry body producing AML guidance notes approved by HM Treasury. Key points: Guidance is not mandatory, but courts take adherence to it into account as evidence of compliance; The FCA references JMLSG guidance when assessing a firm's systems and controls (SYSC)."},{"front":"Bribery Act 2010","back":"UK legislation creating offences of bribing another person, being bribed, and a corporate offence of failing to prevent bribery. Key points: The corporate offence has a defence of having 'adequate procedures' in place to prevent bribery; Applies extraterritorially to UK-connected organisations and their associates."},{"front":"Disclosure and Transparency Rules (DTR)","back":"FCA rules requiring listed companies to disclose price-sensitive information promptly and to notify major shareholdings. Key points: Aim to ensure fair and timely access to information that could affect a security's price; Include notification thresholds for significant shareholdings (major shareholding notifications)."},{"front":"FSCS (Financial Services Compensation Scheme)","back":"The UK's fund of last resort, compensating eligible claimants when an authorised firm is unable to meet its liabilities (in default). Key points: Investment claims: max £85,000 per person per firm; Deposit claims: max £120,000 per person per firm (£240,000 for joint accounts); Funded by levies on authorised firms; primarily accountable to the FCA and PRA; Claims must generally be made within 6 years of the event giving rise to them."},{"front":"FOS (Financial Ombudsman Service)","back":"An independent, free dispute-resolution service for eligible complainants once a firm's own complaints process is exhausted. Key points: Operationally independent of the FCA; Can award compensation for financial loss, distress, inconvenience and damage to reputation; Available to individuals and certain small businesses/charities/trusts — never to authorised firms themselves."},{"front":"Eligible complainant","back":"A person or entity entitled to bring a complaint to the Financial Ombudsman Service. Key points: Individuals (consumers), regardless of financial sophistication; Small businesses, charities (income < £6.5m) and trusts below defined size thresholds; Authorised firms are never eligible complainants for FOS purposes."},{"front":"Data Protection principles (UK GDPR / DPA 2018)","back":"The core rules governing how personal data must be collected, used and stored. Key points: Processed lawfully and fairly; adequate, relevant and not excessive; accurate and kept up to date; Data controllers must register with the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO); Breaches are reported to the ICO, not the FCA."},{"front":"Whistleblowing","back":"The protected disclosure of wrongdoing (breach of law, cover-up, danger) by a worker, under the Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998. Key points: Protection applies regardless of where the wrongdoing took place (not limited to the UK); Firms must appoint a whistleblowing champion of sufficient seniority; Settlement agreements must state that workers retain a legal right to blow the whistle — that right can't be contracted away."},{"front":"Consumer Rights Act 2015","back":"Legislation giving the FCA power to challenge unfair terms in financial services consumer contracts. Key points: The 'grey list' of potentially unfair terms is indicative, not exhaustive; A term can still be unfair even if transparent and individually negotiated; FCA remedies include seeking an undertaking to amend/remove a term, or applying to court for an injunction."}],"regulators":[{"id":"hmt","name":"HM Treasury (HMT)","category":"Government","scope":"UK government department with overall responsibility for the financial system, including its institutional structure and the legislation that governs it (FSMA 2000, Financial Services Act 2012, etc.).","accountability":"Answers to Parliament. Sits above the whole regulatory structure — sponsors the legislation everyone else operates under.","powers":["Appoints the FCA's CEO and chair (via the Chancellor of the Exchequer)","Receives the FCA's mandatory annual report","Designates bodies (e.g. under the Consumer Rights Act 2015)"],"traps":["HMT has overall responsibility for the SYSTEM, not day-to-day supervision of firms — that's the FCA/PRA's job.","Don't confuse 'accountable to HMT' (the FCA) with 'accountable to the Court of the Bank of England' (the PRA) — a classic swapped-answer trap."],"related":["fca","pra","tpr"]},{"id":"boe","name":"Bank of England (BoE)","category":"Central bank","scope":"The UK's central bank. Houses the PRA and two policy committees (FPC, MPC). Acts as lender of last resort in a crisis and manages financial/non-financial operations to protect stability, both at home and abroad.","accountability":"Answerable to Parliament, with strong operational independence.","powers":["Sets and implements monetary policy via the MPC","Oversees systemic risk via the FPC","Is the parent body of the PRA (via the Prudential Regulation Committee)"],"traps":["The PRA is often described as 'part of the Bank of England' — some older-style questions even use 'PRA' and 'Bank of England' near-interchangeably for accountability purposes."],"related":["pra","fpc","mpc","hmt"]},{"id":"pra","name":"Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA)","category":"Regulator","scope":"Prudential regulator for banks, building societies, credit unions, insurers and major ('dual-regulated') investment firms. Focuses on the SAFETY AND SOUNDNESS of firms — can they meet their obligations and survive shocks.","accountability":"Accountable to the Court of the Bank of England — NOT directly to HM Treasury or Parliament in the way the FCA is.","powers":["Grants Part 4A permission jointly with the FCA for dual-regulated firms (PRA leads, FCA must consent)","Sets prudential rules (capital, liquidity) via MIFIDPRU / CRD-CRR / IPRU-INV depending on firm type","Can vary or cancel Part 4A permission for prudential reasons"],"traps":["PRA's objective is safety & soundness — NOT consumer protection or market integrity (that's the FCA).","For SOLO-regulated firms, the FCA alone assesses BOTH conduct AND prudential matters — the PRA has no role there."],"related":["boe","fca","hmt"]},{"id":"fca","name":"Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)","category":"Regulator","scope":"Conduct regulator for the ENTIRE financial services industry, and prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms. Also acts as the UK Listing Authority (UKLA).","accountability":"Accountable to the UK Government via HM Treasury. Chancellor appoints CEO/chair. Reports to HMT at least annually.","powers":["Grants Part 4A permission / authorisation","Sets and enforces the Handbook (Rules and Guidance)","Investigates and prosecutes insider dealing (power from FSMA ss401-402) and takes civil action for market abuse","Bans harmful products for up to 12 months without prior consultation","Oversees the FOS; jointly funds/oversees the FSCS with the PRA"],"traps":["FCA prosecutes insider dealing (criminal, via powers from FSMA) but takes CIVIL action for market abuse — don't mix up which offence gets which treatment.","The FCA is a company limited by guarantee, not a government department — but it's still funded entirely by levies on regulated firms, not by taxpayers."],"related":["pra","hmt","fos","fscs","cma"]},{"id":"fpc","name":"Financial Policy Committee (FPC)","category":"Bank of England committee","scope":"Identifies, monitors and acts on risks to the WHOLE UK financial system (macroprudential/systemic risk) — created under the Financial Services Act 2012.","accountability":"A committee of the Bank of England.","powers":["Meets at least 4 times a year","Publishes the Bank's biannual Financial Stability Report","Can direct macroprudential tools (e.g. countercyclical capital buffers)"],"traps":["Constantly confused with the MPC in exam questions: FPC = systemic/financial stability, meets 4x/year, biannual report. MPC = interest rates, meets monthly (8x/year)."],"related":["boe","mpc"]},{"id":"mpc","name":"Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)","category":"Bank of England committee","scope":"Sets UK interest rates and monetary policy to hit the inflation target.","accountability":"A committee of the Bank of England.","powers":["Meets monthly (8x/year) to set the base rate"],"traps":["Do not confuse with the FPC — the MPC is about interest rates/inflation, not systemic financial stability."],"related":["boe","fpc"]},{"id":"cma","name":"Competition and Markets Authority (CMA)","category":"Cross-sector regulator","scope":"NOT a financial regulator — investigates mergers, anti-competitive behaviour and general consumer protection issues across ALL sectors of the UK economy.","accountability":"An independent non-ministerial government department.","powers":["Investigates mergers and abuses of dominant market position","Enforces general competition law","Works closely with the FCA on financial-sector competition issues, but is organisationally separate"],"traps":["Easy to assume the CMA is 'part of' financial regulation because it's mentioned alongside the FCA/PRA — it isn't; it's a general economy-wide body."],"related":["fca"]},{"id":"tpr","name":"The Pensions Regulator (TPR)","category":"Regulator","scope":"Safeguards members of WORK-BASED pension schemes and enforces employer automatic-enrolment duties under the Pensions Act 2008.","accountability":"UK statutory body, reports to Parliament via the Department for Work and Pensions.","powers":["Compliance notices, penalty notices and fines for automatic-enrolment failures"],"traps":["The FCA regulates advisers/providers of PERSONAL pension plans — TPR covers WORK-BASED/occupational schemes. A very common 'which regulator' trap.","ANY employer with at least 1 employee has an auto-enrolment duty — there's no minimum size threshold."],"related":["fca","hmt"]},{"id":"hmrc","name":"HM Revenue & Customs (HMRC)","category":"Government","scope":"UK tax authority. Regulates Individual Savings Accounts (ISAs) since they are a tax wrapper, not an investment product per se.","accountability":"UK government department.","powers":["Sets ISA eligibility/tax rules","Shares information with the FCA/PRA where relevant (e.g. AML)"],"traps":["Who regulates ISAs? Trick question — it's HMRC (tax angle), not the FCA, even though ISAs wrap regulated investments."],"related":["fca","pra"]},{"id":"fscs","name":"Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS)","category":"Compensation & complaints","scope":"Fund of last resort — pays eligible claimants when an authorised firm is unable to meet its liabilities (in default/insolvent).","accountability":"Accountable to the FCA and PRA jointly (and indirectly to HMT).","powers":["Pays up to £85,000 per person per firm for investments","Pays up to £120,000 per person per firm for deposits (£240,000 joint accounts)","Claims generally must be made within 6 years of the triggering event"],"traps":["AUTHORISED FIRMS CAN NEVER CLAIM on the FSCS themselves — a very frequent correct answer.","Don't swap the £85k (investments) and £120k (deposits) limits — they test this constantly."],"related":["fca","pra","fos"]},{"id":"fos","name":"Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS)","category":"Compensation & complaints","scope":"Independent, free dispute-resolution service for eligible complainants once a firm's own internal complaints process has been exhausted.","accountability":"Operationally independent of the FCA, though the FCA sets its jurisdiction rules and can refer 'mass detriment' issues to/from it.","powers":["Can award compensation for financial loss, distress, inconvenience and reputational damage","Eligible: consumers, micro-enterprises (<10 employees, turnover/balance sheet ≤ €2m), charities (<£6.5m income), trusts (<£5m net assets)"],"traps":["Authorised firms and large corporates are NEVER eligible complainants to the FOS.","A professional client CAN still complain to the FOS about an activity OUTSIDE the one for which they were classified professional — categorisation isn't an automatic bar."],"related":["fca","fscs"]},{"id":"nca","name":"National Crime Agency (NCA)","category":"Law enforcement","scope":"UK law-enforcement body that receives and investigates suspicious activity reports (SARs) relating to money laundering and terrorist financing.","accountability":"UK law enforcement, independent of the FCA.","powers":["Receives MLRO reports of suspicious transactions","Investigates and can refer for prosecution under POCA 2002"],"traps":["Employees report suspicions to their firm's MLRO — NOT directly to the NCA. Only the MLRO escalates to the NCA."],"related":["mlro","fca"]},{"id":"mlro","name":"Money Laundering Reporting Officer (MLRO)","category":"Firm-level role","scope":"Mandatory internal role at every authorised firm — the point of contact for money-laundering suspicions raised by staff.","accountability":"A Senior Manager Function within the firm; reports externally to the NCA.","powers":["Decides which internal suspicions get escalated to the NCA (discretion sits with the MLRO, not the employee)","Must brief senior management/the board at least once every 12 months","Must arrange AML training at least every 24 months (failure here can excuse staff who weren't trained)"],"traps":["The MLRO is NOT a member of the NCA — a common wrong-answer option.","MLRO is always a Senior Manager Function — never merely a certified function."],"related":["nca","fca"]},{"id":"ico","name":"Information Commissioner's Office (ICO)","category":"Regulator","scope":"Enforces the UK GDPR / Data Protection Act 2018 and the Freedom of Information Act 2000.","accountability":"Independent regulator, reports to Parliament (not to the FCA).","powers":["Issues fines for data protection breaches","Serves enforcement notices where a data controller has contravened data protection principles"],"traps":["Data breaches are ALWAYS reported to the ICO — never to the FCA, even for a firm regulated by the FCA.","The ICO does not impose custodial sentences."],"related":["fca"]},{"id":"jmlsg","name":"Joint Money Laundering Steering Group (JMLSG)","category":"Industry guidance body","scope":"Industry body (leading UK trade associations) that publishes AML/CTF guidance, endorsed by HM Treasury.","accountability":"Private-sector body — its guidance is NOT part of the FCA Handbook and is NOT legally binding.","powers":["Publishes risk-based AML guidance firms can choose to follow"],"traps":["Following JMLSG guidance is a strong DEFENCE if a firm is investigated, but it's guidance, not law — a firm cannot be disciplined simply for deviating from it, provided its own risk-based approach is sound."],"related":["fca","nca"]},{"id":"fatf","name":"Financial Action Task Force (FATF)","category":"International","scope":"Inter-governmental body (est. 1989) that sets INTERNATIONAL standards to combat money laundering and terrorist financing.","accountability":"Not a UK body — its standards aren't directly binding here; they shape UK law indirectly via domestic legislation.","powers":["Issues the '40 Recommendations' — the global AML/CTF benchmark","Continuously adapts its standards to address new/emerging risks"],"traps":["FATF does not itself prosecute or lead investigations into drugs/human trafficking — it sets standards and influences legislative reform, nothing more direct."],"related":["jmlsg","nca"]},{"id":"bis","name":"Bank for International Settlements (BIS)","category":"International","scope":"'The central bank for central banks' — sets prudential/capital standards (via the Basel Committee) that feed into UK prudential rules like the CRD/CRR framework.","accountability":"International body, not a UK regulator.","powers":["Publishes the Basel Accords, shaping global bank capital requirements"],"traps":["Not to be confused with FATF (AML focus) or IOSCO (securities focus) — BIS is specifically about BANKING prudential standards."],"related":["pra"]},{"id":"iosco","name":"International Organization of Securities Commissions (IOSCO)","category":"International","scope":"Sets international standards for securities market regulation.","accountability":"International body of securities regulators (the FCA is a member).","powers":["Publishes principles that influence national securities regulation, including UK rules on market conduct and transparency"],"traps":["Non-binding internationally — influences UK regulation indirectly, same pattern as FATF and BIS."],"related":["fca"]},{"id":"rdc","name":"Regulatory Decision Committee (RDC)","category":"FCA internal body","scope":"The FCA's internal committee that makes contested regulatory decisions on the FCA's behalf (e.g. issuing decision notices), separate from the FCA staff who investigated the case.","accountability":"Part of the FCA's governance structure, but operates at arm's length from the investigating team to ensure fairness.","powers":["Sends decision notices following an FCA investigation","Considers representations from the firm/individual before deciding"],"traps":["Enforcement order: FCA investigates → preliminary findings letter → RDC decision notice → disciplinary action. Getting this sequence right is a recurring question type."],"related":["fca"]},{"id":"tribunal","name":"Upper Tribunal (Tax and Chancery Chamber)","category":"Appeals body","scope":"Independent tribunal that hears appeals against FCA/PRA regulatory decisions (e.g. refusal of Part 4A permission, market abuse penalties, prohibition orders).","accountability":"Part of the UK courts/tribunals system — independent of the regulators.","powers":["Can overturn or vary a regulator's decision on appeal"],"traps":["You CANNOT appeal there against penalties set by the courts under the Criminal Justice Act (that's a criminal court matter) — only against REGULATORY decisions (Part 4A, market abuse, listing, approved-person status)."],"related":["fca","pra"]},{"id":"takeover-panel","name":"Takeover Panel","category":"Sector-specific regulator","scope":"Regulates takeovers and mergers of UK public companies under the Takeover Code — separate from the FCA's financial promotion / market abuse rules.","accountability":"Independent statutory body.","powers":["Enforces disclosure and conduct rules during takeover situations","Acting in accordance with the Takeover Code is a recognised defence against market abuse allegations in that context"],"traps":["Communications relating to a takeover bid are governed by the Takeover Code, NOT the FCA's financial promotion rules — a frequently tested distinction."],"related":["fca"]}],"clientRules":[{"id":"suitability","name":"Suitability assessment","appliesTo":["retail","professional"],"detail":"Required for ADVISORY and DISCRETIONARY services. For MiFID business, applies to both retail and professional clients. For non-MiFID business, only retail clients get this protection.","depth":"Per se professional client: firm only needs to ascertain investment objectives. Elective professional client: objectives + financial situation (knowledge/experience is assumed). Retail client: all three — objectives, financial situation, AND knowledge/experience.","traps":["Never required for eligible counterparty business — ECPs are excluded from the suitability rule entirely.","If a client won't give enough information, the firm must NOT make the recommendation at all — it can't just proceed with a disclaimer."]},{"id":"appropriateness","name":"Appropriateness check","appliesTo":["retail","professional"],"detail":"Applies to NON-ADVISED services (execution-only/arranging) in COMPLEX products only (derivatives, warrants, structured products with embedded derivatives). Applies to BOTH retail and professional clients equally.","depth":"Not required at all for execution-only in non-complex products (name and address only). Not required for eligible counterparty business.","traps":["A common trap answer claims appropriateness is 'retail only' — it isn't; professional clients get it too for complex, non-advised business."]},{"id":"client-agreement","name":"Client agreement (durable medium)","appliesTo":["retail","professional"],"detail":"Retail clients: required for ALL designated investment business. Professional clients: only required for MiFID business (and ancillary services).","depth":"Not required where the firm acts as principal in an insurance contract, or for advice-only services with no resultant deals.","traps":["Websites are NEVER a valid durable medium for this — only paper, email, CD-ROM/DVD, etc."]},{"id":"cancellation-rights","name":"Cancellation / withdrawal rights","appliesTo":["retail"],"detail":"Retail clients only. 14 calendar days standard (packaged products, cash ISAs); 30 calendar days for life policies and most pension products.","depth":"Purpose: give the client an opportunity to reconsider within a specified period — not a general right to walk away from any contract at any time.","traps":["Professional clients and eligible counterparties do NOT get statutory cancellation rights under COBS."]},{"id":"periodic-statements","name":"Periodic statements","appliesTo":["retail","professional"],"detail":"Sent at least every 3 months by default for any client with a managed portfolio. Leveraged/geared portfolios need MONTHLY statements regardless of client type.","depth":"If the client gets deal-by-deal confirmations instead, periodic statements can go out just annually.","traps":["The monthly-for-leveraged-portfolios rule applies to ANY client type, not just retail — a frequent trap."]},{"id":"financial-promotions","name":"Financial promotion protections","appliesTo":["retail"],"detail":"The 'fair, clear and not misleading' rule and most financial promotion protections are aimed at RETAIL clients. Eligible counterparties are excluded from most of these protections.","depth":"Promotions to an eligible counterparty do not need prior approval in the way retail-directed promotions do.","traps":["'A financial promotion to which of these groups does not require approval prior to communication?' → eligible counterparty, virtually every time this question type appears."]},{"id":"product-disclosure","name":"Product disclosure (KIDs / KIIDs)","appliesTo":["retail"],"detail":"Key Information Documents / Key Investor Information Documents are a RETAIL-CLIENT-ONLY requirement.","depth":"Purpose: ensure the client has sufficient relevant information to make an informed investment decision before committing.","traps":["Professional clients and ECPs do not get KIDs/KIIDs — don't extend this protection to them."]},{"id":"best-execution","name":"Best execution","appliesTo":["retail","professional"],"detail":"Applies to ALL types of financial instruments, for retail AND professional clients. Retail clients specifically get a 'total consideration' test (price + execution costs, excluding the firm's own charges).","depth":"Does not really bite for eligible counterparty business given most COBS selling/advising rules are disapplied there.","traps":["If a client gives a specific instruction that conflicts with the execution policy, the firm must follow the CLIENT'S instruction — and is deemed to have achieved best execution by doing so."]},{"id":"client-money","name":"Client money rules (CASS)","appliesTo":["retail","professional","ecp"],"detail":"Apply to ALL clients — retail, professional, AND eligible counterparty money must be segregated from the firm's own money.","depth":"Exception: a professional client or ECP can sign a written agreement specifically OPTING OUT of client money protection.","traps":["The single most common trap in this whole topic: people assume client money rules are 'retail only'. They are NOT — this is a rare case where ECPs get full protection by default."]},{"id":"cold-calling","name":"Cold calling restrictions","appliesTo":["retail"],"detail":"The cold calling rules (real-time unsolicited promotions) exist to protect RETAIL clients specifically.","depth":"Permitted only for: an existing client who envisages the call; generally marketable non-geared packaged products; or readily realisable securities (excluding warrants).","traps":["Emails are NEVER classed as a 'cold call' — only unwritten/real-time communications count (phone, in-person)."]},{"id":"consumer-duty","name":"Consumer Duty (Principle 12)","appliesTo":["retail"],"detail":"Applies to RETAIL customers only, for products and services within its scope. Professional clients and eligible counterparties are outside its scope.","depth":"A customer who has ELECTED to be treated as a professional client falls OUTSIDE the Duty's scope for investments — but the process of determining that status is itself within scope.","traps":["Wholesale/manufacturing firms with no direct retail relationship can still be caught by the Duty if their actions materially influence retail customer outcomes further down the distribution chain."]},{"id":"fos-eligibility","name":"Financial Ombudsman Service — who can complain","appliesTo":["retail"],"detail":"Eligible complainants: consumers (individuals), micro-enterprises (<10 employees, turnover/balance sheet ≤ €2m), charities with income < £6.5m, and trustees of trusts with net assets < £5m.","depth":"A professional client or ECP CAN still complain to the FOS about an activity OUTSIDE the one for which they were classified — classification for one purpose doesn't block eligibility for everything.","traps":["Authorised firms and large corporates are NEVER FOS-eligible complainants, no matter their situation."]},{"id":"fscs-eligibility","name":"FSCS — who can claim","appliesTo":["retail","professional"],"detail":"Mainly protects retail clients, but professional clients and even some eligible counterparties CAN claim in certain circumstances (particularly for insurance business).","depth":"Authorised firms themselves can NEVER claim on the FSCS, regardless of client categorisation.","traps":["Don't assume FSCS = 'retail only'. It's broader than that, just with retail as the primary beneficiary."]},{"id":"recat-up","name":"Who can request re-categorisation UP (more risk, less protection)?","appliesTo":["retail","professional"],"detail":"A retail client can ask to become an elective professional client (must pass qualitative test + quantitative test for MiFID business). A professional client can ask to become an elective eligible counterparty.","depth":"The FIRM must give a clear written warning of the protections that will be lost, and the client must give written consent separate from the general contract terms.","traps":["Individuals can NEVER become eligible counterparties, however experienced or wealthy — this caps how far an individual can 'opt up'."]},{"id":"recat-down","name":"Who can request re-categorisation DOWN (more protection)?","appliesTo":["professional","ecp"],"detail":"A per se eligible counterparty can ask to be treated as a professional client (or, with agreement, as a retail client) for some or all business. A professional client can ask to be treated as retail.","depth":"The firm must gain client agreement on the re-categorisation — it can't just unilaterally reclassify someone downward without their consent.","traps":["This is the RIGHT of ANY client to ask for MORE protection at any time — always available, unlike opting up which requires passing tests."]},{"id":"independent-advice","name":"'Independent advice' label — diversity requirement","appliesTo":["retail"],"detail":"To call itself independent when advising a RETAIL client on retail investment products, a firm's advice must be sufficiently diverse across product types AND providers — and NOT limited to products from the firm itself or connected entities.","depth":"This is a COBS 6.2B.11 requirement specific to retail investment product advice.","traps":["'Independent' does not require the client to pay an up-front-only fee — ongoing fee arrangements are fine as long as an ongoing service is genuinely provided."]},{"id":"eligible-counterparty-scope","name":"What COBS rules actually apply to eligible counterparty business?","appliesTo":["ecp"],"detail":"Most COBS selling/advising rules (suitability, appropriateness, financial promotions) are DISAPPLIED for ECP business. What still applies: client categorisation rules AND client money/asset rules.","depth":"An ECP is only an ECP for ECP-specific dealing business — for ADVISORY services, the same client is automatically treated as a per se PROFESSIONAL client instead.","traps":["Individuals, however sophisticated, can never be ECPs. Local authorities are RETAIL, not ECP, under MiFID II."]}]};
